window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("model-internals", {"topics":[{"key":"model-internals/transformer","sources":["Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Radford et al., 2019 — Language Models are Unsupervised Multitask Learners","Touvron et al., 2023 — Llama 2: Open Foundation and Fine-Tuned Chat Models"],"why":"- **Recurrence is sequential.** Before 2017 sequence models were recurrent. An LSTM reads token *t* only\n  after finishing token *t−1*, so a 1,000-token document is 1,000 dependent steps. You cannot parallelize\n  across positions inside a sequence, so GPUs sit mostly idle and wall-clock time grows with length.\n- **Long-range signal decays.** Information from position 1 reaches position 1,000 only by surviving 999\n  updates of a fixed-size hidden vector. Gradients through that path shrink, so long-range dependencies\n  are learned badly or not at all.\n- **Earlier fixes solved only half.** Attention bolted onto RNNs for translation let the decoder look back\n  at every encoder state instead of one summary vector, but the recurrent backbone kept the first problem.\n  Convolutions (ByteNet, ConvS2S) parallelize, yet connecting positions *n* apart needs O(n/k) stacked\n  layers of kernel width *k*, or O(log n) with dilation, so distance still costs depth.\n- **The bet: attention does all the mixing.** Drop recurrence and convolution, so every position in a\n  layer is computed at once and any two positions are one layer apart. The price is attention cost that\n  grows with the square of sequence length and no built-in sense of order, which positional encodings\n  must supply.\n- **Why the bet paid off.** The binding constraint on quality turned out to be how much data and compute\n  you can push through training, and a matmul-parallel architecture scales that far better than a\n  sequential one. For agents it also means a tool result from step 1 is one attention hop from step 20.","how":"### Background: the block\nToken ids go through an embedding table to give an `[n, d_model]` matrix, with position information\nadded or injected into attention. Then *L* identical blocks run in sequence, each with two sub-layers:\n\n1. **Attention sub-layer.** Every position builds a query, scores it against the keys of all positions it\n   may see, and takes the softmax-weighted sum of their values. This is the *only* place where tokens\n   exchange information. Multi-head attention runs several of these in parallel in different subspaces.\n2. **Feed-forward sub-layer (MLP).** Applied to every position independently with the same weights:\n   `FFN(x) = W₂·act(W₁·x)`, inner width about 4×d_model (roughly 2.7× for a gated SwiGLU with three\n   matrices, as in Llama).\n\nWhy the MLP is needed:\n- **Attention can route but not compute.** Its only nonlinearity is the softmax over scores, and its\n  output is a weighted average of linearly projected values. It blends information but cannot compute a\n  new feature of a single token.\n- **The MLP does that work.** Per-position computation, and most of the parameters, live there; it holds\n  roughly two thirds of a layer's weights.\n\n### Background: residuals and normalization\nEach sub-layer is wrapped as `x = x + SubLayer(Norm(x))`.\n\n| Choice | What it is | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| Residual connection | add the sub-layer output back to its input | gives gradients an identity path through 30–100 layers |\n| Residual stream | a width-d_model vector each sub-layer reads and writes a small update into | the useful mental picture of the whole stack |\n| Post-norm (original paper) | normalize *after* the addition | puts a norm on the main path, fragile when deep |\n| Pre-norm (GPT-2 and nearly everything since) | normalize *before* the sub-layer | keeps the identity path clean, trains deep stacks without delicate warmup schedules |\n| RMSNorm (Llama-style) | LayerNorm without mean-centering | cheaper and works as well |\n\n### Background: data flow with shapes\nFor one sequence of *n* tokens:\n1. ids `[n]` → embedding `[n, d]`.\n2. *L* blocks, each mapping `[n, d]` to `[n, d]`. Attention internally builds `[h, n, n]` scores; the MLP\n   briefly widens to `[n, d_ff]`.\n3. Final norm `[n, d]` → LM head `[n, V]`.\n\nBecause every block preserves the shape, depth is just a loop, and every block reads and writes the same\nresidual stream.\n\n### Background: the head\n- **Logits.** After the last block: a final norm, then a linear map from d_model to vocabulary size (the\n  LM head, often weight-tied to the input embedding). A softmax turns logits into a next-token\n  distribution. Tool-call JSON, structured outputs and plain text are all sampled from this one head.\n- **Dense training signal.** Training predicts token *t+1* at every position *t* in the same forward pass\n  under a causal mask, so one pass over a 4k-token sequence yields 4k training signals. That density of\n  signal per FLOP is a large part of why the architecture scales.\n\n### Three layouts, and which agent step uses which\n| Layout | Attention | Good for | Limitation | Agent use |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Encoder–decoder** (original, T5) | bidirectional encoder; causal decoder plus cross-attention into the encoder output | translation, where input and output are distinct sequences | two stacks to scale | rare; some summarization or translation tools |\n| **Encoder-only** (BERT) | bidirectional, every token sees every other | classification and embeddings | cannot generate text autoregressively | retrieval embeddings, rerankers, intent routers, guardrail classifiers |\n| **Decoder-only** (GPT, Llama, most chat models) | one causal stack | generation, general-purpose LLMs | sees only left context | the planner and tool caller itself |\n\nDecoder-only won for LLMs because:\n- **One component to scale**, trained on raw unlabeled text with one objective.\n- **Prompt and completion share one stream**, so any task becomes conditioning. This is why an agent can\n  be \"just a prompt plus a transcript\": tool schemas and results are conditioning text.\n- **Causality enables the KV cache** at inference, which is what makes append-only agent transcripts cheap.\n\n### Where parameters and FLOPs go\nPer layer with hidden size *d*: the four attention projections cost 4d² parameters; a 4× MLP costs 8d²;\na SwiGLU MLP costs 3·d·d_ff.\n\n| Llama-2-7B (d = 4096, L = 32, d_ff = 11008, vocab 32,000) | Parameters |\n|---|---|\n| Attention per layer | 67.1M |\n| MLP per layer | 135.3M |\n| All layers | 202.4M × 32 = 6.48B |\n| Two untied embedding matrices | 131M each |\n| Total | ≈ 6.74B |\n\n- **Forward compute ≈ 2 FLOPs per parameter per token**, because each weight takes part in one\n  multiply-accumulate.\n- **Plus an attention-score term** of roughly 4·n·d per layer per token (QKᵀ and AV against *n* earlier\n  positions).\n- **Crossover.** Against a standard block's 12d² weights, that is 24d² versus 4·n·d. The attention term\n  reaches a sixth of the weight FLOPs at n = d and equals them at n ≈ 6d, about 24k tokens for d = 4096.\n- **Rules of thumb.** Inference ≈ 2N FLOPs per token; training ≈ 6N per token (forward plus a backward\n  that costs about twice the forward). These let you estimate cost before touching a GPU.\n\n### What this means for an agent run\n- **Cost scales with transcript tokens.** Each step's new tokens cost about 2N each, plus an attention\n  term that grows with how far into the transcript they sit. Late steps in a long run are dearer per token.\n- **Order is causal.** A token sees only what came before it, so instructions placed after a long tool\n  result are read with that result in view, while the result was encoded without them.\n- **Nothing is remembered between calls.** The weights are fixed; the stream is the only memory. Anything\n  the agent must know at step 20 has to be in the transcript, retrieved, or summarised into it.\n\n### Background: what the pieces learn\n- **Attention heads route.** Interpretability work finds heads that attend to the previous token, or find\n  an earlier occurrence of the current token and copy what followed it.\n- **MLPs store associations.** MLP layers act like key–value memories storing factual and lexical\n  associations.\n- **Caveat.** Treat this as a useful picture, not a complete theory.","example":"### Worked scenario: compute for a 10-step agent on a self-hosted 7B model\nAn agent on Llama-2-7B (N ≈ 6.74B, d = 4096, L = 32) starts with a 4,000-token prefix (system prompt and\ntool schemas). Each step emits ~200 tokens and appends a ~2,000-token tool result. Illustrative totals\nfrom the 2N rule plus the 4·n·d attention term:\n\n| Step | Context at start | Recompute whole transcript | Append-only with prefix caching |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 4,000 | ~61 TFLOP | ~61 TFLOP |\n| 5 | 12,800 | ~220 TFLOP | ~43 TFLOP |\n| 10 | 23,800 | ~475 TFLOP | ~56 TFLOP |\n| Total, 10 steps | | ~2.5 PFLOP | ~0.47 PFLOP |\n\n- **The 2N-only estimate undercounts.** For the cached run it predicts ~0.32 PFLOP, so the attention term\n  adds roughly half again at these lengths.\n- **The lesson.** Keep transcripts append-only and short; per-step cost rises with position even when\n  each step adds the same number of tokens.\n\n### Estimate an agent run's compute from the model config\nPure Python, no GPU: the same arithmetic as the table, so you can price an agent design before launch.\n\n```python\ndef params(d, L, d_ff, vocab, gated=True, tied=False):\n    attn = 4 * d * d                                  # Wq, Wk, Wv, Wo (no GQA)\n    mlp = (3 if gated else 2) * d * d_ff              # SwiGLU has three matrices\n    return L * (attn + mlp) + vocab * d * (1 if tied else 2)\n\ndef span_flops(start, end, N, d, L):                  # tokens at positions start..end-1\n    n = end - start\n    return 2 * N * n + L * 4 * d * (start + end - 1) * n / 2   # 2N plus attention-score term\n\ndef agent_run(steps, prefix, result_toks, out_toks, cached=True, d=4096, L=32, d_ff=11008, vocab=32000):\n    N, total = params(d, L, d_ff, vocab), 0.0\n    for k in range(steps):\n        ctx = prefix + k * (result_toks + out_toks)\n        new = prefix if k == 0 else result_toks\n        total += span_flops(ctx - new if cached else 0, ctx, N, d, L)   # prefill\n        total += span_flops(ctx, ctx + out_toks, N, d, L)               # decode\n    return total\n\nfor cached in (False, True):\n    print(f\"cached={cached}: {agent_run(10, 4000, 2000, 200, cached)/1e15:.2f} PFLOP\")\n# cached=False: 2.53 PFLOP\n# cached=True: 0.47 PFLOP\n```\n\n### A minimal decoder-only transformer in PyTorch\nA minimal pre-norm decoder-only transformer. The block is the whole architecture; the rest is\nembedding, stacking and the tied LM head. `nn.RMSNorm` needs PyTorch 2.4+; swap in `nn.LayerNorm` otherwise.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn as nn, torch.nn.functional as F\n\nclass Block(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d=512, n_heads=8, d_ff=2048):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.h, self.dk = n_heads, d // n_heads\n        self.n1, self.n2 = nn.RMSNorm(d), nn.RMSNorm(d)\n        self.qkv, self.o = nn.Linear(d, 3 * d, bias=False), nn.Linear(d, d, bias=False)\n        self.up, self.down = nn.Linear(d, d_ff, bias=False), nn.Linear(d_ff, d, bias=False)\n\n    def forward(self, x):                                   # x: [B, n, d]\n        B, n, d = x.shape\n        q, k, v = self.qkv(self.n1(x)).split(d, dim=-1)\n        q, k, v = (t.view(B, n, self.h, self.dk).transpose(1, 2) for t in (q, k, v))  # [B, h, n, dk]\n        a = F.scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v, is_causal=True)\n        x = x + self.o(a.transpose(1, 2).reshape(B, n, d))  # residual 1: tokens have now mixed\n        return x + self.down(F.gelu(self.up(self.n2(x))))   # residual 2: per-position transform\n\nclass TinyLM(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, vocab=32000, d=512, L=6, max_len=1024):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.emb, self.pos = nn.Embedding(vocab, d), nn.Embedding(max_len, d)\n        self.blocks = nn.ModuleList(Block(d) for _ in range(L))\n        self.norm, self.head = nn.RMSNorm(d), nn.Linear(d, vocab, bias=False)\n        self.head.weight = self.emb.weight                  # weight tying: one matrix, two jobs\n\n    def forward(self, ids):                                 # ids: [B, n] -> logits [B, n, vocab]\n        x = self.emb(ids) + self.pos(torch.arange(ids.shape[1], device=ids.device))\n        for b in self.blocks:\n            x = b(x)\n        return self.head(self.norm(x))\n\nm = TinyLM(); ids = torch.randint(0, 32000, (2, 128))\nprint(m(ids).shape, f\"{sum(p.numel() for p in m.parameters())/1e6:.1f}M params\")\n# torch.Size([2, 128, 32000]) 35.8M params  (16.4M of them are the shared embedding)\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a cost model that broke at long context\nA team priced a 7B summarization route at 2N FLOPs per token, validated at 2k context, then launched\n64k-token documents.\n\n| Context | Attention-score term vs 2N (token at that position) | What the 2N-only model predicted |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2k | about +8% | close enough |\n| 8k | about +31% | noticeably low |\n| 32k | about +124% | less than half the real compute |\n| 64k | about +249% | badly wrong |\n\nThe fix was adding the 4·n·d per-layer term to the cost model and routing very long documents through\nchunked summarization instead of one giant prompt. A research agent that stuffs whole documents into its\ntranscript hits the same wall.","patterns":["**Price an agent design before building it.** Multiply expected steps by tokens appended per step, add the prefix, and run the 2N plus 4·n·d estimate (or provider input and output token prices for hosted models). Monitor real tokens per run against the estimate; drift means the agent loops or tool results bloat.","**Compute cost from `config.json` before deploying.** Read `hidden_size`, `num_hidden_layers`, `intermediate_size`, `num_attention_heads` and `num_key_value_heads`, then derive parameters, 2N FLOPs per token and weight memory. Monitor measured tokens per second against the estimate; a large gap points at kernels or batching.","**Use a small encoder where an LLM call is overkill.** For routing, guardrail classification or embeddings, a fine-tuned encoder-only model sees the whole input both ways and is far cheaper per request. Monitor accuracy against the LLM baseline on the same eval set.","**Smoke-test every converted or newly loaded checkpoint.** Load through the model's own class (for example `AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained`, which reads the architecture from the config), then check perplexity or a fixed eval on known text, plus a few tool-calling traces. Fluent garbage is the symptom of a wrong variant.","**Use fused attention kernels.** Call `F.scaled_dot_product_attention` in your own code, or set `attn_implementation=\"sdpa\"` (or `\"flash_attention_2\"` if installed) in Hugging Face Transformers. Monitor peak memory and step time at your real context lengths.","**Price long-context routes separately.** Add the attention-score term per route and cap context length where the per-token cost would exceed the budget. Alert on context-length p95 per route and per agent step."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Your agent's whole state lives in one left-to-right token stream. Every\n  tool result you append is attended to by every later token, so transcript length, not step count,\n  drives cost and latency.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The model's `config.json` when self-hosting, the\n  `attn_implementation` setting in Hugging Face Transformers, input-token pricing on hosted APIs, and the\n  choice between a small encoder classifier and an LLM call for a router or guardrail step.\n- **What you control.** Which layout and size each agent step uses (a big decoder for planning, a small\n  encoder for classification or embeddings), how long the transcript grows, and whether you can estimate\n  cost per run before launch.\n- **What you can skip.** Interpretability details and the exact norm and initialisation recipes. Keep the\n  two sub-layers, the 2N rule and the attention-score term; that is what you use day to day.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Costing an agent before launch.** The ≈2N FLOPs-per-token rule plus expected transcript length gives a\n  first estimate of compute per run, which you compare with hosted per-token pricing.\n- **Choosing a model per agent step.** A small encoder handles routing, guardrail classification and\n  embeddings; a decoder handles planning and tool calling, because only it generates.\n- **Explaining long-run slowdowns.** Every appended tool result is attended to by every later token, so\n  transcript length explains why late steps get slower and dearer.\n- **Reading a self-hosted model card.** Layers, hidden size and head counts in `config.json` tell you\n  memory, speed and context behaviour before you benchmark.","mini_project":"**Estimate the cost of one agent run from the architecture (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** predict compute and token cost of a 10-step tool-calling run from a model's config, then check\n  it against measured usage.\n- **Steps:** pick an open model and read its layers and hidden size from `config.json`; log input and output\n  tokens per step of a small agent with two fake tools; compute ≈2N FLOPs per token and the attention term\n  per step; check your logged token counts against the provider's or engine's reported usage, then\n  price the run from those tokens.\n- **Done when:** you have a per-step table of tokens and estimated FLOPs, and a short note on which steps\n  dominate and why transcript growth, not step count, drives the total."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Attention is where the model's knowledge lives.\"** Attention routes information between positions; the per-position MLP holds roughly two thirds of each layer's weights and is where stored associations appear to live.","**\"Inference costs 2N FLOPs per token, whatever the context.\"** There is also an attention-score term of about 4·n·d per layer per token; it equals the weight FLOPs near n ≈ 6d, about 24k tokens for d = 4096.","**\"An agent run costs roughly steps times cost per step.\"** Cost follows transcript tokens and their positions: without prefix reuse each step re-reads the whole transcript, and even with it late tokens are dearer.","**\"Instructions placed after a tool result change how the model reads that result.\"** Attention is causal: the result's tokens were encoded before the instruction existed and stay unchanged. Only later tokens, the answer included, see both, so a trailing instruction steers the output but not how the result itself was encoded.","**\"Any transformer works for any agent step.\"** An encoder-only model cannot generate, and a decoder's last hidden state is not a good embedding out of the box; match layout to the step."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Token ids\"] --> E[\"Embedding plus position\"]\n    E --> N1[\"Norm\"]\n    N1 --> AT[\"Attention: mixes positions\"]\n    AT --> R1[\"Add to residual stream\"]\n    E -->|\"identity path\"| R1\n    R1 --> N2[\"Norm\"]\n    N2 --> MLP[\"MLP: per position\"]\n    MLP --> R2[\"Add to residual stream\"]\n    R1 -->|\"identity path\"| R2\n    R2 -->|\"repeat L times\"| N1\n    R2 --> H[\"Final norm and LM head\"]\n    H --> O[\"Next-token logits\"]","caption":"Follow one residual stream through a pre-norm block: attention is the only box that mixes positions (it\nis where a tool result reaches later tokens), and both sub-layers add their output back into the stream."},"interview":[{"q":"If attention already mixes tokens, why does every block also need an MLP?","a":"- **Attention routes, it does not compute.** Its output is a softmax-weighted average of linearly\n  projected values, so it can move and blend information between positions but cannot build a new\n  nonlinear feature of one token.\n- **The MLP is the per-position computer.** It applies a nonlinearity to each position independently and\n  holds roughly two thirds of a layer's weights, which is where factual associations appear to live."},{"q":"Walk me through estimating the parameter count of Llama-2-7B from its config.","a":"- **Per layer:** attention projections 4d² = 67.1M with d = 4096; SwiGLU MLP 3·d·d_ff = 135.3M with\n  d_ff = 11008.\n- **Stack and embeddings:** 202.4M × 32 layers = 6.48B, plus two untied 32,000 × 4096 embedding\n  matrices of 131M each, for about 6.74B.\n- **Then FLOPs:** about 2N per token for inference and 6N for training."},{"q":"Your agent's cost per run is 5x the plan, though each step adds about the same number of tokens. Why?","a":"- **The plan priced steps, not positions.** Without prefix reuse every step re-processes the whole\n  transcript, so total work grows roughly with the square of step count.\n- **The attention term grows with position.** Even with caching, a token at position 24k costs well over\n  2N, so late steps are dearer.\n- **Fix:** append-only transcripts with prefix caching, compact old tool results, and cap steps per run."},{"q":"Which agent steps would you move off the main decoder LLM, and why?","a":"- **Classification-shaped steps.** Intent routing, guardrail checks and relevance filtering see the whole\n  input and output a label, which a fine-tuned encoder does cheaply with bidirectional context.\n- **Embedding steps.** Retrieval needs a model trained for embeddings, not a decoder's last hidden state.\n- **Keep on the decoder:** planning, tool-argument generation and final answers."},{"q":"A converted checkpoint loads without errors but produces fluent nonsense. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect a variant mismatch first.** Norm type and placement, activation, positional scheme, head\n  layout and tokenizer all differ by family, and a mismatch still runs.\n- **Check systematically:** compare the config against the source model card, verify the tokenizer\n  round-trips sample text, then measure perplexity on known text against the original implementation."},{"q":"Why does the 2N-FLOPs-per-token estimate fail at long context, and where is the crossover?","a":"- **There is an extra attention-score term** of about 4·n·d per layer per token, for QKᵀ and AV against\n  *n* earlier positions.\n- **Crossover:** against 24d² weight FLOPs per layer it reaches a sixth at n = d and equals them at\n  n ≈ 6d, about 24k tokens for d = 4096, so long-context routes need their own cost model."}],"tradeoffs":["**Parallelism and short paths vs quadratic attention.** Full parallelism across positions and O(1) path length between any two tokens, paid for with attention cost quadratic in sequence length. The trade flips against plain attention when contexts reach hundreds of thousands of tokens, which is where sliding windows, retrieval and state-space hybrids enter, and where agents need compaction or memory.","**No built-in bias vs data hunger.** No locality or order bias means the model needs positional encodings and more data than a CNN or RNN to learn simple structure. The same lack of bias is why one architecture handles prose, code, audio tokens and protein sequences.","**Decoder-only simplicity vs left-only context.** One objective, one stack and a KV cache, but every token sees only its left context. For classification and embedding tasks an encoder that sees the whole input both ways is often better per parameter.","**Pre-norm stability vs growing residuals.** Pre-norm trains deep stacks reliably but lets the residual stream's magnitude grow with depth, which is why final norms and careful initialization matter at scale. Post-norm has the opposite profile.","**One big model vs a mixed fleet.** One decoder for every agent step is simple, but a small encoder for routing and embeddings cuts cost; you pay with another model to serve and evaluate."],"pitfalls":["**Estimating serving cost from parameter count alone.** Ignoring the attention term gives a cost model that is accurate at 2k context and badly wrong at 64k. Fix: add 4·n·d per layer per token.","**Pricing an agent by step count.** Symptom: real cost per run is several times the plan because late steps carry the whole transcript. Fix: model tokens per step and position, as in the agent table.","**Treating the architecture diagram as the whole story.** Tokenizer, positional scheme, norm placement, activation and head layout differ between model families. Loading weights with the wrong variant produces a model that runs and emits fluent garbage. Fix: load through the matching config and test.","**Using the wrong layout for the task.** Assuming an encoder-only model can be prompted to generate, or that a decoder-only model gives good bidirectional embeddings out of the box. Symptom: puzzlingly weak results on a task the architecture was never trained to do. Fix: match layout to task, or use a model trained for embeddings.","**Reading attention maps as explanations.** Many heads over many layers plus the MLP path mean a single map rarely tells you why the model answered as it did, or why an agent picked a tool. Fix: treat a map as a hint and confirm a claim by ablating the component."],"when":"You rarely choose *whether* to use a transformer; you choose which layout and size for each agent step.\n- **Keep the block in your head** to reason downstream: why the KV cache exists, why GQA and\n  FlashAttention target the attention sub-layer, why quantization targets the MLP matrices, and why\n  context length is expensive.\n- **Use an encoder** when the task is classify-or-embed with the whole input visible (routers,\n  guardrails, retrieval).\n- **Use a decoder** when the task is generate: planning, tool calls, answers.\n- **Use an encoder–decoder** when inputs are long and fixed and outputs short (translation, some\n  summarization).\n- **Measure from the config.** Turn `hidden_size`, `num_hidden_layers`, `intermediate_size`,\n  `num_attention_heads` and `num_key_value_heads` into parameter, FLOP and memory numbers.\n- **Interviews:** draw the block, name the two sub-layers and their roles, and derive the\n  2N-FLOPs-per-token estimate from the shapes.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Reads a model's config to compute memory and FLOPs, picks encoder vs decoder models for embeddings vs generation, and knows which sub-layer a given optimization touches.","architect":"Owns the cost and capability model that follows from layout and size: context policy, GPU tier and whether a small encoder can replace an LLM call for classification.","fde":"Explains to a customer why a 70B model costs what it does, why their long documents change the bill non-linearly, and why \"just fine-tune BERT to write emails\" does not work."},"check":{"q":"Decoder-only transformers won for general-purpose LLMs over encoder–decoder designs. Explain the\nreasons, and name what was given up in the process.","a":"A single causal stack is one component to scale and one objective, next-token prediction, that\ntrains on unlabeled text. Because prompt and completion share the stream, any task becomes conditioning\nrather than a separate head, which is what makes in-context learning and instruction following natural.\nCausality also means earlier tokens' keys and values never change, so inference can cache them and\ngenerate in linear time per token. What was given up: the input is read only left-to-right, so per-token\nrepresentations of the prompt are weaker than an encoder's bidirectional ones, which still matters for\nembeddings and classification; and prompt plus output share one quadratic attention budget, whereas an\nencoder–decoder processes the input once and cross-attends to it cheaply from each output step."},"quiz":[{"q":"In a transformer block, which component is the only one that moves information between positions?","options":["The feed-forward (MLP) sub-layer","The attention sub-layer","The layer normalization","The residual connection"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The MLP, norms and residuals all operate on each position independently with shared weights. Only attention computes a weighted sum over other positions, so it is the sole channel for cross-token information flow."},{"q":"Why did pre-norm (`x + f(Norm(x))`) replace the original post-norm in deep decoder stacks?","options":["It reduces the parameter count of each block","It is what makes the KV cache possible","It keeps the identity residual path clean, so very deep stacks train stably without delicate warmup","It lowers attention cost from quadratic to linear"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Post-norm puts a normalization on the main residual path, which makes gradients through deep stacks fragile. Pre-norm leaves the residual stream untouched between blocks. It has nothing to do with the cache or attention complexity, and it changes no parameter count."},{"q":"For a dense transformer with N parameters at modest context, roughly how many FLOPs does one forward token cost?","options":["About N","About 2N","About 6N","About N²"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every weight participates in one multiply-accumulate per token, which is two FLOPs, giving ≈2N; the attention-score term (about 4·n·d per layer) stays minor until context is several times the hidden size. 6N is the training estimate, which includes the backward pass."}]},{"key":"model-internals/attention-mechanism","sources":["Bahdanau et al., 2014 — Neural Machine Translation by Jointly Learning to Align and Translate","Luong et al., 2015 — Effective Approaches to Attention-based Neural Machine Translation","Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need"],"why":"- **The fixed-vector bottleneck.** The 2014 sequence-to-sequence translators read the source sentence\n  with an encoder RNN into a single final hidden state and generated the target from it. It worked for\n  short sentences and degraded sharply with length, because a fifty-word sentence passed through the\n  same few hundred numbers as a five-word one.\n- **Needs change per step.** The decoder needed different parts of the source at different steps. A\n  bigger vector does not help much, because a static summary written before decoding must anticipate\n  every future need at once. Required capacity grows with (input position, output step) combinations,\n  not with any fixed width.\n- **Bahdanau's fix: look up, don't summarize.** Keep every encoder hidden state. At each step the decoder\n  scores its current state against each one, normalizes the scores, and builds a context vector as the\n  weighted sum. The weights, an alignment between output and input words, were learned with no alignment\n  supervision, and quality on long sentences stopped collapsing.\n- **Soft so gradients flow.** The general move replaces \"summarize once, then read the summary\" with\n  \"look up what you need, when you need it\", made soft so it trains end to end.\n- **A long lineage.** That single move is the ancestor of self-attention, cross-attention and, in hard\n  top-k form, of vector retrieval, which is what your agent's RAG step does.","how":"### Three roles\n| Role | Question it answers | Analogy | In an agent |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Query** | what am I looking for | the search term | the user question or the agent's current step |\n| **Key** | what each item advertises about itself | the database index entry | a chunk or tool-description embedding |\n| **Value** | what each item hands over if selected | the row the index points to | the chunk text or tool schema |\n\n- **Keys and values are separate** because the thing you match on need not be the thing you retrieve.\n- **All three are learned.** In neural attention they are usually linear projections `W_Q`, `W_K`, `W_V`\n  of hidden states, so the network learns what to match on and what to pass along.\n\n### Score, normalize, mix\nFor one query **q** and keys **k₁…kₙ**:\n\n```\nscore_i = f(q, k_i)\nα       = softmax(score)              # α_i ≥ 0, Σ α_i = 1\noutput  = Σ_i α_i · v_i\n```\n\nThe choice of *f* is the main design decision:\n\n| Score function | Formula | Property |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Additive** (Bahdanau) | `vᵀ·tanh(W₁q + W₂k)` | a tiny MLP per (query, key) pair; expressive, but not one matmul over all keys |\n| **Dot-product** (Luong) | `qᵀk`, or \"general\" `qᵀ·W·k` | all scores for a query from one matrix–vector product, all queries from one matrix–matrix product |\n| **Scaled dot-product** (Vaswani) | `qᵀk / √d_k` | dot-product with variance restored to one |\n\n- **The transformer uses the general form, factored.** `(x·W_Q)·(y·W_K)ᵀ = x·(W_Q·W_Kᵀ)·yᵀ` is the\n  general form with W split into two thin matrices, so each head learns a similarity of rank at most d_k.\n- **Dot-product maps onto GPUs**, because matrix–matrix products are exactly what GPUs do fastest.\n\n### Why divide by √d_k\n1. If components of **q** and **k** are roughly independent with zero mean and unit variance, `qᵀk` has\n   variance d_k.\n2. With d_k = 64 the scores have standard deviation 8.\n3. Softmax of numbers spread that widely is nearly one-hot; its gradient is close to zero almost\n   everywhere, so learning stalls.\n4. Dividing by √d_k puts the scores back at unit variance.\n5. Evidence: the original paper reports additive and dot-product attention performing similarly at small\n   d_k and unscaled dot-product falling behind at larger d_k, which motivated the scaling.\n\n### Why softmax\n- **Positive weights summing to one**, so the output is a convex combination of values whose scale does\n  not depend on how many keys there are (unlike dividing by the sum of raw scores).\n- **Smooth everywhere**, so it trains.\n- **Its exponential sharpens contrasts**, so the model can approximate a hard selection while remaining\n  trainable.\n- **Hard attention**, which samples one key, needs reinforcement-style gradient estimators and has\n  largely been abandoned for language.\n\n### Where the vectors come from decides the flavour\n- **Cross-attention:** queries from one sequence, keys and values from another. The decoder-to-encoder\n  attention in translation; also how vision-language models inject image features and how text\n  conditions a diffusion model.\n- **Self-attention:** queries, keys and values all from the same sequence, so each token builds its\n  representation from its neighbours. This is the transformer's core and has its own lesson.\n\n### Attention as a soft dictionary\n- **A Python `dict`** looks up one exact key and returns one value.\n- **Attention** looks up every key a little and returns a blend, which is what makes it differentiable.\n- **A vector database** doing top-k dot-product search over stored embeddings is the hard, external,\n  disk-backed version of the same operation.\n- **Retrieval-augmented generation** is attention over documents the model's weights never saw: the\n  retriever picks a few keys hard, then the model attends softly over that slice.\n\n### Cost\n- **One query against *n* keys:** O(n·d) for scores plus O(n·d) for the weighted sum.\n- **With *n* queries** (self-attention): O(n²·d).\n- **The n×n score matrix** is the quantity that drives every later discussion of context length, memory\n  and kernels, and therefore of how long your agent transcripts can grow.","example":"### Attention by hand, then why the scaling matters\nA worked instance in NumPy, first with two-dimensional vectors you can check by hand, then a\ndemonstration of why the √d_k scaling matters.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nnp.random.seed(0)\n\ndef attention(q, K, V, scale=True):\n    scores = K @ q                                     # one dot product per key\n    if scale:\n        scores = scores / np.sqrt(q.shape[-1])\n    w = np.exp(scores - scores.max()); w /= w.sum()    # numerically stable softmax\n    return w, w @ V\n\n# Three memories; the query equals the first key, half-matches the third, is orthogonal to the second.\nK = np.array([[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0], [0.7, 0.7]])\nV = np.array([[10.0, 0.0], [0.0, 10.0], [5.0, 5.0]])\nq = np.array([1.0, 0.0])\nw, out = attention(q, K, V, scale=False)\nprint(np.round(w, 3), np.round(out, 2))\n# scores 1.0, 0.0, 0.7 -> weights [0.474 0.174 0.351] -> output [6.5 3.5]\n# mostly the first value, a third of the third, a little of the second: a blend, never a pick.\n\n# With d_k = 64 and unit-variance vectors, raw scores have std ~8 and softmax is nearly saturated.\nd, n = 64, 16\nK, q, V = np.random.randn(n, d), np.random.randn(d), np.eye(n)\nfor s in (False, True):\n    w, _ = attention(q, K, V, scale=s)\n    entropy = -(w * np.log(w + 1e-12)).sum()\n    print(\"scaled\" if s else \"raw   \", f\"max weight {w.max():.3f}  entropy {entropy:.2f} (uniform = {np.log(n):.2f})\")\n# Typical result: raw max weight ~0.8, entropy well under 1 (uniform = 2.77) <- near one-hot, gradients vanish\n#                 scaled max weight ~0.2, entropy ~2.4                       <- a distribution the model can sharpen\n# Exact values depend on the draw; the gap in entropy is the point.\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: soft blend versus hard top-k for tool selection\nAn agent with many tools cannot list every schema in the prompt. A common move is to embed tool\ndescriptions and pick the top-k by dot product, which is attention with the softmax replaced by a hard\nselection. The toy vectors below stand in for real embeddings.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ntools = [\"search_orders\", \"refund_order\", \"get_weather\", \"create_ticket\"]\nK = np.array([[0.9, 0.1, 0.0], [0.8, 0.5, 0.0], [0.0, 0.0, 1.0], [0.3, 0.9, 0.1]])  # tool-description embeddings\nq = np.array([0.85, 0.45, 0.0])                     # embedding of \"refund my last order\"\nK = K / np.linalg.norm(K, axis=1, keepdims=True); q = q / np.linalg.norm(q)\n\nscores = K @ q\nsoft = np.exp(scores / 0.1); soft /= soft.sum()     # soft attention: every tool gets some weight\ntop_k = np.argsort(-scores)[:2]                     # hard top-k: only these schemas enter the prompt\n\nfor t, s, w in zip(tools, scores, soft):\n    print(f\"{t:14s} score {s:.2f}  soft weight {w:.2f}\")\nprint(\"schemas sent to the model:\", [tools[i] for i in top_k])\n# search_orders  score 0.93  soft weight 0.32\n# refund_order   score 1.00  soft weight 0.64\n# get_weather    score 0.00  soft weight 0.00\n# create_ticket  score 0.72  soft weight 0.04\n# schemas sent to the model: ['refund_order', 'search_orders']\n# The soft weights blend two close tools; the hard top-k gives an auditable list you can log and eval.\n```\n\n### Padding masks: the same input must give the same output\nA batched implementation normalizes across keys and masks padded keys to `-inf`. Without the mask, the\noutput depends on how much padding the batch needed, which shows up when you self-host a model serving\nmany agent sessions at once.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef masked_attention(Q, K, V, key_mask):\n    \"\"\"Q: [m, d], K, V: [n, d], key_mask: [n] with True for real tokens.\"\"\"\n    scores = Q @ K.T / np.sqrt(Q.shape[-1])                   # [m, n]\n    scores = np.where(key_mask[None, :], scores, -np.inf)\n    scores -= scores.max(axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n    w = np.exp(scores); w /= w.sum(axis=-1, keepdims=True)   # normalize across KEYS (last axis)\n    return w @ V\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nK, V, Q = rng.normal(size=(3, 8)), rng.normal(size=(3, 8)), rng.normal(size=(2, 8))\nout_alone = masked_attention(Q, K, V, np.ones(3, bool))\n\n# Same 3 real tokens, padded to length 5 because a batch-mate is longer.\nKp, Vp = np.vstack([K, np.zeros((2, 8))]), np.vstack([V, np.zeros((2, 8))])\nmask = np.array([True, True, True, False, False])\nprint(np.allclose(out_alone, masked_attention(Q, Kp, Vp, mask)))              # True\nprint(np.allclose(out_alone, masked_attention(Q, Kp, Vp, np.ones(5, bool))))  # False: padding leaked in\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: the n² cost of an agent transcript\nSelf-attention score count per head per layer grows with the square of input length (illustrative\narithmetic, one head, one layer). An agent that appends tool results crosses these rows as it runs:\n\n| Input length *n* | Agent state (illustrative) | Scores (n × n) | Growth vs 1k |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1,024 | system prompt plus a few tool schemas | about 1.05 million | 1× |\n| 8,192 | after several steps of search results | about 67 million | 64× |\n| 65,536 | after pasting whole documents into the loop | about 4.3 billion | 4,096× |\n\nThis is the budget question behind \"attend over the whole input or retrieve a short slice\": at long\nlengths a retriever that selects a few thousand tokens is the hard top-k version of the same lookup.","patterns":["**Retrieve before you attend over huge inputs.** When documents exceed what you can afford to put in the agent's context, use top-k vector search to select passages, then let the model attend over those. Monitor retrieval recall on a labelled set and the context length per request.","**Select tools by similarity when the tool list is large.** Embed tool descriptions, retrieve the top few per step and send only those schemas. Monitor tool-selection accuracy on an eval set and the rate of \"no suitable tool\" failures.","**Ground citations with hard retrieval.** When users need provenance, return the retrieved passages and their ids alongside the answer instead of reading attention weights. Monitor citation accuracy in evals.","**Summarise or drop stale tool results.** Long raw outputs dilute attention and grow cost with every step; replace them with short summaries once used. Monitor context length per step and task success.","**Mask padding and causality explicitly when self-hosting.** Pass a boolean `attn_mask` (or `is_causal=True`) to `torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention` and an `attention_mask` to Hugging Face models. Test that one input gives identical outputs alone and inside a padded batch.","**Use fused attention kernels, not hand-rolled softmax.** Library kernels apply the √d_k scale, numerically stable softmax and masking for you. Monitor peak memory and step time as context grows."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Everything in the context window, from system prompt and tool schemas to\n  every tool result, is read by soft attention. That read is a blend, not a citation, and its cost grows\n  with transcript length, which is why long agent loops get slower and dearer.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The model you call, the retriever in your RAG step (top-k dot\n  product over embeddings), and tool selection by embedding similarity when you have too many tools to\n  list in the prompt.\n- **What you control.** What enters the window (retrieve a slice versus paste everything), how many\n  tools and results you append, and whether provenance comes from retrieval ids rather than the model's\n  say-so.\n- **What you can skip.** Additive-attention details and the history of translation models. Keep the\n  query/key/value picture, why softmax blends, and the n² cost.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **RAG over a knowledge base.** The retriever does hard top-k attention over chunk embeddings, and the\n  model attends softly over the few chunks that enter the window.\n- **Tool selection for large toolsets.** Embedding similarity picks which tool schemas an agent sees per\n  step, keeping prompts short and choices auditable.\n- **Deciding paste versus retrieve.** The n² cost table tells you when pasting a whole document into an\n  agent step stops being affordable.\n- **Explaining grounding to stakeholders.** \"The model read your document\" is a blend; citations come\n  from retrieval ids, which is the argument for a retrieval step.","mini_project":"**Build a tool router with hard top-k attention (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** route each user request to the right 2 of 20 tools using embedding similarity, and measure\n  how often the right tool is offered.\n- **Steps:** write 20 short tool descriptions and 40 labelled requests; embed both with any embedding\n  model; score requests against tools by dot product and keep the top 2; compare with a softmax-weighted\n  view to see where two tools blur together.\n- **Done when:** you report top-2 recall on the 40 requests, list the misses, and rewrite at least two\n  tool descriptions to fix a miss."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Attention picks the most relevant item.\"** Softmax gives every unmasked key some weight, so the output is a blend: a query equal to one key still mixes in the others (weights 0.474, 0.174, 0.351 in the first example).","**\"If it is in the context window, the agent will use it.\"** A fact buried in a long tool result gets a small share of attention among thousands of tokens; put critical facts in short, structured fields and eval whether they are used.","**\"Attention weights tell you what the answer was based on.\"** One head's map is a blend that does not reliably explain a prediction; provenance comes from retrieval ids, explanations from attribution or ablation.","**\"Dividing by √d_k is a cosmetic normalization.\"** Unscaled scores have variance d_k (standard deviation 8 at d_k = 64), which saturates the softmax and stalls learning.","**\"Vector search and attention are unrelated tools.\"** Top-k dot-product retrieval is the same lookup with the softmax replaced by a hard selection, done outside the model; RAG chains the hard version in front of the soft one."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"Query q\"] --> S[\"Score q against each key\"]\n    K[\"Keys k1 to kn\"] --> S\n    S -->|\"divide by sqrt of d_k\"| M[\"Mask padding or future\"]\n    M --> SM[\"Softmax over keys\"]\n    SM -->|\"weights sum to 1\"| W[\"Weighted sum\"]\n    V[\"Values v1 to vn\"] --> W\n    W --> O[\"Output: blend of values\"]","caption":"Read it top to bottom: the query is scored against every key, softmax turns scores into weights, and the\noutput is a blend of all values, never a single pick. A retriever replaces the softmax with a hard top-k."},"interview":[{"q":"Explain query, key and value with a concrete example, and why keys and values are separate.","a":"- **Example:** in translation the decoder state is the query, each source word's encoder state is a key\n  advertising what it is, and its value is the content handed over if selected.\n- **Separate because** what you match on need not be what you retrieve, like a database index versus the\n  row it points to; separate projections let the model learn each."},{"q":"Your agent has 200 tools. How do you decide which ones the model sees each step?","a":"- **Retrieve, do not list.** Embed tool descriptions and select the top few by similarity to the current\n  step, which is hard top-k attention outside the model.\n- **Because** listing every schema costs tokens on every step and spreads the model's attention thin.\n- **Measure** tool-selection accuracy on a labelled eval set and log which tools were offered per step."},{"q":"When would you use retrieval instead of putting the whole document in the agent's context window?","a":"- **When the input is far larger than you can afford.** Self-attention costs O(n²·d), so very long inputs\n  get expensive; a retriever is the hard top-k version of the same lookup.\n- **When you need provenance.** Soft attention blends matching keys and does not give reliable citations,\n  so returning retrieved passages gives auditable grounding."},{"q":"Your self-hosted batched inference returns different answers for the same prompt depending on batch size. What do you check?","a":"- **Padding mask first.** If padded keys are not masked to negative infinity before softmax, the weights\n  leak onto padding and the output depends on batch-mates' lengths.\n- **Then the softmax axis.** Weights must sum to one across keys, not queries; a wrong axis makes\n  magnitudes shift with sequence length."},{"q":"Why did dot-product attention win over additive attention?","a":"- **Hardware fit.** All scores for all queries come from one matrix–matrix product, which runs on tensor\n  cores; additive scoring needs a small MLP per (query, key) pair.\n- **Quality parity once scaled.** The original paper found them similar at small d_k, with unscaled\n  dot-product falling behind at larger d_k; the authors attributed that to large scores saturating the\n  softmax, which motivated the √d_k scaling."},{"q":"How does a vector database relate to the attention mechanism?","a":"- **Same operation, different form.** Attention softly weights every key; vector search takes the hard\n  top-k by dot product over stored embeddings, externally and disk-backed.\n- **So RAG is attention over documents the weights never saw**, with the retriever choosing what enters\n  the context window and the model attending softly over that slice."}],"tradeoffs":["**Content-based lookup vs cost.** Lookup over the entire input costs time linear in the number of keys per query. Once every position is a query the cost is quadratic, and past tens of thousands of positions you move work to sparse attention or external retrieval.","**Differentiable vs blurred.** Soft weights train end to end, but the output is always a blend. When several keys match, the result is blurred, so tasks that need exact provenance (citations) use hard top-k retrieval in front of the model.","**Paste everything vs retrieve a slice.** Pasting avoids retrieval misses but pays n² cost and dilutes focus; retrieval is cheap and auditable but caps quality at retrieval recall. Paste when the input is small and fixed; retrieve when it is large or changing.","**Learned similarity vs interpretability.** Learned projections let the model decide what \"similar\" means, at the price of parameters and of attention maps that look like alignments but are not reliable explanations.","**Additive vs dot-product.** Additive scoring is slightly more expressive; dot-product scoring maps onto a single matmul and therefore onto tensor cores, which is why it won once d_k was scaled."],"pitfalls":["**Reading attention weights as feature importance.** Symptom: explanations that change with the random seed or disagree with gradient-based attribution for the same prediction. Fix: use attribution or ablation for explanations, and retrieval ids for provenance.","**Treating \"it is in the context\" as \"the agent used it\".** Symptom: the agent ignores a fact buried in a long tool result. Fix: put critical facts in short, structured fields, and eval whether answers cite the right source.","**Omitting the √d_k scaling** in a from-scratch implementation. Symptom: attention maps nearly one-hot from the first steps and a training loss that plateaus early. Fix: divide scores by √d_k, or call a library kernel that does it.","**Applying softmax along the wrong axis**, so weights sum to one across queries rather than across keys. Symptom: outputs whose magnitude changes with sequence length. Fix: softmax over the key axis and assert each row of weights sums to one.","**Forgetting to mask padding positions.** Symptom: the same input producing different outputs depending on how much padding its batch-mates needed. Fix: mask padded keys to `-inf` before softmax and test alone-vs-batched equality."],"when":"You rarely instantiate attention as a standalone primitive, but you meet the pattern constantly.\n- **Where you meet it:** every transformer layer, cross-attention adapters that fuse modalities, and the\n  retrieval layer of a RAG system, which is attention done externally with hard top-k.\n- **Decide:** whether an agent step needs the model to attend over a long input or a retriever to select\n  a short one, and how to mask padding and future positions when self-hosting.\n- **Measure:** budget the n² cost for your context lengths before committing to \"just put it all in the\n  prompt\".\n- **Interviews:** explain query, key and value with an example, say why softmax and why √d_k, and\n  distinguish self- from cross-attention. A strong answer connects the soft-dictionary view to vector\n  search and says when each is the right tool.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds and debugs attention masks for padding and causality, and recognizes the wrong-axis and missing-scale bugs from their symptoms in training curves.","architect":"Decides where lookup happens: inside the model's context window at quadratic cost, or in a retrieval layer that pre-selects what enters the window.","fde":"Uses the dictionary analogy to explain to customers why \"the model attends to your whole document\" is a blend, not a citation, and why grounding needs a retrieval step."},"check":{"q":"Explain why scaled dot-product attention divides scores by √d_k, and what you would observe in\ntraining if a from-scratch implementation forgot to do it.","a":"A dot product of two d_k-dimensional vectors with independent, zero-mean, unit-variance components\nhas variance d_k, so its standard deviation grows with √d_k; at d_k = 64 raw scores spread with standard\ndeviation around 8. Softmax over values that far apart is almost one-hot, and in that regime its gradient\nwith respect to the inputs is near zero, so the weights stop moving and the layers below receive almost\nno signal. Dividing by √d_k restores unit variance regardless of head size. Without it you would see\nattention distributions that are sharply peaked from initialization, a loss that drops briefly then\nplateaus, and performance that gets worse, not better, as you increase the head dimension."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does the softmax step provide that raw similarity scores do not?","options":["It makes attention run in linear time over the keys","Positive weights that sum to one, so the output is a convex combination independent of how many keys exist","It removes the need for separate key and value projections","It enforces that each position can only see earlier positions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Softmax normalizes scores into a distribution, keeping the output's scale stable and the operation smooth and trainable. Causality comes from a mask, not from softmax, and the cost per query is unchanged by it."},{"q":"In the cross-attention of an encoder–decoder translator, where do queries, keys and values come from?","options":["All three from the encoder outputs","Queries from the encoder, keys and values from the decoder","Queries from the decoder state, keys and values from the encoder outputs","All three from the decoder state"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The decoder asks, at each output step, which source positions are relevant; the source positions advertise themselves as keys and hand over their content as values. All-from-the-same-sequence would be self-attention."},{"q":"Which problem did attention originally solve in neural machine translation?","options":["The quadratic cost of processing long sentences","The fixed-size context vector that forced the whole source sentence through one bottleneck","The lack of positional information in the encoder","The vanishing gradient inside a single LSTM cell"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bahdanau et al. let the decoder look back at all encoder states instead of one summary vector, which fixed the collapse in quality on long sentences. Quadratic cost is a property attention introduced, not one it solved."}]},{"key":"model-internals/self-attention","sources":["Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Xiao et al., 2023 — Efficient Streaming Language Models with Attention Sinks","Olsson et al., 2022 — In-context Learning and Induction Heads"],"why":"- **Meaning depends on neighbours.** \"bank\" next to \"river\" and \"bank\" next to \"account\" need different\n  representations; a tool result means something only next to the call that produced it.\n- **Older layers pay for distance.** A recurrent network reads left to right and accumulates a state,\n  which is sequential and leaks information over distance. A convolution sees a fixed window and needs\n  many stacked layers before distant tokens interact.\n- **One step, any distance.** Self-attention computes a context-dependent representation from all\n  positions in a single step. When sequence length *n* is smaller than model width *d*, as for the\n  sentence-length inputs of 2017, it is also cheaper per layer than recurrence.\n- **Content-based routing.** Which token influences which is decided by the data through learned\n  similarity, not a fixed topology. The same layer can \"look at the previous token\", \"find the matching\n  bracket\" or \"find the earlier occurrence of this name\", which let one architecture serve prose, code,\n  protein sequences and audio tokens without redesign.\n- **Nothing is free.** The n×n interaction is paid for in compute and memory, and the layer has no notion\n  of order unless you give it one.\n\nThe transformer paper compared layer types on three axes:\n\n| Layer | Compute per layer | Sequential operations | Longest path between positions |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Self-attention | O(n²·d) | O(1) | O(1) |\n| Recurrent | O(n·d²) | O(n) | O(n) |\n| Dilated convolution | — | — | O(log n) |","how":"### Projections\nFrom layer input **X** of shape `[n, d]`, three learned projections produce `Q = X·W_Q`, `K = X·W_K`,\n`V = X·W_V`.\n- **Separate matrices** because a token's roles as asker, advertiser and content are different.\n- **Using X for all three fails twice.** The score matrix `X·Xᵀ` would be symmetric (i would want j\n  exactly as much as j wants i, which \"adjective → its noun\" relations are not). And for vectors of\n  similar norm each token would match itself most strongly, so the layer would mostly return each token\n  unchanged.\n- **Separate W_Q and W_K** make the score an asymmetric, learned relation.\n\n### Scores, mask, mix\n1. Compute `S = Q·Kᵀ / √d_k`, an `[n, n]` matrix; entry (i, j) says how much position *i* wants to read\n   from position *j*.\n2. For a decoder, apply a **causal mask**: `S[i, j] = −∞` for every *j > i*.\n3. Softmax each row, then multiply the values: `A = softmax(S)·V`.\n\nWhy the mask is built this way:\n- **−∞ before softmax, not zeros after.** `exp(−∞) = 0` exactly and the remaining weights renormalize over\n  the allowed positions. Zeroing after softmax would leave rows that no longer sum to one.\n- **Causal at all** because training predicts every next token in one pass with teacher forcing. If\n  position *i* could see *i+1*, the task would be trivial in training and impossible at inference, when\n  *i+1* does not exist yet.\n- **Encoders drop it.** BERT-style models let every token see every other; the embedding and reranker\n  models in your RAG step are usually of this kind.\n- **Padding uses the same mechanism:** −∞ on padded key positions.\n\n### Shapes and FLOPs for one layer\nCounting a matmul of `[m, k]·[k, p]` as 2·m·k·p FLOPs:\n\n| Part | FLOPs |\n|---|---|\n| Four projections (Q, K, V and output) | 8·n·d² |\n| `Q·Kᵀ` | 2·n²·d |\n| `A·V` | 2·n²·d |\n| Attention core total | 4·n²·d |\n| Core ÷ projections | n/(2d) |\n\n- **For d = 4096:** at n = 4k the ratio is 0.5, so the linear layers dominate; at 32k it is 4 and at 128k\n  it is 16, so the quadratic term dominates.\n- **Memory follows the same shape.** The score matrix is n×n per head. With 32 heads at n = 8,192 in fp16\n  that is 32 × 8192² × 2 bytes ≈ 4.3 GB per layer per sequence if ever materialized, the problem\n  FlashAttention removes.\n\n### Permutation equivariance\n- **The layer treats input as a set.** With no positional signal, permuting the rows of **X** permutes the\n  output rows identically. That is why positional encodings exist.\n- **A causal mask leaks some order**, since each token can tell roughly how many tokens precede it, and\n  decoders trained with no explicit positions do learn something usable.\n- **But production models add an explicit scheme** nearly always (a few recent families drop it in some\n  layers).\n\n### What happens at inference (each agent step)\n- **Earlier K and V never change** under the causal mask, so they are cached, and an append-only\n  transcript can reuse them across agent steps.\n- **Decode has one query row.** Work per step is `[1, d]·[d, n]` per head, O(n·d) rather than O(n²·d),\n  bound by reading the cache from memory rather than by arithmetic.\n- **Prefill vs decode.** Prefill (the whole prompt at once) is the compute-bound n² case; decode is the\n  bandwidth-bound linear case. That split is the backbone of serving economics, and a large new tool\n  result is a fresh prefill.\n\n### Patterns that emerge\n- **Recurring head types:** previous-token heads, heads that attend to delimiters, and induction heads\n  that find an earlier occurrence of the current token and copy what followed it, a mechanism behind\n  in-context learning (and why few-shot tool-call examples in the prompt work).\n- **Attention sinks.** Many heads dump most of their weight on the first token: softmax must put its mass\n  somewhere, and a constant, content-free position is a convenient no-op.\n- **Why it matters:** evicting the first tokens from a sliding-window cache breaks those heads, which is\n  why streaming schemes keep them.","example":"### Causal self-attention by hand, checked against the fused kernel\nCausal self-attention written out with explicit shapes, then checked against PyTorch's fused kernel.\nThe explicit version is what you should be able to write on a whiteboard; the fused one is what runs in\nproduction.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn.functional as F\ntorch.manual_seed(0)\nB, n, d, h = 2, 6, 64, 4            # batch, tokens, model width, heads\ndk = d // h\nx = torch.randn(B, n, d)\nWq, Wk, Wv, Wo = (torch.randn(d, d) / d ** 0.5 for _ in range(4))\n\ndef heads(t):                       # [B, n, d] -> [B, h, n, dk]\n    return t.view(B, n, h, dk).transpose(1, 2)\n\nq, k, v = heads(x @ Wq), heads(x @ Wk), heads(x @ Wv)\nS = q @ k.transpose(-1, -2) / dk ** 0.5                     # [B, h, n, n] raw scores\nfuture = torch.triu(torch.ones(n, n, dtype=torch.bool), diagonal=1)\nS = S.masked_fill(future, float(\"-inf\"))                    # j > i is invisible to i\nA = S.softmax(dim=-1)                                       # each row sums to 1 over j <= i\nprint(A[0, 0].round(decimals=2))                            # lower-triangular weight matrix\nout = (A @ v).transpose(1, 2).reshape(B, n, d) @ Wo         # merge heads, output projection\n\nref = F.scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v, is_causal=True)\nref = ref.transpose(1, 2).reshape(B, n, d) @ Wo\nprint(torch.allclose(out, ref, atol=1e-4))                  # True: same math, fused kernel\nprint(S.shape, \"scores per head are n x n ->\", n * n, \"entries; at n=8192 that is\", 8192 ** 2)\n```\n\n### Where the FLOPs and memory go as context grows\nPure Python for a d = 4096, 32-head layer: the core-to-projection ratio and the size of the score\nmatrix a naive kernel would materialize.\n\n```python\nd, heads = 4096, 32\nfor n in (2_048, 8_192, 32_768, 131_072):\n    proj = 8 * n * d * d                     # Q, K, V, output projections\n    core = 4 * n * n * d                     # Q·Kᵀ plus A·V\n    scores_gb = heads * n * n * 2 / 1e9      # fp16 n×n scores, if materialized\n    print(f\"n={n:>7}: core/proj = {core/proj:5.2f}   scores {scores_gb:8.1f} GB per layer\")\n# n=   2048: core/proj =  0.25   scores      0.3 GB per layer\n# n=   8192: core/proj =  1.00   scores      4.3 GB per layer\n# n=  32768: core/proj =  4.00   scores     68.7 GB per layer\n# n= 131072: core/proj = 16.00   scores   1099.5 GB per layer\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: a research agent crosses the crossover\nA research agent on a d = 4096 model starts at 2k tokens and appends a search result of roughly 3k tokens\neach step (illustrative sizes). The ratio column is the n/(2d) formula above.\n\n| Step | Context *n* | Core ÷ projections | What dominates the layer |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | ~2,048 | 0.25 | linear layers; attention is a minor cost |\n| 3 | ~8,192 | 1.00 | equal halves; MLP still makes linear work dominant |\n| 11 | ~32,768 | 4.00 | the quadratic attention core |\n| with pasted documents | ~131,072 | 16.00 | attention by far; prefill time balloons |\n\n- **Reading the table:** the agent does not slow down because it takes more steps, but because each step\n  re-reads a longer transcript.\n- **Fix:** keep only summaries of used search results, so the context stays near the left rows.\n\n### Worked incident: short prompts in a batch returned junk\nA team batched agent prompts of different lengths for a self-hosted decoder-only model and saw only the\nlongest prompt answer correctly.\n\n| Step | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Symptom | shorter prompts produce junk; the longest one is fine |\n| Cause | right padding put pad tokens between each prompt and its generated tokens |\n| Fix | left padding plus an attention mask, so every prompt ends at the last position |\n| Check | the same prompt gives the same greedy output alone and inside a batch |\n\n```python\ntok.padding_side = \"left\"                 # Hugging Face tokenizer, decoder-only generation\nif tok.pad_token is None:                 # many causal-LM tokenizers ship without one\n    tok.pad_token = tok.eos_token\nbatch = tok(prompts, return_tensors=\"pt\", padding=True)\nout = model.generate(**batch, max_new_tokens=64)   # passes attention_mask with the ids\n```","patterns":["**Compact the transcript as the agent runs.** Replace used tool output with short summaries and cap raw result size, because every kept token is re-read on every later step. Track context tokens per step and task success before and after compaction.","**Append, do not edit, history.** Earlier K and V stay valid only if the prefix is unchanged, so append new turns and compact at deliberate checkpoints. Monitor cached versus uncached input tokens per step.","**Left-pad for decoder-only generation.** Set `tokenizer.padding_side = \"left\"` and always pass the `attention_mask`. Add a regression test that one prompt gives identical greedy output alone and in a mixed-length batch.","**Profile attention share at real agent lengths before optimizing.** Use the PyTorch profiler on traffic shaped like production; at short context the linear layers dominate and attention kernels will not help. Track the attention share of step time per context bucket.","**Never materialize the n×n scores.** Use `F.scaled_dot_product_attention` or an engine with fused kernels so memory stays linear in n. Monitor peak memory against context length; quadratic growth means a naive path is still in use.","**Keep sink tokens when windowing the cache.** If you evict old positions for long-running sessions, retain the first few tokens plus a recent window, as attention-sink schemes do. Monitor quality on long sessions versus full attention, and guard against fully masked rows that return NaN."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** The transcript is one causal sequence: system prompt, tool schemas,\n  calls and results. Self-attention is the only way information moves between those positions, so a\n  fact helps the agent only if it sits in the window, and every extra token is paid for on later steps.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Context-length limits and input-token pricing on hosted APIs,\n  time-to-first-token on long prompts (prefill), `padding_side` and `attention_mask` when you batch\n  agent sessions on a self-hosted model, and sliding-window or streaming options in serving engines.\n- **What you control.** How fast the transcript grows (summarise or drop stale tool output), whether\n  long inputs go through retrieval instead, and correct left padding when batching generation.\n- **What you can skip.** Induction-head interpretability and the exact FLOP derivation. Keep the n/(2d)\n  crossover, the prefill/decode split and the causal-mask rule.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Long-running research and coding agents.** Transcript growth, not step count, sets per-step latency,\n  so compaction thresholds are set from measured per-step latency, with the n/(2d) crossover showing\n  where attention starts to dominate.\n- **Self-hosted batch serving of agent sessions.** Correct left padding and masks keep outputs identical\n  whether a session runs alone or in a batch.\n- **Choosing encoder or decoder per step.** Bidirectional encoders handle embedding and reranking; causal\n  decoders handle planning and tool calls.\n- **Streaming or very long sessions.** Sliding-window caches keep attention-sink tokens so quality holds\n  as old turns are evicted.","mini_project":"**Profile transcript growth in a tool-calling loop (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show how context length per step drives latency, and how much compaction recovers.\n- **Steps:** build a 10-step agent with a fake search tool that returns about 3k tokens; log input tokens\n  and time-to-first-token per step; rerun with a compaction rule that keeps a 200-token summary of each\n  used result; compute n/(2d) per step for the model's hidden size.\n- **Done when:** you have a two-run table of context tokens, latency and ratio per step, and a note\n  saying which step crossed the crossover and how compaction changed it."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Attention is always the expensive part of a layer.\"** The core-to-projection ratio is n/(2d): at d = 4096 it is 0.5 at 4k tokens and 1 at 8k. With the MLP's linear work added, the quadratic core matches all linear work near 24k and dominates by 32k (ratio 4).","**\"Every generated token pays the n² cost again.\"** Decode has one query row against cached keys and values, O(n·d) per layer and bandwidth-bound; the n² case is prefill, which a big new tool result triggers.","**\"Self-attention knows the order of the tokens.\"** Without positions it is permutation equivariant; the causal mask leaks only a rough count of preceding tokens, so models add an explicit scheme.","**\"Masking is just zeroing the future weights.\"** Zeroing after softmax leaves rows that no longer sum to one; the mask must put −∞ on the logits before softmax.","**\"An attention mask makes padding side irrelevant.\"** In decoder-only generation, right padding puts pad tokens between the prompt and its new tokens; use left padding plus the mask.","**\"The first tokens of a long session are safe to evict.\"** Many heads park their weight on the first token as an attention sink, so window caches keep those tokens."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    X[\"Input X: n by d\"] --> Q[\"Q = X times W_Q\"]\n    X --> K[\"K = X times W_K\"]\n    X --> V[\"V = X times W_V\"]\n    Q --> S[\"Scores Q times K-transpose, scaled\"]\n    K --> S\n    S -->|\"minus infinity where j is after i\"| M[\"Causal mask\"]\n    M --> SM[\"Row-wise softmax\"]\n    SM --> AV[\"Weights times V\"]\n    V --> AV\n    AV --> O[\"Output projection W_O\"]","caption":"One layer of causal self-attention: the same input X feeds all three projections, the mask hides the\nfuture before softmax, and the output projection writes back into the residual stream. In an agent, X\nis the whole transcript so far."},"interview":[{"q":"Why does self-attention use separate W_Q and W_K instead of scoring X against itself?","a":"- **X·Xᵀ is symmetric**, so i would attend to j exactly as much as j to i, which asymmetric relations such\n  as adjective to noun are not.\n- **Self-match dominates.** For vectors of similar norm each token scores itself highest, so the layer\n  would mostly copy its input; separate projections make the relation learned and asymmetric."},{"q":"Each step of your agent gets slower even though the step count is modest. What is happening and what do you do?","a":"- **The transcript grows**, and every step re-reads all of it: prefill cost rises with n and, past the\n  n/(2d) crossover, the quadratic attention core dominates.\n- **Check** context tokens per step and whether the prefix is stable enough for cached K and V to be\n  reused.\n- **Fix** by summarising used tool output, capping raw results and retrieving long documents instead of\n  pasting them."},{"q":"You batch many agent sessions on a self-hosted decoder model and short prompts return junk. Why?","a":"- **Right padding.** Pad tokens sit between the prompt and the generated tokens, so short prompts\n  continue from padding.\n- **Fix** with `padding_side = \"left\"` plus the attention mask, and a regression test that alone and\n  batched greedy outputs match."},{"q":"Why is decode bandwidth-bound while prefill is compute-bound?","a":"- **Prefill** processes the whole prompt: n queries against n keys, large matrix multiplications, the\n  O(n²·d) case.\n- **Decode** has one new query row, `[1, d]·[d, n]` per head, O(n·d), so each step mostly reads cached\n  keys, values and weights from memory with little arithmetic per byte."},{"q":"When would you choose a linear-attention or state-space model over softmax self-attention?","a":"- **When context is very long and cost dominates**, because they run in O(n) instead of O(n²).\n- **But not when exact recall matters.** They compress history into a fixed state and give up some of the\n  precise copying and induction that softmax attention does well, which agents rely on to reuse exact\n  ids and values from earlier tool results."},{"q":"At d = 4096 and 32 heads, how big is the score matrix at 8k context, and why does it matter?","a":"- **Arithmetic:** 32 × 8192² × 2 bytes ≈ 4.3 GB per layer per sequence in fp16, if materialized.\n- **Consequence:** naive kernels run out of memory fast as n grows, which is why FlashAttention computes\n  the same result in tiles without ever storing the n×n matrix."}],"tradeoffs":["**Short paths and parallelism vs O(n²).** O(1) path length and full parallelism across positions, paid for with O(n²) compute and memory. The balance flips against dense attention when n is many times d, when sliding windows, sparse patterns or retrieval take over part of the job.","**Keep everything in context vs compact.** A full transcript lets the agent recall any earlier detail exactly; compaction cuts cost and latency but can drop the one detail a later step needed. Compact outputs you can re-fetch; keep decisions and user constraints verbatim.","**Content routing vs no locality prior.** Routing adapts to any structure in the data but brings no built-in locality bias, so small-data regimes favour architectures with stronger priors and large-data regimes favour attention.","**Bidirectional vs causal.** Bidirectional self-attention gives richer per-token representations; causal self-attention gives up the right context in exchange for generation and a reusable KV cache.","**Exact recall vs linear cost.** Exact softmax attention can retrieve a single specific earlier token (copying, induction). Linear attention and state-space models cut cost to O(n) but compress history into a fixed state and give up some of that precise recall."],"pitfalls":["**Letting tool output grow the transcript unchecked.** Symptom: each agent step is slower than the last and time-to-first-token climbs. Fix: cap and summarise tool results, and move large documents behind retrieval.","**Masking with zeros after softmax, or masking the wrong triangle.** Tokens see the future. Symptom: a training loss that falls suspiciously close to zero, followed by nonsense at inference. Fix: mask logits with −∞ where j > i before softmax, and assert the weights above the diagonal are zero.","**Right-padding a decoder-only batch during generation.** Pad tokens land after the prompt. Symptom: shorter prompts in a batch produce junk while the longest one is fine. Fix: left padding with an attention mask.","**A fully masked row.** Every key −∞, as with an all-padding query row, makes softmax return NaN. Symptom: NaN losses that appear only on certain batches. Fix: guarantee each query row at least one visible key, or zero out fully masked rows explicitly.","**Costing attention with only one term.** Using only the quadratic or only the linear term gives a capacity plan that is right at one context length and wrong by multiples at another. Fix: cost both 8·n·d² and 4·n²·d at every context length you serve."],"when":"Self-attention is not optional in a transformer, so the real decisions are around it.\n- **Decide:** how long an agent transcript you can afford, encoder (bidirectional) or decoder (causal)\n  for a task such as reranking versus planning, how to pad and mask batches correctly, and which kernel\n  serves it.\n- **Measure:** the share of attention time in your profiles at your actual sequence lengths before\n  optimizing anything; at short context the linear layers dominate and attention kernels will not help.\n- **Avoid:** pasting whole documents into every step when a retrieval step can supply the relevant slice.\n- **Interviews:** derive the O(n²·d) cost and the n/(2d) crossover, explain why the mask uses −∞ before\n  softmax, state why positions must be added explicitly, and show how the KV cache and the\n  prefill/decode split follow from causality.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes and debugs the masking and padding logic, chooses left padding for generation, and profiles whether attention or the MLP dominates at the team's real sequence lengths.","architect":"Sets context-length policy from the n/(2d) crossover and the memory curve, and decides where retrieval must shrink inputs instead of paying quadratic attention.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's \"it works for short inputs and breaks for long ones\" by checking context limits, padding direction and attention memory on their hardware."},"check":{"q":"A model with hidden size 4,096 processes an 8k-token prompt. In the attention layer, do the\nQ/K/V/output projections or the score computation dominate FLOPs, and how does that change at 128k?","a":"Projections cost 8·n·d² and the attention core (QKᵀ plus AV) costs 4·n²·d, so their ratio is\nn/(2d). At n = 8,192 and d = 4,096 the ratio is 1: the two halves are equal, and together with the MLP\n(which is larger than the projections) the linear layers still dominate the layer as a whole. At\nn = 131,072 the ratio is 16, so the quadratic core is sixteen times the projections and dwarfs the MLP\ntoo; the layer is now attention-dominated. The practical consequences follow: at 8k, kernel choices for\nmatmuls and quantizing weights matter most; at 128k, attention kernels, memory for the n×n scores (never\nmaterializing them) and possibly sparse or windowed attention are what determine cost and feasibility."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the causal mask applied as −∞ before the softmax instead of zeroing weights after it?","options":["Because −∞ is faster to compute on GPUs than multiplying by zero","Because exp(−∞) is exactly 0 and the remaining weights renormalize to sum to one over allowed positions","Because softmax cannot accept zeros as inputs","Because zeroing after softmax would leak information from earlier positions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Zeroing after softmax leaves rows whose weights no longer sum to one, scaling outputs by how much was masked. Masking the logits lets softmax redistribute probability over the visible positions only."},{"q":"Which property of self-attention makes an explicit positional encoding necessary?","options":["Its quadratic cost in sequence length","The use of separate key and value projections","Permutation equivariance: without positions, reordering the input only reorders the output","The fact that softmax weights sum to one"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Self-attention treats its input as a set; \"dog bites man\" and \"man bites dog\" would yield the same per-token outputs. Positional encodings break that symmetry so order can influence attention."},{"q":"Per the original transformer analysis, when is self-attention cheaper per layer than a recurrent layer?","options":["When sequence length n is smaller than the representation width d","When sequence length n is larger than the representation width d","Always, regardless of n and d","Never; it is chosen only for parallelism"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Self-attention costs O(n²·d) per layer and recurrence O(n·d²), so attention is cheaper when n < d. At long contexts recurrence is cheaper in FLOPs, and attention is kept for its path length and parallelism."}]},{"key":"model-internals/multi-head-attention","sources":["Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Michel et al., 2019 — Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?","Bhojanapalli et al., 2020 — Low-Rank Bottleneck in Multi-head Attention Models"],"why":"- **One softmax, one weighting.** A single head gives each query position one probability distribution\n  over the sequence and one blended output.\n- **Positions need several relations at once.** For the verb in \"The keys that the engineer lost were on\n  the table\", the layer wants \"keys\" for number agreement, \"engineer\" for who did the losing and \"table\"\n  for where things ended up. One softmax serving all three averages them into a blur.\n- **One space, one similarity.** A dot product in a single d_model-dimensional space tests one notion of\n  similarity. \"Is this the subject of my clause\" and \"is this the token right before me\" want different\n  subspaces.\n- **The naive fixes fail.** More layers, each picking one relation, serializes parallel work and deepens\n  the network. Widening d_k does not help at all, because one softmax still yields one weighting.\n- **The 2017 answer.** Project into *h* = 8 subspaces of 64 dimensions, attend independently in each,\n  concatenate the eight 64-dimensional outputs back to 512, and mix with an output projection.\n\nWhy it costs nothing extra, and why it is still a trade-off:\n- **Same cost.** Each head's score computation costs n²·d_k and h·d_k = d_model, so the total matches a\n  single full-width head.\n- **The ablation.** One head at the same budget was measurably worse, and quality dropped again with too\n  many heads: the split is a trade-off rather than \"more is better\". The design has survived unchanged in\n  its essentials.","how":"### Shapes\n1. From input **X** `[n, d]`, head *i* uses its own slices of the projections:\n   `Q_i = X·W_Q^i`, `K_i = X·W_K^i`, `V_i = X·W_V^i`, each `[n, d_k]`.\n2. Each head computes `head_i = softmax(Q_i·K_iᵀ / √d_k)·V_i`.\n3. Outputs are concatenated into `[n, h·d_k]` and multiplied by `W_O` of shape `[h·d_k, d]`.\n\nIn code the per-head matrices are not separate:\n- **One fused `W_QKV`** of shape `[d, 3d]` is applied.\n- **One reshape** from `[B, n, 3d]` to `[B, 3, h, n, d_k]`.\n- **One batched matmul** over the head axis does all heads in one kernel. The heads are a *view* of one\n  big matmul, not eight small modules.\n\n### Typical sizes\n| Model | d_k |\n|---|---|\n| 2017 paper | 64 |\n| Llama-2-7B (4096 / 32), Llama-3-8B, most current open models | 128 |\n\n- **Kernels bound the choice.** Fused attention kernels support a bounded set of head dimensions\n  (FlashAttention-2 goes up to 256) and are most heavily exercised and tuned at 64 and 128.\n- **So designers choose d_k first** and derive *h* from it.\n\n### Why the output projection\n- **Concatenation only lays outputs side by side.** Without `W_O`, head *i* could write only into\n  dimensions `i·d_k … (i+1)·d_k` of the residual stream, tying each head to a fixed slot forever.\n- **`W_O` lets heads combine** and land anywhere in the stream, and it is where a head's contribution can\n  be scaled up or down.\n\n### Parameter and FLOP accounting\nWhat does *not* change with *h*:\n- **Parameters.** `W_Q`, `W_K`, `W_V` and `W_O` are each `d × d`, giving 4d² per layer regardless of *h*.\n- **Score FLOPs.** h × 2·n²·d_k = 2·n²·d, independent of *h*.\n\nWhat *does* change with *h*:\n- **Attention-probability memory.** The materialized `[B, h, n, n]` tensor grows linearly with *h*. At\n  h = 32, n = 8,192, fp16 it is about 4.3 GB per layer per sequence, which is why fused kernels that never\n  materialize it matter.\n- **Per-head rank.** Each head's `Q_i·K_iᵀ` is `[n, n]` but has rank at most d_k (the low-rank bottleneck\n  Bhojanapalli et al. analyse), so more, thinner heads means each expresses less intricate patterns.\n- **KV cache, only via KV heads.** Per token per layer it is 2·(number of KV heads)·d_k, which equals 2d\n  for full multi-head attention and shrinks only when KV heads are shared, the subject of grouped-query\n  attention. This is the number that caps concurrent agent sessions.\n\n### What heads learn\n- **Specialists.** Previous-token heads, heads that track syntactic roles, heads that attend to\n  delimiters or the first token as a sink, and induction heads that find an earlier copy of the current\n  token and attend to what followed it.\n- **Redundancy.** Michel et al. showed that a large fraction of heads in trained translation and BERT\n  models can be removed at test time with little loss.\n- **Consequence.** That redundancy is part of why sharing keys and values across heads (GQA) costs so\n  little quality.\n\n### Choosing h and d_k\n- **More heads** means more distinct relations per layer.\n- **Smaller d_k** means lower-rank score matrices, so sharper patterns become harder to express.\n- **Kernel constraint:** d_k should be a value the attention kernels are tuned for.\n- **Sharding constraint:** *h* should be divisible by the tensor-parallel degree, because heads are the\n  natural unit to shard across GPUs with no communication inside the attention block.","example":"### A multi-head attention module where h changes only the attention shape\nA complete multi-head attention module. The loop at the bottom shows that changing *h* from 1 to 64 leaves\nthe parameter count at exactly 4·512² = 1,048,576 and changes only the shape of the attention tensor.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn as nn\n\nclass MHA(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d, h):\n        super().__init__()\n        assert d % h == 0\n        self.h, self.dk = h, d // h\n        self.qkv = nn.Linear(d, 3 * d, bias=False)      # W_Q | W_K | W_V fused into one matmul\n        self.o = nn.Linear(d, d, bias=False)            # W_O: lets heads mix and land anywhere\n\n    def forward(self, x, causal=True):                  # x: [B, n, d]\n        B, n, d = x.shape\n        q, k, v = self.qkv(x).chunk(3, dim=-1)          # each [B, n, d]\n        split = lambda t: t.view(B, n, self.h, self.dk).transpose(1, 2)   # -> [B, h, n, dk]\n        q, k, v = split(q), split(k), split(v)\n        scores = q @ k.transpose(-2, -1) / self.dk ** 0.5                  # [B, h, n, n]\n        if causal:\n            future = torch.triu(torch.ones(n, n, dtype=torch.bool), 1)\n            scores = scores.masked_fill(future, float(\"-inf\"))\n        attn = scores.softmax(-1)                                          # one distribution per head\n        out = (attn @ v).transpose(1, 2).reshape(B, n, d)                  # concat heads\n        return self.o(out), attn\n\nx = torch.randn(2, 10, 512)\nfor h in (1, 8, 64):\n    m = MHA(512, h)\n    y, attn = m(x)\n    print(f\"h={h:2d} head_dim={512 // h:3d} params={sum(p.numel() for p in m.parameters()):,} \"\n          f\"attn={tuple(attn.shape)} out={tuple(y.shape)}\")\n# h= 1 head_dim=512 params=1,048,576 attn=(2, 1, 10, 10) out=(2, 10, 512)\n# h= 8 head_dim= 64 params=1,048,576 attn=(2, 8, 10, 10) out=(2, 10, 512)\n# h=64 head_dim=  8 params=1,048,576 attn=(2, 64, 10, 10) out=(2, 10, 512)\n```\n\n### Reading head counts off a config\nPure Python: from layers, query heads, KV heads and head_dim, get the KV cache per token and which\ntensor-parallel degrees (up to 8 GPUs) split the query heads evenly.\n\n```python\ndef head_plan(layers, n_heads, n_kv_heads, head_dim, bytes_per_el=2):\n    kv_per_token = 2 * layers * n_kv_heads * head_dim * bytes_per_el\n    tp_ok = [t for t in range(1, n_heads + 1) if n_heads % t == 0 and t <= 8]\n    return kv_per_token, tp_ok\n\ncfgs = {\"Llama-2-7B (MHA)\": (32, 32, 32, 128), \"Llama-3-8B (GQA)\": (32, 32, 8, 128)}\nfor name, cfg in cfgs.items():\n    kv, tp = head_plan(*cfg)\n    print(f\"{name}: KV {kv/2**20:.3f} MiB/token, query heads split evenly on TP={tp}\")\nprint(\"6 GPUs?\", 32 % 6 == 0)   # False: 32 heads do not divide across 6 GPUs\n# Llama-2-7B (MHA): KV 0.500 MiB/token, query heads split evenly on TP=[1, 2, 4, 8]\n# Llama-3-8B (GQA): KV 0.125 MiB/token, query heads split evenly on TP=[1, 2, 4, 8]\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: how many agent sessions fit on one GPU\nA support agent's sessions average 16,384 tokens of transcript (system prompt, tool schemas, results).\nAssume about 40 GiB of GPU memory is left for the KV cache after weights and headroom (illustrative).\nPer-token figures come from the config code above.\n\n| Model | KV per token | KV per 16k session | Sessions in 40 GiB |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Llama-2-7B (full MHA, 32 KV heads) | 0.5 MiB | 8 GiB | about 5 |\n| Llama-3-8B (GQA, 8 KV heads) | 0.125 MiB | 2 GiB | about 20 |\n\n- **Same query heads, same head_dim:** the four-fold difference comes only from KV heads.\n- **Operational consequence:** the agent platform's concurrency limit, and its cost per session, is set\n  by `num_key_value_heads` and transcript length, not by parameter count.\n\n### Worked incident: throughput collapsed after a head-dimension change\nA team trained a small model with a custom head dimension above 256 to \"give each head more room\".\n\n| Step | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Symptom | much lower throughput and attention memory growing with n² at long context |\n| Cause | the fused kernels do not support that head dimension, so attention silently fell back to the math path |\n| Confirm | force the flash backend in a test; it now fails loudly instead of falling back |\n| Fix | return to d_k = 128 and raise *h* instead; parameters and FLOPs are unchanged |","patterns":["**Size agent concurrency from KV heads, not query heads.** Read `num_key_value_heads` and `head_dim` from the config; full MHA has as many KV heads as query heads. Multiply by your p95 transcript length to set the engine's maximum concurrent sequences. Monitor KV-cache utilization and preemptions.","**Prefer GQA models for long agent transcripts.** When two candidate models score similarly on your agent evals, the one with fewer KV heads serves several times more sessions per GPU. Compare sessions per GPU at your real context length.","**Choose the tensor-parallel degree from head counts.** The number of query heads should divide by the TP degree; KV heads either divide too or the engine must replicate them. Check this before buying or allocating a node, and monitor per-GPU memory balance.","**Pick head_dim from the kernels when fine-tuning or training.** Keep d_k at 64 or 128 and derive *h*, so FlashAttention-class kernels apply. In a test, restrict PyTorch's backend with `torch.nn.attention.sdpa_kernel` to the flash backend so an unsupported shape fails loudly.","**Unit-test the reshape.** Compare one head's output with a single-head computation on that slice of the projections, and compare the whole module with `F.scaled_dot_product_attention`. Run it in CI on every attention change."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** When you self-host the model behind your agents, `num_attention_heads`,\n  `num_key_value_heads` and `head_dim` set the KV cache each long agent transcript holds, and therefore\n  how many concurrent sessions one GPU serves.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The model's `config.json`, the tensor-parallel setting of your\n  serving engine, and KV-cache utilization dashboards. On hosted APIs it is hidden, but it is part of why\n  long-context calls cost what they do.\n- **What you control.** Which model you pick (full MHA or GQA layout), how many GPUs you shard it across\n  (a divisor of the head count), and the context cap per agent route.\n- **What you can skip.** What individual heads learn and the low-rank analysis, unless you train models.\n  Keep \"parameters and FLOPs do not change with *h*, KV cache follows KV heads\".","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Capacity planning for self-hosted agents.** KV heads, head_dim and transcript length give sessions\n  per GPU before any load test.\n- **Choosing between candidate open models.** At similar eval scores, the layout with fewer KV heads is\n  the cheaper one to serve for long agent runs.\n- **Planning a multi-GPU deployment.** Head counts decide which tensor-parallel degrees split the model\n  evenly on the hardware you own.\n- **Debugging a slow fine-tuned model.** An unsupported head_dim explains a silent fall-back to slow\n  attention paths.","mini_project":"**Build an agent-capacity calculator from model configs (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** given a model's `config.json` and an agent's transcript profile, report sessions per GPU and\n  valid tensor-parallel degrees.\n- **Steps:** load two or three open-model configs; read layers, query heads, KV heads and head_dim\n  (computing head_dim as hidden size over heads when absent); take p50 and p95 transcript lengths from a\n  logged agent run; compute KV per session and sessions per GPU for a stated free-memory budget.\n- **Done when:** the tool prints a comparison table per model, flags TP degrees that do not divide the\n  head count, and its numbers for Llama-2-7B and Llama-3-8B match 0.5 and 0.125 MiB per token."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"More heads means more parameters and more compute.\"** Heads are slices of the same d×d projections, so parameters stay 4d² per layer and score FLOPs stay 2·n²·d; only the materialized `[B, h, n, n]` tensor and per-head rank change.","**\"KV cache per token follows the number of attention heads.\"** It follows KV heads: 2 × layers × KV heads × head_dim × bytes. Llama-2-7B and Llama-3-8B both have 32 query heads, yet need 0.5 and 0.125 MiB per token.","**\"Each head has one clean job, so a head's attention map explains the output.\"** Many heads are redundant and can be pruned with little loss, and roles spread across heads and layers; test a head's role by ablating it.","**\"Any number of GPUs can shard any model with tensor parallelism.\"** Heads are the shard unit, so the head count must divide by the TP degree: 32 heads split on 1, 2, 4 or 8 GPUs, not 6.","**\"A wider head_dim just gives each head more room.\"** Fused kernels support bounded head dimensions (FlashAttention-2 up to 256); outside them attention silently falls back to a slow path whose memory grows with n²."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    X[\"Input X: n by d\"] --> P[\"Fused W_QKV projection\"]\n    P --> R[\"Reshape to h heads of d_k\"]\n    R --> H1[\"Head 1: own softmax\"]\n    R --> H2[\"Head 2: own softmax\"]\n    R --> HH[\"Head h: own softmax\"]\n    H1 --> C[\"Concatenate to n by d\"]\n    H2 --> C\n    HH --> C\n    C --> O[\"W_O mixes heads\"]\n    O --> S[\"Residual stream\"]","caption":"One fused projection is viewed as *h* heads; each head runs its own softmax in parallel, and `W_O` mixes\nthe concatenated results back into the residual stream."},"interview":[{"q":"Why do several heads beat one head of the same total width?","a":"- **One softmax gives one weighting per position.** A wide single head still blends every relation into\n  one distribution, so agreement, coreference and locality get averaged together.\n- **h heads give h independent weightings** in different subspaces at the same parameter and FLOP cost,\n  because h·d_k = d_model."},{"q":"You must host an agent platform with 16k-token sessions on your own GPUs. How do head counts guide model choice?","a":"- **Compute KV per token** as 2 × layers × KV heads × head_dim × bytes; full MHA at Llama-2-7B scale is\n  0.5 MiB per token, a GQA model like Llama-3-8B is 0.125 MiB.\n- **Multiply by session length** (8 GiB versus 2 GiB at 16k) and divide free GPU memory to get sessions\n  per GPU.\n- **Decide with evals:** if quality is comparable on your agent tasks, the GQA model serves about four\n  times the sessions."},{"q":"Your from-scratch MHA trains without errors but learns slowly and outputs incoherent text. What do you check?","a":"- **The reshape and transpose.** Viewing `[B, n, h·d_k]` directly as `[B, h, n, d_k]` mixes positions\n  into heads; it must be view to `[B, n, h, d_k]`, then transpose.\n- **Then test it:** run with h = 1, compare one head against a single-head computation on its slice, and\n  compare the module with the fused kernel."},{"q":"When would you not increase the number of heads?","a":"- **When d_k would drop below what patterns need.** Each head's score matrix has rank at most d_k; the\n  2017 ablation saw quality fall at 32 heads of width 16.\n- **When kernels or sharding object.** Head dimensions outside the kernels' supported set fall back to\n  slow paths, and *h* must still divide by the tensor-parallel degree."},{"q":"A customer wants to serve a 32-head model for their agents on a 6-GPU node with tensor parallelism. What do you tell them?","a":"- **32 heads do not split evenly across 6 GPUs**, and heads are the unit tensor parallelism shards\n  attention by.\n- **Options:** run TP on 4 GPUs and use the rest for another replica or pipeline stage, pick a model\n  whose head counts fit, or use data-parallel replicas instead."},{"q":"How much KV cache does a 4,096-token sequence need on Llama-2-7B versus Llama-3-8B, and why do they differ?","a":"- **Formula:** 2 (K and V) × layers × KV heads × head_dim × bytes. Both have 32 layers, 32 query heads\n  and head_dim 128 in fp16.\n- **Llama-2-7B (full MHA, 32 KV heads):** 0.5 MiB per token, so about 2 GiB for 4,096 tokens.\n- **Llama-3-8B (GQA, 8 KV heads):** 0.125 MiB per token, about 0.5 GiB, four times less. The query-head\n  count is the same; only the KV heads changed, which is why you size caches from\n  `num_key_value_heads`."}],"tradeoffs":["**More relations vs per-head rank.** More heads give more parallel relations per layer, but each head's score matrix has rank at most d_k. Past a point extra heads buy diversity at the price of per-head expressiveness (the 2017 ablation saw quality fall at 32 heads of width 16); most current models settle at d_k = 128.","**Full MHA vs shared KV heads for agents.** Full MHA keeps every head's own keys and values, at four or more times the cache of a GQA layout; for long agent transcripts that cache, not quality, usually decides which model you can afford to serve.","**Same FLOPs vs bigger attention tensor.** Same FLOPs and parameters as a single wide head, yet the attention-probability tensor is *h* times larger. Harmless with fused kernels, a memory wall without.","**Easy sharding vs layout constraints.** Heads shard across GPUs with no communication inside attention, which makes tensor parallelism simple, but *h* (and the number of KV heads) must divide by the parallel degree, which constrains hardware layouts.","**Redundancy vs wasted compute.** Redundant heads make the model robust to pruning and to KV sharing, but mean some attention compute at inference is doing little useful work."],"pitfalls":["**Sizing agent capacity from parameter count.** Two 7-8B models can differ four-fold in KV cache per token. Symptom: preemptions and queueing once transcripts grow. Fix: compute capacity from KV heads, head_dim and p95 context length.","**Reshaping without the transpose.** Going from `[B, n, h·d_k]` straight to `[B, h, n, d_k]` makes heads mix positions instead of channels. Symptom: a model that trains without error but learns slowly and produces incoherent output.","**An unsupported head dimension.** Above 256, or not a multiple of 8 for PyTorch's flash backend. Symptom: a silent fall-back to the math path, attention memory that grows with n² and much lower throughput. Force the backend in a test to make it fail loudly.","**Leaving out `W_O`, or applying it per head.** The symptom is subtle, usually that adding heads stops helping because they cannot combine. Fix: one `[h·d_k, d]` projection applied after concatenation.","**Explaining a prediction with one head's attention map.** Symptom: contradictory stories from different heads and layers for the same output. Fix: test a head's role by ablating it and measuring the change."],"when":"Multi-head attention is a given inside any transformer; what you decide is the split, or which model's\nsplit you serve.\n- **Reading a config:** `num_attention_heads`, `num_key_value_heads` and `head_dim` tell you the KV cache\n  per token, the shard granularity for tensor parallelism and whether fast kernels apply.\n- **Choosing a self-hosted agent model:** compare sessions per GPU at your transcript length alongside\n  eval scores.\n- **Designing or fine-tuning a small model:** keep d_k at a kernel-friendly size and choose *h* from that\n  rather than the other way round.\n- **Debugging from scratch:** test with h = 1 first, then confirm the reshape by checking that the\n  per-head output for a given head matches a single-head computation on that slice.\n- **Interviews:** explain why several heads beat one wide head at equal cost, what `W_O` is for, and which\n  quantities do and do not change with *h*.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Gets the view/transpose right, keeps head_dim kernel-friendly, and reads head counts off a config to size caches and pick a tensor-parallel degree.","architect":"Treats head layout as a hardware constraint: the number of query and KV heads bounds how a model can be sharded across the GPUs the team actually owns.","fde":"Explains why a customer's chosen model cannot be split evenly across their 6-GPU node and what the options are, using head counts as the argument."},"check":{"q":"You change a 4,096-wide attention layer from 8 heads to 32 heads. State what happens to the\nparameter count, the FLOPs, the attention memory and the per-head expressiveness, and why.","a":"Parameters stay at 4d² ≈ 67M because W_Q, W_K, W_V and W_O remain d×d; the heads are slices of\nthe same matrices. FLOPs stay the same because h × 2·n²·d_k = 2·n²·d regardless of the split. The\nmaterialized attention tensor `[B, h, n, n]` grows four times, from 8 to 32 n×n maps, which matters if\nyou use a kernel that writes it to memory and is irrelevant with FlashAttention. Each head's d_k drops\nfrom 512 to 128, so each head's score matrix is capped at rank 128 instead of 512 and each pattern is\nindividually less expressive, but there are four times as many of them. The KV cache per token is 2d either way for full multi-head attention; it\nonly shrinks if you reduce KV heads through GQA, which the question did not do."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does multi-head attention cost roughly the same as a single head of width d_model?","options":["Because heads share their key and value projections","Because each head works in d_model / h dimensions, so h heads together do the work of one full-width head","Because only one head is active for each token","Because the softmax is computed once and shared across heads"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Splitting the width across heads keeps the projection matrices d×d and the score FLOPs at 2·n²·d. Sharing K/V projections across heads is grouped-query attention, a separate idea."},{"q":"What is the role of the output projection W_O?","options":["It normalizes the attention weights so each row sums to one","It applies the causal mask to the concatenated heads","It mixes information across heads and maps the concatenation back into the residual stream","It reduces the KV cache by compressing the heads"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Concatenation alone pins each head to a fixed slice of the output; W_O lets heads combine and place their contribution anywhere. Masking and normalization happen inside each head before concatenation."},{"q":"Which quantity grows when you increase the number of heads at fixed d_model?","options":["The number of parameters in the attention layer","The FLOPs of the Q/K/V projections","The size of the materialized attention-probability tensor [B, h, n, n]","The KV cache per token for full multi-head attention"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Parameters and projection FLOPs depend only on d_model, and the full-MHA KV cache per token is 2d per layer. The n×n probability map is produced once per head, so its memory scales with h."}]},{"key":"model-internals/grouped-query-attention","sources":["Shazeer, 2019 — Fast Transformer Decoding: One Write-Head is All You Need","Ainslie et al., 2023 — GQA: Training Generalized Multi-Query Transformer Models from Multi-Head Checkpoints","Touvron et al., 2023 — Llama 2: Open Foundation and Fine-Tuned Chat Models"],"why":"- **Decode time is bytes read.** Each decode step processes one new token per sequence. The arithmetic is\n  tiny; the time goes into reading the weights and every cached key and value for every layer from\n  high-bandwidth memory. Time per token ≈ bytes read ÷ bandwidth.\n- **The cache dominates.** For a 7B-class model with full multi-head attention the cache costs about\n  0.5 MB per token, so 64 concurrent 4k-token sequences hold ~128 GB of cache against ~14 GB of weights.\n  The cache, not the model, sets both how many sequences fit and how fast each advances.\n- **MQA went too far.** Shazeer's 2019 multi-query attention kept all query heads but one shared key head\n  and value head, shrinking the cache by *h* (32× for a 32-head model). Quality drops measurably,\n  training can be less stable, and an existing multi-head checkpoint cannot be converted without harm.\n- **GQA is the middle.** Ainslie et al.: with *g* KV heads, each shared by h/g query heads, the cache\n  shrinks by h/g. At g = 8 for a 64-head model, quality is close to full multi-head while speed is close\n  to multi-query.\n- **It targets redundancy.** Fewer layers cuts capacity everywhere; smaller head dimensions weaken every\n  pattern; cache quantization is orthogonal and stacks on top. GQA shares the one factor that was mostly\n  redundant, because neighbouring heads' key and value projections are similar enough to share.","how":"### The grouping\n1. Take *h* query heads and *g* KV heads, with *h* divisible by *g*.\n2. Shrink the key and value projections from `[d, h·d_k]` to `[d, g·d_k]`; the query projection and `W_O`\n   are unchanged.\n3. In the forward pass **K** and **V** come out as `[B, g, n, d_k]`, and query head *i* attends to KV head\n   `i // (h/g)`.\n4. The attention math per head is unchanged.\n\nTwo ways to implement step 3:\n- **Expand:** repeat each KV head h/g times to `[B, h, n, d_k]` (the `repeat_kv` helper in Hugging Face).\n- **Index:** fused kernels read the shared head directly without copying.\n\nParameter saving for Llama-3-8B (h = 32, g = 8, d_k = 128, d = 4096): the K and V projections hold 8.4M\nparameters per layer instead of 33.6M, about 0.8B fewer across 32 layers than an all-MHA layout of the\nsame width.\n\n### Cache arithmetic\nPer token, the cache holds `2 × n_layers × g × d_k × bytes_per_element`. For 32 layers, d_k = 128 and\nfp16:\n\n```\nMHA  g = 32 : 2 × 32 × 32 × 128 × 2 = 524,288 B = 512 KB per token\nGQA  g = 8  : 2 × 32 ×  8 × 128 × 2 = 131,072 B = 128 KB per token\nMQA  g = 1  : 2 × 32 ×  1 × 128 × 2 =  16,384 B =  16 KB per token\n```\n\n| Layout | 64 sequences × 8k tokens | Fits on one 80 GB card? |\n|---|---|---|\n| MHA (g = 32) | 256 GB | no, not even on three |\n| GQA (g = 8) | 64 GB | yes, with room for weights |\n| MQA (g = 1) | 8 GB | yes |\n\n### Decode speed\n- **Fewer bytes per step.** Bytes read ≈ weights + the cache of every sequence in the batch. At batch 64\n  and 4k average length with an 8B fp16 model: ~16 GB of weights plus ~128 GB of MHA cache, against 16 +\n  32 GB with GQA.\n- **So up to ~3× decode throughput** before compute becomes the limit. Treat the factor as illustrative,\n  since real engines overlap and batch differently.\n- **Higher arithmetic intensity.** In decode attention every K/V vector loaded from HBM serves h/g query\n  heads instead of one, so intensity rises by the group size. MHA decode attention does about one\n  multiply-add per cached element loaded, which no GPU can feed from memory; GQA moves it partway toward\n  the compute-bound regime.\n- **Prefill is unaffected.** It is compute-bound, and the attention FLOPs are identical because the\n  queries are not shared. An agent step that ingests a large new tool result is mostly prefill, so GQA\n  does not make that part faster.\n\n### Uptraining existing checkpoints\n1. Take a multi-head checkpoint.\n2. **Mean-pool** the key and value projection matrices of the heads in each group into one, because that\n   preserves the group's average behaviour better than keeping one head or re-initializing.\n3. Continue pre-training for about 5% of the original compute.\n\nThat is why GQA could be retrofitted onto already-trained models; newer families (Llama 3, Mistral)\nsimply train with it from the start.\n\n### Interaction with tensor parallelism\n- **Heads are the shard unit.** With g = 8 KV heads on 8 GPUs, each GPU holds one KV head and the four\n  query heads that use it: ideal.\n- **TP degree above g forces replication.** KV heads must be copied so each GPU has the one its query\n  heads need, and the per-GPU cache saving shrinks accordingly.\n- **Engines handle it**, but the capacity arithmetic changes.\n\n### Beyond GQA\n- **Multi-head latent attention** (DeepSeek-V2) projects keys and values into a low-rank latent per token\n  and caches only that, reconstructing K and V on use. It reports a smaller cache than GQA at comparable\n  or better quality, a vendor claim worth verifying per model.\n- **Cache quantization** to 8-bit or 4-bit stacks multiplicatively with any head layout.\n- **Sliding-window attention** bounds cache *length* rather than width.","example":"### Mapping query heads onto shared KV heads, and the cache table\nThe first half shows how query heads map onto shared KV heads with a `repeat_kv` expansion; the second\ncomputes the cache table for the three layouts from a config.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\ndef repeat_kv(x, n_rep):                      # [B, g, n, dk] -> [B, g * n_rep, n, dk]\n    B, g, n, dk = x.shape\n    if n_rep == 1:\n        return x\n    return x[:, :, None].expand(B, g, n_rep, n, dk).reshape(B, g * n_rep, n, dk)\n\nB, n, dk, h, g = 1, 5, 128, 32, 8\nq = torch.randn(B, h, n, dk)\nk, v = torch.randn(B, g, n, dk), torch.randn(B, g, n, dk)      # only 8 KV heads are ever cached\nk_full, v_full = repeat_kv(k, h // g), repeat_kv(v, h // g)   # query head i uses KV head i // 4\nout = F.scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k_full, v_full, is_causal=True)\nprint(out.shape, torch.equal(k_full[:, 0], k_full[:, 3]), torch.equal(k_full[:, 3], k_full[:, 4]))\n# torch.Size([1, 32, 5, 128]) True False   -> heads 0..3 share KV head 0; head 4 starts group 1\n\ndef kv_bytes_per_token(layers, kv_heads, head_dim, dtype_bytes=2):\n    return 2 * layers * kv_heads * head_dim * dtype_bytes\n\nfor name, kv_heads in ((\"MHA 32 kv heads\", 32), (\"GQA  8 kv heads\", 8), (\"MQA  1 kv head \", 1)):\n    per_tok = kv_bytes_per_token(32, kv_heads, 128)\n    print(f\"{name}: {per_tok // 1024:4d} KB/token, {per_tok * 8192 * 64 / 2**30:6.0f} GiB for 64 x 8k\")\n# MHA 32 kv heads:  512 KB/token,    256 GiB for 64 x 8k\n# GQA  8 kv heads:  128 KB/token,     64 GiB for 64 x 8k\n# MQA  1 kv head :   16 KB/token,      8 GiB for 64 x 8k\n```\n\n### Reading the KV-head count from a real config\nCapacity estimates must use KV heads. Older configs without the field are full MHA, so fall back to the\nquery-head count.\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import AutoConfig\n\ncfg = AutoConfig.from_pretrained(\"meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B\")   # any causal LM id you can access\nkv_heads = getattr(cfg, \"num_key_value_heads\", None) or cfg.num_attention_heads\nhead_dim = getattr(cfg, \"head_dim\", None) or cfg.hidden_size // cfg.num_attention_heads\nper_token = 2 * cfg.num_hidden_layers * kv_heads * head_dim * 2   # fp16 bytes\nprint(cfg.num_attention_heads, kv_heads, per_token // 1024, \"KB/token\")\n# 32 8 128 KB/token\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: resident agent sessions at 32k tokens\nA coding agent's sessions grow to about 32,768 tokens of transcript, and the engine keeps each session's\ncache so the next step reuses its prefix. One 80 GB card with an 8B fp16 model leaves roughly 60 GB for\ncache after weights and headroom (illustrative).\n\n| Layout | Cache per token | Cache per 32k session | Resident sessions in ~60 GB |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| MHA (32 KV heads) | 512 KB | 16 GiB | about 3 |\n| GQA (8 KV heads) | 128 KB | 4 GiB | about 14 |\n| GQA plus FP8 cache | 64 KB | 2 GiB | about 28 |\n\n- **Why it matters:** sessions that do not fit are evicted or preempted, so their next step re-prefills\n  the whole transcript, which is the slow, compute-bound part.\n- **What GQA does not change:** the prefill of each new tool result costs the same on both layouts.\n\n### Worked scenario: bytes per decode step at batch 64\nAn 8B fp16 model, 64 concurrent chats or agent sessions averaging 4k tokens, on a GPU with roughly\n2 TB/s of memory bandwidth (illustrative orders of magnitude, not a benchmark).\n\n| Layout | Weights | Cache read per step | Total bytes | Bandwidth floor per step |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| MHA (32 KV heads) | ~16 GB | ~128 GB | ~144 GB | ~72 ms |\n| GQA (8 KV heads) | ~16 GB | ~32 GB | ~48 GB | ~24 ms |\n\nThe MHA case also does not fit on one 80 GB card at all; GQA fits and runs each step about three times\nfaster. Time-to-first-token barely changes, because prefill is compute-bound.","patterns":["**Plan agent capacity from `num_key_value_heads`.** Put the KV-head count, not the query-head count, into the cache estimator with your p95 transcript length, then set the engine's context and concurrency limits (for example `max_model_len` and `max_num_seqs` in vLLM) from it. Monitor KV-cache utilization and preemptions.","**Prefer GQA models for long-lived agent sessions.** Shortlist models by agent eval score, then by KV bytes per token. Measure sessions per GPU and tokens per second per GPU at the target batch, not single-stream latency, because that is where the saving appears.","**Stack cache quantization on top.** Enable an FP8 KV cache (for example `kv_cache_dtype=\"fp8\"` in vLLM) after running your agent eval set; it halves the cache again on any head layout. Monitor tool-call accuracy and resident sequences.","**Align tensor parallelism with KV heads.** Keep the TP degree at or below *g* and dividing it, such as 8 GPUs for g = 8. Monitor per-GPU cache memory when you add GPUs; if it does not shrink, KV heads are being replicated.","**Use a GQA-aware attention path.** Let the engine's fused kernel index shared heads; in your own PyTorch code, `F.scaled_dot_product_attention` accepts `enable_gqa=True` (PyTorch 2.5+) instead of a materialized `repeat_kv`. Monitor prefill activation memory."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent sessions are long and keep their transcript cached between steps.\n  The KV-head count sets how many of those sessions stay resident on a GPU, and how fast each one\n  decodes its next tool call or answer.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `num_key_value_heads` in the model config, the cache and\n  concurrency limits of your serving engine (for example `max_model_len` and `max_num_seqs` in vLLM),\n  and KV-cache utilization and preemption metrics.\n- **What you control.** Which open model you self-host (GQA layout versus full MHA), the tensor-parallel\n  degree, an FP8 KV cache, and context caps per agent route.\n- **What you can skip.** Uptraining recipes and kernel internals, unless you pre-train. On hosted APIs\n  GQA is invisible; it only matters when you run the model yourself.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Self-hosting a multi-tenant agent platform.** KV-head count and p95 transcript length set how many\n  sessions stay resident per GPU before preemption.\n- **Choosing an open model for long agent runs.** Among models that pass your agent evals, the GQA layout\n  usually serves several times the sessions on the same hardware.\n- **Planning GPU nodes.** A model with 8 KV heads shards cleanly across 8 GPUs; other degrees replicate KV\n  heads and waste cache.\n- **Cutting serving cost further.** An FP8 cache stacks on GQA, checked against tool-call accuracy evals.","mini_project":"**Compare resident agent sessions across head layouts (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show, with numbers from configs and one real engine run, how KV heads change agent capacity.\n- **Steps:** read `num_key_value_heads` for an MHA and a GQA model of similar size; compute cache per 32k\n  session; serve the GQA model in vLLM on one GPU with a fixed `gpu_memory_utilization` and replay 20 synthetic agent sessions\n  of growing length; record KV-cache utilization and preemptions; repeat with an FP8 cache.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of predicted versus observed resident sessions per configuration and a\n  short note on where the prediction was off and why."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"GQA cuts attention compute by sharing work across heads.\"** Only keys and values are shared; every query head still attends, so attention FLOPs are unchanged. The saving is cache memory and cache bytes read per decode step, both down by h/g.","**\"A GQA model makes every agent step faster.\"** Prefill is compute-bound, so ingesting a large tool result and time-to-first-token barely change; the gain shows in decode throughput and resident sessions.","**\"You can turn an MHA checkpoint into GQA by averaging heads.\"** Mean-pooling is only the start; without continued pre-training of about 5% of the original compute, perplexity jumps.","**\"GQA and KV-cache quantization are alternatives.\"** They are orthogonal and multiply: 8 KV heads give 128 KB per token, and an FP8 cache halves that to 64 KB.","**\"More tensor-parallel GPUs always shrink the per-GPU cache.\"** Above g, KV heads must be replicated, so per-GPU cache stops shrinking; keep the TP degree at or below g and dividing it."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    X[\"Hidden states\"] --> QP[\"Query projection: h heads\"]\n    X --> KVP[\"K and V projection: g heads\"]\n    KVP --> C[(\"KV cache: g heads per layer\")]\n    QP --> G1[\"Query heads 0 to 3\"]\n    QP --> G2[\"Query heads 4 to 7\"]\n    C -->|\"KV head 0\"| G1\n    C -->|\"KV head 1\"| G2\n    G1 --> A[\"Attention per head, unchanged math\"]\n    G2 --> A\n    A --> O[\"W_O and residual stream\"]","caption":"Query heads are grouped onto shared KV heads, so only *g* KV heads are ever written to the cache and each\none loaded serves h/g queries. Every agent session's transcript is stored in that smaller cache."},"interview":[{"q":"What problem does GQA solve, and why share keys and values rather than queries?","a":"- **Decode is bandwidth-bound on the KV cache**, which scales with the number of KV heads, so cutting KV\n  heads cuts both memory and bytes read per step.\n- **Queries are not cached.** Only the new token's query exists each step, so sharing queries would save\n  nothing in memory and would cut attention capacity."},{"q":"Your self-hosted agent platform shows frequent preemptions once sessions pass 30k tokens. How does head layout factor in?","a":"- **Compute cache per session:** with 32 layers and head_dim 128, MHA holds 512 KB per token (16 GiB at\n  32k) versus 128 KB with 8 KV heads (4 GiB).\n- **Preempted sessions re-prefill** their whole transcript on the next step, so latency spikes.\n- **Levers:** a GQA model, an FP8 cache after an eval, a context cap or compaction per route, or more\n  replicas."},{"q":"After switching to a GQA model, TTFT did not improve but throughput did. Is something broken?","a":"- **No, that is expected.** Prefill is compute-bound and attention FLOPs are unchanged, because every\n  query head still attends.\n- **The gain is in decode:** fewer cache bytes per step and higher arithmetic intensity, so per-token\n  latency and batch throughput improve while time-to-first-token stays flat."},{"q":"When would you not convert an existing MHA model to GQA yourself?","a":"- **Almost always.** Conversion needs mean-pooled K/V projections plus continued pre-training of about 5%\n  of original compute, or quality drops sharply.\n- **Pick a model trained with GQA instead**, unless you own the pre-training pipeline and the model is\n  unique to you."},{"q":"How does the KV-head count constrain tensor parallelism?","a":"- **Heads are the shard unit.** With g = 8 on 8 GPUs, each GPU holds one KV head and its four query heads.\n- **Above g, KV heads are replicated**, so adding GPUs stops shrinking per-GPU cache memory; plan the TP\n  degree to divide g."},{"q":"Estimate the decode step time floor for an 8B model at batch 64 and 4k context, MHA versus GQA.","a":"- **Bytes per step:** ~16 GB of fp16 weights plus the batch's cache: ~128 GB with 32 KV heads, ~32 GB with\n  8, so ~144 GB versus ~48 GB.\n- **Floor:** at roughly 2 TB/s that is ~72 ms versus ~24 ms per step, so GQA decodes about three times\n  faster, before compute or kernel overheads bite (illustrative orders of magnitude).\n- **Caveat:** the MHA batch does not fit on one 80 GB card at all, so in practice it runs a smaller batch."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cache and decode bytes vs quality.** Both drop by h/g for a small quality loss. The trade moves toward MQA when memory is the hard limit (edge devices, extreme batch) and back toward full MHA for small models at short context, where the cache was never the constraint.","**More resident sessions vs unchanged prefill.** GQA keeps several times more agent transcripts cached, but each step that ingests a large tool result still pays full prefill. For agents dominated by huge tool outputs, prefix caching and shorter results matter as much as head layout.","**Fewer K/V parameters vs unchanged attention FLOPs.** A little memory and prefill compute is saved, but attention FLOPs are unchanged because queries are not shared. GQA is nearly invisible in time-to-first-token and only shows in per-token decode latency and throughput.","**Retrofit vs pick a GQA model.** Uptraining retrofits GQA onto a model you already have but costs around 5% of pre-training compute plus the data pipeline. For almost everyone the right move is to pick a model trained with GQA.","**KV-head count vs hardware fit.** It sets the finest tensor-parallel split without replication, so g = 8 fits 8-GPU nodes neatly and awkwardly fits anything else."],"pitfalls":["**Using `num_attention_heads` instead of `num_key_value_heads` in a capacity estimate.** Symptom: a cache budget four times too large, and a cluster sized or rejected on the wrong number. Fix: read `num_key_value_heads`, falling back to the query-head count only when the field is absent.","**Sizing for average sessions, not long ones.** Agent transcripts have a long tail. Symptom: preemptions and re-prefills whenever a few sessions grow long. Fix: size on p95 transcript length and cap context per route.","**Expecting GQA to cut time-to-first-token.** Symptom: an unchanged TTFT after switching models while only throughput and per-token latency improved. Fix: judge GQA on decode throughput and inter-token latency; attack TTFT with prefix caching or chunked prefill instead.","**Materializing `repeat_kv` copies in a custom attention path at prefill.** Symptom: transient activation memory as large as MHA even though the cache itself is small. Fix: use a kernel that indexes shared heads, or `enable_gqa=True`.","**Converting an MHA checkpoint by slicing or averaging heads without uptraining.** Symptom: a sharp perplexity jump on the first evaluation that only continued pre-training (the paper's ~5% uptraining) recovers. Fix: budget the uptraining run, or pick a model trained with GQA.","**Tensor-parallel degree larger than the KV-head count.** Symptom: per-GPU cache memory that does not shrink as expected when you add GPUs. Fix: keep the TP degree at or below the KV-head count, and use extra GPUs for replicas or pipeline stages."],"when":"- **Choosing a model to serve agents:** at long context or high concurrency, read `num_key_value_heads`\n  before the benchmark table and compute cache-per-token for your sequence lengths. Two models with\n  equal quality on your evaluation can differ by four in how many users one GPU serves.\n- **Prefer GQA** for chat and agent workloads where decode and resident sessions dominate; it matters\n  less for short-prompt classification where prefill dominates.\n- **Training or fine-tuning:** keep the layout the checkpoint was trained with.\n- **Measure:** tokens per second per GPU at your target batch, not single-stream latency, because that is\n  where the saving appears.\n- **Interviews:** derive the cache formula, explain why decode speed follows bytes rather than FLOPs, and\n  place MHA, GQA and MQA on that line.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Plugs `num_key_value_heads` into the cache estimator, sets batch and context limits in the serving engine from it, and checks that the kernel path indexes shared heads instead of copying.","architect":"Decides model families and GPU counts with the KV-head count as a first-class input, since it fixes both cache capacity and how evenly the model shards.","fde":"Shows a customer why the GQA model serves four times their concurrent users on the same hardware as the MHA model they benchmarked, with the per-token bytes as the evidence."},"check":{"q":"Two 7B-class models score identically on your evaluation. One uses full multi-head attention with\n32 KV heads, the other GQA with 8. You serve 4k-token chats on one 80 GB GPU. Quantify the difference and\nsay what else you would check.","a":"Both have 32 layers and head_dim 128, so the cache per token is 2 × 32 × 32 × 128 × 2 bytes\n= 512 KB for MHA and 128 KB for GQA. A 4k chat costs 2 GB versus 0.5 GB. After ~14 GB of fp16 weights,\nroughly 66 GB remain, so about 30 MHA chats fit against about 130 GQA chats, before fragmentation and\nactivations. Per decode step, bytes read drop from weights plus 2 GB per active chat to weights plus\n0.5 GB, so at equal batch the GQA model generates tokens faster, and at equal memory it serves four times\nthe concurrency. I would also check that the two evaluations covered long-context behaviour, that the\nserving engine actually uses a GQA-aware kernel, and that my tensor-parallel plan divides 8 KV heads\nevenly."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does grouped-query attention primarily reduce?","options":["The number of query heads and therefore attention FLOPs","The number of key/value heads, and with them the KV cache size and bytes read per decode step","The number of transformer layers needed for a given quality","The size of the vocabulary embedding"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Queries stay at full head count; only keys and values are shared across groups. The win is memory and bandwidth during decoding, not arithmetic, which is why prefill speed is essentially unchanged."},{"q":"Llama-3-8B has 32 query heads and 8 KV heads. How many query heads share each KV head?","options":["1","2","4","8"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Group size is 32 / 8 = 4: query heads 0–3 use KV head 0, 4–7 use KV head 1, and so on. The cache is therefore a quarter of what full multi-head attention would need."},{"q":"Why does GQA give almost no improvement to time-to-first-token?","options":["Because the prompt is processed without attention","Because the cache is only created after the first token","Because prefill is compute-bound and the attention FLOPs are unchanged when only K and V are shared","Because the output projection dominates prefill time"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Prefill multiplies large matrices and is limited by arithmetic, not by reading the cache. Sharing K/V heads changes how many bytes decode must read per step, which is where GQA's gain appears."}]},{"key":"model-internals/flashattention","sources":["Dao et al., 2022 — FlashAttention: Fast and Memory-Efficient Exact Attention with IO-Awareness","Dao, 2023 — FlashAttention-2: Faster Attention with Better Parallelism and Work Partitioning","Milakov and Gimelshein, 2018 — Online normalizer calculation for softmax"],"why":"- **Standard attention round-trips through HBM.** It runs `S = Q·Kᵀ`, `P = softmax(S)`, `O = P·V` as\n  separate kernels, so **S** is written to GPU memory, read back for softmax, **P** is written, then read\n  again for the final matmul. For 32 heads at 8k tokens in fp16, **S** alone is about 4.3 GB per layer,\n  written and re-read several times per forward pass and again in backward.\n- **The FLOPs were never the problem.** Attention costs about 4·n²·d per layer and the two matmuls run on\n  tensor cores. The trouble is everything between them.\n- **The elementwise steps are memory-bound.** An A100 does on the order of 300 TFLOPs in fp16 but moves\n  only about 1.5–2 TB/s from HBM, so an operation needs well over a hundred FLOPs per byte to be\n  compute-bound. Softmax, masking and dropout do about one FLOP per byte.\n- **So n² traffic set the time and n² memory set the length.** Models of that era trained at\n  512–2,048 tokens, far short of a modern agent transcript.\n- **Approximations missed the point.** Sparse patterns, low-rank projections and kernel feature maps\n  reduce FLOPs but often not wall-clock time, because the memory traffic stayed, and they changed the\n  model's behaviour. FlashAttention optimized the number of HBM reads and writes, as external-memory\n  algorithms do, while leaving the math exact.","how":"### Background: the memory hierarchy the kernel is written for\n| Memory | Size | Speed |\n|---|---|---|\n| HBM | tens of gigabytes | a few TB/s, slow relative to compute |\n| On-chip SRAM (shared memory per streaming multiprocessor) | around 20 MB in total on an A100, in the paper's accounting | an order of magnitude faster |\n\n**Kernel fusion** means doing as much work as possible on data while it sits in SRAM before writing\nanything back.\n\n### Tiling\n1. Split queries into blocks of B_r rows and keys and values into blocks of B_c rows, sized so one block\n   of each fits in SRAM.\n2. For each query block, iterate over the key/value blocks: load K_j and V_j.\n3. Compute the partial scores `S_ij = Q_i·K_jᵀ`, a B_r × B_c tile that lives in SRAM.\n4. Fold it into the running output and move on. The full **S** and **P** never exist in HBM.\n\n**The causal mask becomes a saving.** A K/V block lying entirely to the right of the query block is\nskipped without being loaded, and only blocks on the diagonal need element-wise masking. Causal\nattention does roughly half the work of the unmasked version.\n\n### Online softmax\nSoftmax needs each row's maximum and the sum of exponentials over *all* n columns, unknown until the\nlast block. The trick, from Milakov and Gimelshein, keeps a running max *m* and running denominator *l*\nper row and rescales when a new block raises the max:\n\n```\nm_new = max(m, rowmax(S_ij))\nl_new = exp(m - m_new) · l + rowsum(exp(S_ij - m_new))\nO_new = exp(m - m_new) · O + exp(S_ij - m_new) · V_j\n```\n\n- **Why the rescale works.** Everything accumulated so far was computed relative to the old max;\n  multiplying by `exp(m − m_new)` re-expresses it relative to the new one.\n- **Exact, not approximate.** After the last block, `O / l` is the exact softmax-weighted sum, a\n  re-association of the same sum, so agent evals should move only within fp16/bf16 rounding noise when\n  you switch it on (greedy outputs can still differ on near-tied tokens).\n- **FlashAttention-2 defers the division** by *l* to the very end to cut non-matmul work.\n\n### Backward pass by recomputation (fine-tuning)\n- **Standard backward stores P**, n² per head, from the forward pass.\n- **FlashAttention stores only O and one number per row**, the log-sum-exp `L = m + log l`, then\n  recomputes tiles of **S** and **P** from **Q**, **K** and *L* on the fly inside SRAM.\n- **More FLOPs, less time.** It costs roughly one more matmul's worth of FLOPs, yet is faster than the\n  standard backward because recomputation is cheaper than reading n² values from HBM.\n- **Same idea as gradient checkpointing**, applied at the kernel level; it is what lets you fine-tune on\n  long multi-step agent trajectories.\n\n### IO complexity\n| Algorithm | HBM accesses |\n|---|---|\n| Standard | Θ(n·d + n²) |\n| FlashAttention | Θ(n²·d² / M), with *M* the SRAM size |\n\nFor typical head dimensions (64–128) and SRAM sizes that is many times fewer, and measured speedups\nfollow the IO count rather than the FLOP count.\n\n### Versions\n| Version | What changed |\n|---|---|\n| FlashAttention (2022) | the algorithm above |\n| FlashAttention-2 (2023) | parallelizes across the sequence dimension as well as batch and heads, so long sequences at small batch still fill the GPU; fewer non-matmul FLOPs; warp partitioning cuts shared-memory traffic; roughly doubled throughput over the first version |\n| FlashAttention-3 (2024) | targets Hopper GPUs with asynchronous overlap of tensor-core matmuls and softmax; adds FP8 support |\n\nExact figures vary by shape and hardware, so measure on yours.\n\n### Decode is a different problem\n- **Too little parallel work.** FlashAttention-2 parallelizes over batch, heads and query blocks. At\n  decode there is one query row per sequence, so at small batch there are too few units of work to\n  occupy the GPU, while the cost is all in reading a long K/V cache.\n- **Flash-Decoding splits the *key/value length*** across thread blocks instead, from the same authors.\n  Each block computes a partial output with its own running max and log-sum-exp over its slice of the\n  cache.\n- **A final reduction combines them** with exactly the online-softmax rule above. The same algebra that\n  lets one block stream over keys lets many blocks work on disjoint key ranges in parallel.\n- **So serving engines ship decode-specific kernels**, which matters for agents because each step decodes\n  a short tool call against a long cached transcript.\n\n### Where you meet it\n- **PyTorch:** `F.scaled_dot_product_attention` dispatches to a flash kernel when dtype, head dimension\n  and mask allow, otherwise to a memory-efficient or plain math backend.\n- **Hugging Face:** `attn_implementation=\"flash_attention_2\"` or `\"sdpa\"`.\n- **Serving engines:** vLLM and SGLang use FlashAttention-family or FlashInfer kernels. PagedAttention is\n  FlashAttention-style tiling where K/V tiles are fetched through a block table instead of a contiguous\n  buffer.\n\n### What it does not do\n- **Not sub-quadratic.** It is exact softmax attention, so FLOPs are still quadratic; it changes the\n  constant and the memory, not the asymptotics.\n- **Hardware and dtype limits.** FlashAttention-2 requires fp16 or bf16 inputs, head dimensions up to 256\n  and Ampere or newer GPUs.\n- **Mask limits.** It supports causal, sliding-window, variable-length (packed) sequences and ALiBi\n  slopes, but not an arbitrary dense additive mask; such masks fall back to other backends.\n- **FlexAttention fills the gap.** PyTorch's FlexAttention compiles custom mask patterns into fused\n  kernels for cases FlashAttention does not cover.","example":"### Online softmax in NumPy: tiles give the exact answer\nNo GPU needed. One query block streams over K/V tiles of 16 rows with the running max and sum, and\nmatches the naive result exactly (up to float rounding).\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef naive(Q, K, V):\n    S = Q @ K.T / np.sqrt(Q.shape[1])\n    P = np.exp(S - S.max(1, keepdims=True)); P /= P.sum(1, keepdims=True)\n    return P @ V\n\ndef tiled(Q, K, V, Bc=16):\n    \"\"\"One query block, streaming K/V blocks with an online softmax (no n x n matrix).\"\"\"\n    n, d = Q.shape\n    m = np.full(n, -np.inf); l = np.zeros(n); O = np.zeros((n, V.shape[1]))\n    for j in range(0, K.shape[0], Bc):\n        S = Q @ K[j:j + Bc].T / np.sqrt(d)             # a small n x Bc tile\n        m_new = np.maximum(m, S.max(1))\n        scale = np.exp(m - m_new)                      # rescale what was accumulated so far\n        p = np.exp(S - m_new[:, None])\n        l = scale * l + p.sum(1)\n        O = scale[:, None] * O + p @ V[j:j + Bc]\n        m = m_new\n    return O / l[:, None]                              # divide once at the end (FlashAttention-2)\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nQ, K, V = (rng.normal(size=(64, 32)) for _ in range(3))\nprint(np.allclose(naive(Q, K, V), tiled(Q, K, V)))     # True: exact, just re-associated\n```\n\n### Naive attention against the flash backend on one GPU\nNaive attention against the flash backend on one GPU, comparing peak memory, time and the outputs, at\n8,192 tokens, a modest agent transcript.\n\n- **Naive** allocates three ~4.3 GB tensors in sequence (scores, masked scores, probabilities), which are\n  all live at once at the softmax.\n- **Flash** adds only its output and one statistic per row, so its peak stays well under a gigabyte\n  including the ~200 MB of inputs.\n\n```python\nimport time, torch, torch.nn.functional as F\nfrom torch.nn.attention import sdpa_kernel, SDPBackend          # PyTorch 2.3+\n\nB, h, n, dk = 1, 32, 8192, 128\nq, k, v = (torch.randn(B, h, n, dk, device=\"cuda\", dtype=torch.float16) for _ in range(3))\nfuture = torch.triu(torch.ones(n, n, device=\"cuda\", dtype=torch.bool), 1)\n\ndef naive():\n    s = (q @ k.transpose(-1, -2)) / dk ** 0.5             # [1, 32, 8192, 8192] fp16 -> ~4.3 GB in HBM\n    p = s.masked_fill(future, float(\"-inf\")).softmax(-1)  # another ~4.3 GB, read back for the matmul\n    return p @ v\n\ndef flash():\n    with sdpa_kernel(SDPBackend.FLASH_ATTENTION):          # raises if the flash kernel cannot be used\n        return F.scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v, is_causal=True)\n\nfor name, fn in ((\"naive\", naive), (\"flash\", flash)):\n    torch.cuda.synchronize(); torch.cuda.reset_peak_memory_stats()\n    fn(); torch.cuda.synchronize(); t = time.perf_counter()\n    for _ in range(5):\n        out = fn()\n    torch.cuda.synchronize()\n    print(f\"{name}: {(time.perf_counter() - t) / 5 * 1e3:6.1f} ms/call  \"\n          f\"peak {torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated() / 2**30:5.1f} GiB\")\n\nprint(torch.allclose(naive(), flash(), atol=2e-3, rtol=2e-3))   # True: same result up to fp16 rounding\n# Expect roughly 12 GiB peak for naive (3 x 4 GiB plus inputs) versus well under 1 GiB for flash, and flash several times faster;\n# the exact numbers depend on the GPU. Passing an explicit attn_mask instead of is_causal would disable flash.\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: fine-tuning on agent trajectories, padded versus packed\nA team fine-tunes a model on recorded agent trajectories (tool calls plus results). One batch of 8 traces\nhas lengths 1k, 2k, 2k, 3k, 4k, 6k, 8k and 16k tokens (illustrative).\n\n| Setup | Tokens processed | Real tokens | Waste |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Padded to the longest trace | 8 × 16k = 128k | 42k | about 3× the needed work |\n| Packed with variable-length flash attention | about 42k | 42k | near zero |\n| Naive eager attention on the 16k trace | n² scores ≈ 4× those at 8k per layer | — | likely OOM before any waste matters |\n\n- **Why the last row matters:** at 16k the score matrix per layer is four times the 4.3 GB of the 8k case,\n  so eager attention cannot even hold one long trace on most cards.\n- **Takeaway:** for agent-trace fine-tuning, flash attention plus packing decides whether long\n  trajectories are trainable at all, and at what cost.\n\n### Worked scenario: what the naive path moves per layer\n32 heads, n = 8,192, fp16, on a GPU with about 2 TB/s of HBM bandwidth (illustrative arithmetic, not a\nbenchmark).\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Score matrix **S** per layer | 32 × 8192² × 2 bytes ≈ 4.3 GB |\n| Naive traffic: write S, read S, write P, read P | ≈ 17 GB per layer per forward |\n| Time for that traffic alone | ≈ 8.6 ms per layer, ≈ 275 ms across 32 layers |\n| Q, K, V and O tensors | about 67 MB each |\n| What FlashAttention writes to HBM | O plus one log-sum-exp per row; never S or P |\n\nThe naive time is spent moving bytes the matmuls could have consumed in SRAM, which is why the speedup\ntracks IO, not FLOPs.","patterns":["**Prove the fast path is active.** In tests, wrap attention in `sdpa_kernel(SDPBackend.FLASH_ATTENTION)` so a fallback raises. In staging, check that profiler kernel names contain flash and that peak memory grows linearly with sequence length.","**Select the kernel explicitly for agent-trace fine-tuning.** Load with `attn_implementation=\"flash_attention_2\"` (needs the flash-attn package) or `\"sdpa\"`, never `\"eager\"`, and keep `output_attentions` off. Monitor throughput and OOM counts per sequence-length bucket.","**Pack sequences instead of padding.** Use variable-length attention (for example the flash-attn package's varlen interface) when training on agent trajectories, and a continuous-batching engine such as vLLM or SGLang when serving agents. Monitor GPU time per real token rather than per padded token.","**Check the hardware before promising long agent context.** FlashAttention-2 needs Ampere or newer and FlashAttention-3 targets H100-class parts. On older cards, cap context or fall back to a memory-efficient backend. Track OOMs by GPU type.","**Use FlexAttention for unusual masks.** When you need a custom pattern (document masks between packed traces, prefix-LM), express it as a FlexAttention mask function rather than a dense mask tensor, which would disable flash. Monitor step time against the plain causal kernel."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent transcripts and training traces run to tens of thousands of\n  tokens. Without fused attention, the n² score matrix makes those lengths run out of memory or crawl;\n  with it, memory grows linearly with transcript length.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `attn_implementation` when loading Hugging Face models for\n  fine-tuning on agent traces, `F.scaled_dot_product_attention` in custom code, and the attention\n  backends inside vLLM and SGLang when you serve agents yourself.\n- **What you control.** Dtype (bf16 or fp16), mask form (`is_causal` rather than dense tensors), packing\n  instead of padding, and GPU generation (Ampere or newer).\n- **What you can skip.** The CUDA tiling details and IO-complexity proof. Keep \"exact, memory-bound win,\n  silent fallbacks exist, still quadratic in FLOPs\". On hosted APIs this is the provider's concern.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Fine-tuning on long agent trajectories.** Flash attention plus packing makes 10k-plus-token traces\n  trainable on one GPU without padding waste.\n- **Self-hosting long-context agents.** Linear attention memory lets the serving engine hold long\n  transcripts; a silent fallback shows up as OOMs on the longest sessions.\n- **Hardware planning for customer deployments.** Older GPUs without flash kernels cap how long an agent's\n  context can be.\n- **Custom masks for packed traces.** FlexAttention keeps document masks between packed trajectories on a\n  fused kernel.","mini_project":"**Make a long-context agent model fail loudly instead of silently (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove your agent model's attention stays on the fast path at its maximum transcript length.\n- **Steps:** load a small open model in bf16 with `attn_implementation=\"sdpa\"`; build prompts of 2k, 8k\n  and 16k tokens from a recorded agent transcript; measure peak memory and latency per length; repeat with\n  `\"eager\"`; add a test that runs one forward pass under `sdpa_kernel(SDPBackend.FLASH_ATTENTION)`.\n- **Done when:** you have a table showing linear versus quadratic memory growth across the two settings,\n  and the test fails when you switch the model to fp32."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"FlashAttention is an approximation, like sparse or low-rank attention.\"** It is exact: online softmax rescales earlier partial sums by `exp(m − m_new)`, so outputs differ from eager only by fp16 or bf16 rounding.","**\"It makes long context cheap.\"** FLOPs stay quadratic; it removes the n² score memory and most HBM traffic, so long transcripts fit, but beyond roughly 100k tokens you still need windowing, sparsity or retrieval.","**\"Attention is a small share of FLOPs, so a faster kernel cannot matter much.\"** Naive attention is memory-bound: softmax and masking do about one FLOP per byte, so HBM round-trips of the scores set the time, not the arithmetic.","**\"Calling SDPA or setting `attn_implementation` means flash is running.\"** fp32 inputs, an unsupported head dimension, a dense mask tensor or a pre-Ampere GPU silently fall back; force the backend in a test to make it fail loudly.","**\"The same kernel speeds up decode.\"** Decode has one query row per sequence, so engines use Flash-Decoding, which splits the cached key/value length across thread blocks instead."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"Load query block Q_i into SRAM\"] --> INIT[\"Init running max m, sum l, output O\"]\n    INIT --> SKIP{\"Next K/V tile fully in the future?\"}\n    SKIP -->|\"yes, causal skip, never loaded\"| MORE\n    SKIP -->|\"no\"| LOAD[\"Load K_j, V_j tile into SRAM\"]\n    LOAD --> S[\"Score tile Q_i times K_j in SRAM\"]\n    S --> UP[\"Rescale O and l by new max\"]\n    UP --> MORE{\"More K/V tiles?\"}\n    MORE -->|\"yes\"| SKIP\n    MORE -->|\"no\"| OUT[\"Write O divided by l to HBM\"]\n    OUT --> LSE[\"Save one log-sum-exp per row\"]","caption":"The kernel's loop for one query block: K/V tiles stream through SRAM, the running max and sum are\nrescaled each time, and only the final output goes back to HBM."},"interview":[{"q":"Is FlashAttention an approximation? Explain why or why not.","a":"- **No, it is exact.** Online softmax keeps a running max and denominator per row and rescales earlier\n  partial sums by `exp(m − m_new)`, so the final `O / l` is the same sum re-associated.\n- **Differences versus eager are rounding only**, from a different summation order in fp16 or bf16, so\n  compare with fp16-level tolerances."},{"q":"Your self-hosted agent server OOMs only when a long session arrives, though tests passed. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Assume a silent fallback.** Check for fp32 inputs, an unsupported head dimension, an explicit dense\n  mask instead of `is_causal=True`, `output_attentions=True`, or a pre-Ampere GPU.\n- **Make it loud:** force `SDPBackend.FLASH_ATTENTION` in a test with a transcript at your maximum\n  context, then confirm in the profiler that kernel names contain flash and the memory curve is linear."},{"q":"You are fine-tuning on agent trajectories from 1k to 16k tokens. How do you keep it affordable?","a":"- **Flash attention on**, via `attn_implementation`, so a 16k trace does not materialize n² scores.\n- **Pack, do not pad.** Padding a batch to its longest trace can triple the work; variable-length\n  attention processes only real tokens.\n- **Measure** GPU time per real token and peak memory per length bucket."},{"q":"Why does recomputing attention in the backward pass make it faster rather than slower?","a":"- **The alternative is reading n² values from HBM**, which is bandwidth-bound and slow.\n- **Recomputation from Q, K and one log-sum-exp per row** happens in SRAM on tensor cores; it adds about a\n  matmul's worth of FLOPs but saves far more time in memory traffic."},{"q":"When does FlashAttention not solve your long-context agent problem?","a":"- **When compute, not memory, is the wall.** FLOPs stay quadratic, so beyond roughly 100k tokens you\n  still need windowing, sparsity, sequence parallelism or retrieval.\n- **When the hardware or mask does not qualify**, such as T4 or V100 cards, fp32 requirements or dense\n  custom biases, which fall back to slower backends."},{"q":"Why do serving engines use a different kernel for decode, such as Flash-Decoding?","a":"- **Decode has one query row per sequence**, so parallelizing over batch, heads and query blocks leaves\n  the GPU underused at small batch while it reads a long cache.\n- **Flash-Decoding splits the key/value length** across thread blocks and merges partial results with the\n  online-softmax rule, so many blocks read the cache in parallel."}],"tradeoffs":["**O(n) memory vs constraints.** Exact results with O(n) extra memory instead of O(n²), paid for with constraints on dtype (fp16/bf16), head dimension and GPU generation. The trade flips only when you genuinely need fp32 attention or a dense custom bias the kernel does not support.","**Recomputation vs storage.** The backward pass recomputes probabilities rather than storing them: more FLOPs for less memory. At very short sequences, where the n² term is small anyway, the gain over a good fused baseline is modest.","**Speed vs visibility.** The fused kernel never materializes attention weights, so interpretability and debugging that need the maps must run a separate, slow eager path.","**Fits vs cheap.** It makes long agent contexts fit but not cheap: compute is still quadratic, so beyond roughly 100k tokens you still need windowing, sparsity, sequence parallelism or retrieval."],"pitfalls":["**Believing the flash path is active when it silently fell back.** Causes: fp32 inputs, an odd head dimension or an explicit dense mask. Symptom: memory that scales with n² and no speedup. Force the backend with `sdpa_kernel` during testing so it fails loudly.","**Passing an explicit causal mask tensor instead of `is_causal=True`.** Symptom: the same n² memory curve, because the kernel cannot use the fast path. Fix: pass `is_causal=True` (or the kernel's sliding-window parameter) and drop the mask tensor.","**Comparing flash and eager outputs with fp32-level tolerances.** Symptom: spurious \"mismatch\" alarms caused by the different summation order under fp16 rounding. Fix: compare with fp16-level tolerances such as `atol=2e-3`, or against an fp32 reference.","**Leaving `output_attentions=True` or an eager attention implementation in a production config.** Symptom: throughput that collapses and OOMs at lengths the model handled in testing, often only when a long agent session arrives. Fix: pin `attn_implementation` in the serving config and assert it at startup.","**Padding batches to the longest sequence and running flash on dense tensors.** Symptom: GPU time that scales with the longest prompt or trace rather than the total number of real tokens. Use variable-length (packed) attention or a continuous-batching engine."],"when":"Use it whenever it is available, which in practice means ensuring you have not accidentally disabled it.\n- **Stay on the fast path:** inputs in bf16 or fp16, head dimensions in the supported set, masks as\n  `is_causal` or sliding-window parameters rather than dense tensors, and sequences packed rather than\n  padded.\n- **Verify with a profiler** that attention kernels carry flash names and that peak memory grows linearly\n  with sequence length.\n- **Plan hardware:** FlashAttention-2 needs Ampere or newer and the Hopper-specific version needs\n  H100-class parts, which affects whether a customer's older GPUs can run long-context agent workloads.\n- **Skip worrying** when you only call hosted APIs; there it is the provider's job.\n- **Interviews:** attention was memory-bound, tiling plus online softmax avoids the n² matrix,\n  recomputation makes backward fit, and all of it is exact.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps the model on the fast path (dtype, head_dim, mask form, packing) and checks kernel names and memory curves in the profiler rather than trusting a config flag.","architect":"Sets maximum context and GPU generation knowing that long-context feasibility depends on these kernels existing for the target hardware, and decides when exact attention must give way to windowed or retrieval-based designs.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's out-of-memory at long prompts on V100 or T4 cards, where the flash kernels are unavailable, and explains the fp16 and hardware prerequisites."},"check":{"q":"At 2k context, attention is a small fraction of a transformer's total FLOPs, yet FlashAttention\ndelivered large end-to-end training speedups. Explain why.","a":"FLOP share is the wrong lens for a memory-bound operation. Standard attention writes the n×n\nscores to HBM, reads them for softmax, writes the probabilities, reads them again for the value matmul,\nand repeats most of that in backward. Those elementwise passes do about one FLOP per byte moved, far\nbelow the ratio a GPU needs to be compute-bound, so the streaming multiprocessors idle while waiting on\nmemory, and attention takes wall-clock time out of proportion to its arithmetic. Fusing the steps and\nkeeping the tiles in SRAM removes that traffic, and storing only O(n) state per head frees memory, which\nlets you raise batch size or sequence length and improves utilization of the matmul-heavy layers too.\nThe speedup is a bandwidth saving, not a FLOP saving."},"quiz":[{"q":"FlashAttention is faster than standard attention mainly because it","options":["Computes fewer floating-point operations by approximating the softmax","Performs far fewer reads and writes to high-bandwidth memory by never materializing the n×n matrix","Uses lower precision than the standard implementation","Skips attention for positions far from the query"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The math is exact and the FLOPs are slightly higher because of recomputation; the win is IO. Tiling keeps score tiles in SRAM and avoids writing and re-reading the quadratic matrix."},{"q":"What allows the softmax to be computed block by block without seeing a whole row first?","options":["Rows are sorted before processing so the maximum comes first","Each block's softmax is computed independently and averaged","A running row maximum and running denominator are kept and earlier partial sums are rescaled when the maximum changes","The softmax is replaced by a linear normalization"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Online softmax maintains m and l per row and multiplies accumulated terms by exp(m_old − m_new) when a new block raises the maximum, which reproduces the exact result after the last block."},{"q":"What does the FlashAttention backward pass store instead of the n×n probability matrix?","options":["Nothing; it recomputes everything including the forward output","The forward output and one log-sum-exp value per row, recomputing probability tiles on the fly","A compressed low-rank copy of the probability matrix","Only the attention scores of the diagonal blocks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Keeping O and the per-row log-sum-exp L is enough to regenerate any tile of P from Q and K inside SRAM, which costs some FLOPs but avoids storing and reading n² values from memory."}]},{"key":"model-internals/positional-encoding","sources":["Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Su et al., 2021 — RoFormer: Enhanced Transformer with Rotary Position Embedding","Press et al., 2022 — Train Short, Test Long: Attention with Linear Biases Enables Input Length Extrapolation","Chen et al., 2023 — Extending Context Window of Large Language Models via Positional Interpolation"],"why":"- **Attention is order-blind.** Without position information, \"dog bites man\" and \"man bites dog\" give\n  identical per-token outputs; permuting the input permutes the output and nothing else. An agent could\n  not tell which tool result came last.\n- **A good scheme must do four things at once:** make every position distinguishable; let the model learn\n  *relative* offsets, because most linguistic structure is local (adjective before noun, matching\n  brackets, the previous token); degrade gracefully past trained lengths; and be cheap and compatible\n  with caching keys and values.\n- **Earlier schemes each missed something** (table below): additive signals get mixed into content,\n  learned tables hard-cap context, relative biases complicate the kernel and still extrapolate poorly.\n- **RoPE encodes absolute position as a rotation** of queries and keys, so `qₘ·kₙ` depends only on\n  `n − m` and the contents. No parameters, only a cheap elementwise rotation, any attention kernel, and\n  cached keys stay valid as the sequence grows.\n- **ALiBi is the main alternative.** It adds a fixed distance penalty to the scores; it extrapolates\n  better out of the box but imposes a recency prior that can hurt long-range retrieval. The open-model\n  ecosystem converged on RoPE.\n\n| Scheme | How | Strength | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sinusoidal (original transformer) | fixed vectors added to embeddings | a shift by *k* positions is a linear function of the sinusoids, so offsets are learnable in principle | added once at the input, mixed into values and content; the model must disentangle it |\n| Learned table (GPT-2, BERT) | one vector per position | simple and strong at trained lengths | hard-caps context at the table size; rarely seen positions undertrained |\n| Relative bias (Shaw et al., T5) | learned bias per offset added to scores | better inductive bias | buckets offsets, adds a term to the kernel, still extrapolates poorly |\n| RoPE | rotate q and k by position | relative, parameter-free, cache-friendly | extrapolates poorly past trained length |\n| ALiBi | fixed distance penalty on scores | extrapolates out of the box | recency prior |","how":"### Background: the rotation\n1. Split the head dimension *d* into d/2 pairs.\n2. Give pair *i* a frequency `θ_i = base^(−2i/d)`, with base = 10,000 in the original paper and Llama 2,\n   and 500,000 in Llama 3.\n3. At position *m*, rotate each pair by the angle `m·θ_i`:\n\n```\n[x, y] -> [x·cos(mθ_i) − y·sin(mθ_i),  x·sin(mθ_i) + y·cos(mθ_i)]\n```\n\n4. Apply it to queries and keys, **not** to values.\n\nWhy it works:\n- **The dot product becomes relative.** A 2-D rotation matrix satisfies `R_mᵀ·R_n = R_(n−m)`, so\n  `(R_m q)·(R_n k) = q·R_(n−m) k`. The absolute positions cancel; only the offset and the contents remain.\n- **Values are left alone** because they carry content summed into the output. Rotating them would\n  scramble what is retrieved by where it sits; positions should only influence *who attends to whom*.\n\n### Frequencies and what they encode\n| Pair | Behaviour (d = 128) | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pair 0 | one radian per token, wraps every ~6 tokens | very local order |\n| Slowest pair, base 10,000 | about 27° across 4,096 tokens; full rotation every ~54,000 tokens | near-absolute, long-range channel |\n| Slowest pair, base 500,000 | full turn needs about 2.5 million tokens | more pairs stay inside the arc seen in training at long range |\n\n- **Raising the base** to 500,000 leaves pair 0 at one radian per token and slows every other pair,\n  increasingly so toward the slow end.\n- **The spread lets dimensions specialize** in different ranges, the same geometric schedule the\n  sinusoidal encoding used additively.\n- **A mild recency preference.** The RoPE paper shows the inner product between a fixed query and key\n  decays with distance on average.\n\n### Implementation\nTwo equivalent layouts exist:\n\n| Layout | Pairs | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interleaved | `(2i, 2i+1)` | the paper and Meta's reference Llama code (which multiplies complex numbers) |\n| Rotate-half | dimension *i* with *i + d/2*, computed as `q·cos + rotate_half(q)·sin` | the Hugging Face Llama implementation |\n\n- **They differ by a permutation baked into trained `W_Q` and `W_K`**, so converting checkpoints between\n  layouts requires permuting those weights. The official conversion script does exactly that; forgetting\n  it is a classic porting bug.\n- **Tables:** cos/sin `[max_len, d/2]` are precomputed, usually in fp32.\n- **Order:** apply after the Q/K projections and before the score matmul. Rotating the hidden state\n  *before* `W_Q` and `W_K` puts the projections between the two rotations and destroys the\n  `R_mᵀ·R_n = R_(n−m)` cancellation.\n- **Partial rotation.** Some families rotate only a fraction of each head's dimensions\n  (`partial_rotary_factor` or `rotary_pct` in configs) and leave the rest position-free, so check the\n  config rather than assuming the whole head is rotated.\n\n### Interaction with the KV cache (agent transcripts)\n- **Rotate once, at insertion.** Each key is rotated by its own absolute position when cached, and each\n  query by its position at query time. The relative property emerges in the dot product, so nothing in\n  the cache needs re-rotation as the agent appends steps.\n- **Front eviction keeps old angles.** Evicting from the front of a sliding-window cache leaves the\n  survivors at their original absolute angles; streaming schemes re-assign positions inside the cache\n  for this reason.\n- **Edits invalidate.** Changing anything before position *i* invalidates every cached key from *i*\n  onward, which is why agents should append to history rather than rewrite it.\n\n### Context extension\nPast the training length, the low-frequency pairs reach angles the model never saw, attention patterns\nbreak and perplexity rises sharply soon after the limit.\n\n| Method | What it does | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Position interpolation (Chen et al.) | rescales positions by `L_train / L_new` so all angles stay in the trained range, then fine-tunes briefly | squeezes high-frequency pairs, costing resolution between adjacent tokens |\n| NTK-aware scaling | raises the base: stretches low frequencies a lot, high ones little | a heuristic; still benefits from tuning |\n| YaRN | per-frequency treatment (high frequencies untouched, low ones interpolated) plus a temperature on the logits | needs fewer fine-tuning tokens, more knobs |\n| Llama 3.1 | frequency-dependent scaling of this family on an 8k-trained base | reaches 128k context |\n\nThe common lesson: \"max context\" is a property of the trained angles, not of a config field. And a\nwindow the model accepts is not the same as a window your agent uses well; evaluate at your lengths.\n\n### ALiBi, briefly\n- **Mechanism:** adds `−slope_h·(i − j)` to the score of query *i* and key *j ≤ i*, with per-head slopes\n  forming a geometric series.\n- **Profile:** no parameters, decent extrapolation, used by BLOOM and MPT; its recency bias is the trade.","example":"### RoPE in NumPy: the relative property and why extrapolation fails\nA minimal RoPE in NumPy that demonstrates the relative property numerically and shows why extension\nbeyond the trained length needs care.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef rope(x, pos, base=10000.0):\n    \"\"\"Rotate x (even length d) to position pos: pair (2i, 2i+1) turns by pos * theta_i.\"\"\"\n    d = x.shape[-1]\n    theta = base ** (-np.arange(0, d, 2) / d)       # d/2 frequencies from 1 down to ~1/base\n    ang = pos * theta\n    cos, sin = np.cos(ang), np.sin(ang)\n    x1, x2 = x[0::2], x[1::2]\n    out = np.empty_like(x)\n    out[0::2] = x1 * cos - x2 * sin\n    out[1::2] = x1 * sin + x2 * cos\n    return out\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nd = 128\nq, k = rng.standard_normal(d), rng.standard_normal(d)\n\n# Same offset (3 apart), different absolute positions -> identical attention score.\nprint(round(rope(q, 5) @ rope(k, 2), 6), round(rope(q, 1005) @ rope(k, 1002), 6))\n# Different offsets -> different scores although q and k never changed.\nprint(round(rope(q, 5) @ rope(k, 4), 6), round(rope(q, 5) @ rope(k, 0), 6))\n\n# Why extrapolation fails: the slowest pair barely moves inside a 4k window ...\ntheta_min = 10000 ** (-(d - 2) / d)\nprint(f\"slowest pair: {np.degrees(4096 * theta_min):.1f} degrees over 4096 tokens, \"\n      f\"full turn every {2 * np.pi / theta_min:,.0f} tokens\")\n# ... so positions past the trained length put those pairs at angles the model has never seen.\n# Position interpolation: rope(x, pos * L_train / L_new) keeps every pair inside its trained range.\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: a context guard for a self-hosted agent\nThe agent loop checks the transcript length before every model call and compacts when it nears the\nlength your evals passed at, instead of trusting whatever the engine accepts. The model id and limits are\nplaceholders.\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import AutoConfig, AutoTokenizer\n\nMODEL = \"your-org/your-agent-model\"                  # placeholder id\ncfg, tok = AutoConfig.from_pretrained(MODEL), AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(MODEL)\nprint(\"rope_theta\", getattr(cfg, \"rope_theta\", None),\n      \"rope_scaling\", getattr(cfg, \"rope_scaling\", None),\n      \"max_position_embeddings\", cfg.max_position_embeddings)\n\nEVALUATED_LIMIT = 32_768     # longest length your agent evals passed at, not the config value\nRESERVE = 2_048              # room for the next tool call and answer\n\ndef needs_compaction(messages):\n    n = len(tok.apply_chat_template(messages, tokenize=True, return_dict=False))   # list of ids\n    return n > EVALUATED_LIMIT - RESERVE, n\n\n# in the loop: if needs_compaction(history)[0]: replace old tool results with summaries\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: stretching a 4k model to 8k\nAngles for d = 128, base 10,000, trained at 4,096 tokens, served at 8,192 (computed with the formulas\nabove):\n\n| Setting | Slowest pair at position 8,191 | Trained range | Adjacent-token step, fastest pair |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| No scaling | 54.2° | up to 27.1° | 1.00 rad |\n| Position interpolation (factor 2) | 27.1° | up to 27.1° | 0.50 rad |\n\n- **No scaling** pushes slow pairs to angles never seen in training, so attention breaks right after\n  4k; an agent at step 15 starts looping or emitting broken JSON.\n- **Interpolation** keeps every angle in range but halves local resolution, which the short fine-tune\n  recovers.\n\n### Correct position ids for a left-padded batch\nPositions must come from the attention mask, not from the padded index, or shorter prompts get shifted\nangles. This mirrors what Hugging Face generation does internally.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\nattention_mask = torch.tensor([[0, 0, 1, 1, 1],      # short prompt, left-padded\n                               [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]])     # longest prompt\nposition_ids = attention_mask.long().cumsum(-1) - 1  # real tokens count from 0\nposition_ids.masked_fill_(attention_mask == 0, 1)    # pad slots get a dummy id; they are masked anyway\nprint(position_ids)\n# tensor([[1, 1, 0, 1, 2],\n#         [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]])\n# During decode, the next position is the cache length per sequence, not the step counter.\n```","patterns":["**Cap agent context at the evaluated length.** Set the engine's maximum model length and the agent's compaction threshold from the length your agent evals passed at, not from the config. Alert when context-length p95 approaches that limit.","**Read the angles before trusting max context.** Check `rope_theta`, `rope_scaling` and `max_position_embeddings` in `config.json` and the model card's trained length. Treat any context above the trained or officially extended length as unsupported until evaluated.","**Enable vendor-documented scaling only.** When a model family documents YaRN for longer inputs, add the `rope_scaling` block it specifies (type, factor and original length) to the config or the engine's override. Monitor short-context eval scores too, since static scaling can cost quality on short inputs.","**Evaluate extension at the target length with agent tasks.** Run needle-in-a-haystack, real long-document tasks and long multi-step agent runs at the lengths you will serve, not just perplexity. Track tool-call validity and loop rate by context bucket.","**Test ported checkpoints for layout bugs.** After converting weights between interleaved and rotate-half code, compare perplexity on known text with the source implementation; a value in the hundreds means the Q/K permutation was skipped. Run it in CI for every conversion.","**Derive position ids from mask and cache length.** For left-padded batches use the cumulative-sum pattern above, and keep cos/sin tables in fp32. Test that one prompt gives identical output alone and in a batch."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent transcripts keep growing. When a self-hosted model runs past the\n  length its RoPE angles were trained or extended for, it does not error; it starts repeating itself or\n  emitting malformed tool calls.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `rope_theta`, `rope_scaling` and `max_position_embeddings` in\n  `config.json`, the engine's maximum model length, and the context window advertised by hosted APIs\n  (already extended by the vendor).\n- **What you control.** The context cap per agent route, when to compact or retrieve instead of growing\n  the transcript, and whether you enable only vendor-documented scaling.\n- **What you can skip.** The rotation algebra and checkpoint-porting details, unless you convert weights\n  or write kernels. Keep \"max context is a property of the trained angles, not a config field\".","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Setting context caps for long-running agents.** The trained or extended length, confirmed by evals,\n  sets the compaction threshold and the engine's max length.\n- **Choosing a self-hosted model for long transcripts.** `rope_theta` and `rope_scaling` reveal whether a\n  model was built for 8k or 128k before you benchmark it.\n- **Debugging late-step agent failures.** Loops and broken tool calls after a length threshold point to\n  positions past the trained range.\n- **Deciding extend versus retrieve.** Extension needs a fine-tune and evals; retrieval keeps inputs in\n  the well-trained range.","mini_project":"**Find your agent model's reliable context length (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** measure where a self-hosted model's agent behaviour degrades as the transcript grows, and set\n  a guard there.\n- **Steps:** read `rope_theta`, `rope_scaling` and `max_position_embeddings`; build synthetic agent\n  transcripts at 25%, 50%, 100% and 125% of that length with a fact planted early; ask the model to make a\n  tool call that needs the fact; record tool-call validity and fact recall per length.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of validity and recall by length, a chosen evaluated limit, and the\n  context guard from the example wired into a small agent loop."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"`max_position_embeddings` is how long a transcript the model handles.\"** Reliable length is a property of the angles the model was trained or extended on, and a window the model accepts is not one your agent uses well; set limits from evals at your lengths.","**\"Going past the limit will raise an error.\"** Past the trained angles nothing throws (only an engine's own length cap rejects a request): slow pairs reach unseen angles and the agent quietly starts looping, forgetting instructions or emitting malformed tool calls.","**\"RoPE adds a position vector to the embeddings.\"** It rotates queries and keys (never values) after the projections, so `qₘ·kₙ` depends only on the offset `n − m` and the contents.","**\"Setting `rope_scaling` extends context for free.\"** Interpolation and YaRN assume a fine-tune or a vendor-extended checkpoint; adding the block alone gives partial recovery at long context and can cost short-context quality.","**\"Cached keys must be re-rotated as the transcript grows.\"** Each key is rotated once by its own position; what invalidates the cache is editing anything earlier in the history, so append rather than rewrite."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    H[\"Hidden state at position m\"] --> PQ[\"Q projection\"]\n    H --> PK[\"K projection\"]\n    H --> PV[\"V projection\"]\n    PQ -->|\"rotate by m times theta\"| RQ[\"Rotated query\"]\n    PK -->|\"rotate by m times theta\"| RK[\"Rotated key\"]\n    RK --> C[(\"KV cache: keys stored rotated\")]\n    PV -->|\"no rotation\"| C\n    RQ --> S[\"Score depends on n minus m\"]\n    C --> S\n    S --> A[\"Softmax and weighted sum of V\"]","caption":"Where the rotation sits in one attention layer: after the projections, on queries and keys only, with\nkeys cached already rotated by their own positions."},"interview":[{"q":"Why does RoPE rotate queries and keys but not values?","a":"- **Position should decide who attends to whom**, which is the q·k score; rotating both makes that score\n  depend only on the offset n − m.\n- **Values carry content** that is summed into the output; rotating them would make the retrieved content\n  depend on where it sat, which is not what attention should return."},{"q":"Your self-hosted agent works for short tasks but loops or emits broken tool calls on long ones. What do you check?","a":"- **Context length versus trained angles.** Compare transcript length at the failing step with the model's\n  trained or officially extended length and any `rope_scaling` in the config.\n- **Then position handling:** position ids from mask and cache length, not a step counter.\n- **Fix** with a context guard at the evaluated limit, compaction of old tool results, or a model extended\n  and evaluated for that length."},{"q":"A customer asks to raise their agent's max tokens from 8k to 64k by editing the config. What do you say?","a":"- **The trained angles bound reliable behaviour**, not the config field; past the trained length slow\n  pairs hit unseen angles and quality falls off a cliff.\n- **Options:** use a model trained or officially extended to that length, apply interpolation or YaRN with\n  the fine-tune it assumes and evaluate at 64k, or use retrieval to keep inputs short."},{"q":"A ported Llama checkpoint loads cleanly but has perplexity in the hundreds. What do you suspect?","a":"- **A rotation-layout mismatch.** Interleaved and rotate-half layouts differ by a permutation baked into\n  `W_Q` and `W_K`; converting without permuting those weights breaks every score.\n- **Also check** `rope_theta`, partial rotary settings and whether RoPE is applied after the projections,\n  then compare perplexity with the source implementation on the same text."},{"q":"When would you prefer ALiBi over RoPE?","a":"- **When you need length extrapolation with no fine-tuning**, since ALiBi's fixed distance penalty\n  degrades more gracefully past the trained length.\n- **Not when long-range retrieval matters**, because its built-in recency bias can suppress attention to\n  distant but relevant tokens."},{"q":"What does raising rope_theta from 10,000 to 500,000 change, in numbers?","a":"- **Pair 0 is unchanged** at one radian per token; every slower pair turns more slowly.\n- **The slowest pair (d = 128)** goes from a full turn every ~54,000 tokens to about 2.5 million, so more\n  pairs stay inside angles seen in training at long range, at some cost in mid-range offset resolution."}],"tradeoffs":["**RoPE vs ALiBi.** RoPE gives relative attention with zero parameters and a cache that never needs re-rotation, but extrapolates poorly past the trained length. If you need length generalization with no fine-tuning, ALiBi's fixed penalty behaves better out of the box.","**Extend context vs compact or retrieve.** RoPE scaling lets an agent keep more raw history, but needs a fine-tune or a vendor-extended model and evals at length. Compaction and retrieval keep the transcript in the well-trained range at the risk of dropping a detail.","**Learned absolute tables vs growth.** Simple and strong at trained lengths, with a hard cap on context. Reasonable for fixed-length encoders; wrong for anything that must grow.","**Larger base vs mid-range resolution.** A larger base (500,000 versus 10,000) improves long-context behaviour by slowing the middle and low frequencies, at the cost of fewer pairs that distinguish offsets in the hundreds-to-thousands range. Whether that costs short-context quality depends on model and data, and vendors tune it empirically.","**Interpolation vs YaRN.** Position interpolation is simple and robust after a short fine-tune but blurs local resolution; YaRN preserves high frequencies and needs fewer tokens but has more knobs to get wrong."],"pitfalls":["**Letting an agent run past the trained length.** Symptom: late-step repetition loops, malformed tool calls or forgotten instructions, with no exception raised. Fix: a context guard at the evaluated limit that compacts or hands off to retrieval.","**Running past `max_position_embeddings` with no scaling.** Symptom: output that degenerates into repetition or nonsense right after the trained length, with no exception raised. Fix: cap the engine's max length at the trained or officially extended length.","**Mixing the interleaved and rotate-half layouts** when loading ported weights. Symptom: correct shapes, no errors, perplexity in the hundreds. Fix: permute the `W_Q` and `W_K` rows as the official conversion script does, then re-check perplexity against the source.","**Feeding wrong position ids:** restarting at 0 on each decode step, or counting pad tokens in a left-padded batch. Symptom: output fine for the longest prompt but degraded for shorter ones, or quality that drops right after the first generated token. Positions must come from the attention mask and the cache length.","**Computing the cos/sin tables in bf16 or fp16 for large positions.** Symptom: slowly degrading quality at high positions caused by rounding of large angles. Keep the frequency computation in fp32.","**Setting `rope_scaling` in a serving config without the fine-tuning the method assumes.** Symptom: degraded short-context quality and only partial recovery at long context. Fix: use only the scaling the vendor documents for that checkpoint, or fine-tune for it, and evaluate at short and long lengths."],"when":"You choose a positional scheme indirectly by choosing a model, but you meet it directly in these places:\n- **Reading a config:** `rope_theta` and `rope_scaling` tell you what context the model really supports.\n- **Setting agent context policy:** the compaction threshold and engine max length come from evaluated\n  lengths, not advertised ones.\n- **Context-extension projects:** interpolation or YaRN plus a fine-tune, evaluated at the target length\n  with needle-in-haystack and real tasks, not just perplexity.\n- **Debugging ported checkpoints or custom kernels**, where the rotation layout matters.\n- **Measure** quality at the lengths you will actually serve, because the loss past the trained length is\n  a cliff, not a slope.\n- **Interviews:** derive the relative property from rotation composition, explain why only queries and\n  keys are rotated, and describe why extrapolation fails and how interpolation fixes it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets `rope_scaling` correctly when serving extended context, verifies the rotation layout when converting weights, and tests at target lengths before shipping.","architect":"Decides whether to buy long context through RoPE scaling plus fine-tuning or through retrieval, knowing that the trained length, not the config, bounds reliable behaviour.","fde":"Answers a customer's \"can we just raise max tokens to 64k?\" by explaining trained angles versus config fields and proposing an evaluated extension or a retrieval design."},"check":{"q":"Explain how RoPE encodes absolute position yet produces relative attention, and why a model trained\nat 4k tokens degrades sharply at 8k if nothing is changed.","a":"Each query and key is rotated, pair of dimensions by pair, through an angle equal to its absolute\nposition times a per-pair frequency. Because rotations compose, the dot product between a query rotated\nby *m* and a key rotated by *n* equals the dot product between the original query and the key rotated by\n*n − m*; the absolute positions cancel and only the offset and contents remain. Values are not rotated so\nretrieved content is unaffected. During training at 4k, the low-frequency pairs only ever sweep a small\narc, so the model's weights are tuned to scores produced within that arc. At positions 4k–8k those pairs\nenter angles never seen, the scores they contribute are effectively random, attention patterns break and\nperplexity climbs. Position interpolation rescales positions so 8k maps into\nthe trained arc, then a short fine-tune restores quality."},"quiz":[{"q":"In RoPE, which tensors are rotated?","options":["Queries, keys and values","Queries and keys only","The token embeddings before the first layer","The attention probabilities after the softmax"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rotating queries and keys makes their dot product depend on relative position. Values are left alone because they carry content that is summed into the output, and positions should affect only who attends to whom."},{"q":"After RoPE, the attention score between a query at position m and a key at position n depends on","options":["m and n separately, as with learned absolute embeddings","The offset n − m and the content of the two vectors","Only the content, with no positional influence","The absolute position of the key alone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rotation matrices compose, so R_mᵀ·R_n = R_(n−m) and the absolute positions cancel inside the dot product. Learned absolute tables, by contrast, give the model separate vectors for m and n."},{"q":"Position interpolation extends a RoPE model's context by","options":["Adding new learned position vectors for the extra positions","Increasing the head dimension so more frequencies are available","Scaling positions down so all rotation angles stay within the range seen in training, then fine-tuning briefly","Removing the positional signal for tokens beyond the trained length"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Beyond the trained length the slow frequencies reach unseen angles. Compressing positions keeps every pair inside its trained arc, at the cost of finer-grained local resolution, which a short fine-tune largely recovers."}]},{"key":"model-internals/tokenization","sources":["Sennrich et al., 2016 — Neural Machine Translation of Rare Words with Subword Units","Kudo and Richardson, 2018 — SentencePiece: A simple and language independent subword tokenizer and detokenizer for Neural Text Processing","Kudo, 2018 — Subword Regularization: Improving Neural Network Translation Models with Multiple Subword Candidates","Radford et al., 2019 — Language Models are Unsupervised Multitask Learners"],"why":"- **A model needs a fixed, finite symbol set:** an embedding row for each and a softmax over all of them\n  at every step. Three ways to define the symbols were tried, and two fail.\n- **Characters or bytes are too long.** A tiny vocabulary (256 for bytes) and nothing is ever unknown, but\n  English text becomes roughly four times longer than with a subword tokenizer. That multiplies\n  attention cost and forces the model to learn spelling before meaning; each token carries very little\n  information.\n- **Whole words are unbounded.** Short, meaningful sequences, but every typo, URL, identifier, number and\n  new coinage is a new word, the embedding table explodes, and anything unseen at inference becomes an\n  `<unk>` token. Morphologically rich languages such as Finnish or Turkish break it even on ordinary text.\n- **Subwords are the compromise.** Frequent words stay whole, rare words split into frequent pieces, and a\n  byte or character fallback guarantees any input can be encoded. Sennrich et al. adapted Gage's 1994\n  byte-pair compression algorithm to neural translation for exactly the rare-word problem; byte-level BPE\n  in GPT-2 removed the last unknown-token case.\n- **What it buys.** A bounded vocabulary (tens of thousands to a few hundred thousand entries), short\n  sequences, and fixed, affordable embedding and output layers. Everything about cost and context in\n  practice is downstream of this choice.","how":"### BPE training\n1. **Pre-tokenize.** Split the text into chunks with a regular expression, typically at spaces,\n   punctuation and digit runs, so merges never cross a word boundary. GPT-2's pattern attaches the\n   leading space to the word, which is why `\" hello\"` and `\"hello\"` are different tokens; GPT-4-era\n   tokenizers split digit runs into groups of at most three.\n2. **Start from a base vocabulary.** Every character in the corpus, or all 256 byte values for byte-level\n   BPE, so any string can be represented from the start.\n3. **Count adjacent pairs** of symbols across the corpus, weighted by frequency, and **merge the most\n   frequent pair** into a new symbol. Record the merge as a rule.\n4. **Repeat** until the vocabulary reaches the target size.\n\n| Tokenizer | Vocabulary size |\n|---|---|\n| GPT-2 | 50,257 |\n| Llama 2 | 32,000 |\n| GPT-4's `cl100k_base` | roughly 100k |\n| Llama 3 | 128,256 |\n\n**Why greedy frequency works:** it is a compression objective. Each merge removes the most occurrences of\na pair, so the corpus shrinks fastest in token count. It is cheap, deterministic and good enough that\nnothing has displaced it at scale.\n\n### BPE encoding\n1. Pre-tokenize the new string and start each chunk as bytes or characters.\n2. Repeatedly apply the merge with the lowest rank (earliest learned) that matches, until none applies.\n3. Map the resulting symbols to ids.\n4. Decoding is concatenation; with byte-level BPE the bytes are then decoded as UTF-8.\n\n### SentencePiece\nSentencePiece (Kudo and Richardson) is a library and model format rather than a single algorithm. It\ndiffers from GPT-style BPE in how it sees text:\n- **Raw Unicode stream, whitespace as a symbol.** Whitespace is a normal character (the `▁` symbol,\n  U+2581), so no language-specific pre-tokenizer is needed, and it works unchanged for Chinese, Japanese\n  or Thai, which have no spaces.\n- **Lossless with respect to the *normalized* text.** Join the pieces and replace `▁` with a space.\n- **Normalization.** NFKC-based by default, which folds full-width characters and ligatures, so\n  `decode(encode(x))` can differ from `x`.\n- **`byte_fallback`** lets unknown characters decompose into bytes.\n- **One self-contained model file**, so there is no separate regex to keep in sync. It trains either BPE\n  or the unigram model.\n- **Who uses it.** Llama 1 and 2, T5 and Mistral used SentencePiece models; Llama 3 moved to a\n  tiktoken-style byte-level BPE with a larger vocabulary for better multilingual efficiency.\n\n### The unigram model\nUnigram tokenization (Kudo, 2018), SentencePiece's default, works in the opposite direction from BPE:\n1. Start from a large candidate vocabulary of frequent substrings and assign each a probability.\n2. Tokenize a string as its most probable segmentation, assuming tokens are independent (found with the\n   Viterbi algorithm).\n3. Iteratively remove the tokens whose removal hurts corpus likelihood least until the vocabulary reaches\n   its target size.\n\n- **Probabilistic, so it can sample.** Alternative segmentations during training act as data augmentation\n  (subword regularization), and its pieces tend to align more with morphology.\n- **WordPiece (BERT) is a BPE variant.** It picks merges by likelihood gain rather than raw count (a pair\n  scores high when it occurs together more often than its parts' frequencies predict), marks\n  word-internal pieces with `##`, and encodes by greedy longest-match from the left rather than by\n  replaying merges.\n\n### Consequences you will meet in agents\n- **Cost and context are counted in tokens.** English prose runs around four characters or three\n  quarters of a word per token with OpenAI-style tokenizers; code, numbers and non-Latin scripts cost\n  more per character, often several times more (an order of magnitude figure that varies by tokenizer\n  and language). Vocabularies were enlarged in part to fix this.\n- **Structured data is token-dense.** JSON keys, quotes, braces, UUIDs and hashes split into many short\n  pieces, so tool schemas and raw API responses cost more per character than prose.\n- **Numbers.** Llama 2 splits every digit into its own token; GPT-4-era tokenizers group up to three.\n  Arithmetic behaviour and number formatting sensitivity follow from this.\n- **Spaces and case.** A prompt ending in a trailing space pushes the next token off the distribution\n  the model saw, because the space would normally be attached to the following word.\n- **Character-level tasks fail.** Counting letters, reversing strings or spelling rules are hard because\n  the model never sees characters, only chunks; give the agent a tool for them.\n- **Glitch tokens.** Tokens that exist in the vocabulary but barely appeared in training produce erratic\n  behaviour; the \"SolidGoldMagikarp\" family in GPT-2/3 vocabularies is the known example.\n- **The tokenizer is part of the model.** Fine-tuning must use the same tokenizer, special tokens and\n  chat template; adding tokens means resizing and initializing embeddings; constrained decoding and\n  grammars (JSON mode, tool-call schemas) operate on token boundaries, not characters.","example":"### A BPE trainer and encoder in about thirty lines\nA complete, runnable BPE trainer and encoder in about thirty lines. The corpus is tiny so you can trace\nthe merges by hand; the mechanism is identical to what produced GPT-2's 50k merges.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ncorpus = \"low low low lower lower lowest newer newer newest wider widest\".split()\nwords = Counter(tuple(w) + (\"</w>\",) for w in corpus)      # symbols per word, with end-of-word marker\n\ndef pair_stats(words):\n    stats = Counter()\n    for sym, freq in words.items():\n        for a, b in zip(sym, sym[1:]):\n            stats[(a, b)] += freq\n    return stats\n\ndef apply_merge(seq, pair):\n    out, i = [], 0\n    while i < len(seq):\n        if i < len(seq) - 1 and (seq[i], seq[i + 1]) == pair:\n            out.append(pair[0] + pair[1]); i += 2\n        else:\n            out.append(seq[i]); i += 1\n    return out\n\nmerges = []\nfor step in range(8):                                        # vocab = base symbols + 8 merges\n    stats = pair_stats(words)\n    best = max(stats, key=stats.get)                         # most frequent adjacent pair\n    merges.append(best)\n    words = Counter({tuple(apply_merge(list(s), best)): f for s, f in words.items()})\n    print(step, best, stats[best])\n# 0 ('l','o') 6 | 1 ('lo','w') 6 | 2 ('e','r') 5 | 3 ('er','</w>') 5 | 4 ('low','</w>') 3 | ...\n\ndef encode(word):                                            # apply merges in learned order\n    seq = list(word) + [\"</w>\"]\n    for pair in merges:\n        seq = apply_merge(seq, pair)\n    return seq\n\nprint(encode(\"lowest\"), encode(\"slowest\"))\n# ['low', 'est</w>'] ['s', 'low', 'est</w>']  <- unseen \"slowest\" still encodes, no <unk>\n```\n\nFor real counting use the model's own tokenizer: `tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\").encode(text)`\nfor OpenAI models, `AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)(text).input_ids` for open models.\n\n### Agent scenario: what the tool list costs per run\nTool schemas are re-sent on every step. This estimates their token cost with `tiktoken` and projects it\nover a run. Providers wrap schemas in their own formatting, so treat the count as an estimate and check\nit against the usage fields.\n\n```python\nimport json, tiktoken\n\nenc = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\")       # use the tokenizer of the model you actually call\ntools = [\n    {\"name\": \"search_orders\", \"description\": \"Find a customer's orders by email or order id.\",\n     \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\n         \"email\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}}},\n    {\"name\": \"refund_order\", \"description\": \"Refund an order. Requires order_id and reason.\",\n     \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\n         \"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"reason\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n         \"required\": [\"order_id\", \"reason\"]}},\n]\n\nschema_tokens = sum(len(enc.encode(json.dumps(t))) for t in tools)\nuuid_tokens = len(enc.encode(\"3f2b8c1e-9a4d-4e7b-8f21-6c0d5a9e7b13\"))\nsteps, n_tools = 12, 40                           # a 12-step run with 40 similar tools\nprint(f\"2 tools: {schema_tokens} tokens; one UUID: {uuid_tokens} tokens\")\nprint(f\"~{schema_tokens / 2 * n_tools * steps:,.0f} schema tokens per run before any caching\")\n# 2 tools: 119 tokens; one UUID: 34 tokens\n# ~28,560 schema tokens per run before any caching\n```\n\n- **Reading the output:** a UUID costs 34 tokens, nearly one per character instead of the ~9 that 36\n  characters of prose would cost, and\n  40 schemas re-sent for 12 steps can dominate input cost.\n- **Levers:** retrieve only relevant tools per step, shorten descriptions, keep the tool block stable for\n  prefix caching, and replace raw IDs in results with short aliases mapped back in code.\n\n### Streaming without replacement characters\nByte-level tokens can end mid-character, so decoding each token on its own leaks `U+FFFD` into streamed\ntext, including streamed agent answers in a chat UI. An incremental UTF-8 decoder buffers the incomplete\nbytes; this runs with the standard library.\n\n```python\nimport codecs\n\n# Byte-level tokens can end in the middle of a UTF-8 character: \"café 日本\" split as a stream might be.\npieces = [b\"caf\", b\"\\xc3\", b\"\\xa9 \\xe6\\x97\", b\"\\xa5\\xe6\\x9c\\xac\"]\n\nnaive = \"\".join(p.decode(\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\") for p in pieces)\nprint(chr(0xFFFD) in naive)                  # True: U+FFFD replacement characters leak into the stream\n\ndec = codecs.getincrementaldecoder(\"utf-8\")()    # buffers incomplete bytes between chunks\nstreamed = \"\".join(dec.decode(p) for p in pieces) + dec.decode(b\"\", final=True)\nprint(streamed == \"café 日本\", streamed)          # True café 日本\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a chunker tuned on English truncates other content\nA RAG pipeline feeding an agent sized chunks at 2,048 characters, assuming about four characters per\ntoken, to stay under a 512-token embedding limit. The per-language ratios below are illustrative, not\nmeasured.\n\n| Content | Characters per token (illustrative) | Tokens in a 2,048-character chunk | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| English prose | ~4 | ~512 | fits |\n| Source code | ~3 | ~680 | last quarter silently truncated |\n| A script the tokenizer handles poorly | ~1.5 | ~1,365 | most of the chunk never embedded |\n\nThe fix: chunk by counting tokens with the embedding model's own tokenizer, then measure retrieval\nrecall per language.","patterns":["**Keep a per-step token budget.** Before every model call, count the rendered prompt (system prompt, tool schemas, transcript) with the serving model's tokenizer and compact or truncate tool results when it nears the limit. Log estimated versus reported usage per step and alert on drift.","**Count tokens with the serving model's tokenizer.** Use `tiktoken` for OpenAI models and the model's `AutoTokenizer` for open models, applied to the fully rendered chat template, before every call. Where the provider offers a token-counting endpoint, use it for tool-heavy prompts.","**Trim tool schemas and results.** Short descriptions, only the fields the model needs, and results projected to the relevant keys instead of raw API JSON. Track input tokens per step and task success before and after.","**Measure tokens per character per language before choosing a model.** Run a sample of the real corpus through each candidate tokenizer and put the ratio into the cost model alongside eval scores. Monitor cost per conversation by language after launch.","**Chunk by tokens, not characters.** Split documents using the embedding model's tokenizer and its maximum input length. Monitor the share of chunks at or over the limit.","**Render prompts with the model's chat template.** Use `tokenizer.apply_chat_template` so special tokens match training, and keep the same template for fine-tuning on agent traces and serving. Monitor stop-token rates and runaway generations; stream by decoding the accumulated text and alert on replacement characters."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent pays for its system prompt and every tool schema on every\n  step, plus every tool result it appends. Token counts, not characters or turns, decide when the\n  context fills, what a run costs and when to compact.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `tiktoken` or the model's `AutoTokenizer` for counting, usage\n  fields in API responses, `apply_chat_template` for rendering, chunk sizing in the RAG step, and\n  structured-output or tool-call decoding, which works on token boundaries.\n- **What you control.** Schema and result verbosity, chunk sizes, the counting tokenizer, stop tokens and\n  chat template, and which work (letter counts, exact string edits) goes to code instead of the model.\n- **What you can skip.** Training your own tokenizer, unless you also train a model from scratch. Keep the\n  merge loop, the tokens-per-character ratios and the failure modes.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Per-step context budgets.** Agents count the rendered prompt in tokens before each call and compact\n  tool results when they near the limit.\n- **Tool design and cost control.** Schema length, result projection and ID aliases are tuned in tokens\n  because they are paid on every step.\n- **Multilingual agents.** Tokens per character by language sets cost per conversation and decides\n  between candidate models.\n- **RAG chunking.** Chunks are sized with the embedding model's tokenizer so code and non-English\n  documents are not silently truncated.","mini_project":"**Build a token ledger for an agent run (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show where an agent's input tokens go across a run and cut the total by at least a third.\n- **Steps:** build a small agent with 10 tools and a fake API that returns verbose JSON; log, per step,\n  tokens in the system prompt, tool schemas, transcript and newest tool result using the model's\n  tokenizer; compare with the API usage fields; then shorten schemas, project results to needed keys and\n  alias long IDs, and rerun.\n- **Done when:** you have a per-step stacked table before and after, estimated versus reported tokens\n  within a few percent, and the same task success rate on a 10-task eval."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"A token is about four characters, so characters divided by four is a fine budget.\"** That holds for English prose only; JSON, IDs, code and many non-Latin scripts cost far more per character, and a 36-character UUID can take 34 tokens.","**\"Any tokenizer gives close enough counts.\"** Vocabularies differ (32,000 for Llama 2, 128,256 for Llama 3) and providers wrap tool schemas in their own formatting; count with the serving model's tokenizer and reconcile against usage fields.","**\"Tool schemas are a one-time cost.\"** They are re-sent on every step, so 40 schemas over a 12-step run can dominate input tokens unless you retrieve tools per step and keep the block stable for prefix caching.","**\"The model reads characters, so counting letters or exact string edits are easy.\"** It sees subword chunks, never characters; route character-level work to a tool.","**\"Decoding each streamed token as it arrives is safe.\"** Byte-level tokens can end mid-character, which leaks `U+FFFD` into the stream; use an incremental UTF-8 decoder.","**\"`decode(encode(x))` always returns `x`.\"** SentencePiece normalizes (NFKC by default), so full-width characters and ligatures can come back changed; compare normalized text when round-tripping."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Training corpus\"] --> PT[\"Pre-tokenize with regex\"]\n    PT --> B[\"Base vocab: 256 bytes\"]\n    B --> CNT[\"Count adjacent pairs\"]\n    CNT --> M[\"Merge most frequent pair\"]\n    M -->|\"until target vocab size\"| CNT\n    M --> R[(\"Ordered merge rules\")]\n    T[\"New text\"] --> E[\"Apply merges by rank\"]\n    R --> E\n    E --> IDS[\"Token ids to the model\"]\n    IDS -->|\"concatenate, decode UTF-8\"| D[\"Text back out\"]","caption":"The training loop learns an ordered merge list once; encoding replays those merges on new text, and\ndecoding is concatenation back to bytes and UTF-8. Every prompt, schema and tool result your agent sends\ngoes through the lower half."},"interview":[{"q":"Walk me through how BPE builds its vocabulary and encodes a word it never saw.","a":"- **Training:** pre-tokenize, start from 256 bytes or the corpus characters, then repeatedly merge the\n  most frequent adjacent pair into a new symbol, recording each merge, until the target size.\n- **Encoding:** replay the merges in learned order on the new word's bytes; an unseen word such as\n  \"slowest\" still splits into known pieces, so there is no `<unk>`."},{"q":"Your agent's input cost per run doubled after adding tools, though transcripts are the same length. Why, and what do you do?","a":"- **Schemas are re-sent every step.** Each tool's JSON schema is token-dense, and 40 tools across 12 steps\n  multiplies that cost by every step.\n- **Fix:** retrieve only relevant tools per step, shorten descriptions, keep the tool block byte-stable so\n  prefix caching applies, and confirm with per-step usage fields."},{"q":"Your agent sometimes calls tools with an order id that is one character off. What is going on?","a":"- **Long IDs split into many short tokens**, so copying them exactly is error-prone for the model.\n- **Fix:** give each result a short alias in the transcript and map aliases back to real IDs in code, and\n  validate IDs before executing the tool."},{"q":"Prompts that \"fit\" by your estimate are overflowing the context in production. What do you check?","a":"- **Which tokenizer did the estimate use?** Counting with `cl100k_base` for a Llama model, or counting raw\n  text instead of the rendered chat template, undercounts.\n- **Then content mix:** code and non-English text cost more tokens per character, so a characters-based\n  heuristic breaks on exactly those requests."},{"q":"Streamed responses show replacement characters that vanish in the final text. Why?","a":"- **Byte-level tokens can split a multi-byte UTF-8 character**, so decoding each token on its own yields\n  `U+FFFD` for the incomplete bytes.\n- **Fix:** buffer incomplete bytes with an incremental decoder, or decode the accumulated ids and emit\n  only the new suffix."}],"tradeoffs":["**Larger vocabulary vs bigger layers.** Fewer tokens per text means lower cost, longer effective context and faster generation, but a bigger embedding and output layer (128k × 4096 in fp16 is about 1 GB per untied matrix) and rarer tokens that are undertrained. It pays off when the workload is multilingual or code-heavy.","**Rich tool schemas vs per-step cost.** Detailed descriptions and examples improve tool choice but are paid on every step of every run. Keep detail where evals show it changes decisions; cut the rest.","**Raw tool output vs projected output.** Raw JSON keeps every field the agent might need but is token-dense and dilutes attention; projecting to a few fields is cheaper but can hide something a later step needed. Project, and let the agent re-fetch details by id.","**Byte-level BPE vs character-level SentencePiece.** Byte-level BPE encodes anything and never emits unknown tokens, but scripts whose merges were underrepresented fall apart into byte fragments. Character-level SentencePiece with byte fallback handles CJK more gracefully at the cost of normalization decisions.","**Greedy BPE vs probabilistic unigram.** BPE is greedy and deterministic; unigram supports subword regularization and tends toward morphological pieces, which helps most when training a tokenizer and model from scratch on limited data.","**Aggressive pre-tokenization vs token counts.** Splitting digits and isolating punctuation makes behaviour on numbers and code more consistent but raises token counts on numeric or structured text."],"pitfalls":["**Counting tokens with the wrong tokenizer**, such as `cl100k_base` for a Llama model. Symptom: context-overflow errors or silently truncated prompts in production although the estimate said they fit. Fix: count with the serving model's own tokenizer on the rendered template.","**Ignoring the tool block in the budget.** Symptom: the agent overflows or truncates earlier than the transcript length suggests. Fix: count system prompt plus all schemas plus transcript as one rendered prompt.","**Making the model copy long IDs or hashes.** Symptom: tool calls with an off-by-one-character order id or UUID. Fix: give results short aliases and map them back to real IDs in code.","**Ending a prompt with a trailing space or stitching completions at a non-token boundary.** Symptom: a first generated token that starts mid-word, doubles a space or is otherwise odd. Fix: strip trailing whitespace and let the model emit the space with the next word.","**Chunking documents by a fixed characters-per-token ratio.** Symptom: code or non-English chunks that exceed the embedding model's limit and get truncated without error. Fix: chunk by token count with the embedding model's tokenizer.","**Resizing the vocabulary for new special tokens without initializing the new rows sensibly.** Symptom: a loss spike in the first steps of fine-tuning. Fix: initialize new rows from the mean of existing embeddings rather than at random.","**Asking the model for character-level work** (letter counts, exact string reversal, regex-like validation). Symptom: confident, inconsistent answers that no prompt reliably fixes. Fix: hand that work to code or a tool call.","**Streaming output by decoding each token id on its own.** A multi-byte UTF-8 character (accented letters, CJK, emoji) can span two byte-level tokens, so the symptom is `U+FFFD` replacement characters in streamed text that disappear when the full response is decoded at once. Buffer incomplete bytes.","**Mismatched chat-template special tokens between training and serving.** Symptom: a fine-tuned model that ignores its system prompt or never emits a stop token. Fix: render both with the same `apply_chat_template` and check the stop-token ids in the serving config."],"when":"You do not choose a tokenizer per request, but it shapes several decisions you do make.\n- **Budgeting an agent:** count schemas, prompts and results with the model's own tokenizer, never a\n  character heuristic, and set compaction thresholds in tokens.\n- **Designing tools:** prefer short schemas, projected results and short aliases for IDs, because every\n  token is paid on every step.\n- **Selecting a model for a market:** measure tokens per character on a sample of your real corpus across\n  candidate tokenizers; the ratio can differ by multiples between languages and drives cost and latency\n  directly.\n- **Chunking:** use the embedding model's tokenizer and its input limit.\n- **Training a new tokenizer:** only when also training a model from scratch on a domain whose symbols\n  existing vocabularies handle badly, such as DNA, logs or a new programming language.\n- **Interviews:** walk through the merge loop, contrast byte-level BPE with SentencePiece and unigram, and\n  explain from tokenization why a model miscounts letters or why Hindi costs more than English.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Counts tokens with the correct tokenizer before every call, handles chat-template special tokens exactly, and tokenizes before chunking rather than after.","architect":"Builds the cost model per language and content type from measured tokens per character, and weighs tokenizer efficiency alongside benchmark scores when choosing a model.","fde":"Explains to a customer in Japan or India why bills and latency per character exceed the English pilot, and why a chunker tuned on English truncates their documents."},"check":{"q":"A customer's Hindi support assistant costs about three times more per conversation than the\nEnglish pilot on the same model, with the same number of turns. Explain the mechanism and what you would\ndo about it.","a":"The model's tokenizer learned its merges from a corpus dominated by English, so English words map\nto one or two tokens while Devanagari text falls apart into many short pieces or, with byte-level BPE,\ninto byte fragments; the same message costs several times more tokens, and cost, latency and context\nconsumption all scale with tokens. I would first measure tokens per character on a sample of real Hindi\nconversations across the candidate tokenizers to quantify the ratio. Then I would evaluate a model whose\nvocabulary was built with multilingual data, such as one with a larger vocabulary, since fewer tokens\nper message cuts cost and leaves more room in the context window; check that quality holds on their\ntasks; and revisit the chunking and context budgets, which were tuned on English token counts and are\nprobably truncating Hindi content."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does BPE do at each step of training?","options":["Removes the least likely token from a large initial vocabulary","Merges the most frequent adjacent pair of symbols in the pre-tokenized corpus into a new symbol","Splits the longest word in the corpus into two halves","Assigns a probability to every substring and keeps the top-k"],"answer":1,"explanation":"BPE grows a vocabulary bottom-up by greedy frequency merges. Pruning a large vocabulary by likelihood is the unigram model, which SentencePiece also implements and which runs in the opposite direction."},{"q":"What distinguishes SentencePiece from a GPT-style BPE tokenizer?","options":["It can only produce character-level tokens","It requires a language-specific word splitter before training","It treats raw text, including whitespace, as a stream of symbols and trains BPE or unigram models without a pre-tokenizer","It cannot be used for languages that use spaces"],"answer":2,"explanation":"SentencePiece encodes whitespace as the ▁ symbol and segments the raw Unicode stream directly, which is why it works for Japanese or Thai without a word splitter and decodes losslessly."},{"q":"Why do LLMs make mistakes counting the letters in a word?","options":["Because the softmax cannot represent small integers","Because attention cannot look at more than one token at a time","Because the model sees subword tokens, not characters, so the letters inside a token are not individually visible","Because the tokenizer removes repeated letters"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A word like \"strawberry\" arrives as a few chunks whose internal spelling is only indirectly learned. Character-level questions ask the model to recover information the tokenizer collapsed."}]},{"key":"model-internals/embeddings","sources":["Mikolov et al., 2013 — Efficient Estimation of Word Representations in Vector Space","Press & Wolf, 2017 — Using the Output Embedding to Improve Language Models","Reimers & Gurevych, 2019 — Sentence-BERT","Kusupati et al., 2022 — Matryoshka Representation Learning"],"why":"- **A transformer needs vectors, not strings.** It is a stack of matrix multiplications and cannot consume\n  \"cat\". The obvious encoding is one-hot: a vector of length *V* (the vocabulary size, tens of thousands\n  to over a hundred thousand entries) with a single 1.\n- **One-hot is enormous and almost all zero.** The first layer would multiply a 100k-wide sparse vector\n  on every token.\n- **One-hot carries no similarity.** Every pair of one-hot vectors is equidistant and orthogonal, so\n  \"cat\" is exactly as far from \"kitten\" as from \"parliament\". Every regularity would have to be relearned\n  from scratch for every token.\n- **A learned dense vector fixes both.** It is a few hundred to several thousand dimensions, trained by\n  gradient descent on the task (next-token prediction). Tokens that behave similarly in context land in\n  similar places, and that geometry is free information for every later layer.\n- **Learning transfers between neighbours.** A gradient step from a sentence about \"cat\" moves a vector\n  that sits near \"kitten\", so evidence about one token partly informs similar tokens instead of being\n  locked in one orthogonal axis.\n- **The same idea works one level up.** Comparing strings fails on paraphrase when you look for the most\n  relevant document. Comparing learned vectors succeeds, because they were trained so that meaning, not\n  spelling, determines distance.","how":"### Where embeddings sit in an agent\nAn agent meets embedding models in three places, all the same operation: embed a query, find nearest\nvectors, put the matching text into context.\n\n| Agent capability | What gets embedded | What the query is |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Retrieval tool (RAG)** | document chunks, at index time | the search string the agent writes in its tool call |\n| **Long-term memory** | past facts, summaries, user preferences | the current task or turn |\n| **Tool selection** | each tool's name and description | the user request, when there are too many tools to list them all |\n\n- **The agent writes the query.** In a tool-calling loop the model decides the search string, so query\n  quality depends on the prompt and tool description as much as on the embedder.\n- **Results land in the context window.** Top-k chunks cost tokens on every later step, so k and chunk\n  size are context-budget decisions, not only recall decisions.\n- **The rest of this section explains why the geometry works**, starting with the LLM's own table (as\n  background) and then the embedding models you actually deploy.\n\n### Background: token embeddings inside an LLM\n- **The table.** The model owns a matrix `E` of shape `[V, d_model]`. Looking up token id *i* means taking\n  row *i*.\n- **Lookup is a linear layer.** It equals `one_hot(i) · E`, with the multiplication skipped *because* all\n  but one row would be multiplied by zero.\n- **It is trained like any weight.** Each row is a trainable parameter updated by backpropagation through\n  the whole network. Nothing about the geometry is hand-designed; it emerges because tokens that predict\n  similar continuations receive similar gradients.\n\nThe table is a real fraction of the parameter budget:\n\n| Model | Vocabulary × width | Embedding parameters |\n|---|---|---|\n| GPT-2 small | 50,257 × 768 | ≈ 38.6M of ~124M total |\n| Llama-2-7B | 32,000 × 4,096 | ≈ 131M |\n| Llama 3 (same width) | 128,256 × 4,096 | ≈ 525M |\n\n- **Vocabulary size is a trade-off.** Larger vocabularies tokenize text into fewer tokens (cheaper\n  context, better multilingual coverage) at the price of a bigger table. This is one of the trade-offs\n  behind choosing a tokenizer.\n- **Position comes separately.** Attention itself is permutation invariant, so the model must learn where\n  each token is. Older models add a learned positional vector to the token embedding; most current models\n  apply rotary position embeddings inside attention, so the input embedding carries only identity. The\n  `positional-encoding` topic covers that choice.\n- **Scaling.** The original transformer multiplied the looked-up vector by √d_model to put it on a scale\n  comparable to the positional signal; some current models, Gemma among them, keep a similar scaling.\n\n### Background: the unembedding and weight tying\n1. **Project to the vocabulary.** The final hidden state `h` (dimension `d_model`) becomes a score per\n   vocabulary entry: `logits = h · W_Uᵀ` with `W_U` of shape `[V, d_model]`.\n2. **Same shape as `E`.** Some models (GPT-2 among them) **tie** them, using one matrix for both. Tying\n   saves a full table of parameters and encourages consistency, *because* the vector used to recognize a\n   token on the way in is the one used to predict it on the way out.\n3. **Or untie.** Many larger models (Llama-2, for instance) untie them, trading parameters for\n   flexibility.\n4. **One mechanism, two ends.** Either way the model's output is a dot product between a hidden state and\n   every token's output embedding, which is why embeddings and logits are two ends of one mechanism.\n\n### Sentence and document embeddings (what your retriever uses)\n- **Architecture.** An embedding *model* produces one vector for an entire input: usually a transformer\n  encoder plus a pooling step (mean-pool the last hidden states, take a special first-token state, or take\n  the last token for decoder-based embedders).\n- **Pooling alone is not enough.** Raw hidden states of a language model trained only on next-token\n  prediction make poor sentence vectors, because nothing in that objective asks the pooled vectors of two\n  paraphrases to coincide.\n- **So they are trained contrastively.** Given (query, relevant passage) pairs, pull each pair together\n  and push the query away from the other passages in the batch (in-batch negatives, InfoNCE loss).\n\nFor a batch of *B* pairs with similarity `s(q, p) = cos(q, p)` and temperature τ:\n\n```\nL = − (1/B) Σ_i log [ exp(s(q_i, p_i)/τ) / Σ_j exp(s(q_i, p_j)/τ) ]\n```\n\nIt is a *B*-way softmax classification: \"which passage in this batch is mine?\". That framing explains the\nrecipe:\n\n| Ingredient | Why it is there |\n|---|---|\n| Bigger batches | more negatives per query, a harder and more informative task |\n| **Hard negatives** (look relevant but are not, often mined with BM25 or an earlier model) | random in-batch negatives are too easy to teach fine distinctions |\n| Small τ (for example 0.05) | sharpens the softmax so near-misses are penalized |\n\nThe objective directly optimizes the quantity you later query with, which is what makes cosine similarity\nmeaningful.\n\n### Practical consequences of that training\n- **Metric.** Vectors are compared with cosine similarity or dot product; with L2-normalized vectors these\n  are identical, and most models expect normalization.\n- **Dimension is a design choice.** From 384 (the `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` family) to 1,536 or 3,072 (OpenAI's\n  `text-embedding-3` models).\n- **Matryoshka truncation.** Matryoshka-trained models order information so the first *k* dimensions are\n  usable on their own. You can truncate to save index memory, with a measured recall cost.\n- **Asymmetric models.** Some prefix queries and passages differently (the E5 family uses `query:` and\n  `passage:`), because a short question and a long document should not be embedded the same way even when\n  they mean the same thing.\n- **No cross-model comparison.** A vector is only comparable to vectors from the *same model and\n  version*; re-embedding everything is mandatory when the model changes.","example":"### Lookup, weight tying and paraphrase geometry\nThe first block shows that an LLM's embedding layer is a row lookup equal to a one-hot matmul, and that\nGPT-2 ties its input and output embeddings. The second uses a contrastively trained encoder to show the\ngeometry that retrieval relies on: two paraphrases score far higher than an unrelated sentence.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\")\nlm = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\").eval()\nE = lm.get_input_embeddings().weight                   # [50257, 768] trainable table\nids = tok(\"The cat sat\", return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids # [1, n_tokens]\nx = E[ids]                                             # row lookup -> [1, n_tokens, 768]\nonehot = torch.nn.functional.one_hot(ids, E.shape[0]).float()\nassert torch.allclose(onehot @ E, x)                   # lookup == one-hot matmul\nprint(E.shape, x.shape)\n# Weight tying: the output projection is the very same matrix.\nprint(lm.get_output_embeddings().weight.data_ptr() == E.data_ptr())   # True for GPT-2\n\nst = SentenceTransformer(\"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\")   # 384-dim encoder\nsents = [\"How do I reset my password?\",\n         \"Steps to recover access to my account\",\n         \"Best pizza places in Naples\"]\nv = st.encode(sents, normalize_embeddings=True)        # [3, 384], unit length\nprint((v @ v.T).round(2))     # cosine matrix: [0,1] clearly highest, [0,2] and [1,2] low\n```\n\n### Sizing the table and the vector index\nPure Python, no downloads: embedding-table parameters, and raw float32 index memory for a corpus at\ndifferent dimensions (index structures add overhead on top).\n\n```python\ndef table_params(vocab, d_model):\n    return vocab * d_model\n\nfor name, v, d in [(\"GPT-2 small\", 50_257, 768), (\"Llama-2-7B\", 32_000, 4_096),\n                   (\"Llama 3\", 128_256, 4_096)]:\n    print(f\"{name:12s} {table_params(v, d) / 1e6:6.1f}M embedding params\")\n\ndef index_gb(n_vectors, dim, bytes_per_el=4):          # float32 vectors, no index overhead\n    return n_vectors * dim * bytes_per_el / 1e9\n\nfor dim in (384, 1_536, 3_072):\n    print(f\"10M chunks x {dim:5d} dims = {index_gb(10_000_000, dim):6.2f} GB\")\n# GPT-2 small 38.6M, Llama-2-7B 131.1M, Llama 3 525.3M\n# 384 -> 15.36 GB, 1536 -> 61.44 GB, 3072 -> 122.88 GB\n```\n\n| Choice for 10M chunks | Raw vector memory | What to check before adopting |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3,072 dims | ~123 GB | does recall@k beat 1,536 enough to pay 2× memory? |\n| 1,536 dims | ~61 GB | baseline |\n| Matryoshka truncation to 384 | ~15 GB | recall@k drop on your labelled set |\n\n### Agent scenario: a search tool and tool selection from one embedder\nThe agent calls `search_docs` as a tool; the same embedder also shortlists which tools to expose when\nthe catalog is too big to put every schema in the prompt. The `min_score` value is illustrative and\nmust be calibrated per model (see Pitfalls).\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\nMODEL_ID = \"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\"     # stored with every vector\nst = SentenceTransformer(MODEL_ID)\n\ndocs = [\"Reset a password from Settings > Security.\",\n        \"Refunds are issued within 5 business days.\",\n        \"Error AX-4471 means the license server is unreachable.\"]\ndoc_vecs = st.encode(docs, normalize_embeddings=True)\n\ndef search_docs(query: str, k: int = 3, min_score: float = 0.3) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Tool the agent calls. Returns scores too, so traces show retrieval quality.\"\"\"\n    q = st.encode([query], normalize_embeddings=True)[0]\n    scores = doc_vecs @ q                                # cosine, vectors are unit length\n    top = np.argsort(-scores)[:k]\n    hits = [{\"text\": docs[i], \"score\": round(float(scores[i]), 3)}\n            for i in top if scores[i] >= min_score]\n    return {\"model\": MODEL_ID, \"hits\": hits or \"no relevant documents found\"}\n\ntools = {\"search_docs\": \"Search the product help center for how-to and policy answers.\",\n         \"create_ticket\": \"Open a support ticket for a human agent.\",\n         \"get_invoice\": \"Fetch an invoice by id for the signed-in customer.\"}\ntool_vecs = st.encode(list(tools.values()), normalize_embeddings=True)\n\ndef shortlist_tools(user_msg: str, n: int = 2) -> list[str]:\n    s = tool_vecs @ st.encode([user_msg], normalize_embeddings=True)[0]\n    return [list(tools)[i] for i in np.argsort(-s)[:n]]  # only these schemas go in the prompt\n\nprint(shortlist_tools(\"I was charged twice, where is my invoice?\"))\nprint(search_docs(\"how long does a refund take\"))\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: retrieved text is a context-budget line item\nA support agent averages 6 searches per task. Each search result is appended to the transcript and\nre-read on every later step (illustrative token counts).\n\n| Setting | Tokens per search | Retrieved tokens per task |\n|---|---|---|\n| k = 5, 400-token chunks | 2,000 | 12,000 |\n| k = 3, 300-token chunks | 900 | 5,400 |\n| k = 3, plus reranker dropping hits below threshold | ~600 | ~3,600 |\n\nCutting k is only safe if recall@k on the agent's own queries holds; measure it before shipping.","patterns":["**Evaluate retrieval on the agent's own queries.** Log the search strings the agent writes in its tool calls, label a sample, and compute recall@k on those, not only on human-written questions; agent queries are often terser and keyword-shaped. Monitor recall@k on that set per release of prompt or embedder.","**Shortlist tools by embedding when the catalog is large.** Embed each tool's name and description, retrieve the top few per request, and put only those schemas in the prompt. Monitor how often the needed tool was missing from the shortlist (from traces where the agent said it lacked a tool).","**Return scores and provenance from the search tool.** Include the score, source id and model id in each tool result so traces show weak retrievals, and so the agent can say \"nothing relevant found\" instead of answering from a poor hit. Monitor the share of searches returning no hit above threshold.","**Store the model id with every vector.** When any index is written, save the embedding model name and version as metadata and keep one model per index. Monitor for mixed versions with a periodic count by model field; a non-zero second value means a partial migration.","**Re-index blue/green behind an alias.** On a model upgrade, embed the full corpus into a fresh index, run recall@k on a labelled set that covers old documents, then switch the alias the application queries. Monitor backfill progress and recall@k before and after the switch.","**Normalize once, at write time.** Pass `normalize_embeddings=True` (sentence-transformers) or L2-normalize yourself, and configure the index for inner product or cosine to match. Monitor that offline evaluation and production use the same metric.","**Truncate dimensions only with a measured budget.** For Matryoshka-trained models, keep the first *k* dimensions (re-normalize after cutting) to shrink index memory. Gate the change on recall@k against the full-dimension baseline.","**Chunk to the model's input limit.** Count tokens with the embedder's own tokenizer and split before embedding (sentence-transformers exposes `max_seq_length`). Monitor the share of chunks that would be truncated.","**Add lexical search for exact tokens.** Run BM25 next to vector search and merge by rank (reciprocal rank fusion) for IDs, error codes and rare names. Monitor recall on a query slice of exact-match lookups."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Every RAG step, memory recall and \"pick the right tool from 200\" lookup is\n  a nearest-neighbour search over embeddings. If the vectors are wrong, the agent reasons perfectly over\n  the wrong context and nothing errors.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The embedding model behind your vector store (pgvector, Qdrant and\n  similar), the retriever object in agent frameworks, and the memory store an agent writes to between\n  sessions. You never touch the LLM's own token embedding table directly.\n- **What you control.** Which embedding model and version, dimension, normalization, chunk size, query\n  and passage prefixes, and whether lexical search runs alongside. Each changes what the agent's search\n  tool returns.\n- **What you can skip.** Training an embedding model from scratch and the details of weight tying. Know\n  that the table exists and what contrastive training buys; spend your time on retrieval evals.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **RAG retrieval for a support or docs agent.** Queries and chunks are embedded with the same model, and\n  the agent's retrieval tool returns the nearest chunks by cosine similarity.\n- **Long-term agent memory.** Past facts, decisions and user preferences are embedded and recalled by\n  similarity when a new turn looks related, instead of replaying the whole history.\n- **Tool and skill routing.** With dozens of tools, embedding the tool descriptions and shortlisting the\n  closest few keeps the prompt small and the tool choice sharper.\n- **Semantic caching and deduplication.** Near-duplicate questions or tool results are detected by\n  embedding distance, so the agent can reuse an answer or skip a redundant retrieval.","mini_project":"**Build and measure an embedding-backed retrieval tool for an agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** give a small agent a `search_docs` tool backed by embeddings and measure whether it finds the\n  right chunk.\n- **Steps:** chunk 30-50 pages of documentation; embed the chunks with one embedding model and store the\n  vectors with their model name; write 20 questions with a known correct chunk; expose top-k search as a\n  tool; record recall@5 for the questions; then repeat with a second embedding model or a different chunk\n  size.\n- **Done when:** you have a recall@5 table for both runs, the agent answers at least a few questions using\n  the tool, and you can say which change helped and why."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"An LLM already has embeddings, so pooling its hidden states gives good search vectors.\"** Next-token training never asks two paraphrases to land together, so pooled states cluster in a narrow band. Search needs a contrastively trained embedding model whose loss optimizes exactly the cosine you query with.","**\"After an embedder upgrade, re-embedding new documents is enough.\"** Each model has its own space; a new-model query sits at a meaningless distance from old-model vectors. Re-embed the whole corpus into a fresh index and cut over once recall@k holds.","**\"Cosine above 0.8 means relevant.\"** Score distributions differ by model, and some score unrelated pairs well above zero. A threshold is calibrated per model on labelled pairs, never copied.","**\"More dimensions means better retrieval.\"** Memory grows linearly (10M chunks: about 61 GB at 1,536 versus 123 GB at 3,072 in float32) while recall gains shrink quickly. Pick the size with recall@k on your own queries.","**\"A good embedder will find exact codes like AX-4471.\"** Dense vectors encode meaning, not spelling, so rare identifiers blur. Run BM25 alongside vector search and fuse by rank."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Token ids\"] --> E[\"Lookup row in E\"]\n    E --> X[\"Transformer layers\"]\n    X --> H[\"Final hidden state h\"]\n    H --> U[\"h times W_U transpose\"]\n    U --> L[\"Logits per vocab entry\"]\n    E -.->|\"tied in GPT-2\"| U\n    D[\"Agent search tool call\"] --> M[\"Encoder plus pooling\"]\n    M --> V[\"Normalized vector\"]\n    V --> I[(\"Vector index, one model\")]\n    I -->|\"cosine top-k\"| R[\"Retrieved passages\"]\n    R -->|\"tool result\"| AC[\"Agent context\"]","caption":"Inside the LLM, the same `[V, d_model]` shape appears at both ends; outside it, the agent's search tool\ncall is embedded by an embedding model, the index returns the nearest passages, and they go back into the\nagent's context."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between an LLM's embedding table and an embedding model?","a":"- **The table** is a `[V, d_model]` matrix inside the LLM, one row per token id, trained as a side effect\n  of next-token prediction. It feeds the transformer, and its geometry is not meant for comparing texts.\n- **An embedding model** maps a whole input to one vector and is trained contrastively, so cosine\n  similarity between two outputs tracks meaning. That is why you use it, not pooled LLM states, for search."},{"q":"Why do batch size and hard negatives matter when training an embedding model?","a":"- **InfoNCE is a B-way classification.** Each query must pick its passage out of the batch, so more\n  passages per batch means more negatives and a harder, more informative task.\n- **Random negatives are too easy.** Hard negatives mined with BM25 or an earlier model look relevant but\n  are not, which forces the model to learn fine distinctions; a small temperature penalizes near-misses."},{"q":"Your support agent's search tool misses queries containing product codes like \"AX-4471\". How do you debug it?","a":"- **Read the traces.** Check the exact search string the agent wrote in its tool call and what came back;\n  sometimes the agent paraphrased the code away, which is a prompt or tool-description fix.\n- **Check the slice first.** Build a small labelled set of exact-identifier queries and measure recall@k\n  on it separately; dense vectors often blur rare strings because they encode meaning, not spelling.\n- **Then fix the retrieval mix.** Add BM25 alongside vector search and fuse by rank, or route\n  identifier-shaped queries to lexical search. Re-measure both the identifier slice and general queries."},{"q":"How would you decide between 384, 1,536 and 3,072 dimensions for a 10M-chunk corpus?","a":"- **Cost is linear.** Raw float32 vectors take about 15, 61 and 123 GB, before index overhead, and search\n  cost grows with dimension too.\n- **Benefit is not.** Recall per extra dimension shrinks quickly, so compare recall@k on your own labelled\n  pairs. With a Matryoshka model you can test truncation of one embedding run instead of re-embedding."},{"q":"Your agent has 150 tools and keeps picking the wrong one. How would embeddings help, and what would you measure?","a":"- **Shortlist, then let the model choose.** Embed each tool's name and description, retrieve the top few\n  for each request, and expose only those schemas; fewer, more relevant options are easier for the model\n  and cost fewer prompt tokens.\n- **Measure two things.** Shortlist recall (was the correct tool in the top few, from labelled traces) and\n  end-to-end tool-choice accuracy. Rewrite vague tool descriptions first, since they are what gets embedded."}],"tradeoffs":["**Bigger vocabulary vs bigger table.** Fewer tokens per document and cheaper context, but a larger embedding table and more rarely-updated rows. It pays off when the corpus is multilingual or code-heavy.","**Higher dimension vs index cost.** It stores finer distinctions but multiplies vector-index memory and search cost linearly. Recall gained per extra dimension shrinks quickly, so the bigger vector pays off only on large, diverse corpora; measure the break-even with recall@k, do not assume it.","**Large vocabulary vs under-trained rows.** Rows for tokens rare in training get few gradient updates and stay near initialization. A vocabulary built on one corpus and trained on another can contain such tokens, which surface as bizarre outputs when a prompt contains them (the \"glitch token\" phenomenon).","**Tying vs untying.** Tying saves parameters and helps small models; untying gives large models more capacity. The benefit flips as the table becomes a small fraction of total parameters.","**General vs fine-tuned embedder.** A general-purpose model works out of the box. One fine-tuned on domain pairs improves retrieval on jargon-heavy corpora, at the cost of a training pipeline and re-indexing on every model change."],"pitfalls":["**Trusting the agent's answer when retrieval was weak.** The model writes a fluent answer from whatever the search tool returned. Symptom: confident wrong answers whose traces show low-score hits. Fix: apply a calibrated threshold in the tool, return an explicit \"no relevant documents\" result, and tell the agent in the system prompt what to do then.","**Embedding agent memory with a different model than the query path.** A memory written months ago with an older embedder silently stops being recalled. Fix: the same one-model-per-index rule applies to memory stores, with a re-embedding job when the model changes.","**Mixing models or versions in one index.** Symptom: retrieval looks random for part of the corpus, exactly the part embedded with the other model. Fix: one model per index, model id stored per vector.","**Using raw LLM hidden states as sentence vectors.** Including mean-pooled token embeddings. Symptom: similarity scores cluster in a narrow band and nearest neighbours are dominated by length and frequent words. Fix: use a contrastively trained embedding model.","**Forgetting asymmetric prefixes.** Dropping the query/passage prefixes a model was trained with makes recall drop silently, often by a lot, with no error anywhere. Fix: apply the prefixes from the model card in one shared embedding function used by both indexing and querying.","**Mixing cosine and dot product on unnormalized vectors.** Cosine in one place, dot product in another, and rankings disagree between offline evaluation and the production index. Fix: L2-normalize at write time, after which the two metrics give identical rankings.","**Embedding chunks longer than the input limit.** The tail is truncated silently and content past the cut is never retrievable. Fix: count tokens with the embedder's tokenizer and split before embedding.","**Copying a similarity threshold from another model.** \"Treat cosine > 0.8 as relevant\" does not transfer: score distributions differ by model and some put unrelated pairs well above zero. Symptom: a filter that passes everything or nothing. Fix: calibrate thresholds per model on labelled pairs."],"when":"- **Token embeddings are not a choice.** Every LLM has them. You meet them when you count parameters,\n  extend a vocabulary (new rows must be initialized and trained), or inspect why two tokens behave alike.\n- **Use sentence embeddings when** the problem is \"find things that mean the same as this\" at scale:\n  semantic search, RAG retrieval, deduplication, clustering, and classification with a light head on top.\n- **In agents, use them for** the retrieval tool, long-term memory recall, and tool shortlisting once the\n  tool catalog is too large to list in every prompt. A handful of tools needs no embedding step.\n- **Avoid them when** exact lexical match matters (IDs, error codes, rare names); BM25 or hybrid search\n  wins there.\n- **Measure** with recall@k on a labelled set of query–passage pairs before and after any change of\n  model, dimension or chunking.\n- **Interviews:** explain the difference between the LLM's embedding table and an embedding model, why\n  cosine similarity works for one and not the other, and what re-embedding costs when a model is upgraded.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks the embedding model, normalizes and stores vectors, versions them alongside the model name, and writes the recall@k evaluation that gates any swap.","architect":"Owns the embedding contract across the platform: one model per index, dimension and storage budgets, and the re-indexing plan when a model is deprecated.","fde":"Diagnoses \"search returns nonsense\" at a customer by checking model mismatch, missing prefixes and truncated chunks before touching the retrieval algorithm."},"check":{"q":"A team upgraded their embedding model, re-embedded new documents only, and now users report that\nolder documents never show up in search. Explain the mechanism and what you would do.","a":"Vectors are only comparable within one model's learned space. The new model places new documents\nin a geometry that has no relationship to the old model's, so a new-model query vector lands near new\ndocuments and effectively at random distance from old ones; cosine scores between the spaces are\nmeaningless, not merely slightly off. The fix is a full re-embedding of the corpus with the new model\ninto a fresh index, cut over atomically once it is complete, with the model name and version stored\nwith every vector so the mismatch is detectable next time. While the backfill runs, either keep\nquerying the old index or run both and merge results per index, never across them; raw scores from\ntwo models are on different scales, so merge by rank (for example reciprocal rank fusion) rather than by\nscore. A strong answer also mentions the evaluation that should have caught it: recall@k on a labelled\nset covering old documents, run before the cutover."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is an embedding lookup mathematically identical to a linear layer applied to a one-hot vector?","options":["Because the embedding table is initialized from the identity matrix","Because multiplying a one-hot vector by the table selects exactly one row, so the lookup just skips the zero multiplications","Because embeddings are normalized to unit length after lookup","Because both operations are followed by a softmax"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A one-hot vector has a single 1, so its product with the table is that one row. The lookup is an optimization that avoids a V-wide multiplication whose result is known in advance."},{"q":"Why do contrastively trained embedding models produce useful cosine similarities when an LLM's pooled hidden states do not?","options":["Because embedding models use a larger vocabulary","Because LLM hidden states are not normalized","Because the contrastive objective explicitly pulls paraphrase pairs together and pushes unrelated texts apart, while next-token prediction never asks for that","Because embedding models have more layers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Geometry follows the training objective. Next-token prediction shapes vectors for predicting the next token, not for comparing whole texts; contrastive training directly optimizes the distance you later query."},{"q":"What does weight tying between the input embedding and the output projection buy?","options":["A larger context window","Faster attention computation","Fewer parameters and a shared representation for recognizing and predicting each token","Immunity to tokenizer changes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both matrices have shape [V, d_model], so sharing one saves a whole table and forces the same vector to serve on the way in and on the way out. It matters most when the table is a large fraction of the model."}]},{"key":"model-internals/llm-architecture","sources":["Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Raffel et al., 2020 — Exploring the Limits of Transfer Learning with a Unified Text-to-Text Transformer","Radford et al., 2018 — Improving Language Understanding by Generative Pre-Training","Wang et al., 2022 — What Language Model Architecture and Pretraining Objective Work Best for Zero-Shot Generalization?"],"why":"- **Seq2seq tasks have a shape.** Translation has a complete input you may read in any order, then an\n  output produced left to right. The 2017 transformer mirrored it.\n- **Two stacks for two jobs.** The encoder lets every source token attend to every other, in both\n  directions, *because* understanding a sentence benefits from seeing its end. The decoder is masked so\n  each output token sees only earlier output, *because* at inference later tokens do not exist yet.\n  Cross-attention connects the two.\n- **Question 1: which single objective yields the most general model from unlabelled text?** Next-token\n  prediction over a causal stream turns\n  every token into a training example, with no masking scheme or input/output split to design.\n- **Question 2: how do you handle a conversation?** This turn's output is next turn's input. A two-stack\n  model re-encodes the growing transcript every turn; a one-stack model appends tokens and keeps the cache.\n- **Question 3: how does few-shot prompting work?** Examples and the request live in the same stream as\n  the answer, so in-context learning falls out of the objective instead of needing a separate mechanism.\n- **Decoder-only answered all three with less machinery**, and at scale that simplicity compounded.","how":"### The architecture map of an agent stack\n| Agent component | Family | Why that family |\n|---|---|---|\n| Planner, chat and tool-calling model | decoder-only | multi-turn, open-ended, appends to one cached stream |\n| Embedder for retrieval and memory | encoder-only (or a fine-tuned decoder with pooling) | produces a vector, no generation |\n| Reranker after retrieval | encoder-only cross-encoder | scores a pair, no generation |\n| Speech-to-text for voice agents | encoder–decoder | long fixed input, short output, encode once |\n\nThe rest of this section explains why each family fits its row.\n\n### Three families\n| Family | Examples | Attention | Trained by | Used for |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Encoder-only | BERT and descendants | bidirectional | masking tokens and predicting them | classification, token tagging, embedding models, cross-encoder rerankers |\n| Encoder–decoder | T5, BART, mT5, Whisper | bidirectional encoder, causal decoder plus cross-attention | T5: span corruption (hide spans of the input, generate them in the output) | translation, speech-to-text |\n| Decoder-only | the GPT line, Llama, Mistral, essentially every frontier chat model | one causal stack | next-token prediction | chat, agents, tools, open-ended generation |\n\n- **Encoder-only produces representations, not text.** The job is to *read* an input and score it. Some\n  recent embedding models are fine-tuned decoder-only LLMs with last-token pooling; the family describes\n  the attention pattern, not a fixed job.\n- **Encoder–decoder cross-attention.** Every decoder layer adds a block whose queries come from the\n  decoder state and whose keys and values come from the encoder's final output. Because that output never\n  changes during generation, each layer's cross-attention K and V are projected once and cached for the\n  whole decode, next to the ordinary self-attention cache, which grows with the output.\n- **Decoder-only is one sequence.** Prompt, retrieved documents, tool results and the answer are a single\n  token sequence. The only difference between \"input\" and \"output\" is which positions the loss was\n  computed on.\n\n### What the mask changes\n- **Causal prompt tokens see only the past.** In a decoder-only model a prompt token at position *i*\n  attends only to positions ≤ *i*, so the first word of a question is represented without seeing the rest.\n- **An encoder sees everything at once.** Each token's representation can use the whole input. This is the\n  standard argument that encoders \"understand\" input more richly per token.\n- **Decoder-only compensates.** With depth and scale, and because later prompt positions see everything\n  before them, by the end of the prompt the model has a complete picture.\n\n### Where the compute goes\n- **Encoder–decoder: encode once.** The encoder runs once per input and every decoding step reuses its\n  outputs through cross-attention; the decoder's self-attention cache grows with the output only.\n- **So you can pair a large encoder with a small decoder** when the input is long and the output short.\n  Speech models like Whisper keep the design: the audio encoder is expensive and runs once, the text\n  decoder is cheap and runs per token.\n- **Decoder-only: one stack reads and writes.** The prompt runs through the same full stack as the answer\n  (the prefill phase), populating one KV cache the answer extends. Prefill is fast in wall-clock terms\n  because all prompt positions run in parallel, but in FLOPs per token \"reading\" costs the same full stack\n  as \"writing\"; there is no smaller encoder to delegate it to.\n- **The compensation.** Nothing is re-encoded when the conversation grows: the cache from turn one is still\n  valid for turn two.\n\n### Parameter counts mislead\n- Each token passes through only one stack of an encoder–decoder.\n- So a model with 2N parameters split evenly across encoder and decoder costs roughly the FLOPs per token\n  of an N-parameter decoder-only model (Raffel et al. make this comparison when choosing T5's baseline).\n- \"T5-11B versus a 7B decoder\" is therefore not an apples-to-apples cost comparison; compare FLOPs per\n  token and memory, not headline size.\n\n### Background: why decoder-only scaled better for general use\n- **Objective density.** Every position in a causal stream contributes a loss term. Span-corruption and\n  masked-LM objectives compute loss on a fraction of positions, so each document yields less signal.\n- **Zero-shot behaviour.** Controlled comparisons (Wang et al., 2022) found causal decoder-only models\n  trained with the plain language-modelling objective generalized best zero-shot after pre-training\n  alone; encoder–decoders caught up only after additional multitask fine-tuning.\n- **Serving.** One stack means one set of weights, one KV cache and one batching scheme. Prefix caching and\n  multi-turn reuse are natural because history is just earlier tokens.\n- **Unification.** Instruction following, tool calling and multi-document prompts all become \"put it in\n  the stream\". A prefix-LM variant (bidirectional over the prompt, causal over the answer) is a hybrid that\n  keeps one stack; some models used it, but it did not displace the plain causal design.\n\n### What encoder–decoder still does better\n- **Fixed input, short output, no multi-turn:** translation, summarization pipelines, speech-to-text, OCR\n  post-correction.\n- **Why:** the bidirectional encoder gives a better per-token view of the source, cross-attention gives a\n  direct alignment between output and input, and the encoder cost is paid once.\n- **Encoder-only remains the right tool** for embeddings and rerankers, where no generation happens.","example":"### Two generation loops side by side\nThe T5 encoder runs once and its output is handed, unchanged, to every decoder step; the GPT-2 \"input\"\ngrows by one token per step, with no separate encoder at all. Outputs from these tiny base models will be\nrough; the point is the data flow.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM, AutoModelForCausalLM\n\n# Encoder–decoder: two streams joined by cross-attention\nt5_tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"google/flan-t5-small\")\nt5 = AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM.from_pretrained(\"google/flan-t5-small\").eval()\nsrc = t5_tok(\"Translate English to German: The cat sleeps.\", return_tensors=\"pt\")\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    enc = t5.get_encoder()(**src)                        # bidirectional pass, done ONCE\n    dec = torch.tensor([[t5.config.decoder_start_token_id]])\n    for _ in range(8):\n        out = t5(encoder_outputs=enc, attention_mask=src.attention_mask, decoder_input_ids=dec)\n        dec = torch.cat([dec, out.logits[:, -1].argmax(-1, keepdim=True)], dim=1)\nprint(t5_tok.decode(dec[0], skip_special_tokens=True))\n\n# Decoder-only: prompt and completion are one causal stream\ng_tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\")\ng = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\").eval()\nids = g_tok(\"English: The cat sleeps. German:\", return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    for _ in range(8):\n        ids = torch.cat([ids, g(ids).logits[:, -1].argmax(-1, keepdim=True)], dim=1)\nprint(g_tok.decode(ids[0]))\n# T5 re-reads a fixed encoding each step; GPT-2's \"input\" is the whole growing sequence.\n# A production decoder-only loop would pass past_key_values instead of re-running the prefix.\n```\n\n### Back-of-envelope cost: read-heavy job vs multi-turn chat\nRule of thumb: a token costs about 2 FLOPs per parameter of each stack it passes through. The figures\nbelow are illustrative orders of magnitude that ignore attention and cross-attention cost.\n\n```python\ndef enc_dec_flops(in_tok, out_tok, enc_params, dec_params):\n    return 2 * enc_params * in_tok + 2 * dec_params * out_tok\n\ndef dec_only_flops(in_tok, out_tok, params):\n    return 2 * params * (in_tok + out_tok)\n\n# Summarize a 3,000-token document into 100 tokens.\nprint(enc_dec_flops(3_000, 100, 1e9, 1e9) / 1e12, \"TFLOP\")   # 2B total, split evenly: 6.2\nprint(dec_only_flops(3_000, 100, 2e9) / 1e12, \"TFLOP\")       # 2B decoder-only: 12.4\n\n# Ten chat turns, each adding 300 tokens of history.\nreencoded = sum(300 * t for t in range(1, 11))   # encoder-decoder re-encodes the transcript\nprint(reencoded, \"tokens encoded vs\", 300 * 10, \"prefilled once with a KV cache\")  # 16500 vs 3000\n```\n\n| Workload | Encoder–decoder | Decoder-only | Better fit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 3,000 in, 100 out, one shot | ~6.2 TFLOP (2B split evenly) | ~12.4 TFLOP (2B) | encoder–decoder |\n| 10 turns × 300 tokens | 16,500 tokens re-encoded | 3,000 tokens prefilled, cache reused | decoder-only |\n\n### Agent scenario: a voice support agent, one family per component\nA voice agent transcribes a 5-minute call, retrieves help articles, reranks them and runs a 12-step\ntool-calling loop where each step appends ~800 tokens (tool call plus result). Token counts are\nillustrative.\n\n| Component | Family | Work per call | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Transcribe audio | encoder–decoder (Whisper-style) | audio encoded once | transcript stored as text |\n| Embed query, search | encoder-only embedder | 1 short query per search | vectors precomputed for articles |\n| Rerank 50 candidates | encoder-only cross-encoder | 50 pair scores | replaces 50 chat-model calls |\n| 12-step agent loop | decoder-only | 12 × 800 = 9,600 new tokens prefilled | cache reused step to step |\n| Same loop on an encoder–decoder | (counterfactual) | 800 × (1 + 2 + … + 12) = 62,400 tokens re-encoded | grows quadratically with steps |\n\nThe agent loop belongs on the decoder-only model; everything that only reads and scores goes to cheaper\nencoders.","patterns":["**Keep the agent loop on one decoder-only stream.** Append tool calls and results instead of rebuilding the prompt each step, so the cache from step one stays valid. Monitor cached versus uncached input tokens per agent step from provider usage fields or engine metrics.","**Move read-only sub-tasks out of the agent model.** Relevance filtering, classification of tool results and PII detection run on encoder models called as tools or pipeline steps. Monitor agent-model tokens per task before and after.","**Route by task shape, not by habit.** Send scoring, classification and reranking to an encoder with a head or a cross-encoder; keep the decoder-only model for dialogue and agents. Monitor cost per item and p95 latency per route.","**Transcribe once, chat over text.** For audio workloads run an encoder–decoder ASR model such as Whisper once per file, store the transcript, and pass text to the chat model. Monitor transcription latency and chat prefill tokens per turn.","**Load the right head class.** Use `AutoModelForCausalLM` for decoder-only, `AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM` for encoder–decoder, and an encoder with a task head for scoring. Confirm your serving engine supports the architecture before committing, since many LLM serving stacks are optimized for decoder-only models.","**Lay out causal prompts deliberately.** Put stable instructions and tool definitions first (prefix caching reuses them), place the document, then restate the task after it. Monitor extraction recall on a labelled set when you change the layout.","**Benchmark at your real length ratio.** Replay a sample of production input/output lengths through each candidate and compare cost per request and p95 latency, because the ratio decides, not headline parameter counts."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A tool-calling loop is \"append to one stream and keep going\", which is\n  exactly what decoder-only models do cheaply. Knowing why tells you what breaks the economics: editing\n  history, re-encoding transcripts, or using the chat model for jobs an encoder does at a fraction of the cost.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The planner or chat LLM (decoder-only), the embedding model and\n  reranker in the RAG step (encoder-only), and the speech-to-text front end of a voice agent\n  (encoder–decoder such as Whisper). Each is a separate deployment with its own cost profile.\n- **What you control.** Which family serves each component, prompt layout for a causal reader (task before\n  and after long documents), and whether bulk scoring goes to the agent LLM or a cheap encoder.\n- **What you can skip.** T5's span-corruption recipe and the zero-shot ablations in detail; remember the\n  conclusion (one causal stack scaled best) and the cost rule of thumb.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Choosing models per agent component.** A decoder-only LLM plans and calls tools, an encoder embeds and\n  reranks for RAG, and an encoder–decoder transcribes speech for a voice agent.\n- **Offloading bulk scoring from the agent LLM.** Relevance filtering or classification over thousands of\n  items goes to a cheap encoder, so the planner only sees the shortlist.\n- **Prompt layout for a causal reader.** Because each token only sees what came before, agent teams state\n  the task before long documents and restate it after them.\n- **Costing long agent loops.** Decoder-only models append and reuse their cache, so an append-only\n  transcript keeps per-step cost low; re-encoding the transcript each step would not.","mini_project":"**Split an agent's work across model families (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show the cost and latency difference between scoring with the agent LLM and with an encoder.\n- **Steps:** take 200 query-document pairs; score relevance once by prompting a decoder-only chat model and\n  once with a cross-encoder reranker; log latency, tokens and agreement with a small set of hand labels;\n  wire the cheaper option that still meets your accuracy bar in as the agent's filter step.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of time, cost and accuracy for both approaches and a short note on which\n  one the agent should use for this step and why."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"A chat model has a separate input channel for the prompt and tool results.\"** In a decoder-only model, system prompt, tool results and answer are one token sequence; \"input\" only means positions the loss skipped. A tool result is read exactly like an instruction.","**\"Prompt tokens are nearly free because prefill is fast.\"** Prefill is fast in wall-clock time because positions run in parallel, but each prompt token still costs a full-stack pass, roughly 2 FLOPs per parameter, so a long re-sent prompt dominates compute in read-heavy jobs.","**\"Parameter count tells you the cost.\"** Each token passes through only one stack of an encoder–decoder, so 2N parameters split evenly cost about the FLOPs per token of an N-parameter decoder-only model. Compare FLOPs per token and memory.","**\"Encoder–decoders are obsolete.\"** They lost the general-purpose race, not every job: Whisper-style speech-to-text encodes long audio once, and encoder-only models still serve embedders and rerankers far more cheaply than a chat model.","**\"Rewriting earlier history each step costs nothing.\"** The KV cache is valid only for an unchanged prefix; editing a past message forces recomputation from that point, which removes the decoder-only multi-turn advantage."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Source text or audio\"] --> EN[\"Encoder, bidirectional, runs once\"]\n    EN --> XKV[(\"Cross-attn K, V cached\")]\n    XKV --> DD[\"Decoder step, causal\"]\n    DD -->|\"next token\"| DD\n    DD --> O1[\"Output text\"]\n    P[\"Prompt, tools, tool results\"] --> PF[\"Prefill, same full stack\"]\n    PF --> KV[(\"Single KV cache\")]\n    KV --> DS[\"Decode step\"]\n    DS -->|\"append k, v\"| KV\n    DS --> O2[\"Answer, appended to stream\"]","caption":"Follow the two paths: the encoder output (for example a voice agent's audio) is computed once and read by\nevery decode step, while the decoder-only agent model extends a single causal stream of prompt, tool calls\nand results with its one KV cache."},"interview":[{"q":"Why did decoder-only models win over encoder–decoders for general-purpose LLMs?","a":"- **Denser, simpler objective.** Next-token prediction makes every position a loss term on raw text, with\n  no masking scheme or input/output split to design.\n- **Everything is the stream.** Few-shot examples, tools, documents and chat history are just earlier\n  tokens, so in-context learning and multi-turn reuse come for free.\n- **Simpler serving.** One set of weights, one KV cache and one batching scheme, which made prefix caching\n  and continuous batching natural."},{"q":"What exactly gets cached during encoder–decoder generation?","a":"- **Two caches per decoder layer.** The cross-attention K and V are projected once from the fixed encoder\n  output and reused for the whole decode.\n- **Plus the usual self-attention cache.** It grows by one entry per generated token, like a decoder-only\n  model's cache, but covers only the output, not the source."},{"q":"A team's RAG agent asks the chat model to rerank ten million query–document pairs a day and the bill exploded. What do you propose?","a":"- **Diagnose the shape.** Reranking reads a pair and emits a score; there is no generation, so a full\n  decoder pass per pair is wasted spend, and it also inflates the agent's latency per step.\n- **Replace it.** Use a cross-encoder reranker or an encoder with a classification head, evaluated on a\n  labelled sample against the chat model's rankings, and keep the chat model for the final answer only."},{"q":"Your agent's latency per step grows with conversation length even though replies are short. Which architecture facts do you check?","a":"- **Is the stream append-only?** Decoder-only reuse depends on nothing earlier changing; rebuilt prompts,\n  reordered tools or edited history force a full re-prefill every step.\n- **Is any component re-encoding the transcript?** An encoder–decoder or a summarizer run over the whole\n  history each turn grows cost with every step. Move it to a one-shot step or a cached prefix.\n- **Measure** prefill tokens per step; it should track the newest step's size, not the transcript."},{"q":"How would you compare the cost of T5-11B with a 7B decoder-only model?","a":"- **Not by headline size.** Each token passes through only one stack of an encoder–decoder, so an evenly\n  split 2N-parameter model costs roughly the per-token FLOPs of an N-parameter decoder-only model.\n- **So compute per token.** Weight the encoder and decoder by your input and output lengths, add memory for\n  weights and caches, then confirm with a benchmark at your real length ratio."},{"q":"When would you not use a decoder-only model even though one is available?","a":"- **Fixed input, short output, no dialogue.** Translation at volume or speech-to-text, where an\n  encoder–decoder pays the expensive encoding once and decodes with a small stack.\n- **No text output at all.** Embeddings, PII detection and classifiers, where an encoder-only model is\n  far cheaper per item and easier to calibrate."}],"tradeoffs":["**One stack vs encode once.** Decoder-only gives one stack, one cache and natural multi-turn reuse, but every prompt token costs a full-stack pass. Encoder–decoder pays encoding once and can decode with a smaller stack, which wins when inputs are long and outputs short.","**Bidirectional vs causal reading.** An encoder sees the whole input per token; causal prompts see only the past. The gap matters for small models on reading-heavy tasks and shrinks as decoder-only models scale.","**Dense objective vs left-to-right lock-in.** A single next-token objective is dense and label-free, but it bakes in left-to-right generation. Tasks that are naturally classification or scoring are served more cheaply by an encoder with a head.","**Explicit alignment vs an extra block.** Cross-attention gives a source-to-output alignment (Whisper's word-level timestamps are derived from cross-attention weights), at the cost of a second attention block per layer. The advantage disappears when the \"source\" keeps changing, as in multi-turn use, because the alignment target is re-encoded every time it grows."],"pitfalls":["**Using a chat model for bulk classification or reranking.** Cost per item is far higher than an encoder with a head, and latency budgets blow up at scale. Fix: route scoring to an encoder or cross-encoder, validated against the chat model's labels on a sample.","**Assuming an encoder–decoder can \"continue the conversation\" cheaply.** The transcript is re-encoded every turn, so latency grows with history even for short replies. Fix: keep dialogue on a decoder-only model and use the encoder–decoder for one-shot transformations.","**Treating the prompt as if it were read bidirectionally.** Instructions placed after a long document cannot influence how earlier tokens were represented. Symptom: extraction misses fields the task asked for, because the document was \"read\" before the model knew what to look for.","**Trusting one placement rule.** Vendor guidance on placement differs (several recommend the question after long documents), so stating the task before the document and repeating it after is a common hedge; measure on your task.","**Confusing the architecture family with the training recipe.** A decoder-only base model never instruction-tuned will happily continue your prompt instead of answering it. Fix: use the instruct or chat variant for question answering, and keep base models for completion or fine-tuning."],"when":"- **Default to decoder-only** for anything conversational, agentic, tool-using or open-ended: it has the\n  best-trained weights, and its serving stack (KV cache, prefix caching, continuous batching) is the most\n  mature.\n- **Choose encoder–decoder** for a fixed transformation of a known input with no dialogue (translation at\n  volume, speech recognition, structured summarization) when the input is much longer than the output.\n- **Choose encoder-only** when you need a score or a vector, not text: embeddings, rerankers, classifiers\n  and PII detectors.\n- **Measure** cost per request and p95 latency under your real input/output length ratio before deciding;\n  the ratio is what decides.\n- **Interviews:** draw the three families, explain what cross-attention connects, and argue why one causal\n  stack won.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks `AutoModelForCausalLM` versus `AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM` versus an encoder with a head, and knows the generation loop and cache semantics differ between them.","architect":"Decides the model family per workload in a system, keeping the expensive decoder-only model for dialogue and routing scoring and retrieval tasks to encoders.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their translation or transcription pipeline should not be rebuilt on the chat model, and sizes the two differently."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants to transcribe call-centre audio and then let agents chat about each transcript.\nWhich architecture family fits each half, and why not one model for both?","a":"Transcription is a fixed-input, read-heavy, write-light task with no dialogue: an encoder–decoder\nsuch as Whisper runs its expensive audio encoder once and decodes text with a small decoder, which is\ncheap per token and well aligned to the source. The chat half is multi-turn with growing history, tool\ncalls and open-ended answers, which is exactly what a decoder-only model handles by appending tokens and\nreusing its KV cache. One model for both would either force the chat model to consume audio tokens it was\nnot trained on, or force the transcription model to re-encode the whole transcript plus conversation on\nevery turn. Two models, with the transcript passed as text, is cheaper and better at each job. If the\ncustomer already uses a natively audio-capable chat model, the argument becomes one of cost: audio\ntypically consumes several times more tokens per second of speech than its transcript, and every chat\nturn would pay prefill over it unless it is prefix-cached, so transcribing once is still usually cheaper."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does cross-attention in an encoder–decoder connect?","options":["Decoder queries to decoder keys from earlier layers","Decoder queries to the encoder's final keys and values","Encoder queries to decoder keys and values","Positional encodings to token embeddings"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each decoder layer has a self-attention block over the output so far and a cross-attention block whose keys and values come from the encoder output, letting every generated token consult the full input."},{"q":"Why is multi-turn chat cheaper in a decoder-only model than in an encoder–decoder?","options":["Decoder-only models have fewer parameters","Encoder–decoders cannot use a KV cache","History is just earlier tokens in one stream, so the existing cache stays valid; an encoder–decoder must re-encode the growing transcript","Decoder-only models skip attention over the prompt"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In a single causal stream, nothing before the new tokens changes, so cached keys and values are reused. An encoder–decoder treats the transcript as input, and input is re-encoded whenever it changes."},{"q":"Which task is the weakest fit for a decoder-only chat model?","options":["Multi-step agent with tool calls","Few-shot question answering","Scoring ten million query–document pairs for relevance","Open-ended summarization in a conversation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Scoring is a read-and-rate task with no generation; an encoder with a classification head, or a cross-encoder reranker, does it at a fraction of the cost per pair."}]},{"key":"model-internals/context-window","sources":["Liu et al., 2023 — Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts","Su et al., 2021 — RoFormer: Enhanced Transformer with Rotary Position Embedding","Peng et al., 2023 — YaRN: Efficient Context Window Extension of Large Language Models"],"why":"- **Attention compute grows with length.** Every token is compared with every earlier token, so prefill\n  compute has a term quadratic in length.\n- **KV-cache memory grows with length.** Keys and values are stored for every position in every layer, so\n  memory grows linearly and a single long request's cache can exceed the model's own weights.\n- **Positions are trained up to a length.** Positional encodings are learned or tuned for training\n  lengths; unseen positions degrade quality sharply unless the encoding is deliberately extended. So\n  vendors publish a hard limit and engines enforce one (`max_model_len` in vLLM), because worst-case memory\n  must be reserved in advance.\n- **The model is stateless between calls.** \"The chatbot remembers our conversation\" is wrong in a way\n  that matters: the client re-sends the entire transcript each turn and the model re-reads it. Agent\n  memory is an illusion the application maintains, paid in tokens on every step.\n- **And it ends abruptly.** At the window boundary the request is rejected or something is dropped.\n  Deciding what to drop, summarize or retrieve is the problem the context window forces on every agent.","how":"### Accounting for one agent step\nEvery request must satisfy `input_tokens + max_output_tokens ≤ window`.\n\n- **Input is everything after chat templating:** system prompt, each prior user and assistant turn, tool\n  definitions, tool outputs, retrieved chunks, and the template's own role markers. In agents, tool\n  schemas and tool outputs are often the largest items.\n- **Output is often capped separately.** Many APIs cap output at a figure well below the window, so the\n  practical limit on a long answer is the output cap, not the window.\n- **Count with the model's own tokenizer.** The same text can differ in token count by tens of percent\n  across tokenizers (more for non-English text and code), so budgets need the right one.\n- **Hidden consumers count too.** Images and audio are converted to tokens. On reasoning models the hidden\n  thinking tokens are generated into the same window and usually billed and capped as output, so a request\n  for a short answer can still run out of room.\n- **Edge behaviour differs.** Most APIs reject an over-long request outright; some truncate silently.\n\n### What a full window costs\nTake a GQA model such as Llama-3-8B (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head dimension 128, fp16):\n\n```\nKV per token = 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 bytes = 128 KiB\n128k-token request ≈ 16 GiB of cache   (roughly the model's own fp16 weights)\n```\n\n- **Without GQA** (32 KV heads) the same request needs four times that.\n- **Compute grows faster than memory.** Per layer, projections and MLP cost on the order of 24·d² FLOPs\n  per token regardless of length, while attention scores and the weighted sum cost about 4·n·d for a\n  token attending over *n* positions.\n- **The crossover.** The attention term overtakes the rest once *n* exceeds roughly 6·d, around 25k tokens\n  at d = 4,096 (a rule of thumb for orders of magnitude, not a measurement).\n- **So** below that, long prompts cost roughly linearly; above it, every doubling costs more than double.\n  A long-running agent crosses this line without anyone deciding it should.\n\n### Why the limit sits where it does\n| Constraint | What sets it |\n|---|---|\n| **Training length** | Models are pre-trained at some length, often extended by continued training at longer lengths with the rotary base frequency adjusted (RoPE scaling methods such as YaRN). The advertised window is where that extension was validated. |\n| **Memory** | The engine must hold the KV cache for the longest allowed request, so operators may configure a smaller window than the model supports to fit more concurrent users. |\n| **Compute and latency** | Prefill time grows faster than linearly in prompt length, so time-to-first-token on a window-filling prompt can be seconds, re-paid every turn unless the prefix is cached. |\n\n- **Published windows keep growing:** from about 2k tokens (GPT-3 era) through 4k, 8k and 32k to 128k,\n  200k and, for some models, a million or more.\n- **Treat the exact figures as moving targets;** the mechanisms are not.\n\n### Nominal versus effective window\n- **Not all positions are equal.** A model accepting 128k tokens does not use them equally. Liu et al.\n  (2023) showed retrieval-style accuracy is highest when the relevant passage sits at the start or end of\n  the context, and drops in the middle. Needle-in-a-haystack tests probe the same thing.\n- **The effective window** is the length over which the model reliably uses information. It is\n  task-dependent, usually shorter than the nominal one, and must be measured for your task rather than\n  read off a spec sheet.\n- **For agents** this means an instruction from step 1 or a tool result from step 5 can be technically\n  present at step 30 and still be ignored, because it now sits in the middle of a long transcript.\n\n### Living within it\n- **Sliding window:** drop the oldest turns. Simple, loses early instructions and facts.\n- **Summarization / compaction:** replace old turns with a model-written summary. Keeps the gist, loses\n  detail and costs a call. The usual choice for long agent runs.\n- **Retrieval:** keep history or documents outside the prompt and pull in only what the current turn\n  needs. The default for large corpora and for agent long-term memory; the `long-context-vs-rag` topic\n  covers the trade.\n- **Prompt caching:** does not enlarge the window, but makes a long shared prefix cheap to re-send by\n  reusing its KV cache across requests, which changes the economics of a big system prompt and tool list.\n- **Sliding-window attention** in the model itself (Mistral 7B v0.1 attends over a 4,096-token window per\n  layer) bounds per-layer cost while deeper layers still propagate information further back. It is an\n  architectural choice, not something you configure per request.","example":"### Token budgeter for a support assistant\nAn 8,192-token window. The fixed parts (system prompt, retrieved chunks, the new question) are paid first,\nthe output reservation is held back, and history is admitted newest-first until the budget runs out.\nCount with the deployed model's tokenizer; `tiktoken` is used here only as a stand-in.\n\n```python\nimport tiktoken\n\nenc = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\")          # stand-in; use your model's tokenizer\ncount = lambda s: len(enc.encode(s))\n\nWINDOW, MAX_OUT, TEMPLATE_OVERHEAD = 8_192, 800, 64  # overhead: role markers, illustrative\n\ndef fit_history(system, chunks, history, question):\n    fixed = count(system) + sum(map(count, chunks)) + count(question) + TEMPLATE_OVERHEAD\n    budget = WINDOW - MAX_OUT - fixed\n    if budget < 0:\n        raise ValueError(f\"fixed parts exceed window by {-budget} tokens: trim chunks\")\n    kept = []\n    for turn in reversed(history):                  # newest turns are worth the most\n        n = count(turn)\n        if n > budget:\n            break\n        kept.insert(0, turn); budget -= n\n    return kept, budget\n\nsystem = \"You are a support assistant. \" * 60                 # ~360 tokens\nchunks = [\"Policy section ... \" * 120 for _ in range(6)]        # 6 chunks, ~360 tokens each\nhistory = [f\"turn {i}: \" + \"words \" * 250 for i in range(20)]  # ~250 tokens per turn\nkept, left = fit_history(system, chunks, history, \"How do I get a refund?\")\nprint(f\"kept {len(kept)} of {len(history)} turns, {left} tokens spare\")\n# Roughly: fixed parts ~2,600 tokens, so ~4,800 remain for history and about 18 of the 20\n# ~250-token turns survive. Dropping the oldest turns is the crude option; summarizing them is the\n# usual upgrade, and it is what you would do before ever cutting the system prompt or chunks.\n```\n\n### Compacting an agent loop's tool outputs\nA tool-calling loop that clips large tool results and compacts old turns once the transcript passes a\nthreshold. `summarize` is whatever cheap model call you use; token counts come from your tokenizer.\n\n```python\nMAX_TOOL_TOKENS = 1_500      # clip any single tool result to this (illustrative)\nCOMPACT_AT = 0.7             # compact when the transcript uses 70% of the input budget\nKEEP_RECENT = 6              # messages kept verbatim after compaction\n\ndef add_tool_result(messages, call_id, text, count, store):\n    if count(text) > MAX_TOOL_TOKENS:\n        ref = store.put(text)                          # raw output kept outside the prompt\n        text = text[: MAX_TOOL_TOKENS * 3] + f\"\\n[truncated, full result: {ref}]\"\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call_id, \"content\": text})\n\ndef maybe_compact(system, messages, count, budget, summarize):\n    used = count(system) + sum(count(m.get(\"content\") or \"\") for m in messages)\n    if used < COMPACT_AT * budget:\n        return messages\n    cut = len(messages) - KEEP_RECENT\n    while cut > 0 and messages[cut][\"role\"] == \"tool\":   # never orphan a tool result from its call\n        cut -= 1\n    if cut <= 0:\n        return messages\n    old, recent = messages[:cut], messages[cut:]\n    summary = summarize(old)                           # one call, one cache miss, at a boundary\n    return [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"Summary of earlier steps:\\n{summary}\"}] + recent\n```\n\nThe characters-per-token factor of 3 in the clip is a rough heuristic; clip by tokens if your tokenizer\nis cheap to call. Keep the system prompt and tool definitions out of the compacted region.\n\nNever split an assistant tool call from its tool result: most chat APIs reject a `tool` message whose\ncall is missing, which is why the cut point moves back past any tool messages.\n\n### Worked scenario: a 30-step research agent on a 128k window\nThe agent starts with a 7k-token prefix (system prompt plus 15 tool schemas) and each step appends about\n4k tokens (tool call plus a raw web-page result).\n\n| Step | Input tokens, raw results | Input tokens, results clipped to 1.5k |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 7k | 7k |\n| 10 | 43k | 20.5k |\n| 20 | 83k | 35.5k |\n| 30 | 123k: the next step, plus output room, no longer fits | 50.5k |\n\n- **Raw results** kill the run near step 30, and early findings sit mid-context where they are used least\n  reliably by then.\n- **Clipping alone** cuts growth to about a third; compaction at 70% of the budget then caps input per\n  step for runs of any length (illustrative figures from the stated per-step sizes).\n\n### What filling the window costs on Llama-3-8B\nPure Python using the two formulas above: KV memory per request, and the share of per-layer FLOPs spent\non attention for the last token (rule of thumb, d = 4,096).\n\n```python\ndef kv_gib(tokens, layers=32, kv_heads=8, head_dim=128, bytes_per_el=2):\n    return 2 * layers * kv_heads * head_dim * bytes_per_el * tokens / 2**30\n\ndef attention_share(n, d=4_096):\n    return 4 * n * d / (24 * d * d + 4 * n * d)\n\nfor n in (8_192, 32_768, 131_072):\n    print(f\"{n:>7} tokens: KV {kv_gib(n):5.1f} GiB, attention share {attention_share(n):.0%}\")\n#    8192 tokens: KV   1.0 GiB, attention share 25%\n#   32768 tokens: KV   4.0 GiB, attention share 57%\n#  131072 tokens: KV  16.0 GiB, attention share 84%\n```\n\n| Request length | KV cache | Attention share of compute | Practical consequence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8k | ~1 GiB | ~25% | dozens of such requests fit beside the weights on an 80 GB GPU |\n| 32k | ~4 GiB | ~57% | attention now dominates; TTFT grows faster than length |\n| 128k | ~16 GiB | ~84% | one request holds as much memory as the weights |","patterns":["**Budget every agent step before you call.** Count each request with the deployed model's tokenizer, including template and tool-definition overhead, and trim or reject before the API call. Monitor \"context length exceeded\" errors and truncation events per route as first-class metrics.","**Compact long agent runs.** Keep the last few turns verbatim, summarize older turns and large tool outputs, and store raw outputs outside the prompt for retrieval. Monitor input tokens per turn; it should plateau, not grow linearly.","**Clip tool outputs at the tool boundary.** Each tool returns a bounded, structured result (top rows, key fields, a pointer to the full payload) rather than raw HTML or full logs. Monitor tool-result token size per tool; one noisy tool usually dominates.","**Load only the tools a step needs.** With dozens of tools, schemas alone can cost thousands of tokens every step; shortlist tools per task and keep the shortlist stable within a run so the prefix still caches. Monitor tool-schema tokens per step and how often the needed tool was missing from the shortlist.","**Size the engine window per deployment.** Set `max_model_len` in vLLM to the length the traffic actually uses and route long-document work to a separate deployment, so a few long requests cannot starve short chats. Monitor KV utilization and preemptions.","**Size `max_tokens` from real answers.** Set it near the high percentiles of observed output length, not the cap, and alert on the rate of truncated finishes (`finish_reason` of `\"length\"` on OpenAI-style APIs, `stop_reason` of `\"max_tokens\"` on Anthropic's).","**Cache the stable prefix.** Put the system prompt and tool definitions first and byte-identical, then enable prompt caching. Monitor cached input tokens (for example `cache_read_input_tokens` in Anthropic usage) and time to first token.","**Measure the effective window on your data.** Plant known facts at the start, middle and end of realistic prompts at several lengths and score answers. Re-run when the model or prompt layout changes."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent loop appends a tool call and a tool result every step, so its\n  context only grows. Without a budget and a compaction policy, latency and cost climb each step and the\n  run eventually dies with a \"context length exceeded\" error, often mid-task.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The model's advertised window, the output cap (`max_tokens`),\n  `max_model_len` in vLLM when self-hosting, provider prompt caching, and the token usage fields every API\n  returns per call.\n- **What you control.** What goes into each step's prompt (instructions, tool definitions, retrieved\n  material, history, tool outputs), in what order, how large tool outputs are truncated or summarized,\n  and when old turns are compacted.\n- **What you can skip.** The details of RoPE scaling methods such as YaRN; remember that positions beyond\n  the trained length degrade quality and that the effective window is shorter than the nominal one.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Long-running coding and research agents.** Tool outputs accumulate fastest here, so clipping and\n  compaction decide whether a 50-step run finishes at all.\n- **Support agents with RAG and history.** A per-step budget splits the window between policy chunks,\n  conversation history and the answer, so neither crowds out the other.\n- **Agents with large tool catalogs.** Tool schemas are paid on every step, so shortlisting tools frees\n  room for the actual task.\n- **Choosing long context vs retrieval.** Teams test whether pasting a whole document beats retrieving\n  from it on accuracy, latency and cost before committing to either design.","mini_project":"**Add a context budget and compaction to a tool-calling agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** keep a 30-step agent run inside a fixed input budget without losing the task.\n- **Steps:** build a loop with a search tool that returns long raw text; log input tokens per step; run 30\n  steps and plot the growth; then add tool-result clipping and compaction at 70% of the budget with the\n  last few messages kept verbatim; rerun the same task.\n- **Done when:** you have two per-step token curves (linear vs plateau), the compacted run completes the\n  task, and a short note lists what the summary had to preserve for the agent to finish."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"The model remembers the earlier steps of my agent run.\"** It is stateless between calls; the client re-sends the whole transcript every step and the model re-reads it. Memory is what your application puts back into the prompt, paid in tokens each time.","**\"If it fits in 128k, the model will use all of it.\"** The effective window is task-dependent and usually shorter: accuracy is highest at the start and end and drops in the middle. A step-1 instruction can be present at step 30 and still be ignored.","**\"Only the input has to fit.\"** The rule is `input_tokens + max_output_tokens ≤ window`, and reasoning models spend hidden thinking tokens from the output side. A huge `max_tokens` also shrinks input room and can cut concurrency.","**\"Prompt caching gives me a bigger window.\"** It reuses the KV cache of a byte-identical prefix to cut cost and time to first token. The prefix still occupies the window on every step.","**\"Doubling the prompt doubles the cost.\"** Roughly true for short prompts, but attention overtakes the rest near 25k tokens at d = 4,096, and KV memory on Llama-3-8B reaches about 16 GiB at 128k, as much as the weights."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"New agent step\"] --> B[\"Count fixed parts with model tokenizer\"]\n    B --> C[\"Reserve max output tokens\"]\n    C --> D{\"Budget left for history?\"}\n    D -->|\"no\"| E[\"Trim chunks or reject\"]\n    D -->|\"yes\"| F[\"Admit history newest first\"]\n    F --> G{\"Old turns still matter?\"}\n    G -->|\"yes\"| H[\"Summarize or retrieve them\"]\n    G -->|\"no\"| I[\"Drop oldest turns\"]\n    H --> J[\"Send full prompt, model re-reads all\"]\n    I --> J","caption":"Each agent step rebuilds the whole prompt; fixed parts and the output reservation are paid first, and\nhistory and tool results get whatever is left."},"interview":[{"q":"What actually sets the context window of a deployed model?","a":"- **Training length:** the advertised window is where pre-training or RoPE-scaled continued training was\n  validated; beyond it positions are unseen and quality degrades.\n- **Memory:** the engine reserves KV cache for the longest allowed request, so an operator may configure a\n  smaller window than the model supports.\n- **Latency:** prefill grows faster than linearly with prompt length, which bounds what users will wait for."},{"q":"An agent's latency climbs every turn until it fails with a context error. How do you debug and fix it?","a":"- **Confirm the cause.** Log input tokens per turn; linear growth with tool-output size means the loop\n  appends raw tool results forever.\n- **Fix the loop.** Summarize or truncate large tool outputs, keep only the last few turns verbatim, and\n  store raw results outside the prompt for retrieval. Input tokens per turn should then plateau."},{"q":"Your agent follows an instruction early in a run but ignores it by step 25. Why, and what do you change?","a":"- **Likely cause:** the instruction is still in the prompt but now sits in the middle of a long\n  transcript, where models use information least reliably; or compaction dropped it.\n- **Fix:** keep durable instructions in the pinned system prompt, restate the current goal near the end\n  of each step's prompt, and check the compaction summary preserves constraints. Test by position."},{"q":"How much KV memory does a 128k-token request need on Llama-3-8B, and what does that imply?","a":"- **Per token:** 2 × 32 layers × 8 KV heads × 128 × 2 bytes = 128 KiB.\n- **Per request:** about 16 GiB, roughly the model's own fp16 weights, so a handful of such requests fill a\n  GPU that could serve many short chats. Route long-document traffic separately."},{"q":"What is the difference between the nominal and the effective window, and how would you measure it?","a":"- **Nominal** is what the API accepts; **effective** is the length over which the model reliably uses\n  information, which is usually shorter and weakest in the middle of the prompt.\n- **Measure** by planting known facts at different positions in realistic prompts of increasing length\n  and scoring answers on your own task, not by trusting a needle test from a spec sheet."},{"q":"Why can setting `max_tokens` very high hurt throughput even when answers are short?","a":"- **Some schedulers budget for the worst case.** They reserve KV memory for prompt plus maximum output, so\n  an inflated cap means fewer requests admitted at once.\n- **It also shrinks input room,** because input plus reserved output must fit the window. Size the cap\n  from the observed answer-length distribution."}],"tradeoffs":["**Bigger window vs retrieval.** A larger window lets you skip retrieval and paste everything in, but prefill latency and per-request cost grow with every token on every turn. Retrieval wins once the corpus no longer fits, changes too often to keep cached, or costs more to re-read than a retrieval miss costs.","**Full history vs summary.** Keeping full history maximizes fidelity; summarizing cuts tokens and latency at the price of lost detail. The flip happens when old turns stop being referenced.","**Compaction timing vs cache hits.** Compacting rewrites history, which invalidates the cached prefix from that point. Compact rarely and at a boundary rather than trimming a little every step.","**Output reservation vs input room.** A large output budget protects long answers but shrinks the space for input; size it to the real distribution of answer lengths rather than the maximum.","**Window vs concurrency.** Operators can shrink the engine's window to fit more concurrent users in GPU memory, trading per-request capability for throughput; raise it only when traffic actually uses it."],"pitfalls":["**Wrong tokenizer or missing overhead.** Counting with the wrong tokenizer or forgetting template and tool-definition overhead. Symptom: intermittent \"context length exceeded\" errors on requests that looked within budget. Fix: count the fully templated prompt with the deployed model's tokenizer.","**Assuming the model finds a fact anywhere.** In a 100k-token prompt, the symptom is answers that ignore material in the middle while the same material at the end works. Fix: put key material at the start or end, retrieve less but better, and measure by position.","**Letting an agent loop append tool outputs forever.** Latency climbs turn by turn, cost per turn grows linearly, and the run eventually fails at the boundary. Fix: compact old turns and large tool outputs.","**Truncating from the front.** Cutting the system prompt or a tool's schema changes behaviour abruptly mid-conversation with no error. Fix: pin the fixed parts and trim or summarize history instead.","**Setting `max_tokens` far above real answer lengths.** Engines whose schedulers budget KV memory against a request's worst-case length then admit fewer concurrent requests even though answers are short. Fix: size the cap from observed output-length percentiles.","**Forgetting hidden thinking tokens.** Reasoning models spend them from the same budget. Symptom: empty or cut-off answers with a \"length\" finish reason on hard prompts while easy ones work. Fix: reserve output room for thinking as well as the answer, and track truncated finishes per route."],"when":"- **There is no opting out;** every request has a window. The decisions are how much of it to spend on\n  instructions, tool definitions, examples, retrieved material, history, and on the answer.\n- **Measure** the token distribution of real agent steps, the rate of truncation events, and task accuracy\n  as a function of where key information sits in the prompt.\n- **Watch for the RoPE-scaling trap.** Some model cards note that statically enabling a context-extension\n  method for all requests can slightly degrade quality on short inputs, so enable it only on deployments\n  that actually receive long prompts.\n- **Prefer retrieval** when the corpus is large or changes, long context when a single document must be\n  reasoned over as a whole, and compaction for long agent runs.\n- **Day to day** the window appears as a budgeting function, a truncation policy and a cost line.\n- **Interviews:** expect why windows are finite, what the KV cache has to do with it, whether a bigger\n  window makes RAG unnecessary, and how you keep a long agent run inside it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the budgeting and truncation logic, counts tokens with the right tokenizer, and monitors truncation and context-exceeded errors as first-class metrics.","architect":"Sets the context policy per workload, chooses between long-context and retrieval designs, and ties the window size to GPU memory and concurrency targets.","fde":"Shows a customer why pasting their entire knowledge base into the prompt is slow and expensive, and measures effective context on their real documents."},"check":{"q":"A product manager argues that with a 200k-token window, the team can drop its RAG pipeline and\npaste the whole product manual into every request. Make the engineering case for and against.","a":"For: simpler system, no retrieval failures, and the model can cross-reference the whole manual.\nAgainst: every request pays prefill over the full manual, so time-to-first-token and cost per turn rise\nwith manual size rather than with the question; effective use of information in the middle of a very\nlong prompt is measurably weaker than at the edges, so accuracy can fall even as the window is nominally\nlarge; the KV cache for a 200k-token request is large enough to cut concurrency on a shared GPU or to\nhit vendor rate limits. Prompt caching softens the cost if the manual is a stable prefix, but not the\nattention-quality issue. The right test is accuracy, latency and cost on real questions with the manual\npasted versus retrieved, measured before the pipeline is deleted."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these is NOT a reason the context window is finite?","options":["KV-cache memory grows with the number of positions","Attention compute grows with sequence length","The tokenizer vocabulary has a fixed size","Positional encodings are only trained up to a certain length"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Vocabulary size bounds how many distinct tokens exist, not how many positions a request may contain. Memory, compute and positional training length are the real constraints."},{"q":"A chat assistant \"forgets\" instructions given early in a long session. What is the most likely mechanism?","options":["The model's weights were updated by the conversation","The application truncated or summarized early turns to fit the window, so the instructions are no longer in the prompt","The KV cache was evicted by the GPU","Temperature was too high"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The model has no state between calls; it only sees what the client re-sends. Once early turns are dropped to stay within the window, their content is gone for every later turn."},{"q":"What does prompt caching change about a long shared system prompt?","options":["It raises the model's maximum context length","It makes the model attend to the prompt more accurately","It reuses the prefix's KV cache across requests, cutting prefill cost and latency for that prefix","It compresses the prompt into fewer tokens"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Caching stores the computed keys and values for a byte-identical prefix so later requests skip that prefill work. The window and the model's use of the content are unchanged."}]},{"key":"model-internals/kv-cache","sources":["Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention"],"why":"- **Generation is a loop.** Feed the prompt, get a token, append it, feed everything again. Without a\n  cache, step *t* re-runs the full forward pass over all *t* tokens: 1 + 2 + … + n = O(n²)\n  token-forward-passes.\n- **That is very wasteful.** A 4,000-token answer costs eight million token-passes instead of four thousand.\n- **The recomputed work is identical.** For the newest token only the query **q** is new. The keys\n  **K** and values **V** of earlier positions depend only on earlier tokens and the weights. In a causal\n  decoder position *i* cannot see *j > i*, so they never change. The cache memoizes them per layer.\n- **\"Just recompute, GPUs are fast\" buys nothing.** The recomputed K and V are bit-for-bit what the\n  previous step produced, so the extra FLOPs add no quality or flexibility.\n- **What the cache does not remove.** Attention itself stays linear per step, because the new query reads\n  all *t* cached keys, so total attention work is still quadratic. The cache removes re-running the\n  projections and MLPs over old tokens, which is most of the FLOPs at ordinary lengths.","how":"### What is stored\n- Each layer projects a token's hidden state into a key and a value: `k = x·W_K`, `v = x·W_V`.\n- For position *i* these depend only on tokens ≤ *i*, so once computed they are stored and never\n  recomputed.\n- Each layer has its own W_K and W_V and its own hidden state, so there is no sharing across layers. The\n  cache is a 4-D tensor `[layer, position, kv_head, head_dim]` for K, and the same for V.\n\n### The two phases\n1. **Prefill.** The whole prompt runs in one batched forward pass. It is compute-bound (large matrix\n   multiplications) and fills the cache with K and V for every prompt position in every layer.\n2. **Decode.** One new token per step: compute **q, k, v** for that token, append its **k** and **v**,\n   then attend with `softmax(q·Kᵀ/√d)·V` over the cached K and V. One query row means the step is\n   dominated by *reading* the cache and weights, not by arithmetic.\n\n### Why decode is bandwidth-bound\n| Quantity (7B model, fp16, batch 1) | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Arithmetic per token | ~2 FLOPs per parameter ≈ 14 GFLOP |\n| Bytes read per token | every parameter once ≈ 14 GB |\n| Intensity | about one FLOP per byte |\n| A100-class GPU | ~300 TFLOP/s fp16, ~2 TB/s bandwidth, so ~150 FLOPs per byte available |\n| Latency floor | 14 GB ÷ 2 TB/s ≈ 7 ms per token |\n\n- At batch 1 the GPU uses well under 1% of its arithmetic (rough orders of magnitude, not a benchmark).\n- So tokens/second per request barely improves with a faster GPU if its bandwidth is similar.\n- Batching *B* requests reuses each weight read *B* times, so throughput rises almost linearly with batch\n  size. That holds until each sequence's own K and V reads, every step, dominate the bytes moved.\n- This bandwidth-bound regime is what batching and speculative decoding later exploit.\n\n### How big it is\nPer token, the cache holds one key and one value vector per layer per KV head:\n\n```\nbytes_per_token = 2 (K and V) × n_layers × n_kv_heads × head_dim × bytes_per_element\n```\n\n- Llama-2-7B-style model (32 layers, 32 heads, head_dim 128, fp16): 2 × 32 × 32 × 128 × 2 = 524,288 bytes\n  ≈ 0.5 MB per token.\n- A 4k-token sequence is ~2 GB; 32 concurrent such sequences need ~64 GB, the whole card.\n- This arithmetic is behind the sentence \"LLM serving is memory-bound\".\n\n### Shrinking it\nEach lever attacks one factor of the formula, and each has a cost:\n- **Fewer KV heads.** Multi-query attention shares one K/V head across all query heads. Grouped-query\n  attention keeps a few (8 instead of 32 in Llama-3-8B, cutting the cache 4×); this is why GQA exists.\n  MQA saves more but tends to cost more quality.\n- **Compress what is stored.** Multi-head latent attention (DeepSeek-V2) caches a low-rank latent per\n  token and rebuilds K and V from it, attacking `n_kv_heads × head_dim` directly.\n- **Fewer bytes per element.** FP8 or INT8 caches halve memory relative to fp16.\n- **Fewer positions.** Sliding-window attention caps how many past positions a layer keeps. Eviction\n  schemes (keep the first \"sink\" tokens plus a recent window, or drop low-attention entries) bound the\n  cache but are approximations that lose information exact attention would use.\n\n### Interaction with batching\n- Requests grow their caches at different rates and finish at different times, so naive contiguous\n  allocation fragments GPU memory badly.\n- PagedAttention (vLLM) stores the cache in fixed-size blocks behind a page table, like OS virtual memory.\n  That packs many more sequences per GPU and lets requests share prompt prefixes.","example":"### Decode loop with and without the cache\nThe cached version feeds only the new token each step and passes `past_key_values` back in.\n\n```python\nimport torch, time\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B\"          # any small causal LM\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.float16).cuda().eval()\nids = tok(\"The KV cache exists because\", return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids.cuda()\n\n@torch.no_grad()\ndef generate(use_cache, steps=64):\n    x, past = ids, None\n    out = []\n    for _ in range(steps):\n        if use_cache:\n            r = model(input_ids=x if past is None else x[:, -1:], past_key_values=past, use_cache=True)\n            past = r.past_key_values              # grows by one position per step\n        else:\n            r = model(input_ids=x, use_cache=False)  # re-processes the whole prefix every step\n        nxt = r.logits[:, -1].argmax(-1, keepdim=True)\n        x = torch.cat([x, nxt], dim=1); out.append(nxt.item())\n    return out\n\nfor flag in (False, True):\n    torch.cuda.synchronize(); t = time.time(); generate(flag); torch.cuda.synchronize()\n    print(f\"use_cache={flag}: {time.time()-t:.2f}s\")\n# Expect the cached loop to be several times faster, with the gap widening as steps grows.\n# Inspect size: layer-0 keys have shape [batch, n_kv_heads, seq_len, head_dim]. On recent Transformers\n# `past` is a DynamicCache (past.layers[0].keys); older versions return tuples (past[0][0]).\n```\n\n### Capacity calculator for a serving plan\nHow many concurrent chats fit after the weights are loaded? Plug in the model's config.\n\n```python\ndef kv_bytes_per_token(n_layers, n_kv_heads, head_dim, bytes_per_el=2):\n    return 2 * n_layers * n_kv_heads * head_dim * bytes_per_el\n\ndef max_concurrent(gpu_gb, weights_gb, avg_tokens, overhead=0.9, **cfg):\n    free = (gpu_gb - weights_gb) * overhead * 1e9      # keep ~10% for activations and fragmentation\n    return int(free // (kv_bytes_per_token(**cfg) * avg_tokens))\n\nmha = dict(n_layers=32, n_kv_heads=32, head_dim=128)   # Llama-2-7B style\ngqa = dict(n_layers=32, n_kv_heads=8, head_dim=128)    # Llama-3-8B style\nprint(max_concurrent(80, 14, 6000, **mha))   # 18 chats at 6k tokens\nprint(max_concurrent(80, 16, 6000, **gqa))   # 73 chats: 4x fewer KV heads, 4x the capacity\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: why latency collapsed after raising the context limit\nA team raised `max_model_len` from 8k to 32k on an 80 GB GPU running a 7B MHA model.\n\n| Step | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| Typical chat grows from 3k to 12k tokens | cache per chat goes from ~1.5 GB to ~6 GB |\n| Free memory after weights ≈ 60 GB | resident chats drop from ~40 to ~10 |\n| Requests over capacity are preempted | queueing time and re-prefill appear in TTFT |\n| Batch shrinks | weights are re-read for fewer tokens, so throughput per GPU falls |\n\nThe fix was not more compute: cap context per tier, move to a GQA model, and enable an FP8 cache.\n\n### Worked scenario: a 20-step research agent\nA coding agent starts with a 6k-token prefix (system prompt plus tool schemas) and appends ~1.5k tokens\nper step (tool call plus result).\n\n| Step | Context | Without prefix reuse | With an append-only transcript and prefix caching |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 6k | prefill 6k | prefill 6k (cache written) |\n| 10 | 19.5k | prefill 19.5k | prefill ~1.5k new tokens |\n| 20 | 34.5k | prefill 34.5k | prefill ~1.5k new tokens |\n| Total prefill over 20 steps | | ~405k tokens | ~34.5k tokens |\n\nThe cache also has to hold 34.5k tokens × ~0.5 MB ≈ 17 GB for one agent on a 7B MHA model, which is why\nlong-running agents need GQA models, FP8 caches or context compaction.","patterns":["**Keep agent transcripts append-only.** Put stable content first (system prompt, tool schemas), append tool calls and results, and never rewrite earlier turns mid-run. Monitor cached-token share per agent step; it should climb toward the prefix length on every step after the first.","**Compact on purpose, at a boundary.** When context nears the window, summarize old turns once into a new stable block and accept one cache miss, instead of trimming a little every step and missing the cache every time.","**Size admission from cache math, not weights.** Compute bytes-per-token from the model config, then set `max_model_len` and `max_num_seqs` (vLLM) so the expected mix fits with ~10% headroom. Monitor KV utilization and preemption counts.","**Enable prefix caching for shared prompts.** Keep system prompts and tool definitions byte-identical and first in the request, so engines like vLLM (`enable_prefix_caching`) and SGLang (RadixAttention) reuse their cache. Monitor prefix-cache hit rate and time to first token.","**Quantize the cache before buying GPUs.** Turn on an FP8 KV cache (for example `kv_cache_dtype=\"fp8\"` in vLLM), run your eval set, and accept it if quality holds. It roughly doubles resident sequences.","**Cap context per product tier.** Long-document features get a separate deployment or a retrieval step, so a few 100k-token requests cannot evict every short chat. Alert on context-length p95 per route.","**Prefer GQA models for high-concurrency serving.** When choosing between similar-quality models, fewer KV heads translate directly into more concurrent users per GPU."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent's context only grows: system prompt, tool definitions, then\n  every tool call and result appended step after step. The KV cache (and prefix caching on top of it)\n  decides whether step 20 costs as little as step 1 or re-processes the whole transcript.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Provider prompt caching for hosted models, `enable_prefix_caching`\n  in vLLM or RadixAttention in SGLang for self-hosted ones, and the concurrency limit of any GPU serving\n  your agents.\n- **What you control.** Keep the prefix stable (system prompt and tool schemas first, byte-identical),\n  append instead of editing history, and compact old turns deliberately, because any edit invalidates\n  the cache from that point on.\n- **What you can skip.** The CUDA kernels behind attention. Know the bytes-per-token formula and the\n  prefill/decode split; that is enough to size and debug agent serving.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Long-running coding and research agents.** Prefix caching keeps the cost of step 30 close to step 1\n  when the transcript is append-only.\n- **Multi-tenant agent platforms.** KV-cache math sets how many concurrent agent sessions one GPU can host\n  and when to add replicas.\n- **Agents over large documents.** Deciding between a 100k-token context and a retrieval step is a KV-cache\n  memory decision as much as a quality one.\n- **Cost control on hosted APIs.** Provider prompt caching bills cached input tokens at a fraction of the\n  normal rate, so a stable prefix directly cuts agent cost.","mini_project":"**Measure prefix caching in an agent loop (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show how much a stable prefix saves across a 10-step tool-calling loop.\n- **Steps:** build a tiny agent with two fake tools; run it twice, once with a stable system prompt and tool\n  list, once with a timestamp injected at the top of the prompt; log input, cached-input and output tokens\n  per step from the provider usage fields (or vLLM metrics when self-hosting).\n- **Setup note:** make the stable prefix longer than the provider's minimum cacheable length (often around\n  1,024 tokens) and, on APIs that need it, mark the cache breakpoint explicitly, or nothing will be cached\n  in either run.\n- **Done when:** you have a per-step table and a chart showing cached tokens climbing in the stable run and\n  staying near zero in the timestamped run, plus a one-paragraph explanation of why."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"The KV cache makes attention linear.\"** It removes re-running projections and MLPs over old tokens, but each new query still reads all cached keys, so total attention work stays quadratic.","**\"A faster GPU makes decode proportionally faster.\"** At small batch sizes decode is bandwidth-bound: each step reads every weight for about one FLOP per byte, so tokens per second tracks memory bandwidth far more than peak FLOPs.","**\"Prefix caching works for any similar prompt.\"** Reuse needs a byte-identical prefix; a timestamp or reordered tool list at the top means a miss on every step.","**\"Weights are what limit concurrency.\"** Past short contexts, the cache usually is: about 0.5 MB per token on a 7B MHA model is ~2 GB per 4k-token chat, so about 32 such chats fill an 80 GB card."]},"exercise":"**Goal:** find the real concurrency limit for an agent workload on a model you can run or read the\nconfig of. No solution is given.\n- **Inputs:** the model config (layers, KV heads, head dim, dtype) and your workload: prefix length,\n  tokens per step, steps per run.\n- **Deliver:** bytes per token, peak tokens per run, and concurrent runs that fit with 10% headroom,\n  with and without an FP8 cache.\n- **Acceptance:** one row reproduced by hand; if you can run vLLM, observed KV utilization at your\n  predicted concurrency lands within about 15% and you can explain the gap.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a research agent moves to a 70B GQA model (80 layers, 8 KV heads, head dim 128, fp16) on\ntwo 80 GB GPUs. Runs average 40k tokens and product wants 50 concurrent runs. Possible? What changes?\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- computes about 0.3 MB per token, about 13 GB per run and about 650 GB for 50 runs;\n- notes fp16 weights alone take about 140 GB, so two GPUs cannot even hold them;\n- proposes more GPUs, FP8 weights and cache, compaction below 40k, or fewer runs, and what to measure.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    P[\"Prompt tokens\"] --> PF[\"Prefill: one batched pass\"]\n    PF --> C[(\"KV cache per layer\")]\n    C --> D[\"Decode step t\"]\n    D -->|\"q, k, v for 1 token\"| A[\"Attend: q vs all cached K, V\"]\n    A --> T[\"Next token\"]\n    D -->|\"append k, v\"| C\n    T -->|\"until stop\"| D\n    C -.->|\"~0.5 MB per token\"| M[\"GPU memory limit\"]","caption":"Prefill fills the cache once; each decode step adds one K/V entry and reads all of them."},"interview":[{"q":"Why does decode get faster with a KV cache but not much faster on a GPU with more FLOPs?","a":"- **The cache removes recomputation.** Each past token's K and V are computed once, so a decode step only\n  projects the newest token.\n- **Decode is then bandwidth-bound.** Each step reads all weights and the whole cache for very little\n  arithmetic, so tokens per second tracks memory bandwidth, not peak FLOPs."},{"q":"Walk me through sizing the KV cache for a model you are about to deploy.","a":"- **Formula:** 2 × layers × KV heads × head dim × bytes per element, per token.\n- **Multiply** by expected tokens per request and by concurrent requests.\n- **Compare** with GPU memory left after weights, keeping about 10% headroom; that gives the concurrency\n  limit to configure in the engine."},{"q":"What would you do if long-context traffic started causing preemptions?","a":"- **Diagnose first:** KV utilization near 100% with rising preemption or swap counts means over-admission.\n- **Then pick levers:** cap context per route, lower max concurrent sequences, enable an FP8 cache after an\n  eval, move long-document work to a retrieval step or separate deployment, or choose a GQA model."},{"q":"When is it safe to reuse a cached prefix across requests?","a":"- **Only for byte-identical prefixes.** Keys carry their position and depend on every earlier token.\n- **So** put stable content (system prompt, tool definitions) first and variable content last, and never\n  edit earlier conversation turns if you want cache hits."},{"q":"Your agent's latency grows with every step even though each tool result is small. What do you check?","a":"- **Is the prefix stable?** Timestamps, random IDs or reordered tool schemas at the top of the prompt\n  break prefix reuse, so every step re-prefills the whole transcript.\n- **Is history edited?** Rewriting or trimming earlier turns invalidates the cache from that position on.\n- **Measure** cached versus uncached input tokens per step from the provider usage fields or engine\n  metrics; the uncached count should stay near the size of the newest step."}],"tradeoffs":["**Compute vs memory.** Each token is projected once instead of once per later step, but memory grows linearly with sequence length and concurrent requests. The binding constraint moves from FLOPs to VRAM and bandwidth.","**Swap vs recompute on preemption.** When memory runs out, the engine swaps a request's cache to CPU or drops it and re-runs prefill later. Recompute wins for short sequences (prefill is fast, compute idle); swapping wins for long ones where re-prefill would cost seconds.","**Long context vs cache size.** A 128k-token window is usable only because of GQA, quantized caches and paging, each costing some quality or engineering complexity.","**Prefix caching vs flexibility.** It saves prefill for shared system prompts, but only when prefixes are byte-identical and the cache can stay resident between calls.","**Cache quantization vs accuracy.** 8-bit (or 4-bit) halves memory and raises batch size, at a measurable but usually small accuracy cost that must be evaluated per model and task."],"pitfalls":["**Sizing capacity from weights alone.** Symptom: out-of-memory errors or heavy preemption once traffic has long contexts. Diagnose: compare engine KV utilization and preemption counts with the p95 context length. Fix: add the cache formula to the capacity model.","**Expecting a faster GPU to speed decode proportionally.** Decode is bandwidth-bound, so tokens/second tracks memory bandwidth more than peak FLOPs. Diagnose: GPU compute utilization stays low while time per output token is flat. Fix: compare GPUs on bandwidth.","**Reusing a cache after editing history.** Cached keys carry their position (RoPE is applied to them) and every entry depends on all earlier content, so any change before position *i* invalidates everything from *i* onward. Symptom: garbled output or no cache hits after a history edit. Diagnose: diff the prompt prefix between steps and find the first changed token. Fix: truncate at that position and re-prefill, or rebuild.","**Extrapolating single-request benchmarks.** Every concurrent request's cache competes for the same memory bandwidth. Diagnose: plot time per output token against concurrency. Fix: benchmark at the concurrency you will run."],"when":"Every production inference engine uses a KV cache; your choices are about living within its budget.\n- **Decide:** maximum context length, batch size and concurrency limits, prefix caching for shared system\n  prompts, cache quantization, and GQA vs full multi-head attention for your hardware.\n- **Reach for the formula when:** someone asks \"how many concurrent users can this GPU serve\" or \"why did\n  latency collapse when we raised the context limit\".\n- **Measure:** KV-cache utilization, preemption or swap counts, and time per output token as concurrency\n  rises. A cache pinned near 100% with climbing preemptions means you are over-admitting, not\n  under-provisioned on compute.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets `max_model_len`, batch limits and prefix-caching flags in vLLM or SGLang, and debugs OOMs by computing cache size rather than guessing.","architect":"Owns the capacity model: context window policy, GPU selection and concurrency targets all follow from cache arithmetic and the prefill/decode cost split.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their 100-page-document use case needs a different GPU tier or a retrieval step, using the per-token memory number as the argument."},"check":{"q":"A team serves a 32-layer, 32-head, head-dim-128 model in fp16 on an 80 GB GPU; weights take 14 GB.\nThey want 64 concurrent chats averaging 6k tokens. Does it fit, and what are their options if not?","a":"Cache per token is 2 × 32 × 32 × 128 × 2 bytes ≈ 0.5 MB. Per chat at 6k tokens that is ~3 GB; 64\nchats need ~192 GB, far beyond the ~66 GB left after weights. Options, in rough order of cost: cap\nconcurrency or context (about 20 chats fit); switch to a GQA model with 8 KV heads, cutting the cache 4×\nso ~80 chats fit; quantize the cache to 8-bit for another 2×; use paged attention so memory is not lost to\nfragmentation; add prefix caching if chats share a long system prompt; or shard across more GPUs. A strong\nanswer also notes decode is bandwidth-bound, so higher concurrency raises per-request latency."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the KV cache store keys and values but not queries?","options":["Queries are too large to store efficiently","Only the newest token's query is needed at each step, while past keys and values are reused by every later token","Queries are recomputed because they change after each layer's normalization","Storing queries would break causal masking"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Attention for the new token needs its own query against all previous keys and values. Earlier queries were only needed when those tokens were generated, so caching them would be wasted memory."},{"q":"Roughly how does KV-cache memory scale with sequence length and batch size?","options":["Constant in sequence length, linear in batch size","Quadratic in sequence length, constant in batch size","Linear in sequence length and linear in batch size","Logarithmic in both, because of paging"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each token in each sequence adds one K and one V vector per layer, so memory is proportional to tokens × concurrent sequences. Paging reduces waste from fragmentation but not the total needed."},{"q":"Which change reduces KV-cache size without changing sequence length or precision?","options":["Increasing the number of transformer layers","Switching from RoPE to learned positional embeddings","Using grouped-query attention with fewer KV heads","Enabling speculative decoding"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cache size is proportional to the number of KV heads; GQA shares each K/V head across several query heads, so Llama-3-8B's 8 KV heads need a quarter of the cache of a 32-head layout. Speculative decoding changes compute per step, not cache per token."}]},{"key":"model-internals/logits","sources":["Goodfellow, Bengio & Courville, 2016 — Deep Learning","nostalgebraist, 2020 — interpreting GPT: the logit lens"],"why":"- **A language model is a classifier over the vocabulary.** At every position it needs a score per class.\n  The last layer is linear, and a linear map produces arbitrary reals, not numbers that are non-negative\n  and sum to one.\n- **The naive fixes train badly.** Clamping and rescaling kills gradients for every negative score;\n  squared error against one-hot targets gives almost no gradient to a confidently wrong prediction.\n- **So the jobs are separated.** The linear layer emits an unconstrained score (the logit), softmax\n  normalizes, and cross-entropy is computed straight from the scores in a fused, numerically stable form.\n  For target token *y*, `∂L/∂z = softmax(z) − onehot(y)`: each wrong token is pushed down by the\n  probability it stole and the right one up by its shortfall, with no saturation.\n- **Scores make inference controllable.** Probabilities are locked to the simplex; scores are not. Add a\n  constant to a token's logit and its probability rises smoothly; set it to negative infinity and the\n  token can never be sampled; divide all logits by a temperature to sharpen or flatten the distribution.\n  Grammar-constrained decoding, JSON mode, stop sequences, repetition penalties and the API's `logit_bias`\n  parameter all edit logits between the final layer and the sampler.\n- **The name is older than deep learning.** A logit is a log-odds; in the multi-class case the logits are\n  log-probabilities up to an additive constant.","how":"### Where they come from\n1. **Unembed.** After the last transformer block, each position's hidden state `h` (a `d_model` vector)\n   is multiplied by the unembedding matrix `W_U` of shape `[V, d_model]`: `z = W_U · h`, a vector of *V*\n   scores.\n2. **Training computes every position,** because the loss is computed everywhere.\n3. **Decoding needs only the last position,** since the next token depends on nothing else, and engines\n   compute just that row.\n4. **Shapes.** An inference call returns `[batch, seq_len, vocab]`; you sample from `logits[:, -1, :]`.\n\n### Relationship to probabilities\n`p_i = exp(z_i) / Σ_j exp(z_j)`, so `log p_i = z_i − logsumexp(z)`. Two consequences are used daily:\n- **Shift invariance.** Adding the same constant to every logit leaves the probabilities unchanged, so a\n  logit's absolute value means nothing; only differences matter. Implementations subtract the maximum\n  before exponentiating to avoid overflow.\n- **Logprobs.** What an API returns under `logprobs` is `log_softmax(z)` for the chosen token and a few\n  alternatives. A logprob of −0.05 means about 95% on that token; −2.3 means about 10%. Summing per-token\n  logprobs gives the sequence log-likelihood; exponentiating the negative average gives perplexity.\n\n### Editing logits\nThe sampler sees logits, not text, so steering an agent's output is arithmetic:\n\n| Edit | Operation | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Temperature** | divide the whole vector by *T* before softmax | sharpens (T < 1) or flattens (T > 1) |\n| **Logit bias** | add a per-token offset *b* | multiplies that token's odds against every other by `e^b` |\n| **Masking** | set disallowed tokens to −∞ | exactly zero probability |\n| **Repetition / frequency penalties** | lower logits of tokens already in the context | less repetition |\n\n- **Why bias multiplies odds.** Because `p_i / p_j = exp(z_i − z_j)`, a bias of *b* scales the odds by\n  `e^b`: +1 is about 2.7×, +5 about 150×.\n- **Worked numbers.** Logits `[2.0, 1.0, 0.1]` give probabilities `[0.66, 0.24, 0.10]`; a +1 bias on the\n  third token gives `[0.56, 0.21, 0.23]`.\n- **API range.** OpenAI's API accepts values from −100 to 100; at −100 the factor `e^−100` effectively\n  bans a token, and a large positive value effectively forces it.\n- **Constrained decoding** builds the mask each step from a grammar or JSON schema, which is why structured\n  output, and therefore a parseable tool call, can be guaranteed rather than merely requested.\n- **Order matters.** The edits are not commutative; engines apply them in a fixed pipeline, and the\n  `sampling-strategies` topic covers the consequences.\n\n### Background: numerical care and interpretability\n- **Softmax and cross-entropy in fp32.** Logits come out in the model's compute dtype, but `exp` of a\n  half-precision score overflows easily and small probability differences vanish in 16-bit.\n- **Some architectures bound the logits.** Gemma 2 applies a `tanh` soft cap to its final logits to keep\n  training stable.\n- **The logit lens.** Since `W_U` can be applied to *any* hidden state, applying it (after the final\n  normalization layer) to intermediate layers shows what the model would predict if it stopped early. It\n  is a standard interpretability tool and a reminder that logits are just a dot product between a\n  residual-stream vector and each token's output embedding.","example":"### Reading and editing GPT-2's next-token logits\nThe script pulls the next-token logits, shows their range and shape, converts them to logprobs the way an\nAPI does, demonstrates shift invariance, then does the two edits every serving stack performs: banning a\ntoken and biasing another.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\n\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\")\nm = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\").eval()\nids = tok(\"The capital of France is\", return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    z = m(ids).logits[0, -1]                 # [50257]: one raw score per vocabulary entry\nprint(z.shape, round(z.min().item(), 1), round(z.max().item(), 1))   # unbounded reals\n\nlogp = torch.log_softmax(z.float(), -1)      # what APIs return as \"logprobs\"\ntop = torch.topk(logp, 5)\nfor lp, i in zip(top.values, top.indices):\n    print(f\"{tok.decode(i)!r:12} logit={z[i]:7.2f} logprob={lp:6.2f} p={lp.exp():.3f}\")\n\n# Shift invariance: a constant added to every logit changes nothing after softmax.\nassert torch.allclose(torch.softmax(z + 123.0, -1), torch.softmax(z, -1), atol=1e-6)\n\n# Steering happens here, before the sampler ever sees a probability.\nz2 = z.clone()\nz2[top.indices[0]] = float(\"-inf\")           # ban the top token: probability exactly 0\nz2[tok.encode(\" Berlin\")[0]] += 10.0         # logit bias: push an alternative up\nprint(\"after edits:\", repr(tok.decode(torch.softmax(z2, -1).argmax())))\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: a confidence-gated tool router\nA routing step must pick one of three tools. Masking the logits to the label tokens guarantees a valid\nchoice; the renormalized probabilities give a score, and low-confidence steps escalate to a stronger model.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\n\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\")              # stand-in for your router model\nm = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\").eval()\nTOOLS = [\" search\", \" calculator\", \" calendar\"]          # leading space: GPT-2 word tokens\nlabel_ids = [tok.encode(t)[0] for t in TOOLS]            # derive ids from the deployed tokenizer\n\ndef route(user_msg, threshold=0.6):\n    prompt = f\"User: {user_msg}\\nBest tool:\"\n    ids = tok(prompt, return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids\n    with torch.no_grad():\n        z = m(ids).logits[0, -1].float()\n    mask = torch.full_like(z, float(\"-inf\"))\n    mask[label_ids] = 0.0\n    p = torch.softmax(z + mask, -1)[label_ids]           # only the three tools can win\n    best = int(p.argmax())\n    return TOOLS[best].strip(), round(p[best].item(), 2), p[best].item() < threshold\n\nprint(route(\"What is 17% of 2,340?\"))   # (tool, confidence, escalate?)\n```\n\nThe threshold of 0.6 is illustrative: pick it from a labelled set of real routing decisions, because raw\nprobabilities from an untuned model are not calibrated. Each label here is one token; multi-token labels\nneed their full sequence scored.\n\n### What a logit bias does, in numbers\nPure Python, no model: apply biases to the third of three logits and watch the odds move by `e^b`.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef softmax(z):\n    m = max(z)                                  # subtract the max: shift invariance, no overflow\n    e = [math.exp(x - m) for x in z]\n    return [x / sum(e) for x in e]\n\nz = [2.0, 1.0, 0.1]\nprint(\"base\", [round(p, 3) for p in softmax(z)])\nfor b in (1.0, 5.0, -100.0):\n    p = softmax(z[:2] + [z[2] + b])\n    print(f\"bias {b:+6.1f}\", [round(x, 3) for x in p])\n# base          [0.659, 0.242, 0.099]\n# bias   +1.0   [0.564, 0.207, 0.229]   odds of token 3 up ~2.7x\n# bias   +5.0   [0.042, 0.016, 0.942]   odds up ~148x: effectively forced\n# bias -100.0   [0.731, 0.269, 0.0]     effectively banned\n```\n\n| Bias on token 3 | p(token 3) | Use it for in an agent |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 0.099 | baseline |\n| +1 | 0.229 | a gentle nudge toward a preferred word |\n| +5 | 0.942 | near-forcing one option |\n| −100 | ~0 | banning a token (still finite, unlike a −∞ mask) |","patterns":["**Enforce tool-call structure with constrained decoding.** When output must parse, use the provider's or engine's structured-output feature with a JSON schema (for example `response_format` with a JSON schema on OpenAI's API, or the guided or structured decoding option in vLLM). Monitor parse failures, which should be zero, and task accuracy, which can drop.","**Classify and route over a fixed label set by masking.** Restrict sampling to the label tokens with a logits processor (a `LogitsProcessor` in Hugging Face Transformers), then read the label logprobs as a score. Monitor accuracy and calibration on a labelled set, not just format compliance.","**Gate agent steps on logprob confidence, after calibration.** Request `logprobs`, compute the minimum or mean token logprob over the answer span or tool arguments, and send low-confidence steps to review, retrieval or a stronger model. Pick the threshold on labelled data and monitor the filter's precision and recall.","**Ban a word by banning all its token ids.** Tokenize every variant (leading space, capitalized, plural) with the deployed tokenizer and set each id's `logit_bias` to −100, or mask it. Monitor leakage with an output scan for the word.","**Keep the loss math in fp32 and off the full tensor.** When fine-tuning on agent traces, cast with `logits.float()` before `log_softmax`, and compute cross-entropy in chunks of positions so the full `[seq_len, vocab]` tensor is never materialized. Monitor NaN losses and peak memory."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent breaks when a tool call does not parse or names a tool that does\n  not exist. Constrained decoding fixes that by masking illegal tokens in the logits each step, which is\n  why structured output and strict tool calling can be guaranteed rather than merely requested.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Structured-output and JSON-schema options on hosted APIs, guided or\n  structured decoding in vLLM, the `logit_bias` and `logprobs` request parameters, and a `LogitsProcessor`\n  in Hugging Face Transformers when self-hosting. Not every hosted API exposes `logprobs` or\n  `logit_bias` (Anthropic's Messages API offers neither), so check before designing a gate around them.\n- **What you control.** Whether tool calls are schema-enforced or validated after the fact, whether\n  routing and classification steps are restricted to a fixed label set, and whether you read logprobs\n  to gate low-confidence steps.\n- **What you can skip.** The logit lens and training-time memory tricks, unless you fine-tune; know shift\n  invariance, the odds rule for bias, and that logprobs need calibration.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Reliable tool calling.** Schema-constrained decoding guarantees the agent's tool calls parse, so the\n  loop never stalls on malformed JSON.\n- **Routers and classifiers inside an agent.** Intent detection, tool selection and \"is this safe to\n  execute\" checks restrict output to a label set and read a score from the logprobs.\n- **Confidence-based escalation.** Low-logprob steps are sent to a stronger model, a retrieval step or a\n  human reviewer instead of being executed.\n- **Output guardrails.** Banned terms, competitor names or unsafe commands are blocked by biasing or\n  masking every token variant.","mini_project":"**Build a logprob-gated tool router (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** route user requests to one of three tools and escalate when the router is unsure.\n- **Steps:** write 40 labelled requests across three tools; implement the masked router from the example\n  (or request `logprobs` from a hosted model on a constrained label); log the chosen tool and its\n  probability; sweep the escalation threshold from 0.4 to 0.9.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of accuracy on non-escalated requests and escalation rate per threshold,\n  and you can justify one threshold for production."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"A logit of 12 means the model is more confident than a logit of 8 on another prompt.\"** Adding a constant to every logit changes nothing after softmax, so absolute values carry no meaning. Compare logprobs, or differences within one vector.","**\"A logit bias of +5 adds a fixed amount of probability.\"** It multiplies the token's odds against every other token by `e^b`, about 148× for +5. The resulting probability depends on the rest of the vector.","**\"Banning a word means biasing its token id.\"** A word has variants (leading space, capitals, plurals) and often several pieces, so biasing one id lets it leak. Also, −100 is finite; only a −∞ mask gives exactly zero probability.","**\"Constrained decoding makes tool calls correct.\"** It guarantees the call parses against the schema, not that the arguments are right; masking the token the model wanted can produce valid JSON with a wrong value. Measure task accuracy alongside parse failures.","**\"A high logprob means the step is right.\"** Instruction-tuned models are often confidently wrong, so raw logprobs are an uncalibrated signal. Set any gating threshold on labelled agent traces."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    H[\"Final hidden state h, last position\"] --> U[\"z = W_U times h\"]\n    U --> Z[\"Logits, one per vocab entry\"]\n    Z --> B[\"Add logit bias, penalties\"]\n    B --> M[\"Mask disallowed to minus infinity\"]\n    M --> T[\"Divide by temperature\"]\n    T --> S[\"Softmax in fp32\"]\n    S --> SM[\"Sampler picks token\"]\n    Z -.->|\"log_softmax\"| L[\"Logprobs, perplexity\"]\n    G[\"Tool schema or JSON grammar\"] -->|\"mask per step\"| M","caption":"Every control knob sits between the unembedding and the sampler; a tool schema becomes a per-step mask,\nand logprobs are read off the same vector. The order shown (penalties and bias, then masks, then\ntemperature) is a common one; engines differ."},"interview":[{"q":"What are logits, and why does a model output them rather than probabilities?","a":"- **Definition:** one unconstrained real score per vocabulary entry, `z = W_U · h`, which softmax turns into\n  probabilities.\n- **Why:** a linear layer cannot produce a simplex, and computing cross-entropy from scores gives the\n  clean gradient `softmax(z) − onehot(y)`, which never saturates even when the model is badly wrong."},{"q":"How does constrained decoding guarantee a parseable tool call when prompting cannot?","a":"- **It edits logits each step.** A grammar or schema state machine computes which tokens are legal next and\n  sets every other logit to −∞, so invalid tokens have exactly zero probability.\n- **The cost:** if the model wanted an illegal token, it is pushed into a lower-probability continuation,\n  so measure argument accuracy and task success, not just parse rate."},{"q":"Your agent's router sometimes picks the wrong tool. How would you use logprobs to reduce the damage?","a":"- **Score the choice.** Restrict the routing step to the tool labels and read their logprobs, or request\n  `logprobs` from the API on the tool-name tokens.\n- **Gate on it.** Below a threshold chosen on labelled routing data, escalate to a stronger model or ask a\n  clarifying question. Track precision and recall of the gate, since raw logprobs are not calibrated."},{"q":"You set a logit bias of −100 on a competitor's name and it still appears in outputs. Why?","a":"- **Tokenization.** The name probably splits into several tokens, or appears with a leading space or\n  different capitalization, each a different id you did not bias.\n- **Fix:** enumerate the variants with the deployed tokenizer, bias or mask every id, and scan outputs to\n  confirm. Ids copied from another model's tokenizer point at unrelated tokens."},{"q":"A training run OOMs at the LM head after moving to a 128k vocabulary. What is happening?","a":"- **The logits tensor is huge.** One 8,192-token sequence × 128,256 entries is about 1.05 billion logits,\n  ~4.2 GB in fp32, before gradients and batch size.\n- **Fix:** chunked or fused cross-entropy that never materializes the full tensor; decoding is unaffected\n  because only the last row is computed."},{"q":"Would you use token logprobs to detect hallucinations?","a":"- **Only as a calibrated signal.** Instruction-tuned models are often confidently wrong, so a high top-1\n  logprob does not imply correctness.\n- **If you do,** pick the aggregation (minimum or mean over the answer span) and threshold on labelled\n  data, measure precision and recall, and combine it with retrieval grounding checks."}],"tradeoffs":["**Logprobs vs exposure.** Returning logprobs gives callers calibration signals and cheap confidence filters, but exposes information about the model and adds payload, which is why vendors cap how many alternatives you get.","**Logit bias vs fine-tune.** Bias is instant and needs no retraining; a fine-tune changes behaviour more robustly. Bias wins for a handful of tokens and loses when the desired change is semantic rather than lexical.","**Hard masking vs content quality.** Masking guarantees format compliance with zero probability leakage, but a mask that excludes the token the model wanted can push it into low-probability continuations and hurt content, for example valid JSON with a wrong argument value.","**Bigger vocabulary vs logits tensor size.** Finer-grained tokens, larger tensor: one 8,192-token training sequence with a 128,256-entry vocabulary produces about 1.05 billion logits, ~4.2 GB in fp32. So chunked or fused cross-entropy kernels that never materialize the full tensor matter as vocabularies and sequences grow. At decode time only the last row is computed, so the cost vanishes there."],"pitfalls":["**Reading logits as probabilities.** Or comparing absolute values across prompts. Symptom: \"confidence\" numbers that make no sense, since only differences within one vector are meaningful. Fix: convert with `log_softmax` and compare logprobs.","**Softmax or cross-entropy in fp16.** Symptom: NaN losses or probabilities that collapse to exactly 0 and 1, especially for long sequences. Fix: cast to fp32 first.","**Biasing only the first piece of a multi-token word.** Symptom: the model starts the word and then veers off. Fix: tokenize the full word and its variants, and bias or mask every piece.","**Using top-1 logprob as a hallucination detector without calibration.** Instruction-tuned models are often confidently wrong, so high probability does not imply correctness. Fix: set thresholds on labelled data and pair them with grounding checks.","**Copying token ids across tokenizers.** The token you bias must exist in the vocabulary of *this* tokenizer; an id copied from another model points at an unrelated token. Fix: derive ids at runtime from the deployed tokenizer, which matters when an agent swaps router models."],"when":"- **Use when** you need control or measurement prompting cannot give: forcing a routing step into a fixed\n  label set, guaranteeing tool-call JSON, banning a competitor's name, computing perplexity on an\n  evaluation set, or scoring candidate answers by likelihood.\n- **Avoid** logit edits to fix what is really a prompt or data problem, and do not over-read logprobs as\n  truth signals without a calibration study.\n- **Measure** the effect of any edit on task accuracy, not just on format compliance.\n- **Interviews:** explain the path from hidden state to logits to softmax to sampler, why shift invariance\n  makes masking with −∞ clean, and how constrained decoding plugs in to make tool calls reliable.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Uses `logit_bias`, `logprobs` and logits processors in generation code, and knows to compute scores in fp32 and only for the last position.","architect":"Decides whether structured output is enforced at the logit level or validated after the fact, and whether logprobs are exposed to downstream services.","fde":"Uses per-token logprobs to show a customer where the model was unsure in a bad answer, and bans or biases tokens to meet a hard output constraint on site."},"check":{"q":"You need a model to answer only \"yes\" or \"no\". Compare doing it by prompt, by logit bias and by\nhard masking, including what each guarantees and what can go wrong.","a":"A prompt asks politely; most of the time it works, but nothing prevents \"Yes, because...\" or an\nempty answer, so you need parsing and retries. Logit bias adds a large positive offset to the two tokens\nand a negative one to others; it strongly favours them but is probabilistic, and you must bias the exact\ntoken ids of this tokenizer, including leading-space variants. Hard masking sets every other logit to −∞\nso only the two tokens have non-zero probability; the output is guaranteed, and you can read the two\nlogprobs as a calibrated-ish score. The risk with masking is that the model's preferred continuation was\nsomething else, so the forced choice can be lower quality than a free answer followed by classification.\nAny of the three should be evaluated on labelled data rather than trusted on format alone."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does adding the same constant to every logit leave the sampled distribution unchanged?","options":["Because logits are clipped to a fixed range before sampling","Because softmax divides each exponentiated logit by their sum, and the common factor cancels","Because the model is trained with weight decay","Because the tokenizer normalizes scores"],"answer":1,"explanation":"exp(z + c) = exp(c)·exp(z), so the constant multiplies numerator and denominator alike. Only differences between logits carry information, which is why absolute logit values are meaningless."},{"q":"What does setting a token's logit to negative infinity achieve that a large negative bias does not?","options":["It removes the token from the vocabulary permanently","It makes generation faster","Its probability becomes exactly zero, so it can never be sampled regardless of temperature","It forces the model to pick the next-highest token deterministically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A finite bias leaves a tiny but non-zero probability that temperature or long sampling runs can hit; −∞ becomes exactly 0 after softmax, which is what constrained decoding relies on."},{"q":"An API returns a logprob of −0.69 for the chosen token. Roughly what probability did the model assign?","options":["About 69%","About 7%","About 50%","About 99%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Probability is exp(logprob); exp(−0.69) ≈ 0.50. Logprobs are natural logs of probabilities, not percentages, and −0.69 is ln(0.5)."}]},{"key":"model-internals/softmax","sources":["Bridle, 1990 — Probabilistic Interpretation of Feedforward Classification Network Outputs","Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Milakov & Gimelshein, 2018 — Online normalizer calculation for softmax","Xiao et al., 2023 — Efficient Streaming Language Models with Attention Sinks"],"why":"- **Argmax cannot be learned through.** It is piecewise constant: a tiny change in scores does nothing or\n  flips the answer, so the gradient is zero almost everywhere and nothing reaches the scores not chosen.\n- **Dividing by the sum fails too.** Scores can be negative, the result is not a distribution, and the map\n  does nothing to sharpen or soften preferences.\n- **Exponentiating first fixes both.** `exp` is positive, so normalized values are a valid distribution\n  for any real input; smooth, so every score gets a gradient; monotone, so ranking is preserved. It is the\n  \"soft\" argmax: a growing gap approaches one-hot, converging scores approach uniform.\n- **The training signal is clean.** Paired with cross-entropy, the gradient with respect to the logits is\n  simply `p − y`, prediction minus target.\n- **It has a principled reading.** Among all distributions with a given expected score it has maximum\n  entropy, the same form as the Boltzmann distribution in physics, which is where \"temperature\" comes from.","how":"### What the agent builder uses\n- **Temperature is the knob you touch.** Dividing logits by `T < 1` widens gaps and sharpens the\n  distribution; `T > 1` flattens it. As `T → 0` softmax becomes argmax (greedy); as `T → ∞` it\n  becomes uniform. That is why tool-calling and extraction steps usually run at low temperature.\n- **Probabilities never reach zero.** Only a `−∞` logit gives an exact zero, so unlikely tokens are always\n  possible at temperature above zero. Over thousands of agent steps, a rare bad token in a tool argument\n  will eventually be sampled unless you truncate (top-k, top-p) or constrain.\n- **Probabilities are not correctness.** A routing step that reports 0.95 may be right far less often than\n  95% of the time. Fit a temperature on labelled agent decisions before you threshold on it.\n- **Sums to one forces attention sinks.** Models park unused attention on the first tokens, so KV eviction\n  schemes for long agent sessions must keep them.\n\n### Background: the function and its properties\nFor scores `z` and temperature `T > 0`:\n\n```\np_i = exp(z_i / T) / Σ_j exp(z_j / T)\nlog p_i = z_i / T − logsumexp(z / T)\n```\n\n- **Shift invariance.** `softmax(z + c) = softmax(z)` for any constant *c*, because `exp(c)` factors out\n  of numerator and denominator. Implementations subtract `max(z)` before exponentiating *because* the\n  result is identical and the largest exponent becomes `exp(0) = 1`, so nothing overflows.\n- **Strictly positive.** Every output is greater than zero for finite inputs. Only `−∞` produces an exact\n  zero, which is how masks and token bans work.\n- **Sums to one.** Mass removed from one entry must go somewhere. This is a constraint, not a feature, and\n  it has consequences in attention (below).\n- **Gradient.** `∂p_i/∂z_j = p_i(δ_ij − p_j)`. With cross-entropy against a one-hot target the combined\n  gradient is `p − y`.\n- **Two classes.** With `K = 2`, softmax reduces to the sigmoid of the score difference; the logistic\n  function is the special case.\n\n### Background: at the output layer\n- **Next-token distribution.** The vocabulary softmax converts the last position's logits into a\n  distribution over tens of thousands of entries.\n- **A long tail.** Every entry stays positive, so there are many individually tiny but collectively\n  significant probabilities. Sampling from the full distribution eventually draws one, which is why top-k\n  and top-p truncation exist.\n- **The softmax bottleneck.** Log-probabilities are a linear function of a `d_model`-dimensional hidden\n  state (up to the normalizer), so the matrix of log-probabilities across many contexts has rank at most\n  about `d_model`. Yang et al. (2018) named this; a large `d_model` keeps it from biting in practice, but\n  it is why the output layer is not a fully general distribution.\n- **Temperature here is a user-facing knob;** temperature inside attention is not.\n\n### Background: inside attention\n1. **Weights over positions.** Each head computes `softmax(q·Kᵀ / √d_k)`, weights that sum to one across\n   the positions the query may see.\n2. **`√d_k` is a fixed temperature.** Dot products of *d_k*-dimensional vectors grow in magnitude with\n   *d_k*; without the scale the softmax would saturate early in training, giving near-one-hot weights and\n   vanishing gradients.\n3. **Causal masking** adds `−∞` to future positions before the softmax, so they get exactly zero weight.\n4. **Attention sinks.** Weights must sum to one even when no position is relevant, so models learn to dump\n   excess attention onto an uninformative position, usually the first token. Streaming methods that evict\n   old tokens must keep these **sink tokens** or quality collapses.\n\n### Background: computing it efficiently\nA naive softmax over a long row needs the whole row in memory to find the max and the sum. The online\nformulation keeps a running maximum *m* and a running sum *s* and updates both in one pass:\n\n```\nfor each score x:   m_new = max(m, x)\n                    s     = s · exp(m − m_new) + exp(x − m_new)\n                    m     = m_new\nsoftmax(x_i) = exp(x_i − m) / s\n```\n\n- **Exact, not approximate.** When a larger maximum arrives, the factor `exp(m − m_new)` converts\n  everything summed so far to the new reference point.\n- **Tile-friendly.** It can be accumulated in tiles that never leave fast on-chip memory, and the same\n  rescaling applies to a partial weighted sum of value vectors.\n- **That trick is the heart of FlashAttention:** exact attention without materializing the full `n × n`\n  score matrix, which is part of why long agent contexts are feasible at all.","example":"### Stable softmax and temperature\nThe code shows a stable softmax with temperature on a three-way score vector and prints how entropy moves\nwith *T*; the numbers are worked out below so you can check them by hand. The last two lines show the\noverflow the max-subtraction trick prevents.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef softmax(z, T=1.0):\n    z = np.asarray(z, dtype=np.float64) / T\n    z = z - z.max()                      # shift invariance: same result, no overflow\n    e = np.exp(z)\n    return e / e.sum()\n\nz = [2.0, 1.0, 0.1]\nfor T in (1.0, 0.5, 2.0):\n    p = softmax(z, T)\n    H = -(p * np.log(p)).sum()\n    print(f\"T={T}: p={p.round(3)} entropy={H:.2f} nats\")\n\nbig = np.array([1000.0, 0.0], dtype=np.float32)\nprint(np.exp(big) / np.exp(big).sum())   # overflow: inf/inf -> nan in the first slot\nprint(softmax(big))                      # [1. 0.]\n```\n\nWorked by hand (entropy printed by the script, in nats; the uniform maximum for three options is 1.10):\n\n| T | Scaled scores | Exponentials | Sum | p | Entropy |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `T = 1` | `[2.0, 1.0, 0.1]` | `exp(2.0) = 7.389`, `exp(1.0) = 2.718`, `exp(0.1) = 1.105` | `11.213` | `p ≈ [0.659, 0.242, 0.099]` | 0.85 |\n| `T = 0.5` | `[4, 2, 0.2]` | `[54.598, 7.389, 1.221]` | `63.209` | `p ≈ [0.864, 0.117, 0.019]` | 0.45 |\n| `T = 2` | `[1, 0.5, 0.05]` | `[2.718, 1.649, 1.051]` | `5.418` | `p ≈ [0.502, 0.304, 0.194]` | 1.03 |\n\n- **At `T = 0.5`** the leader gained and the tail shrank by a factor of five.\n- **At `T = 2`** the distribution is close to uniform.\n- **Same ranking in all three cases;** only the confidence changed.\n\n### Agent scenario: calibrating a router's confidence before gating on it\nAn agent's routing step outputs scores over three tools, and low-confidence decisions escalate to a\nstronger model. Fit one temperature on labelled routing decisions so that \"0.9\" means roughly 90%.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef softmax(z, T):\n    z = z / T\n    z = z - z.max(axis=1, keepdims=True)\n    e = np.exp(z)\n    return e / e.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\n\ndef nll(z, y, T):\n    p = softmax(z, T)\n    return -np.log(p[np.arange(len(y)), y] + 1e-12).mean()\n\ndef fit_temperature(z, y):                       # z: [n, k] router scores, y: true tool index\n    grid = np.linspace(0.25, 5.0, 96)\n    return grid[np.argmin([nll(z, y, T) for T in grid])]\n\n# load router scores and labels from your agent's logged, hand-labelled decisions\n# T = fit_temperature(z_val, y_val)\n# conf = softmax(z_live, T).max(axis=1); escalate = conf < threshold\n```\n\nTemperature scaling never changes which tool wins (ranking is preserved); it only changes how confident\nthe score claims to be, which is exactly what an escalation threshold depends on. The grid range is\nillustrative.\n\n### Online softmax equals the two-pass version\nPure Python, the one-pass running-max update from above checked against the ordinary max-then-sum\nversion on 10,000 random scores.\n\n```python\nimport math, random\n\ndef two_pass(xs):\n    m = max(xs)\n    s = sum(math.exp(x - m) for x in xs)\n    return [math.exp(x - m) / s for x in xs]\n\ndef online(xs):\n    m, s = float(\"-inf\"), 0.0\n    for x in xs:                                  # one pass: running max and rescaled sum\n        m_new = max(m, x)\n        s = s * math.exp(m - m_new) + math.exp(x - m_new)\n        m = m_new\n    return [math.exp(x - m) / s for x in xs]\n\nrandom.seed(0)\nxs = [random.uniform(-20, 20) for _ in range(10_000)]\nerr = max(abs(a - b) for a, b in zip(two_pass(xs), online(xs)))\nprint(f\"max abs difference: {err:.1e}\")          # zero or float-rounding level\nprint(online([1000.0, 0.0]))                     # [1.0, 0.0]: no overflow either\n```","patterns":["**Set temperature per agent step.** Run tool selection, argument filling and extraction at low temperature for consistency, and reserve higher values for drafting or brainstorming steps. Monitor tool-call validity and run-to-run variance on a fixed eval set when you change it.","**Calibrate before exposing or gating on probabilities.** If an agent thresholds softmax confidence (routing, guardrails, \"ask the user\" decisions), fit a single temperature on a held-out labelled set (temperature scaling) and track expected calibration error over time.","**Use sigmoids for multi-label outputs.** When labels are not mutually exclusive (a request can need both search and calendar), train independent sigmoids (for example `torch.nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss`) instead of a softmax. Monitor per-label precision and recall.","**Keep sink tokens when evicting KV.** Any streaming or sliding-cache scheme for long agent sessions should pin the first few tokens in the cache along with the recent window. Monitor quality on long sessions against a full-cache baseline.","**Normalize in fp32.** When fine-tuning or self-hosting, cast before normalizing (`torch.softmax(x.float(), dim=-1)`) or compute losses on fp32 logits, even when the model runs in bf16 or fp16. Monitor NaN and inf counters on losses and sampled probabilities.","**Use fused attention kernels.** Call `torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention` or a FlashAttention build instead of hand-writing `softmax(q @ k.T / sqrt(d))`, so the online softmax, the scale and masking are handled for you. Monitor peak memory and step time at long sequence lengths.","**Mask before, and guard empty rows.** Add `−∞` (or the dtype's most negative value, `torch.finfo(dtype).min`) before the softmax, and zero the output of rows where every key is masked. Monitor NaNs specifically in padded batches."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** The `temperature` you set on every agent call is a softmax parameter:\n  low values make tool calls and extractions consistent, high values add variety. Any probability you read\n  (logprobs on a routing step, a classifier score used as a guardrail) is a softmax output, not a\n  calibrated correctness estimate.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The `temperature` request parameter, `logprobs` on hosted APIs,\n  classifier heads in guardrail and routing models, and attention kernels inside the serving engine.\n  Some reasoning models fix or restrict `temperature`, so check the model's docs before tuning it.\n- **What you control.** Temperature per agent step (planner vs tool-argument filling vs creative\n  drafting), whether probabilities are calibrated before thresholds use them, and softmax versus sigmoids\n  when training a multi-label classifier for the agent.\n- **What you can skip.** Deriving the Jacobian and the online-softmax recurrence; recognise that\n  FlashAttention relies on the latter and that NaNs from softmax are a precision or masking bug.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Choosing temperature per agent step.** Teams run planners and tool-argument steps cold and drafting\n  steps warmer, because softmax temperature trades consistency for variety.\n- **Confidence gates and escalation.** Router or guardrail probabilities decide when an agent asks the\n  user or hands off, and only work after calibration.\n- **Multi-label intent detection.** A request needing several tools at once is modelled with sigmoids, not\n  a softmax that forces tools to compete.\n- **Long-session serving.** Attention sinks explain why streaming KV schemes for long agent sessions pin\n  the first tokens.","mini_project":"**Calibrate an agent's routing confidence (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** make a router's probabilities mean what they say before an escalation gate uses them.\n- **Steps:** collect 100 routing decisions with scores over three or four tools and hand-labelled correct\n  tools; plot a reliability diagram (confidence bins vs accuracy); fit a temperature with the example\n  code on half the data; re-plot on the other half; choose an escalation threshold.\n- **Done when:** you have before and after reliability plots, the calibration error dropped on held-out\n  data, and the chosen threshold comes with its expected escalation rate and accuracy."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"A router that says 0.95 is right 95% of the time.\"** Softmax guarantees a distribution, not calibration; trained models are often overconfident. Fit a temperature on labelled agent decisions before any escalation threshold relies on the number.","**\"Changing temperature changes which option is best.\"** Dividing by *T* preserves the ranking; it only moves confidence and how often lower-ranked tokens are sampled. Logits `[2.0, 1.0, 0.1]` keep the same leader at `T = 0.5`, 1 and 2.","**\"At low temperature a bad token cannot appear.\"** Any `T > 0` leaves every finite logit with positive probability, so over thousands of agent steps a rare token eventually gets sampled. Only truncation or a `−∞` mask removes it.","**\"Softmax is the natural head for picking tools.\"** Its outputs sum to one, which forces labels to compete; a request that needs both search and calendar wants independent sigmoids.","**\"The first tokens of a long session are the safest to evict.\"** Because attention weights must sum to one, models park spare attention on the first tokens. Evicting these sink tokens collapses quality, so pin them."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Attention scores q times K\"] -->|\"divide by sqrt d_k\"| M[\"Add minus infinity to masked\"]\n    L[\"Output logits\"] -->|\"divide by temperature T\"| M\n    M --> X[\"Subtract row max\"]\n    X --> E[\"Exponentiate in fp32\"]\n    E --> N[\"Divide by row sum\"]\n    N --> W[\"Attention weights over positions\"]\n    N --> P[\"Next-token probabilities\"]\n    W --> V[\"Weighted sum of values\"]\n    P --> S[\"Truncate and sample\"]","caption":"Both uses share one recipe (scale, mask with minus infinity, subtract the max, exponentiate, normalize);\nonly the scores and what consumes the probabilities differ. The temperature you set enters on the logits\npath."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is softmax paired with cross-entropy, and what is the resulting gradient?","a":"- **The combined gradient is `p − y`,** prediction minus one-hot target, because the log in cross-entropy\n  cancels the exp in softmax.\n- **Why it matters:** the signal is proportional to the error and never saturates when the model is\n  confidently wrong, unlike squared error on probabilities. Frameworks fuse the two for numerical stability."},{"q":"Your agent escalates to a human when the router's top probability is below 0.8, yet wrong tool calls still slip through. What is going on?","a":"- **The scores are not calibrated.** Softmax outputs reflect training, not accuracy, so many wrong\n  decisions carry 0.9 or more.\n- **Fix:** log routing decisions with labels, fit a temperature on them, re-pick the threshold from the\n  calibrated scores, and track the gate's precision and recall over time."},{"q":"How would you set temperature across the steps of a tool-calling agent?","a":"- **Low for structure.** Tool selection, argument filling and extraction need consistency, so run them at\n  or near zero, where softmax approaches argmax.\n- **Higher only where variety helps,** such as drafting alternatives; evaluate any change on a fixed task\n  set, since the tail never reaches zero and rare bad tokens accumulate over many steps."},{"q":"Your training run shows NaN losses, but only on batches that contain padding. What do you check?","a":"- **Fully masked rows.** A padding query whose every key is masked gives an all `−∞` row and `0/0` in the\n  softmax.\n- **Mask style and precision.** Check that masks are added before the softmax, that fp16 is not\n  underflowing a finite mask value, and that normalization runs in fp32. Guard empty rows or use a fused\n  kernel that does."},{"q":"When would you use independent sigmoids instead of a softmax?","a":"- **When labels are not mutually exclusive.** A support ticket can be both \"billing\" and \"urgent\"; softmax\n  forces the probabilities to compete and sum to one.\n- **Softmax remains right** for exactly one class per input, such as next-token prediction, where the\n  sum-to-one constraint is the truth."},{"q":"What happens numerically when you lower temperature from 1 to 0.5 on logits [2.0, 1.0, 0.1]?","a":"- **Gaps double.** The scaled scores become [4, 2, 0.2], so probabilities move from about\n  [0.659, 0.242, 0.099] to [0.864, 0.117, 0.019].\n- **Entropy roughly halves** (0.85 to 0.45 nats) and the tail shrinks by a factor of five, while the\n  ranking is unchanged."}],"tradeoffs":["**Clean gradients vs no hard zero.** Smooth normalization gives gradients everywhere, but it can never express a hard zero, so sampling must be truncated externally and attention must park unused mass somewhere.","**Low vs high temperature.** Lower temperature raises consistency and accuracy on tasks with one right answer, such as tool arguments; higher raises diversity. The right value flips with whether the task rewards variety.","**Exact row vs online tiles.** The plain softmax needs the whole row, which is memory-heavy for long attention rows; the online version is exact and tile-friendly at the cost of extra rescaling work per tile.","**Fixed `√d_k` vs learned sharpness.** Scaling by `√d_k` stabilizes attention at initialization, but it is a fixed temperature; heads that need very sharp or very flat attention must learn to grow or shrink their query and key norms."],"pitfalls":["**Treating \"99% probability\" as 99% correctness.** Softmax outputs are scores shaped by training; calibration must be measured rather than assumed. Symptom: an agent's confidence gate lets through wrong tool choices. Fix: measure calibration error on labelled data and apply temperature scaling.","**Exponentiating in half precision or without subtracting the max.** Symptom: NaN or inf probabilities on long or confident rows, often only on some batches. Fix: subtract the row max and normalize in fp32.","**Masking after the softmax.** Multiplying probabilities by zero instead of adding `−∞` before leaves rows that no longer sum to one. The sibling bug, masking with a large finite negative such as −1e4 that underflows to nothing in fp16, shows up as attention leaking to masked positions. Fix: add the mask before the softmax, using `−∞` or the dtype's own minimum.","**A fully masked row.** For example a padding query whose every key is masked: the row is all `−∞`, softmax computes `0/0`, and NaNs appear only in batches with padding. Kernels guard this case explicitly, and hand-written attention must too.","**Evicting the first tokens of a long context.** To save KV memory; attention that used to land on the sink is redistributed and output quality degrades sharply. Fix: pin the first few tokens in the cache.","**Mixing up the two temperatures.** Reusing an output-layer temperature inside attention or vice versa; they are separate knobs with separate meanings."],"when":"- **You inherit it, not choose it:** at two places in every transformer and in nearly every classifier.\n- **What you choose:** the temperature at the output for each agent step, the truncation rules layered on\n  top, the precision the normalization runs in, and multi-class (softmax) versus multi-label (independent\n  sigmoids, because labels are not mutually exclusive).\n- **Measure** calibration if you intend to read probabilities as confidence.\n- **Day to day** it shows up when debugging NaNs, tuning sampling, and reasoning about why attention\n  behaves as it does.\n- **Interviews:** derive the gradient with cross-entropy, explain the max-subtraction trick, say why\n  attention scores are scaled by `√d_k`, and explain why agent confidence gates need calibration.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets temperature per endpoint, computes softmax and losses in fp32, and masks with `−∞` before normalization rather than zeroing afterwards.","architect":"Decides whether a classification problem is mutually exclusive (softmax) or multi-label (sigmoids), and whether exposed probabilities are calibrated enough for downstream policy.","fde":"Explains to a customer why temperature 0.2 made their extraction consistent and why the \"confidence\" column still needs a calibration check on their data."},"check":{"q":"Why are attention scores divided by the square root of the key dimension before the softmax, and\nwhat would you expect to see in training if that scale were removed?","a":"A dot product of two *d_k*-dimensional vectors with roughly unit-variance components has variance\nproportional to *d_k*, so with `d_k = 128` raw scores are about eleven times larger in spread than with\n`d_k = 1`. Softmax of large-magnitude inputs is nearly one-hot, and in that regime its gradient\n`p_i(δ_ij − p_j)` is close to zero for every entry, so the query and key projections receive almost no\nlearning signal. Dividing by `√d_k` restores unit variance, keeping the softmax in its responsive range\nat initialization. Without it you would expect attention maps that collapse to single positions early,\nslow or stalled loss decrease, and sensitivity to initialization, which is exactly the instability the\nscale was introduced to prevent."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do implementations subtract the maximum score before exponentiating?","options":["To make the output sum to one","Because the result is identical by shift invariance and the largest exponent becomes exp(0), preventing overflow","To apply the temperature","To zero out masked positions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Softmax is unchanged by adding a constant to all inputs, so subtracting the max costs nothing in accuracy and bounds every exponent at or below 1, which keeps the sum finite in fp32 or fp16."},{"q":"What happens to the next-token distribution as temperature approaches zero?","options":["It becomes uniform over the vocabulary","It becomes undefined because of division by zero","It concentrates all mass on the highest-logit token, equivalent to greedy decoding","It removes tokens below a probability threshold"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dividing by a tiny T widens the gaps between scores without bound, so the top entry dominates the sum and the rest vanish. Engines special-case T = 0 as argmax rather than dividing."},{"q":"Why do attention sinks arise in trained transformers?","options":["Because the first token has a special embedding","Because rotary position encoding gives position zero a distinctive rotation","Because attention weights must sum to one even when no position is relevant, so the model learns to dump excess mass onto a convenient token","Because of weight decay"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The normalization constraint forces every query to allocate its full weight somewhere. Early tokens are visible to all later positions, so they become the default place to park attention mass."}]},{"key":"model-internals/sampling-strategies","sources":["Holtzman et al., 2020 — The Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration","Fan et al., 2018 — Hierarchical Neural Story Generation","Official docs: Hugging Face Transformers generation strategies"],"why":"- **The model outputs a distribution, not a token.** A 100k-entry distribution has a long tail of\n  individually unlikely tokens, and something must collapse it to one choice. Every obvious way fails\n  somewhere.\n- **Greedy is locally best, globally not.** Picking the argmax each step is deterministic and cheap, but a\n  sequence of locally best tokens is not the best sequence. Greedy text falls into repetition loops: once a\n  phrase appears, repeating it becomes the most likely continuation, which makes repeating it again even\n  more likely.\n- **Beam search finds the bland answer.** Keeping the *B* best partial sequences fixes local-versus-global,\n  yet Holtzman et al. (2020) showed the highest-likelihood open-ended continuations are exactly the bland,\n  repetitive ones. Human text is consistently *less* likely than beam output, because people say\n  surprising things.\n- **Pure sampling derails.** The tail holds a few percent of mass per step, and small risks compound. If\n  2% sits on tokens that do not fit (an illustrative figure), a 300-token answer avoids all of them with\n  probability 0.98³⁰⁰ ≈ 0.2%. Once a derailment is in the context, the model conditions on it and degrades,\n  and in an agent the derailed text may be a tool argument that gets executed.\n- **So reshaping and truncation exist.** Temperature, top-k and top-p keep sampling's diversity while\n  cutting off the tail that causes incoherence.","how":"### Reshaping: temperature\n- **Mechanism.** Logits `z` are divided by *T* before softmax. `T < 1` sharpens toward the top token,\n  `T > 1` flattens, and `T = 0` is treated as greedy by hosted APIs and engines such as vLLM rather than\n  as a division. Hugging Face Transformers instead expresses greedy as `do_sample=False` and rejects a\n  zero temperature when sampling is on.\n- **Ranking is unchanged.** Because temperature rescales all logits, it changes only how much mass the\n  leaders hold.\n- **The most used knob.** Near 0 for extraction, classification, code and tool arguments, where\n  consistency matters; around 0.7–1.0 for drafting and brainstorming.\n\n### Truncating: top-k, top-p and min-p\n| Method | Keeps | Strength | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Top-k** | the *k* highest-probability tokens, then renormalizes | simple and bounded | blind to shape: when the model is certain, 40 candidates include junk; among 200 reasonable words, 40 is too few |\n| **Top-p (nucleus)** | the smallest set whose cumulative mass reaches *p*, then renormalizes | adapts: a peaked distribution yields one or two tokens, a flat one many | can admit many tokens on very flat distributions |\n| **Min-p** | tokens with `p_i ≥ min_p · p_max` | adapts and scales with the leader, so it behaves well at high temperature | supported in vLLM and llama.cpp among others, not everywhere |\n\n- **Why nucleus became the default over top-k:** its candidate set adapts to the model's confidence.\n- **It is a pipeline.** Engines typically apply temperature first, then top-k, then top-p. Order matters\n  because truncation after temperature sees a reshaped distribution.\n- **Penalties come earlier.** Repetition, frequency and presence penalties sit earlier in the same pipeline\n  and lower the logits of tokens that already appeared.\n\n### Searching: greedy versus beam\n**Why greedy is not optimal, in numbers.** The first step offers A at 0.5 and B at 0.4. The best\ncontinuation after A has probability 0.3; after B, 0.9.\n\n| Path | Sequence probability |\n|---|---|\n| Greedy commits to A | 0.5 × 0.3 = 0.15 |\n| Keep both prefixes alive, take B | 0.4 × 0.9 = 0.36 |\n\nA locally weaker token can open a far more likely sequence. Greedy is beam search with `B = 1`.\n\n1. **Expand** each of *B* partial sequences by the vocabulary.\n2. **Score** all candidates by cumulative log-probability.\n3. **Keep** the best *B*, and repeat until each beam ends.\n4. **Apply a length penalty,** usually, because longer sequences accumulate more negative log-prob and\n   would otherwise lose unfairly.\n\n- **Cost.** *B* sequences' worth of compute per step (one batched pass) and *B* KV caches.\n- **No clean streaming,** because the winning beam is only known at the end.\n- **Right tool** when there is a single correct target and the metric rewards likelihood: machine\n  translation, speech recognition, grammar correction. For chat, tool calling and creative generation it\n  is rarely used.\n\n### Determinism, and what it means for agent replay\n- **`T = 0` fixes the choice, not the logits.** Batched kernels, different batch compositions and\n  non-associative floating-point reductions can change low-order bits; when two tokens are nearly tied the\n  argmax flips.\n- **A seed** makes sampling reproducible under the same conditions, not across hardware or engine versions.\n- **So** treat \"temperature 0\" as \"much more stable\", not \"reproducible\". One flipped token early in an\n  agent run (a different tool chosen) can send the whole trajectory elsewhere; replay harnesses record\n  model outputs rather than expecting to regenerate them.\n\n### Interactions worth knowing\n- **Constrained decoding** (JSON schemas, grammars) masks logits *before* sampling, so it composes with any\n  strategy and is the usual way to make tool calls parse.\n- **Some reasoning-oriented APIs fix the temperature** and reject the parameter, because their training\n  assumed a particular sampling regime.\n- **Change temperature *or* top-p, not both.** Vendor documentation generally advises this because their\n  effects overlap and tuning both obscures which one moved the result.","example":"### A sampler with the usual pipeline\nA compact sampler that applies temperature, top-k and top-p in the usual order, run against a toy\nseven-token distribution so the effect of each setting is visible:\n- **`top_p = 0.9`:** the nucleus is tokens 0–4 (cumulative mass 0.93 is the first to reach 0.9).\n- **`top_k = 3`:** keeps tokens 0–2.\n- **Adding `temperature = 0.5`:** sharpens the distribution so the same `top_p` nucleus shrinks to tokens\n  0–1.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef sample_next(logits, temperature=1.0, top_k=0, top_p=1.0, generator=None):\n    \"\"\"logits: [vocab]. Order matches common engines: temperature, then top-k, then top-p.\"\"\"\n    if temperature == 0:                                   # \"T = 0\" means greedy, not a division\n        return int(logits.argmax())\n    z = logits.float() / temperature\n    if top_k > 0:\n        kth = torch.topk(z, top_k).values[-1]\n        z = z.masked_fill(z < kth, float(\"-inf\"))          # drop everything below the k-th score\n    if top_p < 1.0:\n        sz, idx = torch.sort(z, descending=True)\n        p = torch.softmax(sz, -1)\n        before = p.cumsum(-1) - p                          # mass strictly ahead of each token\n        sz = sz.masked_fill(before >= top_p, float(\"-inf\"))  # keep the smallest set reaching top_p\n        z = torch.full_like(z, float(\"-inf\")).scatter(0, idx, sz)\n    probs = torch.softmax(z, -1)                           # renormalize over the survivors\n    return int(torch.multinomial(probs, 1, generator=generator))\n\nprobs = torch.tensor([0.50, 0.20, 0.10, 0.08, 0.05, 0.04, 0.03])\nlogits = probs.log()\ng = torch.Generator().manual_seed(0)\nfor cfg in [dict(temperature=0), dict(top_k=3), dict(top_p=0.9),\n            dict(temperature=0.5, top_p=0.9), dict(temperature=1.5)]:\n    draws = [sample_next(logits, generator=g, **cfg) for _ in range(2000)]\n    print(f\"{cfg}: tokens seen = {sorted(set(draws))}\")\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: per-step sampling profiles\nDifferent steps of one agent want different settings. Keep them in config next to the model version so\nevals and replays know exactly what ran.\n\n```python\n# One profile per agent step; values are common starting points to tune on your eval set.\nPROFILES = {\n    \"plan\":        {\"temperature\": 0.3},                    # some exploration, still focused\n    \"tool_args\":   {\"temperature\": 0.0},                    # plus schema-constrained output\n    \"final_answer\":{\"temperature\": 0.2},\n    \"draft_ideas\": {\"temperature\": 0.8, \"top_p\": 0.9, \"n\": 3},\n}\n\ndef call(client, model, step, messages, **extra):\n    params = PROFILES[step]\n    resp = client.chat.completions.create(model=model, messages=messages, **params, **extra)\n    log_step(step=step, model=model, params=params)          # record what ran, for replay and evals\n    return resp\n```\n\n`log_step` stands for your tracing call. The `n` parameter is offered by some APIs and engines, not all;\nwhere it is missing, issue several requests.\n\n### Worked scenario: compounding error over a 20-step agent run\nAssume each step independently has some chance of a malformed or wrong tool call (illustrative rates).\n\n| Per-step failure rate | Clean 20-step runs | A setting that might land there (illustrative, measure yours) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2% | 0.98²⁰ ≈ 67% | free sampling at temperature 1 |\n| 1% | 0.99²⁰ ≈ 82% | low temperature, no schema |\n| 0.5% | 0.995²⁰ ≈ 90% | temperature 0 plus validation and retry |\n| 0.1% | 0.999²⁰ ≈ 98% | schema-constrained tool calls at temperature 0 |\n\nPer-step reliability that looks fine in a single-call eval becomes a large failure rate across a run,\nwhich is why agent evals score whole trajectories.\n\n### Why the tail matters: compounding per-step risk\nPure Python: the chance that a long answer never draws a misfit token, for a few illustrative per-step\ntail masses. This is the arithmetic behind truncation.\n\n```python\nfor tail in (0.005, 0.01, 0.02):\n    for length in (50, 300):\n        clean = (1 - tail) ** length\n        print(f\"tail {tail:.1%} per step, {length:3d} tokens: {clean:6.1%} chance of no misfit\")\n# tail 0.5%: 50 tokens 77.8%, 300 tokens 22.2%\n# tail 1.0%: 50 tokens 60.5%, 300 tokens  4.9%\n# tail 2.0%: 50 tokens 36.4%, 300 tokens  0.2%\n```\n\n| Per-step misfit mass (illustrative) | 50-token answer clean | 300-token answer clean |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0.5% | ~78% | ~22% |\n| 1% | ~60% | ~5% |\n| 2% | ~36% | ~0.2% |\n\nLong outputs are where untruncated sampling fails, which is why top-p or min-p matters more as answers\ngrow.","patterns":["**Pin a sampling profile per agent step.** Store temperature, top-p and penalties in config next to the model version, for example `temperature=0` for extraction and tool arguments and `temperature=0.8, top_p=0.9` for drafting. Monitor format-failure rate and re-run the eval whenever the model or profile changes.","**Sample n and select for reasoning steps.** When a verifier or majority vote exists, request several samples (vLLM's `SamplingParams(n=5, temperature=0.8)`, or the API's `n` parameter where offered) and pick by vote or verifier (tests for a coding agent). Monitor accuracy gain against the linear increase in output tokens.","**Record outputs for replay, do not regenerate them.** Log sampling parameters, engine version and the model's actual outputs per agent step, so a failed run can be replayed exactly with tools stubbed. Monitor how often re-running a trace diverges.","**Test outputs semantically, not byte-for-byte.** Even at `T = 0`, assert on parsed fields, chosen tools or similarity rather than exact strings. Monitor golden-test flakiness after engine upgrades.","**Penalize repetition only on free text.** Enable a mild frequency or presence penalty on prose endpoints and keep it off where constrained decoding produces JSON. Monitor a repetition metric, such as the share of repeated n-grams, per endpoint.","**Use beam search only for likelihood-scored tasks.** For translation or grammar correction with Hugging Face, `model.generate(..., num_beams=4, length_penalty=1.0)`. Monitor the task metric and latency, since beams block streaming.","**Tune one knob at a time over a grid.** Sweep temperature with top-p fixed (or the reverse) on a labelled set and choose by task accuracy and format-failure rate, not by values copied from another model."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent makes many model calls per task, so small per-step error rates\n  compound: a 1% chance of a malformed or wrong tool call per step leaves only about 82% of 20-step runs\n  clean. Sampling settings are one of the cheapest levers on that rate.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The `temperature`, `top_p`, `n` and penalty parameters on every\n  chat or tool-calling request; `SamplingParams` in vLLM; `model.generate(...)` arguments in Hugging Face\n  Transformers when self-hosting.\n- **What you control.** A sampling profile per agent step (planner, tool-argument filling, final answer,\n  drafting), whether to sample several candidates and vote or verify, and how your eval and replay\n  harness treats non-determinism.\n- **What you can skip.** Beam search internals, unless your agent includes translation or transcription;\n  it is rarely used for chat or tool calling.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Reliable tool-calling agents.** Cold sampling plus schema constraints keeps per-step failure low\n  enough that long runs finish.\n- **Coding agents with tests.** Several samples at moderate temperature, filtered by running the tests,\n  beat a single greedy attempt when a verifier exists.\n- **Agent evals and replay.** Knowing temperature 0 is not reproducible shapes how teams record traces\n  and assert on outcomes.\n- **Creative or ideation agents.** Higher temperature with top-p or min-p truncation gives variety\n  without derailing long drafts.","mini_project":"**Measure sampling settings on a tool-calling agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** find the sampling profile that maximizes clean whole-run completion for a small agent.\n- **Steps:** build an agent with three tools (stubbed, so runs are fast and free of side effects) and 20\n  tasks that each need 3-6 tool calls; run every task 3 times at temperature 0, 0.5 and 1.0, with and\n  without schema-constrained tool calls, using a small, cheap model; log per-step\n  parse failures, wrong-tool choices and whole-run success.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of per-step failure rate and run success for each setting, the observed\n  run success roughly matches per-step reliability compounded over the run, and you can name the profile\n  you would ship."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Temperature 0 makes the agent reproducible.\"** It fixes the choice rule, not the logits: batch composition and floating-point reductions shift low-order bits, and near-tied tokens flip. Record outputs for replay and assert on parsed fields.","**\"Greedy decoding returns the most likely answer.\"** It returns a chain of locally best tokens. With A at 0.5 then 0.3 versus B at 0.4 then 0.9, greedy gets 0.15 while the better sequence has 0.36.","**\"Beam search gives better chat and tool-call output.\"** The highest-likelihood open-ended text is bland and repetitive, and beams block streaming. Beam search fits single-target tasks such as translation and speech recognition.","**\"Top-k 40 is a safe universal cut.\"** Top-k ignores the distribution's shape: it admits junk when the model is certain and starves a flat distribution. Top-p or min-p adapt the candidate set to confidence.","**\"A 1% per-step failure rate is good enough.\"** Agent steps compound: 0.99²⁰ ≈ 82%, so almost one 20-step run in five has a bad step. Judge settings on whole trajectories."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Z[\"Next-token logits\"] --> PEN[\"Repetition and frequency penalties\"]\n    PEN --> MASK[\"Constraint mask, if any\"]\n    MASK --> Q{\"Temperature is 0?\"}\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| G[\"Greedy: take argmax\"]\n    Q -->|\"no\"| T[\"Divide by temperature\"]\n    T --> K[\"Top-k cut\"]\n    K --> P[\"Top-p or min-p cut\"]\n    P --> R[\"Renormalize and draw\"]\n    G --> OUT[\"Append token, next step\"]\n    R --> OUT\n    BEAM[\"Beam search: keep B best sequences\"] -.->|\"alternative to sampling\"| OUT","caption":"One decode step: penalties and temperature reshape the logits, truncation cuts the tail, then the\nstrategy decides how the next token is chosen. An agent runs this for every token of every step."},"interview":[{"q":"Compare top-k, top-p and min-p. Which would you default to and why?","a":"- **Top-k** keeps a fixed number of tokens, which is blind to how confident the model is at each step.\n- **Top-p** keeps the smallest set reaching a cumulative mass, so it shrinks when the model is sure and\n  grows when it is not; that adaptivity made it the default.\n- **Min-p** keeps tokens above a fraction of the leader's probability and holds up better at high\n  temperature, so prefer it where the engine supports it."},{"q":"Show why greedy decoding is not the same as finding the most likely sequence.","a":"- **Counterexample:** step one offers A at 0.5 and B at 0.4; the best continuation after A is 0.3, after B\n  0.9. Greedy ends at 0.15, while B then its continuation reaches 0.36.\n- **Beam search** keeps several prefixes alive to catch such cases, at *B* times the compute and KV memory."},{"q":"How would you set sampling parameters across the steps of a tool-calling agent?","a":"- **Cold for structure.** Tool selection and arguments at or near temperature 0 with schema-constrained\n  output, because errors compound across steps (1% per step leaves about 82% of 20-step runs clean).\n- **Warmer only where variety pays,** such as drafting or generating candidates for a verifier, and vary\n  the prompt or temperature on retries so the agent does not repeat the same failed call."},{"q":"Golden tests at temperature 0 started failing after an inference engine upgrade. How do you respond?","a":"- **Expect it.** Kernel and batching changes alter low-order bits of the logits, and near-tied tokens flip\n  their argmax, so byte-exact outputs were never guaranteed.\n- **Fix the tests.** Assert on parsed fields or semantic similarity, log sampling parameters and engine\n  version per request, and re-baseline only after the eval suite confirms accuracy held."},{"q":"When would you choose beam search in production, and what does it cost?","a":"- **Choose it** when there is one correct target and the metric tracks likelihood: translation, speech\n  recognition, grammar correction, with a modest width and a length penalty.\n- **Cost:** *B* times the per-step compute and *B* KV caches, and no clean streaming because the winner is\n  known only at the end. Avoid it for chat, where likelihood favours bland text."},{"q":"Sampling five answers and majority-voting improved your agent's accuracy. When is that worth it?","a":"- **When a selection signal exists.** Majority vote on short final answers or a verifier such as tests or\n  a checker model; without one, extra samples add cost and no accuracy.\n- **Do the maths.** Output tokens rise roughly fivefold, so compare the accuracy gain with the cost per\n  request and latency budget, and consider sampling more only on low-confidence requests."}],"tradeoffs":["**Low temperature vs diversity.** It buys consistency and higher per-token accuracy on single-answer tasks but costs diversity; the trade flips when the task rewards variety, such as multiple candidates or an agent that must try a different approach after a failure.","**Top-p vs top-k.** Top-p adapts the candidate set to the model's confidence; top-k gives a hard bound. Top-k is safer when a flat distribution must never admit hundreds of options.","**Beam search vs cost and streaming.** It raises likelihood and translation-style accuracy, but multiplies compute and memory by the beam width and blocks streaming. It loses for open-ended text, where likelihood is the wrong objective.","**Many samples vs tokens.** Sampling several diverse answers and voting or reranking improves accuracy on reasoning tasks, at a linear cost in tokens that only pays off when a verifier or majority signal exists."],"pitfalls":["**Expecting identical outputs at temperature 0.** Across runs or deployments, the symptom is flaky golden tests or agent replays that diverge on near-tied tokens after an engine or batch-size change. Fix: assert on parsed fields or semantic similarity, not exact strings, and replay recorded outputs.","**Greedy or beam for long free-form generation.** Symptom: paragraphs that repeat a sentence or phrase verbatim, sometimes indefinitely until the token limit. Fix: sample with top-p or min-p truncation.","**Raising temperature to \"make the model more creative\" on extraction or code.** Symptom: more malformed output and invented fields, which evaluation then attributes to the prompt. Fix: keep these endpoints near 0 and change the prompt or data instead.","**One temperature for every agent step.** A single high value makes tool arguments flaky; a single zero makes retries repeat the same failed action. Fix: per-step profiles, and vary the prompt or raise temperature only on retry.","**Tuning temperature and top-p simultaneously.** Results move but it is unclear which knob did it, and settings copied between models behave differently because logit scales differ. Fix: sweep one knob per model on a labelled set.","**A strong repetition penalty on structured output.** Symptom: JSON that avoids repeating a required key or closing bracket because the token was penalized. Fix: turn penalties off on structured endpoints."],"when":"- **One right answer and a downstream parser (tool calls, extraction):** temperature at or near 0,\n  optionally with constrained decoding, and keep top-p at its default.\n- **Drafting, ideation or candidates for a reranker:** temperature 0.7–1.0 with top-p around 0.9, or min-p\n  if the engine offers it.\n- **Translation or transcription scored by likelihood-correlated metrics:** beam search with a modest width\n  and a length penalty.\n- **Measure** task accuracy and format-failure rate across a small grid rather than copying numbers from a\n  blog; the same temperature means different things for different models.\n- **Interviews:** day to day the strategy is a few request parameters; expect to explain why greedy\n  repeats, why nucleus beat top-k, why beam search is wrong for chat, and how you set sampling per agent step.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets temperature, top-p and penalties per endpoint, pins them in config alongside the model version, and tests format-failure rate when either changes.","architect":"Defines sampling policy per workload class, from deterministic-leaning extraction to diverse candidate generation, and how determinism claims are communicated to customers.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's \"the model is inconsistent\" or \"it keeps repeating itself\" by reading the sampling parameters before touching the prompt."},"check":{"q":"A team generating product descriptions complains that greedy decoding repeats phrases, but when\nthey switched to plain sampling at temperature 1 the text occasionally became nonsensical. What would you\nrecommend and why does each failure happen?","a":"Greedy repeats because repetition is self-reinforcing under the model: once a phrase appears,\nrepeating it is the single most likely continuation, and taking the argmax every step never escapes.\nPlain sampling fixes that but draws from the full tail, where a few percent of mass per step sits on\ntokens that make no sense in context; over a long description one of them is eventually drawn, and the\nmodel then conditions on its own mistake. The fix is to keep sampling but truncate the tail: top-p\naround 0.9 (or min-p) so the candidate set adapts to confidence, temperature around 0.7–0.8 to favour\nthe leaders without flattening, and a mild frequency penalty if repeats persist. Validate with a\nrepetition metric and a human spot-check across a small parameter grid rather than one setting."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why did nucleus (top-p) sampling largely replace top-k as the default truncation method?","options":["It is cheaper to compute than top-k","Its candidate set adapts to the shape of the distribution, keeping few tokens when the model is confident and many when it is not","It guarantees deterministic output","It removes the need for temperature"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A fixed k admits junk when the model is sure and excludes good options when it is unsure. A cumulative-mass threshold sizes the set to the model's confidence at each step."},{"q":"Why is beam search a poor choice for open-ended chat responses?","options":["It cannot be combined with a KV cache","It only works with encoder–decoder models","It maximizes sequence likelihood, and for open-ended text the most likely continuations are bland and repetitive","It ignores the system prompt"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Human text is less likely than beam output under the model because it is surprising. Beam search finds high-likelihood text, which is what you want in translation and exactly what you do not want in chat."},{"q":"With temperature set to 0, why can two identical requests still produce different outputs?","options":["The engine ignores temperature 0 and samples anyway","Floating-point reductions and batch composition can change logits slightly, flipping near-tied argmax choices","The tokenizer is non-deterministic","Top-p is still applied randomly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Argmax is deterministic given the logits, but the logits themselves vary at the low-order bits with kernel choice and batching; when two tokens are almost tied, that is enough to change the winner."}]},{"key":"model-internals/speculative-decoding","sources":["Leviathan et al., 2023 — Fast Inference from Transformers via Speculative Decoding","Chen et al., 2023 — Accelerating Large Language Model Decoding with Speculative Sampling","Cai et al., 2024 — Medusa: Simple LLM Inference Acceleration Framework with Multiple Decoding Heads","Li et al., 2024 — EAGLE: Speculative Sampling Requires Rethinking Feature Uncertainty"],"why":"- **Decode is bandwidth-bound.** The large model produces one token per forward pass, and that pass is\n  dominated by moving weights (and the KV cache) from GPU memory into the compute units, not by\n  arithmetic.\n- **A second token in the same pass is nearly free.** The weights are read once either way, so the\n  compute for another row costs little until the batch grows large enough to become compute-bound.\n  Single-request latency has a bandwidth floor, and a GPU with more FLOPs does little.\n- **The autoregressive dependency blocks the obvious use.** Token *t+1* is unknown until token *t* is\n  chosen, so you cannot simply ask for two tokens at once. \"Use a smaller model\" trades quality for speed,\n  which is usually unacceptable for an agent's planner.\n- **You only need to guess, not know.** If something cheap proposes a continuation, the large model scores\n  every proposed position in one pass, because given a candidate prefix its distributions at all positions\n  are computed in parallel, exactly as in prefill.\n- **Asymmetric payoff.** Guess right and you advance several tokens for the price of one pass; guess wrong\n  and you lose only the cheap draft work.","how":"### The loop\n1. **Draft.** A small model (or a cheaper heuristic) generates γ candidate tokens autoregressively from\n   the current prefix, recording its probability `q(x)` for each. γ is typically a handful.\n2. **Verify.** The target model runs one forward pass over `prefix + γ candidates`. Because a causal\n   model computes a distribution at every position in parallel, this yields the target's `p(·)` at all\n   γ draft positions plus one more after the last candidate, with the cost profile of a short prefill.\n3. **Accept or reject, left to right.** Accept each candidate *x* with probability `min(1, p(x) / q(x))`.\n   On the first rejection, sample a replacement from the residual distribution `normalize(max(0, p − q))`\n   and stop. If all γ are accepted, sample one bonus token from the target at the extra position.\n4. **Roll back.** KV-cache entries for rejected positions, in both models, are discarded; the accepted\n   tokens are appended and the loop repeats.\n\n### Background: why the output is exactly the target's\n- **It is rejection sampling.** Where the draft puts too little mass on a token (`q < p`), it is always\n  accepted when drafted and the shortfall is made up by the residual on rejections. Where it puts too much\n  (`q > p`), it is accepted only a fraction `p/q` of the time.\n- **Summed over cases,** the probability of emitting each token equals `p(x)` exactly, which makes the\n  method lossless rather than an approximation.\n- **The one-line proof.** The chance a drafted token is accepted is\n  `α = Σ_x q(x)·min(1, p(x)/q(x)) = Σ_x min(p(x), q(x))`, and the residual's normalizer is\n  `Σ_x max(0, p(x) − q(x)) = 1 − α`, the rejection probability. So\n\n```\nP(emit x) = q(x)·min(1, p(x)/q(x)) + (1 − α) · max(0, p(x) − q(x)) / (1 − α)\n          = min(p(x), q(x)) + max(0, p(x) − q(x)) = p(x)\n```\n\n- **Expected acceptance falls out of the same algebra:** `α = Σ min(p, q) = 1 − TV(p, q)`, one minus the\n  total-variation distance. Acceptance measures how much the drafter's distribution overlaps the\n  target's, not whether its single top guess is right.\n- **Under greedy decoding** the rule collapses to \"accept while the draft matches the target's argmax\",\n  the easiest version to reason about, and the usual case for tool-calling steps run at temperature 0.\n\n### What the speedup depends on\nLet α be the probability a draft token is accepted. Treating each position as an independent trial, the\nexpected tokens per target pass is `(1 − α^(γ+1)) / (1 − α)`:\n\n| α | γ | Tokens per target pass |\n|---|---|---|\n| α = 0.8 | 5 | about 3.7 |\n| α = 0.5 | 5 | about 2 |\n\nThe wall-clock gain divides that by the cycle's relative cost (γ draft steps plus one slightly longer\ntarget pass). With *c* the cost of one draft step relative to one target pass, treating verification as\none target pass:\n\n```\nspeedup ≈ (1 − α^(γ+1)) / ((1 − α) · (γ·c + 1))\n```\n\n- **Both conditions are needed.** The draft must be much cheaper than the target *and* agree with it\n  often; either alone is not enough.\n- **Agreement depends on the text.** Highest on predictable text such as code, boilerplate, structured\n  output (tool calls) and anything that copies from the prompt; lowest on creative text at high\n  temperature.\n- **γ has an optimum.** Raising γ lifts the ceiling but lowers average acceptance of later positions, and\n  past a point the extra verification compute costs more than it saves.\n\n### Where the draft comes from\n| Drafter | How it works | Strengths and costs |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Separate small model** | same tokenizer (so draft ids mean the same to the target), usually same family, an order of magnitude or more smaller | flexible, reusable across a family; extra weights and cache |\n| **N-gram or prompt lookup** | propose the continuation that followed the same recent tokens earlier in the context | no second model, near-zero cost; strong on RAG answers that quote sources and code edits that reproduce input, weak elsewhere |\n| **Extra heads (Medusa)** | heads on the last hidden state predict offsets +2, +3, ...; candidates verified as a tree in one pass | drafting rides on the target's own pass through small extra heads, no second model; heads trained per target |\n| **Feature-level head (EAGLE)** | a light autoregressive module predicts the next hidden state, not the next token | reuses the target's representations for higher acceptance |\n\n- **Check the acceptance mode.** Medusa also offers a relaxed \"typical acceptance\" rule that trades\n  exactness for higher acceptance; only the rejection-sampling rule above is lossless, so check which mode\n  a deployment uses before claiming identical outputs.\n- **Agents favour prompt lookup.** Agent contexts are full of text the output will copy: file contents a\n  coding agent edits, retrieved passages, earlier tool results. N-gram drafting exploits that for free.\n\n### Why it stops helping\n- **High concurrency removes the idle compute.** The server already feeds the GPU many tokens per pass, so\n  verifying γ extra tokens per request costs real compute and can lower total throughput.\n- **So it is a latency tool** for workloads with few concurrent requests, or a throughput tool only while\n  the batch is still bandwidth-bound. Many concurrent agents on one GPU look like a batch workload.\n- **Tune it by measurement.** Engines such as vLLM expose draft-model and n-gram modes with a configurable\n  number of speculative tokens; find the setting by measuring tokens/second per request and per GPU at the\n  real batch size.","example":"### Illustrative cost model: when speculation pays\nEvery number here is invented for the arithmetic, not a benchmark. A target pass takes 25 ms, a draft\npass 2 ms, γ = 5, and verification is slightly longer than a single-token pass, say 27 ms. A cycle costs\n5 × 2 + 27 = 37 ms.\n\n| Acceptance | Tokens per cycle | ms per token | vs 25 ms baseline |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| α = 0.8 | `(1 − 0.8⁶) / 0.2 ≈ 3.69` | about 10 | a 2.5× gain |\n| α = 0.5 | about 1.97 | 18.8 | a 1.3× gain |\n| α = 0.3 | about 1.43 | 25.9 | *slower* than not speculating |\n| α = 0.8, draft costs 8 ms instead of 2 | 3.69 | 67 / 3.69 ≈ 18 | gain mostly gone |\n\nAcceptance rate and draft cost are the two numbers to measure before enabling it. The same model as a\npure-Python calculator:\n\n```python\ndef ms_per_token(alpha, gamma, draft_ms, verify_ms):\n    tokens = (1 - alpha ** (gamma + 1)) / (1 - alpha)   # expected tokens per cycle\n    return (gamma * draft_ms + verify_ms) / tokens\n\nBASE = 25.0                                             # target ms per token, no speculation\nfor alpha, draft_ms in [(0.8, 2), (0.5, 2), (0.3, 2), (0.8, 8)]:\n    t = ms_per_token(alpha, 5, draft_ms, 27)\n    print(f\"alpha={alpha} draft={draft_ms}ms: {t:5.1f} ms/token, speedup {BASE / t:.2f}x\")\n# alpha=0.8 draft=2ms:  10.0 ms/token, speedup 2.49x\n# alpha=0.5 draft=2ms:  18.8 ms/token, speedup 1.33x\n# alpha=0.3 draft=2ms:  25.9 ms/token, speedup 0.96x\n# alpha=0.8 draft=8ms:  18.2 ms/token, speedup 1.38x\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a self-hosted coding agent\nA coding agent runs 15 sequential steps on a low-concurrency pool; each step emits about 300 output\ntokens (a tool call carrying a code edit). Same illustrative costs as above.\n\n| Setup | ms per token | Decode time per step | Decode time per 15-step run |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| No speculation | 25 | 7.5 s | 112.5 s |\n| Drafter, α = 0.5 (novel code) | 18.8 | ~5.6 s | ~84.6 s |\n| Prompt lookup or drafter, α = 0.8 (edits copying the file) | ~10 | ~3.0 s | ~45 s |\n\n- **Same output, faster run.** The lossless rule means the agent's trajectory and eval scores are\n  unchanged at temperature 0; only wall-clock time moves. In practice the verify pass uses a different\n  kernel shape, so in fp16 or bf16 a near-tied token can occasionally flip, exactly as with any batching\n  change.\n- **Measure acceptance per step type.** Tool-call JSON and edits tend to sit near the top row; free-text\n  reasoning sits lower.\n\n### A runnable verification step\nToy distributions that exercise the accept, reject and bonus paths, checking measured tokens per pass\nagainst the formula.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef speculative_step(p_target, q_draft, draft_tokens, g=None):\n    \"\"\"p_target: [gamma+1, V] target probs at each verified position (ONE target pass);\n    q_draft: [gamma, V] draft probs; draft_tokens: [gamma]. Returns the emitted tokens,\n    which are distributed exactly as if sampled from the target alone.\"\"\"\n    out = []\n    for i, x in enumerate(draft_tokens):\n        p, q = p_target[i], q_draft[i]\n        if torch.rand((), generator=g) < min(1.0, (p[x] / q[x]).item()):\n            out.append(int(x))                                 # accept the draft token\n        else:\n            resid = torch.clamp(p - q, min=0)                  # residual corrects the distribution\n            out.append(int(torch.multinomial(resid / resid.sum(), 1, generator=g)))\n            return out                                         # stop at the first rejection\n    out.append(int(torch.multinomial(p_target[-1], 1, generator=g)))  # all accepted: bonus token\n    return out\n\nV, gamma = 50, 5\ng = torch.Generator().manual_seed(0)\ntarget = torch.softmax(2 * torch.randn(V, generator=g), -1)\ndraft = torch.softmax(target.log() + 0.5 * torch.randn(V, generator=g), -1)  # similar, not identical\nlengths = []\nfor _ in range(2000):\n    toks = torch.multinomial(draft, gamma, replacement=True, generator=g)\n    lengths.append(len(speculative_step(target.expand(gamma + 1, V), draft.expand(gamma, V), toks, g)))\nalpha = torch.minimum(target, draft).sum().item()                   # = 1 - TV(target, draft)\nprint(f\"alpha={alpha:.2f} predicted={(1 - alpha**(gamma + 1)) / (1 - alpha):.2f}\",\n      \"measured:\", sum(lengths) / len(lengths))   # agree within sampling noise on this i.i.d. toy\n# A stationary toy: real positions have different distributions, but the accept/reject logic is identical.\n```","patterns":["**Try prompt lookup first on copy-heavy agent routes.** For RAG answers that quote sources or coding agents that rewrite files, use n-gram drafting: no second model to host. In Hugging Face Transformers, `generate(..., prompt_lookup_num_tokens=10)`; in vLLM, its n-gram speculative method. Monitor acceptance rate and time per output token.","**Use assisted generation with a same-family drafter.** In Hugging Face, `model.generate(..., assistant_model=draft)` with a much smaller model sharing the tokenizer; in vLLM, its draft-model speculative mode with a set number of speculative tokens. Monitor acceptance, draft memory and p95 time per output token.","**Split latency and throughput pools.** Enable speculation on low-concurrency interactive pools (chat, coding, sequential agents) and leave high-batch pools, such as offline agent evals or bulk jobs, without it. Monitor per-GPU tokens/second in each pool, since that is what speculation can lower.","**Tune γ at production concurrency.** Sweep the number of speculative tokens (for example 2 to 6) under replayed real agent traffic and pick the best p50/p95 time per output token that does not cut per-GPU throughput. Re-tune when the batch size or model changes.","**Gate on output equivalence.** With greedy decoding and the lossless rule, outputs should match the non-speculative path; diff a sample of agent steps after enabling it, and confirm the acceptance mode (strict vs typical) before promising identical outputs."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent makes many sequential model calls, and each waits on decode, so\n  time per output token multiplies across the run. Tool-call JSON, code edits and answers that quote\n  retrieved text are predictable, which is exactly where drafts are accepted most often.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The speculative decoding settings of a self-hosted engine (vLLM's\n  draft-model and n-gram modes, a configurable number of speculative tokens), `assistant_model` and\n  `prompt_lookup_num_tokens` in Hugging Face `generate`. Hosted APIs may use it internally without\n  exposing a setting; OpenAI's Predicted Outputs (the `prediction` request parameter) is a related hosted\n  option for edits where most of the output is known in advance.\n- **What you control.** Whether to enable it per serving pool, which drafter (n-gram lookup, a small\n  same-family model, trained heads), and how many tokens to speculate, all measured at real concurrency.\n- **What you can skip.** The full rejection-sampling proof; remember that the lossless rule keeps outputs\n  exactly the target's, so your agent evals do not need re-baselining for quality, only latency.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Self-hosted coding agents.** Edits that reproduce most of a file are ideal for prompt lookup, cutting\n  per-step latency without changing the edit.\n- **RAG answer steps.** Answers that quote retrieved passages give n-gram drafting high acceptance.\n- **Interactive agent pools.** Low-concurrency pools serving users who wait on each step get speculation;\n  batch pools for evals and offline jobs do not.\n- **Tool-call heavy loops.** Structured JSON with repeated keys and tool names is predictable, so drafts\n  are accepted often at temperature 0.","mini_project":"**Measure speculative decoding on agent-shaped output (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** find out how much prompt lookup speeds up an agent's code-edit and tool-call steps.\n- **Steps:** collect 30 prompts from an agent's logs or write them (file-rewrite edits, tool-call JSON,\n  free-text reasoning); with a small open model in Hugging Face, time `generate` greedily with and without\n  `prompt_lookup_num_tokens`; confirm outputs are identical; compute ms per output token per step type.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of speedup per step type, a check that every output matched the\n  baseline (any mismatch traced to a near-tied token, not a bug), and a recommendation for which agent\n  steps or pools should enable it."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"It is a quality-for-speed trade, like switching to a smaller model.\"** The strict accept rule `min(1, p/q)` plus residual sampling emits each token with exactly the target's probability, so agent evals need re-baselining for latency, not quality. Relaxed modes such as Medusa's typical acceptance are the exception.","**\"Acceptance means the draft's top guess matched.\"** Under sampling, α = Σ min(p, q) = 1 − TV(p, q): it measures overlap of whole distributions. Only at temperature 0 does it reduce to argmax agreement.","**\"Turning it on always makes things faster.\"** With the illustrative 37 ms cycle and γ = 5, α = 0.3 gives 25.9 ms per token, slower than the 25 ms baseline. At high concurrency, verification also eats compute that was no longer idle and per-GPU throughput falls.","**\"More speculative tokens means more speed.\"** Later positions are accepted less often, so each extra token adds verification work with shrinking payoff; γ has an optimum that drops as batch size grows.","**\"You need to host a second model.\"** Prompt lookup drafts from n-grams already in the context, which is strongest exactly where agents spend output: file edits, quoted RAG passages and repeated tool results."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant D as Draft model\n    participant T as Target model\n    participant A as Accept rule\n    D->>D: Generate gamma tokens, record q\n    D->>T: Prefix plus gamma candidates\n    T->>T: One pass, p at gamma plus 1 positions\n    T->>A: p and q per position\n    A->>A: Accept x with prob min of 1 and p over q\n    Note over A: First rejection: sample from max of 0 and p minus q, stop\n    Note over A: All accepted: sample bonus token from target\n    A->>D: Accepted tokens, roll back rejected KV\n    A->>T: Accepted tokens, roll back rejected KV","caption":"One speculation cycle: a cheap draft proposes γ tokens, one target pass scores them all, and the accept\nrule decides how many survive."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is speculative decoding lossless, unlike just using a smaller model?","a":"- **Rejection sampling.** Each draft token is accepted with probability `min(1, p/q)`, and on rejection a\n  replacement comes from the residual `max(0, p − q)`; together the emitted token is distributed exactly\n  as `p(x)`.\n- **So the draft only affects speed.** A bad drafter lowers acceptance and wastes work, but never changes\n  what the target would have produced."},{"q":"With α = 0.8 and γ = 5, how many tokens per target pass do you expect, and what speedup?","a":"- **Tokens:** `(1 − 0.8⁶) / 0.2 ≈ 3.69` per cycle.\n- **Speedup depends on draft cost.** With 2 ms draft steps and a 27 ms verify pass against a 25 ms\n  baseline, about 10 ms per token, roughly 2.5×; with 8 ms draft steps it falls to about 1.4×."},{"q":"Your self-hosted coding agent spends most of each run waiting on decode. Would speculative decoding help?","a":"- **Probably, if concurrency is low.** Agent steps are sequential and their output (tool-call JSON, edits\n  that copy file contents) is predictable, so acceptance is high and per-step latency falls.\n- **Check first:** start with prompt lookup, measure acceptance per step type and p95 time per output\n  token, and confirm per-GPU throughput holds at the pool's real number of concurrent agents."},{"q":"Acceptance is only 20% after you enable a draft model. What do you check?","a":"- **Tokenizer and prompt format.** The drafter must share the tokenizer and see the same chat template, or\n  its token ids and context differ from the target's.\n- **Workload and settings.** High temperature or creative routes lower overlap; compare acceptance by route,\n  try a closer same-family drafter, n-gram lookup on copy-heavy routes, or a smaller γ."},{"q":"Your agent platform serves RAG answers, code edits and open chat. How do you choose drafters?","a":"- **N-gram lookup** costs almost nothing and shines when outputs copy the prompt (RAG quotes, code\n  edits), but helps little on novel text.\n- **A draft model** works across tasks without training, at the cost of extra memory and a shared-tokenizer\n  constraint.\n- **Medusa or EAGLE heads** usually reach higher acceptance with no second model, but must be trained per\n  target, and some Medusa modes are not lossless."},{"q":"How would you pick γ for a production deployment?","a":"- **Sweep it under real load.** Replay production traffic at real concurrency for several values and\n  measure p50/p95 time per output token and per-GPU throughput.\n- **Expect a moving optimum.** Later positions are accepted less often, and verification compute is less\n  idle as batches grow, so the best γ falls with concurrency and must be re-tuned."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fewer target passes vs draft overhead.** Single-request latency drops, but the draft's weights and KV cache take GPU memory and its passes take time. The gain flips negative when acceptance is low or the draft is not much cheaper than the target.","**Lossless vs fastest.** The rejection rule is lossless by construction; simply trusting the draft would be faster but changes outputs. The guarantee costs residual-sampling bookkeeping and cache rollback.","**Separate drafter vs attached heads.** A separate draft model is flexible and reusable across a family; attached heads (Medusa, EAGLE) avoid a second model and achieve higher acceptance, at the cost of training heads per target model.","**Larger γ vs wasted verification.** It raises best-case tokens per pass but spends more verification on positions increasingly likely to be rejected; the optimum shifts lower as batch size grows.","**Draft memory vs agent concurrency.** A draft model's weights and cache take GPU memory that could hold more agents' KV caches; prompt lookup avoids that cost."],"pitfalls":["**Enabling it on a heavily batched server.** Throughput falls: verification tokens compete for compute that was no longer idle, so per-GPU tokens/second drops even as a lone request speeds up. Fix: enable it only on low-concurrency pools, measured at real batch size.","**A draft model with a different tokenizer.** Symptom: near-zero acceptance or outright errors, because draft token ids do not mean the same thing to the target. Fix: use a drafter from the same family with the identical tokenizer.","**Benchmarking on greedy code completion only.** Assuming the speedup holds for creative writing at temperature 1; acceptance falls with entropy and the gain shrinks or inverts. Fix: measure acceptance per route at each route's real sampling settings.","**Forgetting to roll back rejected KV entries.** Symptom: corrupted continuations that reference text the model never emitted. Fix: truncate both caches to the last accepted position every cycle.","**Treating a reported speedup as a property of the method.** It belongs to the draft–target pair and the workload; the same engine setting can help one customer and hurt another. Fix: benchmark on the customer's own prompts before promising a number."],"when":"- **Use when** per-request latency (time per output token) matters more than aggregate throughput:\n  interactive assistants, coding tools, agents making many sequential calls, or any deployment with few\n  concurrent requests per GPU.\n- **Expect the most** from predictable outputs: code, structured data and tool calls, RAG answers that\n  quote context, and templated text; n-gram drafting is nearly free to try there.\n- **Avoid or measure carefully** when the server runs large batches or outputs are high-entropy.\n- **Measure** acceptance rate, tokens per target pass, p50/p95 time per output token and per-GPU\n  throughput before and after, at production concurrency.\n- **Interviews:** the memory-bandwidth argument, the accept/reject rule and why it is lossless, the\n  expected-tokens formula, and the conditions under which the technique backfires.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Enables draft-model or n-gram speculation in the serving engine, tunes γ, and reads acceptance-rate metrics to decide whether it is paying off.","architect":"Decides where speculation belongs in the fleet: latency-sensitive, low-concurrency pools get it, high-throughput batch pools do not, and the draft model becomes a managed artifact.","fde":"Demonstrates the latency gain on a customer's own prompts and warns when their workload's acceptance rate makes it a loss rather than a win."},"check":{"q":"A team enabled speculative decoding with a small draft model and saw a large speedup in their\nsingle-user demo, then deployed it to a shared endpoint and total throughput fell. Explain both results\nand what you would change.","a":"In the demo the batch size was one, so each target pass was memory-bandwidth bound and the\narithmetic units were idle; verifying γ draft tokens per pass used that free compute, and with a good\nacceptance rate they got several tokens per target pass. On the shared endpoint the engine already\nbatches many requests, so the GPU is close to compute-bound; the extra γ verification tokens per\nrequest now consume real compute, and the draft model's passes and memory add further cost, so\naggregate tokens per second drops even though a lone request would still be faster. I would measure\nacceptance rate and per-GPU throughput at production concurrency, lower γ or switch to a cheaper\ndrafter such as n-gram lookup, and most likely restrict speculation to a latency-sensitive pool with\nlow concurrency while leaving the high-throughput pool without it."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can the target model verify several draft tokens for roughly the cost of generating one?","options":["The draft tokens are already in the KV cache","Decode is memory-bandwidth bound, so the weights are read once per pass and scoring extra positions in that pass is nearly free until compute saturates","The target model skips its attention layers during verification","Verification only runs the last transformer layer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A causal model computes distributions at every input position in parallel. When the pass is dominated by reading weights rather than arithmetic, adding a few positions barely changes its duration."},{"q":"What does the accept-with-probability min(1, p/q) rule guarantee?","options":["That at least one draft token is always accepted","That the draft model's output distribution is used when it is confident","That the emitted tokens follow exactly the target model's distribution, making speculation lossless","That greedy decoding is always used for verification"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Combined with sampling from the residual max(0, p − q) on rejection, the rule is rejection sampling from the target distribution using the draft as a proposal, so outputs match the target exactly."},{"q":"Under which condition is speculative decoding most likely to slow a server down?","options":["Low temperature on code completion","A draft model from the same family as the target","High concurrency where each target pass is already compute-bound","Prompts that quote long passages from retrieved documents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Speculation converts idle compute into extra tokens. When batching has already saturated compute, the verification tokens and draft passes cost real time and total throughput can fall."}]},{"key":"model-internals/mixture-of-experts","sources":["Shazeer et al., 2017 — Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer","Fedus, Zoph and Shazeer, 2021 — Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity","Jiang et al., 2024 — Mixtral of Experts","DeepSeek-AI, 2024 — DeepSeek-V3 Technical Report"],"why":"- **Dense models pay for every parameter on every token.** Scaling laws say loss keeps falling as\n  parameters grow, but FLOPs per token grow in lockstep. Doubling the model doubles training compute per\n  token and doubles the weight bytes read on every decode step.\n- **The FFN is the obvious target.** With the usual 4× expansion it holds roughly two thirds of the\n  per-layer non-embedding parameters (8d² for the FFN versus 4d² for the Q, K, V and O projections), yet\n  for any single token most of that capacity is irrelevant. The circuits for Python indentation are not\n  needed while the model writes French prose.\n- **\"Make the dense FFN bigger\" fails at inference too.** Decode is memory-bandwidth bound (see the KV\n  cache lesson), so a model that reads twice the weights per token runs about twice as slowly at small\n  batch, no matter how good the kernels are.\n- **\"Separate specialist models, one per request\" fails too.** The useful choice is per token and per\n  layer, not per request, and separate models cannot share the attention layers and embeddings that carry\n  most of the general language ability.\n- **Conditional computation resolves it.** Keep a large pool of parameters and let a learned router\n  activate a small subset per token. Shazeer et al. made this trainable at scale in 2017; Switch Transformer\n  routed to a single expert and showed better loss per training FLOP than dense models; Mixtral and\n  DeepSeek-V3 made it the default for the largest open-weight systems.","how":"### What the agent builder needs from this section\n- **Two numbers per model.** Total parameters set memory and GPU count; active parameters set per-token\n  compute and, at low concurrency, decode speed for each agent step.\n- **One curve.** As more agent loops share a server, the batch touches more experts until weight traffic\n  approaches the dense total (see \"The batch-size effect\" below).\n- **The rest is background** for when you fine-tune or debug an MoE: routing, load balancing, capacity.\n\n### The layer\n1. **Attention is unchanged** and shared by all tokens.\n2. **The FFN becomes N experts** E₁ … E_N, each shaped like an ordinary FFN (or narrower).\n3. **A router** is one linear map W_r from the hidden state **x** to N logits, turned into a distribution\n   `p = softmax(x·W_r)`.\n4. **The top-k experts are selected** and the output is a weighted sum of their outputs:\n\n```\ny = Σ_{i ∈ topk(p)}  p_i · E_i(x)        (the k weights usually renormalised to sum to 1)\n```\n\n| Model | Routing |\n|---|---|\n| Switch Transformer | k is 1 |\n| Mixtral | k is 2, of 8 experts |\n| DeepSeek-V3 | 8 of 256 small routed experts plus one always-on shared expert: fine-grained specialisation with a common pathway every token passes through |\n\n### How routing behaves and learns\n- **Per token and per layer.** The same token can go to expert 3 in layer 10 and expert 7 in layer 11,\n  and adjacent tokens in a sentence usually take different paths.\n- **Trained end to end** with the rest of the network.\n- **Why outputs are scaled by p_i.** Top-k selection is not differentiable, so gradient reaches the router\n  only through the p_i weights that multiply the chosen experts' outputs. Simply summing the outputs would\n  give the router no learning signal.\n\n### Active versus total parameters\nCount Mixtral 8x7B: 8 experts per layer, top-2 routing.\n\n| Quantity | Value | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Total parameters | about 47B, not 8 × 7 = 56B | attention, embeddings and norms are shared across experts |\n| Active per token | roughly 13B | only 2 of the 8 FFNs run |\n| Tracks the active figure | training FLOPs per token, weight bytes read per decode step at batch 1 | only active experts are computed and read |\n| Quality | far better than a dense 13B | more capacity overall |\n\n- **It is not free capacity.** At equal total parameters a dense model is generally stronger, because\n  every one of its parameters sees every token.\n- **An MoE sits between its active and total sizes.** Buying that position at active-size compute is the\n  whole point of the architecture.\n\n### Load balancing\nLeft alone, routers collapse: experts that are slightly better early receive more tokens, learn faster and\nattract still more, until most experts are dead weight. Two mechanisms push load back toward even, and\ntwo more keep routing stable and bounded:\n- **Auxiliary load-balancing loss (Switch style).** For each expert multiply the fraction of tokens routed\n  to it, f_i, by the mean router probability it received, P_i; sum over experts and scale by N. The sum is\n  minimised when both are uniform, and because P_i is differentiable it pushes the router toward\n  spreading tokens.\n- **Bias-based balancing (DeepSeek-V3).** Add a per-expert bias to the scores used for top-k selection\n  (not to the gating weights) and nudge it after each step: up for underloaded experts, down for\n  overloaded ones. That avoids most of the quality penalty a strong auxiliary loss costs; only a very small\n  sequence-level balance loss remains.\n- **Router stability.** Router logits can grow until the softmax saturates and training diverges. ST-MoE\n  (Zoph et al., 2022) added a small \"router z-loss\" that penalises large logits. Routers are commonly\n  computed in fp32 even when the model runs in bf16, because tiny rounding differences flip top-k choices.\n- **Expert capacity.** Each expert accepts at most `capacity_factor × tokens / N` tokens per batch.\n  Overflow tokens are dropped from that expert (they pass through the residual connection only) or sent to\n  their next choice. This exists because experts run as fixed-size batched matrix multiplications, often on\n  different devices, and one overloaded expert would stall all the others.\n\n### Routing variants\n| Variant | How | Consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Token choice | each token picks its top-k experts | balance must be encouraged; used by open decoder LLMs |\n| Noisy top-k (Shazeer et al.) | tunable noise added to router logits before top-k | rarely chosen experts still get sampled and can learn |\n| Expert choice | each expert takes its top-scoring tokens from the batch up to a fixed capacity | balanced by construction, but a token can get zero or many experts, and its routing depends on other tokens in the batch |\n\n- **Why decoders use token choice.** Batch dependence is awkward for autoregressive decoding, where the\n  batch is a handful of unrelated requests.\n\n### Why memory and parallelism change\n- **Every expert must be resident,** because the next token might be routed anywhere. Mixtral 8x7B needs\n  about 47B × 2 bytes ≈ 94 GB in fp16 for weights alone, more than one 80 GB card, even though it\n  computes like a 13B model.\n- **Expert parallelism.** At larger scale each layer's experts are spread across devices, and an\n  all-to-all exchange moves each token's hidden state to the devices hosting its chosen experts and back.\n- **That communication, twice per MoE layer,** is the dominant engineering cost of training and serving\n  MoE models.\n\n### The batch-size effect at inference\n1. **At batch 1** a decode step reads only the k active experts per layer, so an MoE is far faster than a\n   dense model of equal total size.\n2. **As the batch grows,** different tokens pick different experts until every expert is touched on every\n   step.\n3. **Weight traffic then approaches the dense total,** and the advantage shifts from bandwidth to FLOPs.\n4. **So capacity planning** for an MoE must be done at the concurrency you will actually run.","example":"### A minimal top-k MoE layer with a Switch-style balancing loss\n- **Sizes.** With `d_model=512, d_ff=2048` one FFN has 2 × 512 × 2048 ≈ 2.1M parameters; eight experts\n  hold ≈ 16.8M in total, but each token runs through two, so ≈ 4.2M are active per token.\n- **Expected output.** With f_i normalised to sum to 1, `aux` is about 1.0 when tokens and probability\n  are spread evenly and rises toward N as routing concentrates on one expert. An untrained router is close\n  to uniform, so printing it on random inputs should give a value near 1.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn as nn, torch.nn.functional as F\n\nclass MoELayer(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d_model=512, d_ff=2048, n_experts=8, k=2):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.k, self.n = k, n_experts\n        self.router = nn.Linear(d_model, n_experts, bias=False)\n        self.experts = nn.ModuleList(\n            nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(d_model, d_ff), nn.SiLU(), nn.Linear(d_ff, d_model))\n            for _ in range(n_experts))\n\n    def forward(self, x):                                  # x: [tokens, d_model]\n        probs = self.router(x).softmax(-1)                 # [tokens, n_experts]\n        topv, topi = probs.topk(self.k, dim=-1)            # [tokens, k]\n        topv = topv / topv.sum(-1, keepdim=True)           # renormalise over the chosen k\n        out = torch.zeros_like(x)\n        for e in range(self.n):                            # one batched matmul per expert\n            hit = (topi == e)                              # [tokens, k] bool\n            rows = hit.any(-1).nonzero(as_tuple=True)[0]\n            if rows.numel() == 0:\n                continue\n            w = (topv * hit)[rows].sum(-1, keepdim=True)   # router weight for expert e\n            out[rows] += w * self.experts[e](x[rows])      # gradient reaches the router via w\n        frac = F.one_hot(topi, self.n).float().sum(1).mean(0) / self.k   # f_i: share of routings per expert\n        mean_p = probs.mean(0)                                  # P_i: mean router probability per expert\n        aux = self.n * (frac * mean_p).sum()                    # balancing loss, add lambda * aux to LM loss\n        return out, aux\n\nlayer = MoELayer()\ny, aux = layer(torch.randn(4096, 512))\nprint(y.shape, round(aux.item(), 3))\nprint(sum(p.numel() for p in layer.experts.parameters()) / 1e6, \"M expert params total\")\n```\n\n- **Not production code.** Production kernels do not loop over experts in Python; they sort tokens by\n  expert and run grouped matrix multiplications, which is why engines such as vLLM and SGLang ship fused\n  MoE kernels.\n\n### Worked scenario: how the batch erodes the MoE bandwidth advantage\nHow many of Mixtral's 8 experts per layer does one decode step touch? Assume each token picks 2 of 8\nuniformly and independently. Real routing is skewed and correlated, so treat this as an illustrative upper\nbound on spread, not a benchmark.\n\n```python\nfrom math import comb\n\ndef experts_touched(batch, n=8, k=2):\n    p_miss = comb(n - 1, k) / comb(n, k)        # chance one token skips a given expert: 0.75\n    return n * (1 - p_miss ** batch)\n\n# Back out per-expert size from 47B total and 13B active: 6 experts' worth = 34B\nexpert_b, shared_b = (47 - 13) / 6, 13 - 2 * (47 - 13) / 6   # ~5.7B per expert, ~1.7B shared\nfor b in (1, 4, 8, 16):\n    t = experts_touched(b)\n    gb = (shared_b + t * expert_b) * 2           # fp16 weight bytes read per step\n    print(f\"batch {b:>2}: {t:.1f} experts/layer, ~{gb:.0f} GB weights read\")\n```\n\n| Concurrent sequences | Experts touched per layer | fp16 weights read per step |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 2.0 | ~26 GB (the 13B active figure) |\n| 4 | 5.5 | ~65 GB |\n| 8 | 7.2 | ~85 GB |\n| 16 | 7.9 | ~93 GB (nearly the 94 GB dense total) |\n\n- **Reading.** By a batch of about 8 the step reads most of the model, so the comparison with a dense\n  model shifts from bandwidth to FLOPs, where the MoE still does only active-size arithmetic per token.\n\n### Agent scenario: one coding agent versus a shared agent platform\nA team self-hosts Mixtral 8x7B in fp16 on two 80 GB GPUs for a 20-step agent that emits ~300 tokens per\nstep (6,000 decode tokens per task). Latency floors below assume a conservative ~2 TB/s of usable\nbandwidth and ignore KV-cache reads: illustrative orders of magnitude, not a benchmark.\n\n| Deployment | Agent loops in flight | Weights read per step | Floor per token | Decode floor per 20-step task |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Developer's private agent | 1 | ~26 GB | ~13 ms | ~78 s |\n| Team platform | 16 | ~93 GB | ~47 ms | ~280 s, shared by 16 loops |\n\n- **Reading.** For one loop the MoE behaves like a 13B model; at 16 loops each step costs about what a\n  dense 47B model would, though aggregate throughput is still far higher than serving 16 loops one at a time.\n- **Decision.** Benchmark at the platform's real concurrency before promising \"13B-speed\" agents, and put\n  latency-sensitive single-user agents on their own replica if the platform is busy.","patterns":["**Pick agent models on your agent eval, not the parameter count.** Run candidate MoE and dense models through the same tool-calling eval (valid calls, task success, steps per task) and compare cost per successful task at your concurrency. Monitor tool-call validity per model version after every upgrade.","**Size memory from total parameters.** Before promising hardware, compute weights as total parameters × bytes per weight, add the KV cache at target concurrency, and keep headroom. For Mixtral in fp16 that means two 80 GB GPUs, for example `--tensor-parallel-size 2` in vLLM. Monitor peak GPU memory and preemptions under load.","**Use an engine with fused MoE kernels and expert parallelism.** Serve with vLLM or SGLang rather than a plain PyTorch loop; on multi-GPU nodes try expert parallelism (vLLM has an `--enable-expert-parallel` option) and compare it with tensor parallelism. Monitor tokens per second and interconnect utilisation.","**Benchmark at production concurrency.** Load-test at the batch sizes you will really run, not batch 1, because weight traffic per step rises until all experts are read. For agents, load-test with realistic concurrent loops and track p95 wall-clock per task, plus time per output token and throughput.","**Quantise weights to fit fewer cards.** 8-bit roughly halves the 94 GB fp16 footprint and 4-bit roughly quarters it. Accept only after running your own eval set, and keep room for the KV cache.","**Watch the router when fine-tuning.** Log expert-utilisation histograms per layer, keep the balancing loss on (Hugging Face Mixtral exposes `output_router_logits` and `router_aux_loss_coef`), or freeze the router weights on small datasets. Alert on a few hot experts and many near zero."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agents make many sequential model calls, so per-token decode speed\n  multiplies by step count. An MoE gives near-large-model quality at small-model speed for a single loop,\n  but GPU memory is sized by total parameters, not the active count on the datasheet.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Model cards that list \"total\" and \"active\" parameters (Mixtral,\n  DeepSeek-V3), vLLM or SGLang flags for tensor and expert parallelism, and the GPU count your self-hosted\n  agent tier needs.\n- **What you control.** Which model backs which agent role, how many GPUs and which quantisation you deploy,\n  and the concurrency you load-test at. You almost never train or re-route an MoE yourself.\n- **What you can skip.** Balancing-loss maths, router z-loss and expert-choice routing, unless you\n  fine-tune or continue pre-training an MoE. Know active versus total and the batch-size effect.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Self-hosted agents with data-residency rules.** Teams that cannot call a hosted API pick an open MoE to\n  get frontier-adjacent planning and tool use on their own GPUs.\n- **Capacity planning for an agent platform.** Total parameters set the GPU count; the batch-size curve\n  sets how many concurrent agent loops each replica can carry within the per-task latency budget.\n- **Model upgrades behind an agent.** Swapping a dense model for an MoE (or back) is decided by the agent\n  eval plus a load test, not by the headline parameter count.\n- **Choosing a model per agent role.** A large MoE for the planner and a small dense model for cheap\n  sub-steps such as summarising tool output.","mini_project":"**Measure the MoE batch-size effect on an agent loop (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show how per-task latency of a multi-step agent changes as concurrent loops grow on an MoE\n  versus a dense model of similar active size.\n- **Steps:** serve a small open MoE and a dense model with vLLM (for example OLMoE-1B-7B, about 1B active\n  and 7B total, against a dense 1-2B model; with no GPU, model the effect with the `experts_touched` script\n  above); write a 10-step agent with two fake tools; run 1, 4, 8 and 16 loops concurrently and\n  log time per step and per task.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of p50 and p95 per-task latency by concurrency for both models, and a\n  short note stating at what loop count the MoE stops looking like its active size."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"An MoE needs only as much GPU memory as its active parameters.\"** Any token can be routed to any expert, so all experts must be resident: Mixtral 8x7B needs about 94 GB of fp16 weights even though it computes like a roughly 13B model.","**\"8x7B means a 56B model.\"** Attention, embeddings and norms are shared, so the total is about 47B. Total governs memory; active (about 13B) governs per-token compute and batch-1 decode speed.","**\"An MoE decodes at small-model speed however many agent loops share it.\"** That holds near batch 1. As concurrent loops grow, the batch touches nearly every expert per step, so weight traffic approaches the dense total and the bandwidth edge mostly disappears.","**\"Each expert is a topic specialist you could prune.\"** Routing is per token and per layer and tracks token type and syntax far more than domain, so there is no \"legal expert\" to keep and no \"code expert\" to drop.","**\"The router just picks experts; their outputs are summed.\"** Outputs are weighted by router probabilities because top-k selection is not differentiable; those weights are the router's only gradient path, and without balancing it collapses onto a few experts."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    X[\"Token hidden state x\"] --> AT[\"Shared attention\"]\n    AT --> R[\"Router: softmax of x times W_r\"]\n    R -->|\"top-k, k = 1 or 2\"| S[\"Selected experts\"]\n    S --> E1[\"Expert i\"]\n    S --> E2[\"Expert j\"]\n    R -.->|\"weights p_i, p_j\"| M[\"Weighted sum\"]\n    E1 --> M\n    E2 --> M\n    M --> Y[\"Residual add, next layer\"]\n    R -.->|\"balance loss or bias\"| B[\"Even expert load\"]","caption":"Follow one token through an MoE layer: shared attention, then the router picks top-k experts whose\noutputs are mixed by their router probabilities."},"interview":[{"q":"You self-host an MoE for an internal agent platform. Pilots felt fast, production tasks are slow. What happened?","a":"- **The batch-size effect.** One pilot user meant each decode step read only the active experts; many\n  concurrent agent loops touch every expert, so weight traffic per step approaches the dense total.\n- **Agents amplify it.** A 20-step task pays the slower per-token cost on every step, so per-task latency\n  grows far more than a single chat would.\n- **Fix:** load-test at real loop counts, add replicas or a dedicated low-latency replica, and quote p95\n  per task rather than batch-1 tokens per second."},{"q":"How would you choose between an MoE and a dense model to back a tool-calling agent?","a":"- **Start from the eval.** Run both on your agent tasks and score tool-call validity, task success and\n  steps per task; parameter counts do not predict tool use.\n- **Then the hardware.** Size memory from total parameters plus the KV cache for long agent transcripts,\n  and check whether one card is enough for the dense option.\n- **Then cost per successful task** at the concurrency you will run, since the MoE speed edge depends on it."},{"q":"Why is Mixtral 8x7B about 47B parameters rather than 56B, and what do the active and total counts each govern?","a":"- **Shared parts are counted once.** Only the FFNs are replicated into 8 experts; attention, embeddings\n  and norms are shared, so the total is about 47B, not 8 × 7.\n- **Total governs memory.** Every expert must be resident because any token may route anywhere, so fp16\n  weights are about 94 GB.\n- **Active governs compute.** With top-2 routing roughly 13B parameters run per token, which sets training\n  FLOPs per token and weight bytes read per decode step at batch 1."},{"q":"Your continued pre-training run on an MoE plateaus early. How do you check for router collapse, and how do you fix it?","a":"- **Look at routing, not just loss.** Plot per-layer expert-utilisation histograms; a few hot experts and\n  many near zero means early winners attracted most tokens and the rest stopped learning.\n- **Fix the balance.** Restore or raise the auxiliary load-balancing loss, or use DeepSeek-style\n  per-expert selection biases that are nudged toward uniform load.\n- **Check stability too.** Add a router z-loss against saturating logits and compute the router in fp32,\n  because rounding in bf16 flips top-k choices."},{"q":"When can an MoE be slower or more expensive to serve than a dense model?","a":"- **At high concurrency.** Once the batch touches every expert, each step reads close to the full weight\n  set, so the bandwidth advantage over a dense model of equal total size disappears.\n- **On weak interconnects.** Expert parallelism adds an all-to-all exchange twice per MoE layer, which can\n  cancel the compute savings.\n- **Without fused kernels or on one card.** A Python loop over experts is slow, and a dense model that fits\n  the same VRAM may beat an MoE squeezed in by heavy quantisation."},{"q":"What does the capacity factor control, and what happens if you set it too low?","a":"- **It caps tokens per expert** at `capacity_factor × tokens / N` per batch, because experts run as\n  fixed-size batched matrix multiplications and one overloaded expert would stall the rest.\n- **Too low drops tokens.** Overflow tokens skip that expert and pass through the residual connection only,\n  so quality quietly degrades on unusual token mixes such as code in a chat model or a rare language."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fast single agent vs busy shared platform.** One agent loop gets near-large-model quality at active-size speed, but as many loops share the server the per-step cost climbs toward a dense model of total size; it flips once concurrency touches every expert.","**Quality per training FLOP vs weight memory.** Capacity grows without proportional compute, but memory grows with total parameters. When a single GPU's VRAM is the binding constraint, a dense model with the same memory footprint can be the better choice.","**Fast small-batch decode vs concurrency.** Decode at small batch beats a dense model of equal total size because only k experts are read per layer, but the gain shrinks as concurrency rises and every expert is touched on every step.","**Expert parallelism vs communication.** Total parameters can exceed any one device, at the cost of an all-to-all exchange in every MoE layer; on slow interconnects that communication can cancel the compute savings.","**Capacity vs fine-tuning stability.** Fine-tuning an MoE on a small dataset is reported to be less stable than a dense model, because the router can drift and overfit. Freezing the router or using more data helps, but frequent per-customer fine-tunes are simpler with dense models.","**Fine-grained experts vs kernel overhead.** Many narrow experts plus a shared expert (the DeepSeek design) specialise more finely than a few wide ones, but produce more small matrix multiplications and more routing overhead, which GPUs handle efficiently only with fused kernels."],"pitfalls":["**Promising agent latency from a single-user demo.** Symptom: the agent felt fast in the pilot, then each task takes several times longer once a team shares the server. Fix: load-test with the real number of concurrent agent loops and quote per-task p95.","**Sizing serving hardware from active parameters.** Symptom: an out-of-memory error at model load, because all experts must be resident regardless of how few run per token. Fix: size from total.","**Reading \"8x7B\" as a 56B model.** Benchmarking it against dense 56B models is wrong too: shared attention makes it about 47B total and 13B active, and the two numbers answer different questions. Fix: quote both and say which one each comparison uses.","**Expecting human-interpretable specialists.** \"The code expert\" and \"the legal expert\" do not exist: the Mixtral authors found routing correlates with token type and syntax far more than with topic, so you cannot prune \"the experts you don't need\" by domain. Fix: keep every expert loaded.","**Setting the capacity factor too low to save memory.** Symptom: quality degrades on inputs with an unusual token mix (code in a chat model, a rare language), because overflow tokens are silently dropped. Fix: log the dropped-token rate and raise the factor or route overflow to the next choice.","**Router collapse during training or continued pre-training.** Symptom: an expert-utilisation histogram with a few hot experts and many near zero, plus a loss plateau the parameter count does not explain. Fix: restore the balancing loss or bias and add a router z-loss.","**Extrapolating batch-1 throughput to production.** Weight traffic per step rises with batch until all experts are read, so the dense-versus-MoE comparison can invert. Fix: benchmark at real concurrency."],"when":"You rarely decide to train an MoE; you decide whether to deploy one behind your agents.\n- **Use for agents when:** a self-hosted agent needs top open-weight quality for planning and tool calls,\n  and step latency matters more than squeezing onto one card.\n- **Use when:** you need top-tier quality from open weights, you have a multi-GPU node or enough host\n  memory, and your traffic leaves a bandwidth advantage at the batch sizes you run.\n- **Prefer dense when:** you are on a single card, you fine-tune often on small data, or your serving stack\n  lacks fused MoE kernels and expert-parallel support (a homegrown PyTorch loop will be slow).\n- **Measure:** tokens per second at your real concurrency, peak memory including the KV cache, and quality\n  on your own eval set rather than the model card.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"why is Mixtral 47B and not 56B\", \"what do active versus total parameters\n  govern\", \"why is load balancing needed and how does it work\", and \"when can an MoE be slower than a dense\n  model\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Reads the model card for total and active parameters, picks an engine with fused MoE kernels, and verifies memory from weights plus KV cache before promising a GPU count.","architect":"Chooses between MoE and dense for the open-weight tier from VRAM, interconnect, concurrency profile and fine-tuning frequency, and writes the batch-size effect into the capacity plan.","fde":"Explains to a customer why the \"13B-speed\" model on the datasheet still needs two 80 GB GPUs in their data centre, and why single-user latency numbers will not hold under shared load."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants to self-host a hypothetical open MoE listed as \"about 140B total, 17B active\" on\ntheir existing single 80 GB GPU because \"it runs like a 17B model\". What do you tell them, and what would\nchange your answer?","a":"Active parameters describe compute and bandwidth per token, not memory. All 140B parameters must be\nresident because any token can be routed to any expert, so fp16 weights alone are about 280 GB, and even\n4-bit quantisation (roughly 70 GB) leaves almost no room for the KV cache on an 80 GB card. Their options\nare a multi-GPU node with expert or tensor parallelism, aggressive quantisation plus expert offloading to\nCPU memory with a large latency penalty, or a dense model in the 13–30B range that fits the card. The speed\nclaim also holds only at low concurrency; as batch grows, every expert is read each step. I would change the\nrecommendation if they have a multi-GPU box with fast interconnect, or if their concurrency is near one and\nthey accept quantised weights."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does an MoE layer multiply each chosen expert's output by its router probability instead of simply summing the chosen outputs?","options":["To keep the output magnitude equal to that of a dense FFN","Because top-k selection is not differentiable, so the multiplication is the only path through which the router receives gradient","To penalise experts that receive too many tokens in a batch","Because experts have different hidden sizes and must be rescaled"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The hard top-k choice has no gradient. Scaling each expert's output by its softmax probability makes the router's parameters part of the differentiable graph, so it can learn which expert helps. Load balancing is handled separately by the auxiliary loss or bias terms, not by this scaling."},{"q":"Mixtral 8x7B has about 47B parameters but computes like a roughly 13B model. Which statement about serving it is correct?","options":["It fits wherever a 13B fp16 model fits, since only 13B parameters are used per token","Its KV cache is eight times larger than that of a 13B dense model","All 47B parameters must be in memory, but at small batch each decode step reads only the two active experts per layer","It needs memory for 56B parameters because there are eight 7B experts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Memory is set by total parameters because routing can pick any expert; compute and weight traffic per token at low batch are set by the active parameters. The total is 47B rather than 56B because attention and embeddings are shared, and the KV cache depends on attention shape, which the experts do not change."},{"q":"What failure does the load-balancing auxiliary loss prevent during training, and what is the observable symptom without it?","options":["Vanishing gradients; the symptom is a loss that never decreases","Router collapse onto a few experts; the symptom is a skewed expert-utilisation histogram and a loss plateau despite the parameter count","Overfitting of the attention layers; the symptom is a train/validation gap","Token dropping from capacity overflow; the symptom is NaN losses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without a balancing term, early winners attract more tokens and the rest of the experts stop learning, so the model behaves like a much smaller dense network. Capacity overflow is a related but separate mechanism handled by the capacity factor, and it drops tokens rather than producing NaNs."}]},{"key":"model-internals/small-language-models","sources":["Hoffmann et al., 2022 — Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models","Gunasekar et al., 2023 — Textbooks Are All You Need","Hinton, Vinyals and Dean, 2015 — Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network","Liu et al., 2024 — MobileLLM: Optimizing Sub-billion Parameter Language Models for On-Device Use Cases"],"why":"- **Most production requests are narrow.** A ticket classifier, entity extractor, query rewriter or PII\n  redactor needs one skill executed millions of times at low latency and cost, not encyclopaedic knowledge.\n- **Inference cost scales with parameters.** Every decode step reads every active weight, so a 70B model\n  moves about 20× the bytes of a 3.5B model for the same output token, and prefill FLOPs scale the same way.\n- **Small models got much better.** Chinchilla showed earlier small models were badly under-trained, and\n  Llama showed loss keeps falling far past the compute-optimal token count. Distillation and synthetic\n  \"textbook\" data (the Phi line) or teacher-generated corpora give far more signal per token than raw web\n  text.\n- **Deployment constraints are real.** On-device assistants, air-gapped networks, data-residency rules and\n  offline operation cannot call a hosted frontier API.\n- **The naive alternatives fail.** \"Just prompt the big model\" fails on cost, time-to-first-token,\n  availability and privacy. \"Just quantise the big model\" buys roughly 4× in memory, not the 20× that\n  separates a 70B from a 3B, and still needs a server GPU. A well-trained small model, fed facts through\n  retrieval and fine-tuned to the task, actually fits.","how":"### Where SLMs sit in an agent\n| Agent step | Good SLM fit? | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intent or tool routing over a fixed set | yes | narrow classification, labelled data easy to collect |\n| Query rewriting before retrieval | yes | short input, short output, facts not needed |\n| Extracting fields from a tool result | yes | the answer is in the input |\n| Guardrails and PII redaction | yes | high volume, latency-critical |\n| Open-ended planning over many tools | usually no | multi-step composition degrades fastest with size |\n| Answering from memory | no | long-tail facts shrink first |\n\n### What \"small\" changes architecturally\n- **Same decoder-only transformer,** with fewer layers, a smaller hidden size, and almost always\n  grouped-query attention.\n- **The embedding matrix becomes a big share.** A 128k vocabulary with a 2,048-dimensional hidden state is\n  about 262M parameters, roughly a fifth of a 1.2B model. So input and output embeddings are usually tied\n  (one shared matrix) to avoid paying twice.\n- **Depth is kept where possible.** MobileLLM reported that deep-and-thin layouts beat shallow-and-wide\n  ones at sub-billion scale, because reasoning-like composition benefits from more sequential layers.\n\n### Training recipe\n- **Over-training.** Chinchilla-optimal is about 20 training tokens per parameter. Llama 3 8B was trained\n  on more than 15 trillion tokens, close to 2,000 tokens per parameter. The loss curve flattens but keeps\n  improving, and since serving cost per token is about 2N FLOPs, a smaller N that will serve trillions of\n  tokens justifies the extra training. Inference dominates a popular model's lifetime cost, so the extra\n  training compute is repaid many times over.\n- **Data quality.** With few parameters every one must earn its place, so small models lean on heavily\n  filtered web data, code, and synthetic or curated educational text.\n- **Distillation.** The student matches the teacher's full probability distribution (a KL term against\n  teacher logits), which carries information about which wrong answers are nearly right. Or it trains on\n  teacher-generated outputs (sequence-level distillation). Reasoning distillation fine-tunes a small model\n  on long chain-of-thought traces from a reasoning model and transfers a surprising share of the capability.\n- **Pruning from a larger model.** Remove whole layers, attention heads or FFN channels from a trained\n  larger model, chosen by an importance score on calibration data, then recover quality with a short\n  distillation run against the original. Sheared-LLaMA and NVIDIA's Minitron used this route because the\n  starting weights already encode much of what the small model must learn, so far fewer training tokens are\n  needed than training the same size from random initialisation.\n- **Post-training.** The same supervised fine-tuning and preference tuning as large models, with less\n  headroom: a small model follows complex, multi-constraint instructions less reliably.\n\n### What is lost, and why\nParameters are memory. Long-tail facts live in FFN weights, so fewer and smaller FFNs mean fewer facts,\nstored less reliably.\n\n| Weakness | Compensation (architectural, not model-level) |\n|---|---|\n| Hallucinates more on factual recall | supply the facts (retrieval) |\n| Degrades faster on multi-step composition | supply the computation (tools) |\n| More sensitive to prompt wording | narrow the job (fine-tuning) |\n| Loses more quality under aggressive quantisation | put a larger model behind a router for the cases that need it |\n\n### The inference arithmetic\n- **Weights cost `params × bytes_per_param`.** A 3B model is 6 GB in bf16, 3 GB in int8, and about 2 GB\n  at 4-bit once scales are included.\n- **Decode is bandwidth-bound,** so single-stream tokens per second is roughly memory bandwidth divided by\n  bytes read per step.\n\n| Device (illustrative orders of magnitude) | Bandwidth | 2 GB model, single stream |\n|---|---|---|\n| Laptop GPU | a few hundred GB/s | moves the weights over 100 times a second, so 100+ tokens/s is plausible |\n| Phone | tens of GB/s | tens of tokens/s |\n| Server GPU | several TB/s | weights almost free per request; KV cache and batching become the limits |\n\n- **So both claims hold, for different reasons:** \"runs on a phone\" and \"cheap on a server\".\n\n### Specialist versus generalist\nA fine-tuned 1–8B model routinely matches or beats a zero-shot frontier model on a narrow task when three\nconditions hold:\n1. **The task is well defined.**\n2. **You have thousands of labelled examples.**\n3. **Production inputs look like the training data.**\n\nIt does not when the task is open-ended, needs novel reasoning, or depends on world knowledge the\nfine-tuning set and the prompt do not contain.","example":"### Agent scenario: which steps of a research agent move to an SLM\nA research agent makes 10 model calls per task. The planner stays on a frontier model; narrow steps move\nto a self-hosted 3B, with 10% escalated back. All prices are illustrative placeholders, not quotes.\n\n```python\nFRONTIER = (3.0, 15.0)      # placeholder $ per million input / output tokens\nSLM = (0.10, 0.40)          # placeholder $ per million tokens for a self-hosted 3B\nsteps = [                   # (name, calls per task, input tokens, output tokens, runs on)\n    (\"plan / choose tool\", 3, 4000, 300, \"frontier\"),\n    (\"rewrite search query\", 2, 600, 40, \"slm\"),\n    (\"extract fields from tool result\", 3, 1500, 80, \"slm\"),\n    (\"summarise long tool output\", 2, 3000, 150, \"slm\"),\n]\nESCALATE = 0.10             # share of SLM calls that fail the margin or schema check\n\ndef cost(price, n, i, o):\n    return n * (i * price[0] + o * price[1]) / 1e6\n\nall_frontier = sum(cost(FRONTIER, n, i, o) for _, n, i, o, _ in steps)\nmixed = 0.0\nfor _, n, i, o, where in steps:\n    if where == \"frontier\":\n        mixed += cost(FRONTIER, n, i, o)\n    else:   # every call runs on the SLM, escalated ones also pay the frontier price\n        mixed += cost(SLM, n, i, o) + ESCALATE * cost(FRONTIER, n, i, o)\nprint(f\"all frontier ${all_frontier:.4f}/task, mixed ${mixed:.4f}/task, \"\n      f\"saving {1 - mixed / all_frontier:.0%}\")\n# all frontier $0.0939/task, mixed $0.0554/task, saving 41%\n```\n\n- **Reading.** Seven of ten calls moved, but the saving is only 41%: the three planner calls carry about\n  half the tokens at frontier prices, so they are about 89% of the mixed bill. The next lever is a shorter\n  planner prompt (stable prefix, prompt caching), not more SLM steps.\n- **Latency.** The SLM steps also return faster, so wall-clock per task drops on every step that moved.\n\n### Cost model and escalation rule for a ticket classifier\nA support organisation receives 2 million tickets a month, classifies each into 40 queues and extracts\nthree fields. All prices and rates are illustrative placeholders to show the shape of the calculation, not\nquotes.\n\n```python\ntickets = 2_000_000\nin_tok, out_tok = 700, 60                       # per ticket\napi_in, api_out = 3.0, 15.0                     # placeholder $ per million tokens\ngpu_hour = 1.0                                  # placeholder $ per GPU-hour, one 24 GB card\n\napi = tickets * (in_tok * api_in + out_tok * api_out) / 1e6\nslm = 730 * gpu_hour                            # one card running all month\nprint(f\"API ${api:,.0f}/mo, SLM ${slm:,.0f}/mo, {api / slm:.1f}x cheaper\")\nprint(f\"avg load {tickets / (30 * 86_400):.2f} tickets/s\")\n\ndef route(probs, schema_ok, margin=0.2):\n    \"\"\"Escalate when the top-two class gap is small or the output fails validation.\"\"\"\n    top2 = sorted(probs, reverse=True)[:2]\n    return \"slm\" if schema_ok and top2[0] - top2[1] >= margin else \"frontier\"\n\nprint(route([0.81, 0.10, 0.09], schema_ok=True))   # slm\nprint(route([0.45, 0.40, 0.15], schema_ok=True))   # frontier\n```\n\n- **Margin threshold.** The `0.2` value is a placeholder; set it on held-out data so the tickets kept on\n  the SLM meet the accuracy target.\n\n### Worked scenario: frontier API versus fine-tuned 3B\nEach ticket is about 700 input tokens and 60 output tokens, so the monthly volume is 1.4B input and 120M\noutput tokens.\n\n| | Frontier API path | SLM path |\n|---|---|---|\n| Model | hosted frontier model | 3B instruct model, LoRA fine-tune on 8,000 labelled tickets (a few GPU-hours) |\n| Serving | API calls | bf16 (6 GB of weights) on one 24 GB GPU, batches of 32–64 fit easily because sequences are short and the KV cache small |\n| Monthly cost | $3 per million input + $15 per million output = $4,200 + $1,800 = $6,000 | about $730 at $1 per GPU-hour, roughly 8× cheaper |\n| Latency | multi-second per ticket | sub-second |\n| Data | tickets leave the company's network | no data egress |\n\n- **Load fits easily.** Average load is 2M / (30 × 86,400 s) ≈ 0.8 tickets per second, with bursts an\n  order of magnitude higher, well inside what one card handles with continuous batching.\n- **Decide on the golden set, not the invoice.** Run both models on 1,000 held-out tickets. Ship the SLM if\n  it is within the accuracy tolerance the business accepts and its errors are not concentrated in the\n  expensive queues.\n- **Route the remainder.** Low-confidence cases (a small logit margin between the top two classes, or a\n  schema-validation failure) go to the frontier model. In practice that is a minority of traffic, so the\n  blended cost stays close to the SLM figure while the quality ceiling is preserved.\n\n### Logit distillation loss\nThe classic soft-target loss: the student matches the teacher's temperature-softened distribution, scaled\nby T² so gradients keep their size as T changes.\n\n```python\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\ndef distill_loss(student_logits, teacher_logits, labels, T=2.0, alpha=0.5):\n    soft = F.kl_div(F.log_softmax(student_logits / T, -1),\n                    F.softmax(teacher_logits / T, -1),\n                    reduction=\"batchmean\") * (T * T)\n    hard = F.cross_entropy(student_logits, labels)\n    return alpha * soft + (1 - alpha) * hard\n```","patterns":["**Split the agent by step type.** Keep planning and tool selection on a strong model and move narrow steps (query rewriting, extraction, tool-output summaries, guardrails) to an SLM. Log model, tokens and latency per step so you can see which steps carry the cost before moving more.","**Validate SLM output before the agent acts on it.** Parse tool arguments against the tool's JSON schema and retry or escalate on failure, because small models break formats more often. Monitor schema-failure and escalation rates per step.","**Cascade with confidence escalation.** Serve the SLM first and escalate on a small top-two class probability margin or a failed schema check, as in the `route` function above. Tune the threshold on held-out data and monitor the escalation rate, accuracy on SLM-kept traffic, and blended cost per task.","**Per-task LoRA with an eval gate.** Fine-tune adapters with Hugging Face PEFT (`LoraConfig` with a rank such as 16) on thousands of labelled examples, version each adapter, and block release unless it beats the current one on the golden set. Monitor drift between production inputs and training data.","**Batch on servers, quantise at the edge.** On servers use continuous batching in vLLM and raise `--max-num-seqs` so each weight read serves many requests. On devices ship a 4-bit GGUF build for llama.cpp. Re-run JSON-validity and multilingual evals after every quantisation step.","**Retrieval for facts, SLM for form.** Put the source text in the prompt and ask the SLM to extract, classify or format, never to recall. Monitor groundedness by checking extracted values appear in the source.","**Keep prompts short.** Fine-tune the instructions into the weights instead of carrying a long system prompt, and cap retrieved context. Track prefill latency and accuracy by input length."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent makes many model calls per task, and most are narrow: classify\n  the intent, rewrite the query, extract fields from a tool result. Running those on an SLM and keeping a\n  frontier model for planning cuts cost and step latency several times over.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Model routers and cascades, guardrail and PII classifiers, RAG query\n  rewriters and rerank-style steps, on-device or air-gapped agents, and LoRA adapters served with vLLM or\n  llama.cpp.\n- **What you control.** Which steps go to which model, the escalation rule (logit margin, schema check),\n  how facts reach the SLM (retrieval, tool results), and the eval gate each fine-tune must pass.\n- **What you can skip.** Pruning recipes and embedding-tying details unless you train SLMs yourself. Keep\n  the rule \"SLM for form, retrieval and tools for facts\".","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Cheap sub-steps in a multi-model agent.** Query rewriting, field extraction and tool-output\n  summaries run on a 1–8B model while the planner stays on a frontier model.\n- **Intent and tool routers.** A fine-tuned SLM picks the workflow or sub-agent for each request in tens\n  of milliseconds.\n- **Guardrails in the loop.** PII redaction and policy classifiers run on every message and tool result\n  without adding a frontier-sized bill.\n- **On-device and air-gapped agents.** Field, factory or regulated deployments where no data may leave the\n  device or network.","mini_project":"**Move one agent step to an SLM behind an eval gate (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** cut cost per task of a small RAG agent by moving its query-rewriting step to a local SLM\n  without lowering answer quality.\n- **Steps:** build a 3-step agent (rewrite query, retrieve, answer); log tokens and latency per step;\n  build 50 test questions with expected sources; swap the rewriter for a 1–3B model served locally (for\n  example llama.cpp or vLLM) and add a fallback to the original model when the rewrite is empty or invalid.\n- **Done when:** a table shows retrieval hit rate, final answer accuracy, cost and p95 latency per task for\n  both setups, with the fallback rate, and you can state whether the SLM version ships."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"An SLM is just a frontier model that is a bit worse at everything.\"** Losses are uneven: long-tail factual recall and multi-step composition shrink first, while narrow, well-defined skills such as classification or extraction can match a zero-shot frontier model after fine-tuning.","**\"Quantising a 70B model gets you the same savings.\"** 4-bit buys roughly 4× in memory, not the 20× that separates a 70B from a 3B, and the quantised 70B still needs a server GPU.","**\"Fine-tuning an SLM on our documents teaches it our facts.\"** Few parameters means few facts stored reliably, so memorised answers come out confidently wrong. Feed facts through retrieval or tool results and fine-tune for the form of the output.","**\"Moving most agent calls to an SLM cuts the bill by about that share.\"** Cost follows tokens times price, not call count: in the research-agent example seven of ten calls moved but the bill fell only 41%, because the three planner calls stay on the frontier model and are about 89% of the mixed bill.","**\"Small models are trained compute-optimally, so more data would not help them.\"** Modern SLMs are deliberately over-trained (Llama 3 8B at close to 2,000 tokens per parameter versus about 20) because inference, not training, dominates lifetime cost."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"Incoming request\"] --> RAG[\"Retrieve facts into context\"]\n    RAG --> S[\"Fine-tuned SLM\"]\n    S --> C{\"Confident and schema valid?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes, most traffic\"| OUT[\"Return answer\"]\n    C -->|\"no, low margin or invalid\"| L[\"Frontier model\"]\n    L --> OUT\n    OUT -.->|\"sampled for evals\"| G[(\"Golden set and audit log\")]","caption":"The cascade most SLM deployments end up with: the small model handles the bulk, and low-confidence or\ninvalid outputs escalate to a frontier model."},"interview":[{"q":"Your agent costs too much per task. How do you decide which steps can run on a small model?","a":"- **Measure first.** Log tokens, cost and latency per step type; the steps that carry the tokens are the\n  only ones worth moving.\n- **Move narrow, input-grounded steps.** Routing, query rewriting, extraction and tool-output summaries\n  fit an SLM; planning over many tools usually does not.\n- **Gate each move** with a per-step eval and an escalation rule (logit margin or schema failure), and\n  track task success, not just step accuracy."},{"q":"An SLM in your agent occasionally emits tool arguments that break the downstream API. What do you do?","a":"- **Validate before acting.** Parse the arguments against the tool's JSON schema and never execute an\n  invalid call.\n- **Recover cheaply.** Retry once with the validation error in the prompt, then escalate to the larger\n  model, and log both rates.\n- **Fix the source:** constrained or structured decoding where your engine supports it, or fine-tune on\n  examples of valid calls for that tool."},{"q":"Why not just quantise a 70B model instead of using a 3B one?","a":"- **Quantisation buys about 4×,** from bf16 to 4-bit, while the gap between 70B and 3B is about 20× in\n  bytes read per token.\n- **So the 70B still needs a server GPU** and still reads far more weight per decode step, whereas a 3B at\n  4-bit is about 2 GB and runs on a laptop or phone.\n- **Quality is the open question,** settled on your golden set, not by parameter count."},{"q":"A 3B model on an H100-class server costs as much per token as a 30B deployment. What is wrong?","a":"- **Likely single-stream serving.** Decode is bandwidth-bound, so at batch 1 the card reads the weights\n  for one token per step and sits mostly idle.\n- **Fix:** turn on continuous batching and raise concurrency so each weight read serves dozens of requests,\n  then re-measure cost per token and p95 latency at target load."},{"q":"When would you refuse to replace a frontier model with a fine-tuned SLM?","a":"- **Open-ended or knowledge-heavy tasks** such as research assistance, where breadth of world knowledge and\n  multi-step synthesis are exactly what shrinks with parameter count.\n- **No labelled data or shifting inputs,** because the specialist advantage needs thousands of examples and\n  production inputs that resemble them.\n- **Low volume,** where the frontier bill is small and a fine-tune pipeline costs more to own than it saves."},{"q":"Why do vendors train small models far past the Chinchilla-optimal token count?","a":"- **Chinchilla-optimal is about 20 tokens per parameter,** but loss keeps falling well beyond it; Llama 3 8B\n  saw more than 15 trillion tokens, close to 2,000 per parameter.\n- **Inference dominates lifetime cost.** Serving costs about 2N FLOPs per token, so a smaller N that will\n  serve trillions of tokens repays the extra training compute many times over."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cheap agent steps vs a harder system to debug.** Mixing models per step cuts cost and latency, but a failed task may now trace to any of several models and prompts; it is worth it only with per-step logging and evals.","**Cost and latency vs breadth.** Both fall roughly in proportion to parameter count, but factual breadth and multi-step reasoning fall too; the trade flips the moment the task needs knowledge not supplied in context.","**Cheap fine-tuning vs pipeline burden.** Fine-tuning per task or per customer fits on a single GPU, but every fine-tune is a model to version, evaluate and redeploy, whereas prompting a frontier model has no such pipeline.","**Privacy vs predictability.** On-device or air-gapped deployment gives privacy and offline operation, but hardware fragmentation and quantisation variance make quality less predictable than on a controlled server fleet.","**Over-training vs training compute.** Small models get better per parameter but cost more training compute per parameter; it pays only at high inference volume, which is why model vendors, not application teams, absorb that cost.","**Cascade savings vs routing risk.** A router or cascade captures most of the savings while keeping a frontier-quality ceiling, but a miscalibrated router sends hard cases to the small model and nobody notices until an audit."],"pitfalls":["**Making the SLM the agent's planner.** Symptom: loops that pick the wrong tool, repeat calls or stop early on multi-step tasks, because composition degrades fastest with size. Fix: keep planning on a larger model and give the SLM bounded, single-step jobs.","**Judging an SLM with zero-shot prompts written for a frontier model.** Symptom: \"it fails\" on tasks it would handle after a few-shot prompt or a small fine-tune, and a premature conclusion that SLMs are useless. Fix: evaluate after few-shot prompting or a small fine-tune.","**Asking an SLM for facts from its weights.** Symptom: confident fabrication of dates, citations and API names. Fix: give it the source text and let it extract.","**Comparing on a public benchmark that may be in the training data.** Symptom: a benchmark score far above the in-house eval on your own data. Fix: choose between candidates on your own golden set.","**Quantising to 4-bit and skipping re-evaluation.** Symptom: subtle regressions in JSON validity and multilingual output, because small models lose more under quantisation than large ones. Fix: re-run those evals on every quantised build.","**Serving single-stream on a server GPU.** Symptom: a 3B model whose cost per token rivals a well-batched 30B model, because the card is bandwidth-bound and mostly idle at batch 1 while the larger model amortises each weight read over dozens of requests. Fix: continuous batching at real concurrency.","**Front-loading a 6k-token system prompt plus retrieved context.** Symptom: slow prefill and answers that ignore the middle of the context, since small models degrade faster with long inputs. Fix: fine-tune the instructions in and cap retrieved context."],"when":"- **Use in an agent for:** routing, query rewriting, extraction from tool results, summarising tool output\n  and guardrail checks, each behind a validation or escalation rule.\n- **Use when:** the task is narrow and high-volume, the inputs carry the facts the answer needs, latency or\n  cost budgets are tight, data cannot leave a device or network, or you have labelled data to fine-tune on.\n- **Avoid as the only model for:** open-ended assistance, novel reasoning, or knowledge-heavy question\n  answering without retrieval.\n- **Measure:** task accuracy on your golden set against a frontier baseline, calibration of its confidence\n  signal for routing, p95 latency at target concurrency, and cost per successful task.\n- **Day to day and interviews:** \"can we move this step off the API\", and \"when would you fine-tune a 3B\n  model instead of prompting a frontier model, and how would you know it worked\".\n- **Related:** the architecture decision is covered in system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs, and the\n  capability transfer in training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Fine-tunes a 1–8B model with LoRA on task data, quantises and serves it with vLLM or llama.cpp, and wires logit-margin escalation to a larger model for low-confidence cases.","architect":"Owns the model portfolio: which pipeline steps run on SLMs, where the cascade boundary sits, and how per-task fine-tunes are versioned and gated by evals before release.","fde":"Finds the customer's high-volume narrow workloads, proves an SLM on their data with a side-by-side eval, and handles the on-prem or edge constraints that made a hosted API unusable."},"check":{"q":"A bank wants to classify documents and extract fields on-prem across 5 million pages a month and\nsays \"we will just run the biggest open model we can fit\". How would you argue for an SLM, and what would\nmake you concede?","a":"Frame it as cost per correct extraction and throughput, not model size. The task is narrow, the\ninputs contain the answers, and the bank already has labelled outputs from its back office, so a fine-tuned\n3–8B model at int8 on one or two GPUs can process 5 million pages with headroom and sub-second latency,\nwhile a 70B model on the same hardware runs at a fraction of the throughput with no accuracy advantage on\nin-distribution forms. Propose a golden set built with their SMEs, fine-tune, compare both on it, and route\nlow-confidence or schema-invalid outputs to the larger model. I would concede if the eval shows a material\naccuracy gap, if the documents are heterogeneous free text that needs world knowledge, or if volume is low\nenough that the big model is affordable anyway."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a 3B model trained on 10 trillion tokens outperform an older 7B model trained on 1 trillion, even though both are \"small\"?","options":["Because the 3B model has a larger vocabulary","Because loss keeps falling far past the compute-optimal token count, and vendors now over-train small models since inference, not training, dominates lifetime cost","Because smaller models have less capacity to overfit","Because quantisation works better on 3B models"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Chinchilla-optimal is about 20 tokens per parameter, but the curve keeps improving beyond it. A 7B model on 1T tokens is near-optimal and under-trained by modern standards; a 3B on 10T tokens has absorbed far more data per parameter, and its lower serving cost is what pays for that training."},{"q":"A team fine-tunes a 1B model to answer medication-dosage questions from memory and sees frequent confident errors. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["The learning rate was too high","The model needs a longer context window","Parametric knowledge is what shrinks first with size; the facts should be supplied through retrieval, with the small model doing extraction and formatting","1B models cannot be fine-tuned"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Small models have few weights in which to store long-tail facts, so recall from memory is their weakest mode. Putting the dosage tables in context turns the task into extraction, which a 1B model does well. Hyperparameters and context length do not create knowledge that is not there."},{"q":"Which workload is the weakest fit for a small language model?","options":["High-volume ticket classification with 20,000 labelled examples","On-device dictation cleanup with no network access","Open-ended research assistance that depends on broad world knowledge and multi-step synthesis","Query rewriting in front of a search index"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The first, second and fourth are narrow, in-distribution tasks where the needed information is in the input. Open-ended research needs breadth of knowledge and long chains of reasoning, which are exactly the capabilities that shrink fastest with parameter count."}]},{"key":"model-internals/scaling-laws","sources":["Kaplan et al., 2020 — Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models","Hoffmann et al., 2022 — Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models","Schaeffer, Miranda and Koyejo, 2023 — Are Emergent Abilities of Large Language Models a Mirage?","Muennighoff et al., 2023 — Scaling Data-Constrained Language Models"],"why":"- **Frontier runs are too expensive to guess.** A frontier run is on the order of 10²⁵ FLOPs (illustrative)\n  and months of accelerator time, so you cannot grid-search the design. Deciding size and training length\n  used to be craft.\n- **Extrapolation replaces guessing.** Train a ladder of small models spanning several orders of magnitude\n  of compute, fit a curve, and read off the best configuration for the budget you actually have.\n- **Kaplan (2020) licensed \"just scale it\".** Loss is a clean power law in N, D and C across many orders of\n  magnitude, and depth versus width matters far less than scale. But the fit said grow parameters much\n  faster than data, which produced under-trained giants such as Gopher (280B parameters on about 300B\n  tokens).\n- **Chinchilla (2022) corrected the split.** Hoffmann et al. matched the learning-rate schedule to each\n  run's length and found compute-optimal training scales N and D roughly equally, about 20 tokens per\n  parameter. Chinchilla, 70B parameters on 1.4T tokens, beat Gopher at the same training compute. Nothing\n  about the architecture changed; the budget split did.\n- **\"Bigger is always better\" fails twice.** At a fixed budget it wastes compute on parameters that never\n  see enough data. And every parameter is paid for again on every token served, which for a popular model\n  is far more compute than training.","how":"### What you use as an agent builder\n1. **Cost per agent task.** Every processed token (prompt and generated) costs about 2N FLOPs, so an agent\n   step over a 30k-token transcript on a 70B model costs about 9× the same step on an 8B.\n2. **Model-card sanity check.** Tokens per parameter tells you whether a model is under-trained (about 1),\n   compute-optimal (about 20) or over-trained for cheap serving (hundreds to thousands).\n3. **Do not extrapolate task success.** Loss falls smoothly with scale; agent success is a thresholded\n   metric (the task finished or it did not), so it can jump. Measure it on your own agent eval.\n4. **Three levers, not one.** A larger base model, more test-time compute per hard step, or better context\n   (retrieval, tools). Price each on your eval.\n\nThe subsections below are background for reading model cards and vendor claims.\n\n### Background: the compute identity\n| Pass | Cost |\n|---|---|\n| Forward | about 2N FLOPs per token (one multiply-add per parameter) |\n| Backward | about twice the forward |\n| Training total | approximately `C = 6 N D` FLOPs |\n| Inference | about 2N FLOPs per generated token, plus attention, which is small unless contexts are very long |\n\nThis identity is the bridge between the training-side and serving-side arguments below.\n\n### Background: the loss fit\nChinchilla's parametric form is\n\n```\nL(N, D) = E + A / N^α + B / D^β\n```\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| E | irreducible entropy of the text under the tokenizer |\n| A/N^α | approximation error from finite capacity |\n| B/D^β | estimation error from finite data |\n\n- **Published constants:** E ≈ 1.69, A ≈ 406.4, B ≈ 410.7, α ≈ 0.34 and β ≈ 0.28, for their data and\n  tokenizer. The constants do not transfer to other setups; the shape does.\n- **The optimal split.** Minimising L subject to 6ND = C gives N_opt ∝ C^a and D_opt ∝ C^b with\n  a = β/(α+β) and b = α/(α+β).\n- **Why the ratio is constant.** When a ≈ b ≈ 0.5, N and D grow in lockstep as compute grows, so the\n  compute-optimal tokens-per-parameter ratio is a constant rather than a function of budget.\n- **This fit leans to data.** With the constants above a ≈ 0.45, so it favours data slightly more; the\n  other two methods gave close to 0.5 each.\n\n### Background: three ways to fit it\nHoffmann et al. used three methods and got the same answer; the agreement is what made it convincing.\n1. **Fix N, vary training length,** and take the lower envelope of the curves.\n2. **IsoFLOP curves:** at fixed C, vary N and find the loss-minimising size.\n3. **Fit the parametric form directly.**\n\nMethod matters. Kaplan's different exponents came partly from:\n- **One learning-rate schedule regardless of run length,** which penalises long runs.\n- **Counting parameters without embeddings,** which distorts the small-model end of the ladder.\n\nIf you fit your own law, tune the schedule per run and state what you count.\n\n### Background: inference-aware over-training\n- **Compute-optimal means \"cheapest training for a given loss\".** It ignores serving.\n- **Lifetime compute** for a model that generates T tokens over its life is roughly `6ND + 2NT`.\n- **When T is much larger than D,** true for any widely used model, shrinking N and raising D at the same\n  training budget raises the first term only slightly and cuts the second in proportion.\n- **That is why** Llama 3 8B was trained on more than 15T tokens, close to 2,000 tokens per parameter, and\n  why strong small models exist at all. The loss curve flattens past the optimum but does not stop\n  improving.\n\n### Background: what the laws do and do not predict\n- **They predict** pre-training loss, equivalently perplexity, on held-out text.\n- **Not downstream accuracy directly.** Thresholded metrics (exact match, pass/fail) can jump abruptly\n  while loss falls smoothly: the \"emergent abilities\" phenomenon. Schaeffer et al. argued much of the\n  discontinuity comes from the metric, since smoother measures such as the probability assigned to the\n  correct answer improve gradually.\n- **They assume a fixed data distribution.** Filtered or synthetic data shifts the constants, and repeated\n  data behaves like fresh data for only a few epochs before returns diminish sharply (Muennighoff et al.).\n- **MoE changes what N means,** since active and total parameters scale loss and cost differently.\n- **Reasoning models added a third lever.** For some tasks, more inference compute per query buys more\n  accuracy than a larger base model; see the test-time compute lesson.","example":"### Three ways to spend a 10²⁴ FLOP budget\nTake a 10²⁴ FLOP training budget and a model that will serve 10¹³ tokens over its life (both\nillustrative). Use Chinchilla's published fit as a stand-in for the curve, and compare the\n20-tokens-per-parameter rule, the minimum of the fitted curve, and a model 10× smaller than the rule.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\nE, A, B, alpha, beta = 1.69, 406.4, 410.7, 0.34, 0.28   # Chinchilla's published fit; setup-specific\n\ndef loss(N, D):\n    return E + A / N**alpha + B / D**beta\n\nC = 1e24                                     # training budget in FLOPs (illustrative)\nT = 1e13                                     # tokens served over the model's life (illustrative)\ntokens_for = lambda N: C / (6 * N)           # spend the whole budget: 6 N D = C\n\nN_rule = math.sqrt(C / (6 * 20))             # the \"20 tokens per parameter\" rule\nN_fit = min((10 ** (9 + i / 100) for i in range(300)),\n            key=lambda N: loss(N, tokens_for(N)))   # argmin of the fitted curve itself\nN_small = N_rule / 10                        # deliberately over-trained\n\nfor tag, N in [(\"20:1 rule\", N_rule), (\"fit argmin\", N_fit), (\"over-trained\", N_small)]:\n    D = tokens_for(N)\n    print(f\"{tag:12s} N={N/1e9:5.1f}B  D={D/1e12:5.1f}T  D/N={D/N:6.0f}  loss={loss(N, D):.3f}  \"\n          f\"lifetime={6*N*D + 2*N*T:.2e} FLOPs\")\n# 20:1 rule    N= 91.3B  D=  1.8T  D/N=    20  loss=1.918  lifetime=2.83e+24 FLOPs\n# fit argmin   N= 41.7B  D=  4.0T  D/N=    96  loss=1.911  lifetime=1.83e+24 FLOPs\n# over-trained N=  9.1B  D= 18.3T  D/N=  2000  loss=1.936  lifetime=1.18e+24 FLOPs\n```\n\nThree lessons sit in those rows:\n1. **Over-training is cheap in loss.** The 9B model is only about 0.02–0.025 nats worse than either\n   alternative yet costs 10× less per served token, so its lifetime compute is less than half the 20:1\n   model's.\n2. **The loss surface is flat near its minimum.** Models spanning a 10× range of N land within a few\n   hundredths of a nat, which is why the budget split can be bent toward inference economics at little\n   cost.\n3. **The published constants and rule disagree.** Minimising this fit puts the optimum near 96 tokens per\n   parameter, not 20. The paper's other two methods gave the roughly 20:1 split, and a later replication\n   (Besiroglu et al., 2024) argued the published constants were poorly fitted. Trust the shape, not the\n   constants to the second decimal place.\n\nThe decision then moves to a question the law cannot answer: does 0.02 nats of loss matter on the tasks\nyou care about? That is measured with task evals, not predicted.\n\n### Agent scenario: 2N FLOPs per token, applied to a 12-step agent\nThe serving side of the compute identity, applied to an agent: a 6k-token prefix, ~1.5k tokens appended\nper step, 300 generated tokens per step. It ignores attention cost, which grows with context.\n\n```python\ndef agent_tokens(steps=12, prefix=6_000, per_step=1_500, out=300, prefix_cache=False):\n    \"\"\"Tokens the model processes for one agent task (prompt tokens plus generated tokens).\"\"\"\n    total = 0\n    for s in range(steps):\n        context = prefix + per_step * s              # transcript grows every step\n        new = context if (s == 0 or not prefix_cache) else per_step\n        total += new + out\n    return total\n\nfor cache in (False, True):\n    t = agent_tokens(prefix_cache=cache)\n    for name, n in [(\"8B\", 8e9), (\"70B\", 70e9)]:\n        flops = 2 * n * t                            # about 2N FLOPs per processed token\n        print(f\"cache={cache!s:5} {name:>3}: {t:>7,} tokens/task  {flops:.1e} FLOPs/task  \"\n              f\"{flops * 1e6:.1e} FLOPs per million tasks\")\n```\n\n| Setup | Tokens per task | 8B FLOPs per task | 70B FLOPs per task |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Re-process the transcript every step | 174,600 | 2.8 × 10¹⁵ | 2.4 × 10¹⁶ |\n| Append-only with prefix caching | 26,100 | 4.2 × 10¹⁴ | 3.7 × 10¹⁵ |\n\n- **Reading.** Model size scales cost linearly (70B is ~9× the 8B); transcript handling scales it by ~7×.\n  Both levers matter as much as each other, and only an agent eval can say whether the 8B finishes enough\n  tasks.\n- **At a million tasks a month** the gap between the worst and best row is about 2.4 × 10²² versus\n  4.2 × 10²⁰ FLOPs, roughly 57× from the rounded figures (58.5× unrounded).\n\n### Worked scenario: sanity-checking model cards\nTokens per parameter is a quick proxy for how well-trained a model is. All figures come from this lesson.\n\n```python\ndef tokens_per_param(params_b, tokens_b):\n    return tokens_b / params_b\n\nfor name, n, d in [(\"Gopher\", 280, 300), (\"Chinchilla\", 70, 1_400),\n                   (\"vendor 7B\", 7, 2_000), (\"Llama 3 8B\", 8, 15_000),\n                   (\"claim: 70B on 100B\", 70, 100)]:\n    print(f\"{name:20s} {tokens_per_param(n, d):7.1f} tokens/param  \"\n          f\"train C = {6 * n * 1e9 * d * 1e9:.1e} FLOPs\")\n```\n\n| Model | Tokens per parameter | Reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| Gopher, 280B on 300B | about 1 | badly under-trained |\n| Chinchilla, 70B on 1.4T | 20 | compute-optimal; about 5.9 × 10²³ FLOPs by 6ND, similar to Gopher's |\n| Vendor 7B on 2T | about 286 | about 14× past the 140B-token optimum, deliberately |\n| Llama 3 8B on 15T+ | close to 2,000 | heavily over-trained for cheap serving |\n| \"70B on 100B tokens\" | about 1.4 | badly under-trained; be sceptical of its quality |","patterns":["**Compare agent models on cost per successful task.** Estimate tokens per task from traces, multiply by price or 2N FLOPs, and divide by the task success rate on your agent eval. Re-run it whenever a newer, smaller over-trained model ships, because those often close the gap on narrow steps.","**Read tokens per parameter before shortlisting.** A model card that implies about 1 token per parameter is a weak candidate for any agent role; heavily over-trained small models are the usual picks for cheap sub-steps. Confirm on your eval, not the card.","**Run a ladder before committing a big budget.** Train rungs spanning several orders of magnitude of compute, give each its own learning-rate schedule decayed to its own length, and state whether embeddings are counted. Hold out the largest rung and check the fit predicts its loss before trusting the extrapolation.","**Choose deployable models by lifetime cost.** Write `6ND + 2NT` with your forecast of served tokens T and compare candidates on it, not on training cost. Re-run the estimate when traffic forecasts change, since T decides how far to over-train.","**Budget epochs for data-constrained training.** For continued pre-training on a fixed corpus, count repeated epochs separately from fresh tokens and stop at a few repeats, where Muennighoff et al. saw returns fall off. Monitor held-out loss on unseen data, not training loss.","**Track smooth metrics next to thresholded ones.** Log the probability assigned to the correct answer, or per-token loss on the eval set, alongside exact match across checkpoints and model sizes, so capability growth is visible before a pass/fail metric jumps.","**Price test-time compute against a bigger model.** For hard, low-volume queries, compare accuracy per dollar of a reasoning mode against an upgrade of the base model on your own eval set."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent multiplies per-token cost by many calls and long transcripts,\n  so the 2N-FLOPs-per-token rule is how you compare candidate models on cost per task. The laws also explain\n  why a recent 8B can replace an older 70B for some agent steps.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Model cards (parameters, training tokens), model-selection and\n  cost spreadsheets, and the \"bigger model or more reasoning effort\" decision for hard agent steps.\n- **What you control.** Which model size backs each agent role, and whether you buy accuracy with a bigger\n  model, more test-time compute or better retrieval. You do not fit scaling laws yourself.\n- **What you can skip.** The fitting methods, the published constants and the Kaplan-versus-Chinchilla\n  history beyond one sentence. Keep 6ND, 2N per token, tokens per parameter, and \"loss is smooth, task\n  metrics are not\".","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Model selection for an agent role.** Shortlisting candidates by size and tokens per parameter before\n  running them through the agent eval.\n- **Cost forecasting for a new agent feature.** Turning traced tokens per task and 2N FLOPs per token into\n  a monthly compute or API budget.\n- **Bigger model or more reasoning.** Deciding whether a hard agent step gets a larger model, a reasoning\n  mode or better retrieval, priced on the same eval.\n- **Reading vendor claims.** Spotting an under-trained model or an emergence claim that is really a\n  thresholded metric.","mini_project":"**Cost-per-successful-task model for an agent (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** decide between a small and a large model for a tool-calling agent using numbers, not\n  parameter counts.\n- **Steps:** run a 5-step agent on 30 tasks with two model sizes; log prompt and generated tokens per step;\n  compute tokens per task, estimated FLOPs (2N per token) or API cost, and task success rate; extrapolate\n  to 100k tasks a month with and without prefix caching.\n- **Done when:** a one-page table shows cost per task, success rate and cost per successful task for both\n  models, plus a recommendation and the failure types that drove it."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Scaling laws say bigger is always better.\"** At a fixed budget they say there is an optimal size, and going past it loses: Gopher (280B on about 300B tokens) lost to Chinchilla (70B on 1.4T) at similar compute, because parameters that never see enough data are wasted.","**\"Compute-optimal is the size you should deploy.\"** Compute-optimal minimises training cost for a loss and ignores serving. With lifetime compute of `6ND + 2NT`, a widely used model is cheaper overall when smaller and over-trained, which is why Llama 3 8B saw close to 2,000 tokens per parameter.","**\"A smooth loss curve means agent success will improve smoothly too.\"** The laws predict pre-training loss. Task success is thresholded, so it can sit flat and then jump; only your own agent eval says whether a larger model finishes more tasks.","**\"The Chinchilla constants are a law of nature.\"** E, A, B, α and β are specific to that data and tokenizer, and minimising the published fit gives about 96 tokens per parameter rather than 20. Trust the shape, not the constants.","**\"Model size is the main lever on agent cost.\"** At about 2N FLOPs per processed token it is one lever; in the 12-step example, prefix caching cut tokens per task about 7×, roughly as much as moving from 70B to 8B."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    L[\"Ladder of small runs\"] --> F[\"Fit L of N and D\"]\n    F --> O[\"Minimise loss with 6ND = C\"]\n    O --> CO[\"Compute-optimal N and D\"]\n    CO --> I[\"Add serving term 2NT\"]\n    I --> S[\"Smaller N, more tokens\"]\n    S --> E[\"Task evals on candidates\"]\n    E -->|\"loss gap matters\"| CO\n    E -->|\"gap acceptable\"| D[\"Train and deploy\"]","caption":"How a team turns a scaling law into a model-size decision: fit on cheap runs, optimise for training,\nthen bend the answer toward serving cost and check it on task evals."},"interview":[{"q":"Derive the 6ND training cost and explain how it connects to serving cost.","a":"- **Forward pass:** about one multiply-add per parameter per token, so 2N FLOPs per token.\n- **Backward pass:** about twice the forward, so training is about 6N per token and `C = 6 N D` overall.\n- **Serving:** each generated token costs about 2N, so a model serving T tokens adds 2NT. For popular models\n  T dwarfs D, which is why parameter count drives lifetime cost."},{"q":"Why did Chinchilla's recommendation differ so much from Kaplan's?","a":"- **Learning-rate schedules.** Kaplan used one schedule regardless of run length, which made long runs look\n  worse and pushed the fit toward bigger models trained on less data.\n- **Parameter counting.** Excluding embeddings distorted the small-model end of the ladder.\n- **Result:** with schedules matched to run length, three methods agreed that N and D should scale roughly\n  equally, about 20 tokens per parameter."},{"q":"A benchmark sits near zero for three model sizes, then jumps at the fourth. Is that emergence?","a":"- **Maybe the metric, not the model.** Thresholded metrics such as exact match can jump while loss falls\n  smoothly; Schaeffer et al. showed smoother measures often improve gradually.\n- **How to check:** plot the probability assigned to the correct answer, or per-token loss on the task,\n  across the same sizes. A smooth trend means the capability was building all along.\n- **Why it matters:** it changes whether you predict the next scale's behaviour or abandon a capability one\n  scale too early."},{"q":"Your agent fails 30% of tasks on an 8B model. Do you move to a 70B?","a":"- **Classify the failures first.** Missing facts point to retrieval, wrong tool arguments to schemas or\n  validation, and bad multi-step plans to model capability.\n- **Price the upgrade.** At about 2N FLOPs per processed token, the 70B costs roughly 9× per task; compare\n  cost per successful task, not cost per call.\n- **Test the alternatives** on the same agent eval: the larger model for the planner only, more test-time\n  compute on hard steps, or better context."},{"q":"How would you use scaling-law reasoning to estimate what a new agent feature will cost?","a":"- **Tokens per task from traces:** prompt plus generated tokens for every step, including the growing\n  transcript.\n- **Multiply by 2N** for self-hosted FLOPs (or by the provider's per-token prices) and by expected task\n  volume.\n- **Then adjust** for prefix caching and per-step model choice, and validate against a week of real\n  usage."}],"tradeoffs":["**Bigger agent model vs better context.** A larger model raises capability on every step and every token, while retrieval, tools and a tidy transcript raise it only where needed; for knowledge-bound agent failures better context usually wins, for planning failures a bigger model may.","**Compute-optimal vs over-trained.** Compute-optimal minimises training cost per unit of loss, but yields a large model that is expensive to serve; it flips to over-training once lifetime served tokens exceed a few multiples of the training tokens.","**Cheap extrapolation vs reliability.** Extrapolating from small runs is cheap and reliable for loss, but reliability degrades when you change the data mix, tokenizer or architecture, or extrapolate many orders of magnitude past the fitted range.","**Data quality vs compute.** Better data shifts the whole curve down, often more cheaply than extra compute, but the shift cannot be measured without training ladder models on the new data.","**Test-time compute vs a better base model.** It improves hard-task accuracy without retraining, but is paid per query forever; it wins for low-volume hard queries and loses for high-volume easy ones."],"pitfalls":["**Predicting agent success from a leaderboard trend.** Symptom: a model one size up is expected to fix your agent and does not, or a smaller one is rejected without testing. Fix: task success is thresholded, so measure it on your own agent eval for each candidate.","**Treating 20 tokens per parameter as a law of nature.** It is a fit for Chinchilla's data and objective; the ratio for a model you will deploy is set by inference economics, often in the hundreds or thousands. Fix: pick the ratio from lifetime cost, `6ND + 2NT`.","**Expecting downstream accuracy to follow the smooth loss curve.** Symptom: a benchmark flat near zero across several scales and then jumping, leading either to \"emergence\" claims or to abandoning a capability one scale too early. Fix: track a smooth metric such as correct-answer probability as well.","**Counting parameters inconsistently.** With or without embeddings, or active versus total for MoE. Symptom: fits that disagree with published ones by a large factor, especially at the small end. Fix: state the counting rule and apply it to every rung.","**One learning-rate schedule for runs of every length.** Symptom: long runs look worse than they are, biasing the fit toward bigger models, which is a large part of the Kaplan/Chinchilla discrepancy. Fix: decay each run's schedule to its own length.","**Counting repeated epochs as fresh tokens.** Beyond a few repetitions returns fall off sharply, so the D in your formula is not the D the model effectively saw. Fix: count unique tokens and epochs separately."],"when":"Most practitioners never fit a scaling law; they use its implications.\n- **Agent builders:** estimate cost per agent task from 2N per processed token, shortlist models by tokens\n  per parameter, and settle the choice on an agent eval.\n- **Choose which model to deploy:** a heavily over-trained 8B can match a compute-optimal 20B.\n- **Estimate inference cost** from 2N FLOPs per token.\n- **Sanity-check claims:** \"70B parameters trained on 100B tokens\" means badly under-trained.\n- **Frame bigger model versus more test-time compute.**\n- **Continued pre-training or large fine-tunes:** the data-constrained results tell you how many epochs are\n  worth running. Training from scratch: run a ladder and fit before committing the budget.\n- **Interviews:** derive 6ND, explain why Chinchilla overturned Kaplan, and why Llama-style models are\n  trained far past the compute-optimal point.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Uses 2N FLOPs per token and bytes per decode step to estimate cost and latency of candidate models, and reads tokens-per-parameter off a model card as a quick proxy for how well-trained it is.","architect":"Frames model-size decisions as lifetime cost, training plus inference, and decides where a reasoning mode substitutes for a larger base model within the portfolio.","fde":"Turns a customer's \"bigger is better\" instinct into a measured accuracy-versus-cost curve on their task, and explains why a well-trained small model can beat an older large one."},"check":{"q":"Your company has a fixed training budget and a product that will serve a very high token volume for\nyears. A colleague proposes training the Chinchilla-optimal model size. What do you push back on, and how\ndo you quantify it?","a":"Chinchilla optimality minimises training compute for a target loss and ignores serving. Write\nlifetime cost as 6ND + 2NT where T is tokens served; when T is far larger than D the inference term dominates,\nso it is worth shrinking N and raising D at the same training budget and accepting a slightly higher loss.\nQuantify it with a fitted L(N, D): at 10²⁴ FLOPs the 20:1 rule gives about 90B parameters on 1.8T\ntokens, while a 9B model on 18T tokens is roughly 0.02 nats worse yet 10× cheaper per served token, because\nthe loss surface is flat near its optimum. Then check\nwhether that loss gap shows up on the downstream evals that matter, note that over-training has diminishing\nreturns, and confirm you can actually source 18T tokens without heavy repetition."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why did Chinchilla (70B parameters, 1.4T tokens) outperform Gopher (280B parameters, 300B tokens) at similar training compute?","options":["It used a better tokenizer","Gopher was under-trained: at fixed compute, loss is minimised by scaling parameters and tokens roughly equally, about 20 tokens per parameter","Larger models overfit on web text","It used mixture-of-experts layers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both runs spent similar compute, but Gopher put it into parameters that saw barely one token each. Rebalancing toward data at the same budget lowers loss; neither tokenizer nor architecture was the lever."},{"q":"A vendor's 7B model was trained on 2T tokens. Roughly how far is that from Chinchilla-optimal, and why might it be deliberate?","options":["At the optimum; 7B × 300 ≈ 2T","Under-trained; a 7B model needs about 7T tokens to be optimal","About 14× past the roughly 140B-token optimum; serving cost scales with parameters, so a smaller over-trained model is cheaper over its lifetime","Over-training is a mistake because loss stops improving after 20 tokens per parameter"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Chinchilla-optimal for 7B is about 7B × 20 = 140B tokens, so 2T is roughly 14× more. Loss keeps falling past the optimum, and since inference FLOPs are about 2N per token, a smaller model that will serve trillions of tokens repays the extra training many times over."},{"q":"Which quantity do scaling laws predict most reliably?","options":["Accuracy on a multiple-choice benchmark","Held-out pre-training loss, that is cross-entropy per token","Hallucination rate","Instruction-following quality after RLHF"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The laws are fits of pre-training loss against N, D and C. Thresholded task metrics can jump discontinuously as loss falls smoothly, and post-training quality depends on data and reward signals the laws do not model."}]},{"key":"model-internals/perplexity","sources":["Jelinek et al., 1977 — Perplexity: a measure of the difficulty of speech recognition tasks","Official docs: Hugging Face Transformers (perplexity of fixed-length models)"],"why":"- **The raw score is unusable.** The probability a model gives a held-out corpus is a product of thousands\n  of probabilities, astronomically small, and it shrinks with text length.\n- **Each transformation fixes one problem.** Taking the log turns the product into a sum, dividing by token\n  count removes length dependence, and negating gives the cross-entropy loss the model was trained on.\n  Exponentiating that loss gives perplexity.\n- **It is interpretable.** A loss of 2.0 nats is a perplexity of e² ≈ 7.4: at each step the model is as\n  uncertain as if choosing uniformly among 7.4 options. That is why the metric outlived the\n  speech-recognition systems it was invented for.\n- **Accuracy cannot replace it, because many continuations are valid.** A model that assigns 30% to the\n  actual next token and spreads the rest over sensible alternatives beats one that assigns 31% and then\n  garbage; accuracy cannot tell them apart. Perplexity scores the whole distribution.\n- **It is dense, cheap and label-free.** It is differentiable (the training loss in disguise) and any text\n  is an evaluation set. At pre-training scale it is the only such signal: benchmarks are expensive, noisy\n  and arrive late, while perplexity is computed on every batch.","how":"### Where it enters an agent workflow\n| Agent task | Perplexity or logprobs role | Decides on its own? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Quantising the agent's self-hosted model | regression gate before agent evals | no, agent evals decide |\n| Fine-tuning on agent traces | held-out loss shows over- or under-fitting | no, tool-call accuracy decides |\n| Escalating an uncertain tool call or label | mean token logprob of the output | only with a threshold tuned on data |\n| Screening documents for a RAG corpus | outliers under a reference model | flags for review |\n| Trusting a benchmark for model choice | contamination probe | signal, not proof |\n\n### Definition\nFor a token sequence x₁ … x_T under a model p:\n\n```\nPPL = exp( −(1/T) · Σ_t log p(x_t | x_<t) )\n```\n\n| Unit or value | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Cross-entropy H in nats | PPL = e^H |\n| H in bits | PPL = 2^H |\n| Perplexity 1 | every token predicted with certainty |\n| Perplexity equal to vocabulary size | uniform guessing |\n\n### Computing it (teacher forcing)\n1. **Feed the real text** through the model.\n2. **Take the logits at every position** and apply softmax.\n3. **Read off the probability of the token that actually came next.**\n4. **One forward pass yields all T probabilities.** No sampling is involved, so temperature plays no role\n   unless you deliberately rescale the logits.\n\n### Why it has a floor\n- **Decomposition:** `H(P, Q) = H(P) + KL(P ‖ Q)`, where P is the true distribution of the text and Q is\n  the model.\n- **H(P) is irreducible:** the inherent unpredictability of the text, which no model can remove. KL is the\n  model's excess surprise.\n- **Consequences.** Perplexity can never fall below e^H(P); the gap between two models on the same text is\n  a difference in KL; and gains get harder near the floor. This is the irreducible term E in scaling-law\n  fits.\n- **It is a compression rate.** With arithmetic coding, a model at 1.0 bits per byte could store the text\n  in about one bit per byte, which is why \"language modelling is compression\" is literal, not a metaphor.\n\n### Reading the number\n- **A few surprising tokens dominate.** Perplexity is a geometric mean of inverse probabilities. Four tokens\n  each predicted at 0.5 give exactly 2; change one to 0.01 and it jumps to about 5.3.\n- **Rare tokens outweigh easy ones.** A rare proper noun, a typo or an unknowable number contributes far\n  more than hundreds of easy function words.\n- **Double-edged sensitivity.** Useful for detecting distribution shift, a nuisance when comparing two\n  similar models, so always evaluate on enough text that the estimate is stable.\n- **Orders of magnitude (illustrative, not targets).** Modern models on clean English prose sit in the\n  single digits to low teens depending on tokenizer and domain; code is lower; random strings far higher.\n\n### Why the tokenizer matters\n- **Per-token loss depends on how much text each token carries.** A larger vocabulary produces longer\n  tokens: each is harder to predict, so per-token loss rises, but there are fewer of them, so total loss on\n  the document can be identical.\n- **So different tokenizers cannot be compared on per-token perplexity.**\n- **Tokenizer-independent alternatives:** bits per byte (total negative log-likelihood in bits divided by\n  the UTF-8 byte count) or bits per character. Older literature also reports per-word perplexity, which is\n  different again.\n- **Always ask which unit a perplexity is per.**\n\n### Context length and striding\n- **Context changes the score.** Token t conditions on everything before it, so a 1,024-token window sees\n  less context than 8,192 and scores worse on the same text.\n- **Non-overlapping chunks are cheap but pessimistic,** because the first tokens of every chunk have almost\n  no context.\n- **The standard recipe is a sliding window:** move the window forward by `stride` tokens, feed the whole\n  window, and score only the last `stride` tokens.\n- **Smaller stride** gives each token more context at the cost of more forward passes.\n- **Report window and stride** with every number.\n\n### What it does not measure\n- **Assistant quality.** Instruction-tuned and RLHF models are optimised to produce preferred responses,\n  not to model web text, so their perplexity on raw text often rises while usefulness improves. Low\n  perplexity does not mean a good assistant.\n- **Factuality:** a fluent false sentence can have low perplexity.\n- **Generation quality:** the perplexity of a model's own samples under itself is low by construction.\n\n### What it is used for in practice\n1. **Training.** The loss curve is log-perplexity; plateaus, spikes and divergence show up there first.\n2. **Quantisation and pruning.** Measure perplexity on a fixed text (WikiText-2 is the common choice in\n   llama.cpp-style tooling) before and after. A small rise is the usual price of 8-bit or 4-bit weights; a\n   large rise means the compression damaged the model, and task evals will confirm it later.\n3. **Long-context checks.** Perplexity as a function of position: if it climbs past the training length,\n   the model is not using the extended window.\n4. **Data work.** Filter documents whose perplexity under a reference model is far outside the normal\n   range, flag anomalous or adversarial prompts, and check contamination: a suspiciously low perplexity on\n   a benchmark's test set suggests the model has seen it.\n5. **Likelihood scoring.** Many multiple-choice benchmarks compare the (length-normalised) log-probability\n   of each answer option given the question, which is perplexity restricted to the answer tokens.\n   Normalisation matters: unnormalised sums favour short options.\n6. **Domain fit.** Among candidate base models, the lowest bits per byte on a sample of your corpus is a\n   reasonable first filter before expensive task evals.","example":"### Agent scenario: gate a tool call on its own perplexity\nSelf-hosted engines and some hosted APIs can return per-token log-probabilities. Perplexity over just the\ngenerated tool call, plus its least confident token, is a cheap signal for \"execute\" versus \"escalate\".\n\nCheck that your provider returns logprobs for tool-call output, not only for text content; if it does not,\ngenerate the call as JSON text on a self-hosted engine.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef call_perplexity(token_logprobs):\n    \"\"\"Perplexity of one generated tool call from its per-token log-probabilities (nats).\"\"\"\n    return math.exp(-sum(token_logprobs) / len(token_logprobs))\n\ndef decide(token_logprobs, max_ppl=1.5, min_token_p=0.2):\n    ppl = call_perplexity(token_logprobs)\n    worst = math.exp(min(token_logprobs))           # least confident single token\n    if ppl > max_ppl or worst < min_token_p:        # placeholder thresholds: tune on labelled traces\n        return f\"escalate (ppl={ppl:.2f}, worst token p={worst:.2f})\"\n    return f\"execute  (ppl={ppl:.2f}, worst token p={worst:.2f})\"\n\n# Logprobs for the tokens of '{\"tool\": \"refund\", \"order_id\": \"A1043\", \"amount\": 120}' (illustrative)\nconfident = [-0.01, -0.02, -0.05, -0.01, -0.03, -0.02, -0.10, -0.04, -0.02, -0.08]\nshaky     = [-0.01, -0.02, -0.05, -0.01, -0.03, -0.02, -2.30, -0.04, -0.02, -1.90]\nprint(decide(confident))   # execute  (ppl=1.04, worst token p=0.90)\nprint(decide(shaky))       # escalate (ppl=1.55, worst token p=0.10)\n```\n\n- **Why the worst token matters.** Perplexity averages, so a single uncertain argument value (an order ID,\n  an amount) can hide in a long call; checking the minimum token probability catches it.\n- **Limit.** Confidence is not correctness: a model can be confidently wrong, and RLHF-tuned models are\n  often poorly calibrated. Tune thresholds on labelled traces and keep schema and business-rule checks.\n\n### Sliding-window perplexity with Hugging Face Transformers\nEach window of `max_len` tokens is fed to the model, but only the final `trg_len` tokens are scored;\nearlier tokens are masked with `-100` so they serve as context only. The script also reports bits per\nbyte, which is comparable across tokenizers.\n\n```python\nimport math, torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B\"                              # any causal LM\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.float16).cuda().eval()\n\ntext = open(\"eval.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read()\nids = tok(text, return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids.cuda()    # [1, T]\nT, max_len, stride = ids.size(1), 2048, 512\nnll_sum, n_scored, prev_end = 0.0, 0, 0\n\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    for begin in range(0, T, stride):\n        end = min(begin + max_len, T)\n        trg_len = end - prev_end                         # tokens not yet scored\n        window = ids[:, begin:end]\n        labels = window.clone()\n        labels[:, :-trg_len] = -100                      # context only; HF shifts labels internally\n        out = model(window, labels=labels)               # out.loss = mean NLL over scored tokens\n        nll_sum += out.loss.item() * trg_len             # first token of the first window is a tiny approximation\n        n_scored += trg_len\n        prev_end = end\n        if end == T:\n            break\n\nppl = math.exp(nll_sum / n_scored)\nbits_per_byte = nll_sum / math.log(2) / len(text.encode(\"utf-8\"))\nprint(f\"tokens={n_scored} window={max_len} stride={stride} ppl={ppl:.2f} bpb={bits_per_byte:.3f}\")\n```\n\n- **Within one model:** run it before and after quantising with the same `eval.txt`, window and stride,\n  and the difference is a meaningful regression signal.\n- **Across tokenizers:** only the `bpb` line is comparable.\n\n### Worked numbers: outliers, units and tokenizers\nPure Python, no model needed: how one bad token moves perplexity, nats versus bits, and why bits per byte\nsurvives a tokenizer change.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef ppl(probs):\n    return math.exp(-sum(math.log(p) for p in probs) / len(probs))\n\nprint(ppl([0.5] * 4))                    # 2.0\nprint(ppl([0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.01]))        # ~5.32: one surprising token dominates\nprint(math.exp(2.0), 2 ** 2.0)           # loss 2.0 in nats -> 7.39, in bits -> 4\n\ndoc_bytes, doc_nll = 1_000, 600.0        # same document, same total NLL in nats (illustrative)\nfor name, n_tokens in [(\"32k vocab\", 300), (\"128k vocab\", 200)]:\n    per_tok = doc_nll / n_tokens\n    print(f\"{name}: ppl={math.exp(per_tok):.1f}  bpb={doc_nll / math.log(2) / doc_bytes:.3f}\")\n```\n\n| Tokenizer | Tokens for the document | Per-token loss | Perplexity | Bits per byte |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 32k vocab | 300 | 2.0 nats | 7.4 | 0.866 |\n| 128k vocab | 200 | 3.0 nats | 20.1 | 0.866 |\n\n- **Same model quality, different perplexity.** Both assign the document identical probability; only the\n  per-token denominator changed. Bits per byte is identical.","patterns":["**Two-stage gate for the agent's model.** When you quantise or fine-tune the self-hosted model behind an agent, run the perplexity check first because it takes minutes, then the agent eval (tool-call validity, task success). Block on either; never ship on perplexity alone.","**Watch held-out loss when fine-tuning on agent traces.** Keep a held-out split of traces and track its loss next to tool-call accuracy per checkpoint; held-out loss rising while training loss falls means the run is memorising traces.","**Logprob-based escalation for risky tool calls.** For actions with side effects, compute call perplexity and minimum token probability, route low-confidence calls to a stronger model or a human, and monitor escalation rate and the error rate of executed calls.","**Perplexity gate for quantisation and pruning.** Check a fixed eval file, window and stride into the repository and run them before and after every compression change; llama.cpp ships a `llama-perplexity` tool for GGUF models. Put the delta in the pull request next to task evals, and block on a rise far beyond what similar 8-bit or 4-bit builds showed.","**Reference-model data filtering.** Score each document under a small reference model trained on clean text and drop documents far outside the normal range at either end. Monitor the share removed per source so a filter change does not silently delete a domain.","**Contamination probe.** Compare a model's perplexity on a benchmark's test set with its perplexity on freshly written text of the same style; a suspiciously low score on the test set suggests it was in training data. Run it before trusting a public benchmark result."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** When you quantise, prune or fine-tune the model behind an agent (for\n  example on agent traces), perplexity on a fixed text is the fastest regression check, and token\n  log-probabilities on a tool call are a cheap confidence signal for escalation.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Fine-tuning loss curves (log-perplexity), `llama-perplexity` in\n  llama.cpp, the logprobs option of self-hosted engines such as vLLM and of some hosted APIs, and\n  contamination checks in evals.\n- **What you control.** The fixed eval text, window and stride you check into the repo, the threshold\n  that blocks a quantised build, and whether low-logprob tool calls are retried or escalated.\n- **What you can skip.** Compression theory and the history of the metric. Know why it is not comparable\n  across tokenizers and why it says nothing about tool use, instruction following or facts.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Quantising a self-hosted agent model.** A perplexity delta on a fixed file is the first gate before\n  the slower agent eval.\n- **Fine-tuning on agent traces.** Held-out loss per checkpoint shows when a run starts memorising traces.\n- **Confidence-based escalation.** Token logprobs on a tool call decide whether a side-effecting action runs\n  or goes to a stronger model or a human.\n- **Curating a RAG corpus.** Documents with extreme perplexity under a reference model are flagged as\n  boilerplate, garbage or a different language.","mini_project":"**Logprob confidence gate for a tool-calling agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** test whether tool-call perplexity predicts wrong tool calls well enough to drive escalation.\n- **Steps:** run a small model with logprobs enabled (a local engine or an API that returns them) on 50\n  tool-calling tasks with known correct calls; record call perplexity and minimum token probability per\n  call; label each call right or wrong; sweep thresholds.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of escalation rate versus error rate among executed calls, a chosen\n  threshold, and a note on how many wrong calls were confident anyway."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Lower perplexity means a better agent model.\"** Perplexity measures how well a model predicts a fixed text, not tool use, instruction following or facts. RLHF models often score worse on raw web text while being far more useful, so agent evals decide.","**\"You can compare perplexity across models.\"** Only with the same tokenizer, text, window and stride. The same document can score 7.4 with a 32k vocabulary and 20.1 with a 128k one at identical quality; bits per byte is the comparable unit.","**\"Low perplexity on a tool call means the call is correct.\"** It measures confidence, not correctness, and an average can hide one shaky argument token. Check the worst token too, and keep schema and business-rule validation.","**\"Sampling temperature affects measured perplexity.\"** It is computed by teacher forcing in one forward pass over the real text; no sampling happens, so temperature plays no role unless you rescale the logits yourself.","**\"A good enough model drives perplexity toward 1.\"** Text has irreducible entropy H(P), so perplexity cannot fall below e^H(P), and gains shrink near that floor. The gap between two models on the same text is a difference in KL to the true distribution, not progress toward 1."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Eval text, fixed file\"] --> K[\"Tokenize\"]\n    K --> W[\"Window of max_len tokens\"]\n    W --> M[\"Mask all but last trg_len with -100\"]\n    M --> F[\"Forward pass, teacher forcing\"]\n    F --> N[\"Add NLL of scored tokens\"]\n    N -->|\"slide by stride\"| W\n    N -->|\"end of text\"| P[\"PPL = exp of mean NLL\"]\n    N -->|\"end of text\"| B[\"Bits per byte\"]","caption":"Sliding-window evaluation: each window feeds full context, but only its newest tokens are scored, so\nevery token is counted once with as much context as the window allows."},"interview":[{"q":"What is perplexity, and why is it preferred over next-token accuracy for language models?","a":"- **Definition:** the exponential of mean per-token cross-entropy, read as the effective number of equally\n  likely choices the model faces per token.\n- **Why not accuracy:** many continuations are valid, and accuracy ignores how probability is spread over\n  the alternatives; perplexity scores the whole distribution.\n- **Bonus:** it is the training loss in disguise, so it is differentiable, label-free and available on\n  every batch."},{"q":"After 4-bit quantisation, perplexity on your fixed eval file rose. How do you decide whether to ship?","a":"- **Confirm the comparison is fair:** same eval file, tokenizer, window and stride before and after.\n- **Judge the size:** a small rise is the usual price of 4-bit weights; a large rise means the compression\n  damaged the model.\n- **Then run task evals,** with special attention to JSON validity and other fragile outputs, because\n  perplexity is a gate for model-level changes, not the acceptance criterion."},{"q":"You fine-tuned your agent's model on 5,000 tool-use traces. Held-out loss improved. Is it ready?","a":"- **Not yet.** Lower loss means the model fits the trace distribution better, not that it calls tools\n  correctly or finishes tasks.\n- **Run the agent eval:** tool-call validity, argument accuracy, task success and steps per task on\n  held-out tasks, plus a regression set of general instructions.\n- **Check for memorisation:** held-out loss should be measured on traces from tasks the model never saw."},{"q":"How could you use token log-probabilities to make an agent safer?","a":"- **Score each generated tool call:** perplexity over its tokens plus the least confident token, which\n  catches a single shaky argument value.\n- **Route by risk:** execute confident read-only calls, escalate low-confidence calls with side effects\n  to a stronger model or a human.\n- **Caveat:** calibration varies by model, so fit thresholds on labelled traces and keep schema checks."},{"q":"How would you use perplexity to test whether a model has seen a benchmark's test set?","a":"- **Compare two texts of the same style:** the benchmark test items and freshly written items the model\n  cannot have seen.\n- **A much lower perplexity on the test items** suggests memorisation, so benchmark scores overstate\n  generalisation.\n- **Caveat:** rare or easy wording also shifts perplexity, so treat it as a signal to investigate, not proof."},{"q":"Why does a model's perplexity on web text get worse after RLHF, and should you worry?","a":"- **RLHF moves the output distribution on purpose,** toward preferred responses and away from modelling raw\n  web text, so fit to that text worsens.\n- **Not a defect.** Judge the assistant with task accuracy, faithfulness and preference metrics; perplexity\n  on web text does not measure what it is meant to do."}],"tradeoffs":["**Free confidence signal vs calibration.** Token logprobs cost nothing extra to collect and catch many shaky tool calls, but tuned chat models can be confidently wrong; it pays only when thresholds are fitted on labelled agent traces.","**Dense and cheap vs blind to quality.** A label-free signal at any scale, but blind to correctness, helpfulness and safety; it flips to task metrics the moment you leave pre-training.","**Checkpoint comparability vs tokenizer lock-in.** Comparable across checkpoints of one model and tokenizer, not across vocabularies; bits per byte restores comparability at the cost of the intuitive \"number of choices\" reading.","**Shift detection vs noise.** Sensitivity to a few high-surprise tokens makes it a good detector of distribution shift and a noisy judge of small quality differences; average over enough text and report the evaluation size.","**Fair number vs compute.** Sliding-window evaluation gives the fairest number but costs up to `window / stride` times more forward passes than chunking; chunking is cheap but systematically pessimistic."],"pitfalls":["**Shipping a quantised agent model on perplexity alone.** Symptom: perplexity barely moved, yet tool-call JSON breaks more often or arguments drift. Fix: always follow the perplexity gate with the agent eval, with a separate check on structured outputs.","**Treating low perplexity as a correct tool call.** Symptom: confident calls with the wrong ID or amount get executed. Fix: keep schema and business-rule validation regardless of confidence.","**Comparing per-token perplexity across tokenizers.** Symptom: a model with a 32k vocabulary \"beats\" one with 128k on the same text purely because its tokens are shorter. Fix: bits per byte.","**Reporting without dataset, tokenizer, window and stride.** Symptom: numbers nobody can reproduce, which drift by double-digit percentages between evaluations. Fix: check the recipe into the repository.","**Using perplexity to choose a chat model.** Symptom: a base model \"wins\" and then cannot follow instructions. Fix: choose on task evals and preference.","**Evaluating on text that was in the training set.** Symptom: an implausibly low number that does not predict generalisation, which is the contamination test seen from the other side. Fix: evaluate on text written after the training cutoff.","**Mixing nats and bits.** Symptom: a \"loss of 2.0\" reported as perplexity 4 (2²) rather than 7.39 (e²), or the reverse. Fix: exponentiate in the base the loss was measured in, and label the unit.","**Scoring padding tokens or forgetting the one-position label shift.** Symptom: a perplexity near 1 (padding predicted perfectly) or an absurdly high one. Fix: set padding labels to `-100` and let the library shift labels."],"when":"- **Use in agent work for:** a fast regression gate before agent evals when the model changes, held-out\n  loss during fine-tuning on traces, and logprob confidence for escalating risky tool calls.\n- **Use when** the question is \"how well does this model fit this distribution of text\": pre-training and\n  continued pre-training, checking a quantised or pruned model against the original, choosing a base model\n  for domain adaptation, and detecting shifted or anomalous inputs.\n- **Do not use** as the acceptance metric for a product feature; use task accuracy, faithfulness and human\n  preference there.\n- **Measure** with a fixed recipe, dataset, tokenizer, window and stride, checked into the repository so\n  every number is reproducible.\n- **Interviews:** derive it from cross-entropy, explain why it is not comparable across tokenizers, why RLHF\n  models score worse on it, and name two production uses beyond training curves.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs a fixed perplexity script on a standard text before and after quantisation and puts the delta in the pull request next to the task evals.","architect":"Sets the rule that perplexity is a gate for model-level changes such as quantisation, pruning and continued pre-training, but never a substitute for task-level acceptance criteria.","fde":"Uses bits per byte on a sample of the customer's documents to show whether a candidate open model \"speaks their language\" before investing in a full evaluation."},"check":{"q":"Two open models report WikiText perplexities of 5.2 and 6.8. A colleague says the first is clearly\nbetter for your legal-contract summarisation product. What is wrong with that reasoning, and what would you\ndo instead?","a":"Three things. The numbers are comparable only if the tokenizers, window and stride match, and a\nlarger vocabulary mechanically raises per-token perplexity. WikiText is encyclopaedia prose, not contracts,\nso fit to the actual domain is unmeasured. And summarisation quality depends on instruction-following and\nfaithfulness, which perplexity does not capture; an RLHF model can have worse perplexity and better\nsummaries. Instead, compute bits per byte on a sample of the firm's contracts as a quick domain-fit filter,\nthen run a task evaluation with faithfulness, coverage and human preference on one or two hundred\nsummaries. Bring perplexity back later only as a regression check when quantising the chosen model."},"quiz":[{"q":"A model's average cross-entropy on a test set is 1.5 nats per token. What is its perplexity, and what does that number mean?","options":["1.5; the model is 1.5 times better than chance","2^1.5 ≈ 2.8; the model needs 2.8 bits per token","e^1.5 ≈ 4.5; on average the model is as uncertain as choosing uniformly among about 4.5 tokens","1/1.5 ≈ 0.67; the average probability of the correct token"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Perplexity is the exponential of the cross-entropy in the same base. With a loss in nats that is e^1.5 ≈ 4.5. The 2^1.5 option is the right formula for a loss measured in bits, which is the classic unit confusion, and the others are not transformations of the loss at all."},{"q":"Why can you not directly compare per-token perplexity between a model with a 32k-token vocabulary and one with a 128k vocabulary?","options":["Larger vocabularies make the softmax numerically unstable","Tokens carry different amounts of text, so per-token loss differs even when the two models assign identical probability to the document; compare bits per byte instead","The 128k model always has lower perplexity because it has more parameters","Perplexity is only defined for byte-level tokenizers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Total log-probability of a document is tokenizer-independent, but dividing it by a different token count gives a different per-token average. Normalising by bytes or characters removes the tokenizer from the comparison."},{"q":"After RLHF, a model's perplexity on raw web text increased while users rate its answers higher. What is the best interpretation?","options":["The RLHF run damaged the model and should be rolled back","Perplexity was computed incorrectly","Perplexity measures fit to the web-text distribution, which RLHF deliberately moves the model away from toward preferred responses; use task and preference metrics for the chat model","Users are wrong; perplexity is the ground truth"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Preference tuning changes the output distribution on purpose, so fit to raw text getting worse is expected and not a defect. The right evaluation for an assistant is the task it is meant to perform, which perplexity on web text does not measure."}]},{"key":"model-internals/hallucination","sources":["Ji et al., 2023 — Survey of Hallucination in Natural Language Generation","Maynez et al., 2020 — On Faithfulness and Factuality in Abstractive Summarization","Manakul, Liusie and Gales, 2023 — SelfCheckGPT: Zero-Resource Black-Box Hallucination Detection for Generative Large Language Models","Farquhar et al., 2024 — Detecting hallucinations in large language models using semantic entropy"],"why":"- **The objective cannot tell known from unknown.** A next-token model learns p(next token | context).\n  Nothing distinguishes \"the capital of France is Paris\", seen millions of times, from \"the 2019 revenue of\n  a small private company\", seen once or never. The model must emit a token anyway, the distribution still\n  peaks somewhere plausible, and the sampler emits it with the same fluency as a well-known fact.\n- **Parametric knowledge is lossy compression.** Rare facts are stored weakly or not at all, with no\n  explicit \"not stored\" signal. The signal is not wholly absent: pre-trained models are often reasonably\n  calibrated on multiple-choice questions (Kadavath et al., 2022). But free-form generation never consults\n  it before committing to a token, and post-training tends to degrade it.\n- **Autoregression commits.** Once a wrong token is sampled, say a fabricated paper title, every later token\n  is conditioned on it, and the strong prior for coherence makes the model elaborate rather than retract,\n  so errors snowball.\n- **Post-training rewards guessing.** Human raters and binary-graded benchmarks reward complete, confident\n  answers and penalise hedging, so the policy learns a guess beats an abstention, like students on exams\n  with no negative marking. The GPT-4 technical report showed calibration getting worse after RLHF.\n- **The naive fixes fail.** More data shrinks the long tail but cannot close it. Temperature zero cuts\n  variance, not bias: greedy decoding still takes the argmax of a wrong distribution. A \"do not\n  hallucinate\" prompt helps only at the margin, since it creates no \"I don't know\" signal decoding acts on.\n  Fine-tuning can teach some facts, but on knowledge the base model lacks it has been reported to increase\n  hallucination elsewhere.","how":"### How it shows up in an agent loop\n| Agent failure | Example | Cheapest guard |\n|---|---|---|\n| Invented tool or parameter | calls `issue_refund` when the tool is `refund` | validate name and arguments against the registry |\n| Invented identifier | refunds order `A9999` that no tool ever returned | check IDs against values seen in tool results |\n| Fabricated observation | \"the test suite passed\" without running it | require the claim to cite a tool result in the trace |\n| Snowballing plan | a wrong fact at step 3 drives steps 4-10 | verify key facts before acting on them |\n| Memory poisoning | a hallucinated preference is saved and recalled as truth | write to memory only facts with a source |\n\n- **Errors compound.** If each step has an independent error rate p, a task of n steps succeeds with\n  probability about (1 − p)ⁿ, so long agents need far lower per-step rates than chat (see the worked\n  table under Examples).\n\n### A taxonomy that determines the fix\n| Type | What it is | Detect with | Reduce with |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Faithfulness: *intrinsic* | output contradicts the given source, such as a summary that reverses a figure in the document | comparison against the source | grounding and verification |\n| Faithfulness: *extrinsic* | output adds content the source neither states nor contradicts, such as an invented figure, which may or may not be true in the world | comparison against the source | grounding and verification |\n| Factuality | output contradicts the world, such as a wrong date or an invented citation in a closed-book answer | external knowledge | retrieval, tools and abstention |\n| Structural | invented tool names, parameters that do not exist, API functions never defined; common in agents and code generation | schema checks | schema validation, which eliminates it almost entirely |\n\n- **The faithfulness split** into intrinsic and extrinsic comes from the NLG literature (Maynez et al.,\n  Ji et al.).\n- **The axes are different.** An extrinsic addition can be factually true, and a faithful summary of a\n  wrong document is factually wrong.\n- **Why it matters.** Each type has a different evaluation and a different mitigation, so a\n  \"hallucination rate\" that mixes them is not actionable.\n\n### Where in the pipeline it arises\n1. **Knowledge.** The question is outside the training distribution or after the training cutoff.\n2. **Retrieval.** In RAG the retriever returns irrelevant, partial, conflicting or stale passages, and a\n   model instructed to answer bridges the gaps with its priors. A large share of \"model hallucinations\"\n   reported for RAG products are retrieval failures.\n3. **Decoding.** High temperature or top-p widens the tail; long generations accumulate risk; the most\n   surprising tokens are usually the fabricated specifics, such as numbers, names and URLs.\n4. **Prompt pressure.** \"Always answer\", \"be definitive\", role-play framing, and false-premise questions\n   (\"Why did X do Y?\" when X never did) push the distribution toward fabrication.\n5. **Sycophancy.** The user asserts something and the model agrees, because agreement was rewarded in\n   preference tuning.\n\n### Detection mechanisms\n| Method | How | Cost and limits |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sampling consistency (SelfCheckGPT) | sample N answers; known facts are stable, fabrications vary | N× compute; misses confident, consistent errors |\n| Semantic entropy | cluster samples by meaning, compute entropy over clusters | N× samples plus meaning checks (an NLI model); flags unreliable answers better than token-level entropy, because paraphrases of one answer no longer count as disagreement |\n| Token-level signals | low log-probability on entity and number tokens | cheap flag, but miscalibrated after RLHF, so thresholds must be tuned per model |\n| Claim-level verification | split the answer into atomic claims, check each against retrieved passages with an NLI model or LLM judge, report an unsupported-claim rate | one extraction call plus one call per claim, and the judge can err; the faithfulness metric in evals, most actionable because it names the claim that failed |\n| Tool-based verification | run the code, call the API, look up the citation | cheapest and most reliable wherever a checker exists |\n\n### Reduction mechanisms\nThe durable fix is architectural: supply evidence, verify outputs, and make abstention cheap.\n- **Grounding.** Retrieve evidence, instruct the model to quote or cite spans, verify the citations after\n  generation, and show the sources to the user.\n- **Abstention paths.** Put an explicit \"insufficient information\" option in the output schema and score it\n  as correct on unanswerable items in your evals, so neither prompt iteration nor fine-tuning trains it away.\n- **Constrained outputs.** Enumerations, JSON schemas and tool-call validation against the real tool\n  registry remove whole classes of structural hallucination.\n- **Decoding.** Lower temperature for factual and extractive steps; keep sampling diversity for creative\n  ones.\n- **Verification loops.** Draft, generate verification questions, answer them independently, revise.\n  Reasoning models perform a version of this inside their thinking trace.\n- **Training-side.** Preference data that rewards calibrated abstention and penalises confident errors more\n  than \"I don't know\". A vendor-level lever, but it tells you what to look for in model choice.\n- **Product design.** Surface confidence and sources, make verification one click, keep a human in the\n  loop for consequential outputs.","example":"### Agent scenario: block invented tools, arguments and IDs before they execute\nStructural hallucination is the one you can almost eliminate: check every proposed call against the live\nregistry, its JSON Schema, and the identifiers that actually came back from tools in this run.\n\n```python\nfrom jsonschema import validate, ValidationError\n\nTOOLS = {   # the live registry: name -> JSON Schema for the arguments\n    \"get_order\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"required\": [\"order_id\"], \"additionalProperties\": False,\n                  \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}},\n    \"refund\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"required\": [\"order_id\", \"amount\"], \"additionalProperties\": False,\n               \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"amount\": {\"type\": \"number\", \"minimum\": 0}}},\n}\nSIDE_EFFECTS = {\"refund\"}   # state-changing tools: IDs must come from a tool result, not the model\n\ndef check_call(call, seen_ids):\n    \"\"\"Return an error to reflect back to the model, or None if the call may run.\"\"\"\n    name, args = call.get(\"name\"), call.get(\"arguments\", {})\n    if name not in TOOLS:                                    # invented tool name\n        return f\"unknown tool {name!r}; available: {sorted(TOOLS)}\"\n    try:\n        validate(args, TOOLS[name])                          # invented or mistyped arguments\n    except ValidationError as e:\n        return f\"invalid arguments for {name}: {e.message}\"\n    if name in SIDE_EFFECTS and args[\"order_id\"] not in seen_ids:   # invented identifier\n        return f\"order_id {args['order_id']} never appeared in a tool result; call get_order first\"\n    return None\n\nseen = {\"A1043\"}                     # IDs that actually came back from tools in this run\nfor call in [{\"name\": \"issue_refund\", \"arguments\": {\"order_id\": \"A1043\", \"amount\": 20}},\n             {\"name\": \"refund\", \"arguments\": {\"order_id\": \"A1043\", \"amount\": \"20\"}},\n             {\"name\": \"refund\", \"arguments\": {\"order_id\": \"A9999\", \"amount\": 20}},\n             {\"name\": \"refund\", \"arguments\": {\"order_id\": \"A1043\", \"amount\": 20}}]:\n    print(check_call(call, seen))\n# unknown tool 'issue_refund'; available: ['get_order', 'refund']\n# invalid arguments for refund: '20' is not of type 'number'\n# order_id A9999 never appeared in a tool result; call get_order first\n# None\n```\n\n- **Why only side-effecting tools.** A lookup such as `get_order` must accept IDs the user typed, or the\n  agent could never find a new order; a wrong lookup fails harmlessly, a wrong refund does not.\n- **Reflect, then cap.** Return the error string to the model as the tool result, allow one or two\n  retries, then stop or escalate, so a confused model cannot loop forever.\n\n### Worked scenario: why per-step error rates matter more for agents\nTask success if every step must be right and steps fail independently at rate p (a simplification:\nreal errors are correlated, and some are recoverable).\n\n| Per-step error rate | 1 step | 10 steps | 20 steps | 40 steps |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2% | 98% | 81.7% | 66.8% | 44.6% |\n| 1% | 99% | 90.4% | 81.8% | 66.9% |\n| 0.5% | 99.5% | 95.1% | 90.5% | 81.8% |\n\n- **Reading.** A 2% rate that looks fine in chat fails a third of 20-step tasks. Validation that removes\n  structural errors, plus verification of the few facts each action depends on, is how agent teams buy\n  the lower rows.\n\n### Claim-level faithfulness check for a RAG answer\n- **Cost:** one call to extract claims plus one call per claim, so it runs as an offline eval on sampled\n  traffic and as an online gate only for high-stakes categories.\n- **Caveat:** the judge is itself a model and can be wrong in the same direction as the generator, so\n  calibrate its verdicts against human labels before the rate goes on a dashboard.\n\n```python\nimport json\n\ndef llm(prompt, temperature=0.0):\n    \"\"\"Call your model provider here and return the text.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n\ndef extract_claims(answer):\n    out = llm(\"Split this answer into atomic factual claims. \"\n              f\"Return a JSON list of strings.\\n\\nANSWER:\\n{answer}\")\n    return json.loads(out)\n\ndef verify(claim, passages):\n    ctx = \"\\n\\n\".join(f\"[{i}] {p}\" for i, p in enumerate(passages))\n    out = llm(\"Decide whether the CLAIM is supported by the PASSAGES. Reply with JSON \"\n              '{\"verdict\": \"supported\" | \"contradicted\" | \"not_found\", \"source\": <index or null>}.\\n\\n'\n              f\"PASSAGES:\\n{ctx}\\n\\nCLAIM: {claim}\")\n    return json.loads(out)\n\ndef faithfulness_report(answer, passages):\n    claims = extract_claims(answer)\n    verdicts = [verify(c, passages) for c in claims]\n    unsupported = [(c, v[\"verdict\"]) for c, v in zip(claims, verdicts) if v[\"verdict\"] != \"supported\"]\n    return {\"claims\": len(claims), \"unsupported\": unsupported,\n            \"unsupported_rate\": len(unsupported) / max(1, len(claims))}\n\nretrieved_passages = [\"Refunds are issued within 14 days of a returned item being received.\"]\nanswer_text = \"Refunds take 14 days and are paid to the original card.\"   # second claim is unsupported\nreport = faithfulness_report(answer_text, retrieved_passages)\n# Expected: 2 claims; \"paid to the original card\" comes back not_found, so unsupported_rate = 0.5.\nif report[\"unsupported_rate\"] > 0:\n    # Options: regenerate with the unsupported claims listed as \"do not state\",\n    # return only the supported claims with their [source] indices, or route to review.\n    ...\n```\n\n- **Free error analysis.** Unsupported claims that are \"not_found\" with weak retrieval scores point at the\n  retriever; \"contradicted\" claims point at the generator.\n\n### Semantic entropy on sampled answers\nA runnable sketch of the detection idea for closed-book questions. Farquhar et al. decide \"same meaning\"\nwith bidirectional entailment from an NLI model; here a toy rule (same set of years) stands in for it.\n\n```python\nimport math, re\n\ndef semantic_entropy(samples, same_meaning):\n    clusters = []\n    for s in samples:\n        for c in clusters:\n            if same_meaning(c[0], s):\n                c.append(s)\n                break\n        else:\n            clusters.append([s])\n    n = len(samples)\n    return sum(-(len(c) / n) * math.log(len(c) / n) for c in clusters)\n\nyears = lambda s: set(re.findall(r\"\\b\\d{4}\\b\", s))\nsame_year = lambda a, b: years(a) == years(b)\n\nknown = [\"Founded in 1998.\", \"1998\", \"It was founded in 1998\", \"In 1998.\", \"1998, in Ohio\"]\nguess = [\"Founded in 1998.\", \"2003\", \"It was founded in 1987\", \"In 2003.\", \"2011\"]\nprint(round(semantic_entropy(known, same_year), 2))   # 0.0: one meaning, likely known\nprint(round(semantic_entropy(guess, same_year), 2))   # 1.33: four meanings, flag or abstain\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: splitting a reported 10% rate\nA customer says the assistant \"hallucinates about 10% of the time\". Labelling 200 failing answers gives a\nbreakdown like this (illustrative counts, the shape is typical):\n\n| Root cause | Count | Share | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Retrieval missed the answer passage | 110 | 55% | chunking, embeddings, missing documents |\n| Question unanswerable from the corpus | 30 | 15% | abstention path, scored as correct |\n| Extrinsic addition beyond the passage | 35 | 17.5% | quote-or-cite instruction, claim verification |\n| Intrinsic contradiction of the passage | 15 | 7.5% | lower temperature on the answer step, verification |\n| Citation attached to a passage that does not say it | 10 | 5% | post-hoc citation check |\n\n- **Reading.** Most of the \"model\" problem is retrieval and missing abstention, which is why fixing\n  retrieval first usually gives the largest drop.","patterns":["**Ground agent claims in the trace.** Require completion claims (\"tests pass\", \"email sent\") and key facts in the final answer to reference a tool result ID from this run, and check it mechanically before replying. Apply the same rule to memory: store a fact only with the tool result or user message it came from. Monitor the share of answers and memory writes without a source.","**Abstention as a schema state.** Add a status field such as `answered` or `insufficient_information` to the structured output, include unanswerable items in the eval set and score abstention on them as correct. Monitor abstention rate separately on answerable and unanswerable items, so over-refusal shows.","**Log retrieval scores with every answer.** Store the top-k relevance scores and passage ids next to each response, and route answers whose best score is below a tuned threshold to abstention or review. Monitor the share of flagged failures with low retrieval scores to separate retriever from generator.","**Mechanical citation checks.** Require quoted spans with source indices and verify after generation that each quote appears in the cited passage, a plain string match. Monitor the citation-mismatch rate per release.","**Validate every tool call.** Check tool names against the live registry and arguments against the tool's JSON Schema (for example with the Python `jsonschema` package), reflect the validation error back to the model, and cap retries. Monitor invalid-call rate per tool.","**Calibrate the judge before the dashboard.** Have SMEs label a sample, measure agreement between the LLM judge and humans, and only then publish an unsupported-claim rate. Re-check agreement when the judge model or prompt changes.","**Tier verification by stakes.** Run claim-level verification online only for high-stakes categories and offline on sampled traffic for everything else. Track the latency and cost it adds per tier."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A chatbot's hallucination is a wrong sentence; an agent's is a wrong\n  action. Invented tool names, made-up order IDs, fabricated \"I already checked\" claims and hallucinated\n  facts written to memory all execute or persist, and every later step builds on them.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Tool-call validation against the live registry, RAG grounding and\n  citation checks, memory writes, final-answer verification, and the faithfulness metrics in your agent\n  evals and traces.\n- **What you control.** Schemas and validation on every call, an abstention or \"need more info\" state,\n  checks that IDs and facts came from tool results, verification before side-effecting actions, and\n  temperature per step.\n- **What you can skip.** The research taxonomy beyond intrinsic, extrinsic, factual and structural, and\n  vendor-side training fixes. Your levers are architectural.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Tool-calling agents with side effects.** Refund, booking and ticketing agents validate every call and\n  every identifier before anything executes.\n- **RAG assistants and research agents.** Claim-level faithfulness checks and citation verification on\n  answers built from retrieved passages.\n- **Coding agents.** Running the code or tests is the verification; claims of success must match a real\n  tool result.\n- **Agent memory.** Only source-backed facts are written, so a single hallucination does not become a\n  permanent \"user preference\".","mini_project":"**Hallucination guardrails for a small tool-calling agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** measure and cut structural and identifier hallucinations in a 3-tool support agent.\n- **Steps:** build an agent with `get_order`, `refund` and `search_faq` over a fake order table; write 30\n  tasks, including ones that mention non-existent orders or tempt guessing; run it once without guards and\n  once with the `check_call` validator plus a trace check on completion claims; log every rejected call.\n- **Done when:** a table compares invalid-call rate, invented-ID rate and task success with and without\n  guards, and you can point to three traces where a guard stopped a wrong action."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Temperature 0 stops hallucination.\"** It cuts variance, not bias: greedy decoding still takes the argmax of a wrong distribution, so a confidently wrong answer becomes reproducibly wrong.","**\"RAG solves hallucination.\"** It moves the problem: a large share of reported RAG hallucinations are retrieval failures, where the model bridges missing or stale passages with its priors. Fix retrieval and add an abstention path before tuning the generator.","**\"A bigger or newer model will make it go away.\"** The training objective has no \"not stored\" signal and post-training rewards confident guesses, so every model has a long tail. In a 20-step agent even a 1% per-step error rate fails about 18% of tasks.","**\"Fine-tuning on our documents makes the model stop inventing facts about them.\"** Training on knowledge the base model lacks has been reported to increase hallucination elsewhere; facts belong in retrieval and tool results, fine-tuning in format and behaviour.","**\"Hallucination is a text-quality problem, not an agent-safety one.\"** In an agent it is an action: an invented order ID gets refunded and a fabricated \"tests passed\" gets merged. Structural cases are almost fully preventable with registry, schema and seen-ID checks before side effects.","**\"Our LLM judge's hallucination rate is the true rate.\"** The judge is a model that can err in the same direction as the generator; calibrate it against human labels before trusting the number."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"User question\"] --> R[\"Retrieve passages\"]\n    R --> G{\"Evidence relevant?\"}\n    G -->|\"no\"| AB[\"Answer: insufficient information\"]\n    G -->|\"yes\"| GEN[\"Generate with cited spans\"]\n    GEN --> V[\"Validate schema and tool calls\"]\n    V -->|\"invalid\"| GEN\n    V --> CV[\"Verify each claim vs passages\"]\n    CV -->|\"unsupported\"| FIX[\"Drop claim, regenerate or review\"]\n    CV -->|\"all supported\"| OUT[\"Answer with sources\"]\n    FIX --> OUT","caption":"A grounded answer path with the three exits that keep hallucinations from reaching users: abstain on weak\nevidence, repair invalid structure, and verify claims before answering. In an agent the same gates sit\non every step, before a tool call executes or a fact enters memory."},"interview":[{"q":"Why do language models hallucinate at all?","a":"- **Objective:** next-token training rewards plausible continuations and has no \"not stored\" signal, so an\n  unknown fact still yields a confident-looking peak.\n- **Decoding:** autoregression commits to sampled tokens and the coherence prior elaborates on an early\n  error instead of retracting it.\n- **Incentives:** preference tuning and binary-graded benchmarks score guesses above abstentions, and\n  calibration measurably worsens after RLHF."},{"q":"Your support agent refunded an order that does not exist. How do you stop this class of error?","a":"- **Name the type:** a structural or identifier hallucination; the model produced a plausible ID instead of\n  one returned by a lookup tool.\n- **Guard mechanically:** validate tool names and arguments against the schema, and reject a refund on any\n  ID that did not come back from a lookup tool this run, returning the error to the model.\n- **Gate the action:** side-effecting tools such as refunds get a verification step or human approval, and\n  the eval set gains cases that tempt the agent to guess an ID."},{"q":"Why is hallucination a bigger problem for a 20-step agent than for a chatbot?","a":"- **Errors compound:** at a 2% independent per-step error rate, about two thirds of 20-step tasks\n  succeed, versus 98% of single answers.\n- **Errors act:** a wrong fact becomes a wrong tool call, a wrong memory or a wrong change, not just a\n  wrong sentence.\n- **Errors snowball:** later steps condition on earlier outputs, so one invented fact can steer the whole\n  plan; verify the facts each action depends on."},{"q":"There are no source documents for a closed-book QA feature. How would you detect likely hallucinations?","a":"- **Sample several answers** and measure agreement, or better, semantic entropy over meaning clusters, so\n  paraphrases do not count as disagreement.\n- **Act on it:** above a tuned threshold, abstain, add a caveat or route to retrieval or a tool.\n- **Know the limits:** it costs N× compute and misses confident, consistent errors the model learned from\n  training data."},{"q":"Your LLM judge reports a 2% hallucination rate but SMEs find 15%. What happened and what do you do?","a":"- **Likely cause:** the judge shares the generator's priors, so it accepts the same plausible fabrications,\n  and it was never calibrated against human labels.\n- **Fix:** label a sample with SMEs, measure judge agreement, switch to claim-level verdicts against the\n  retrieved passages, and tune the judge prompt or model until agreement is acceptable.\n- **Then** report only the calibrated rate, split by failure type."},{"q":"Why might fine-tuning on a customer's documents make hallucination worse?","a":"- **Fine-tuning teaches format faster than facts.** Training on knowledge the base model lacks has been\n  reported to increase hallucination elsewhere.\n- **The result** is a model that sounds like the domain and fabricates fluently about everything it did not\n  memorise. Supply the documents through retrieval with citations instead."}],"tradeoffs":["**Verification per step vs agent latency.** Checking every fact before acting makes long agents reliable but adds calls to each step; verify only the facts a side-effecting action depends on, and verify everything only when actions are irreversible.","**Grounding vs retrieval dependence.** Retrieval cuts factual hallucination sharply but moves the failure to retrieval quality; when the corpus lacks the answer, a model told to \"answer from context\" either abstains or bridges the gap, so a check is still needed.","**Label-free detection vs cost and coverage.** Sampling-based detection is model-agnostic and needs no labels, but multiplies cost by N and only catches instability, not confident errors the model learned consistently from training data.","**Low temperature vs diversity.** It reduces variance on factual tasks but does not fix a wrong argmax and hurts tasks that need diversity; the trade flips for brainstorming and creative generation.","**Abstention vs over-refusal.** Rewarding abstention reduces confident errors, but overdone it produces a model that refuses or hedges on answerable questions; users punish both failures, so tune the abstention rate to your task's cost asymmetry.","**Claim verification vs latency.** It gives precise, actionable numbers but adds latency and a second model that can be wrong; it earns its cost on high-stakes outputs and as an offline eval, rarely on every chat turn."],"pitfalls":["**Trusting the agent's own report of what it did.** Symptom: final answers say \"I verified the balance\" or \"tests pass\" with no matching tool call in the trace. Fix: check completion claims against the trace before replying, and fail the eval case when they do not match.","**Blaming the model for a retrieval failure.** Symptom: hallucinations cluster on questions whose top-k passages have low relevance scores or whose answers are not in the corpus at all. Fix: log retrieval scores and label whether the answer passage was retrieved before touching the prompt.","**Reporting a rate from an uncalibrated LLM judge.** Symptom: a judge that shares the generator's priors and a dashboard reading 2% while SMEs find 15%. Fix: measure judge agreement on SME labels first.","**Treating temperature 0 as \"hallucination off\".** Symptom: deterministic, repeatable fabrications that look like facts precisely because they are consistent. Fix: grounding and verification, not decoding.","**Scoring \"I don't know\" as a failure in evals and demos.** Symptom: after a round of prompt or fine-tuning iteration, abstentions vanish and confident errors rise. Fix: score abstention as correct on unanswerable items.","**Fine-tuning on unseen domain facts to \"teach it the knowledge\".** Symptom: a model that learned the format and a few facts and now fabricates fluently about the rest. Fix: supply the facts by retrieval.","**Accepting a cited answer without checking the citation.** Symptom: real document titles attached to statements they do not contain, since citation hallucination survives retrieval. Fix: verify each quoted span appears in the cited passage.","**Letting an agent invent tool names or arguments.** Symptom: \"function not found\" errors or calls with parameters that do not exist. Fix: validate every call against the live schema and reflect the error back."],"when":"You do not choose whether hallucination applies; you choose how much to spend against it per use case.\n- **Low-stakes creative or conversational features:** light measures, sources where available and a\n  temperature appropriate to the task.\n- **Extraction and summarisation:** ground every output in the input and measure faithfulness on a golden\n  set.\n- **Factual question answering:** retrieval with citations, an abstention path and offline claim-level\n  verification.\n- **Agents:** validate every tool call against the real schema and verify consequential actions before\n  executing them.\n- **Measure:** unsupported-claim rate, abstention rate on unanswerable items, calibration of confidence\n  against correctness, and per-category error analysis.\n- **Interviews:** explain the mechanism (objective, decoding, incentives), the faithfulness/factuality split\n  and the intrinsic/extrinsic subdivision, three mitigations with their costs, and why temperature 0 is not\n  a fix.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the grounding pipeline, adds an \"insufficient evidence\" output path, validates tool calls against schemas, and ships a claim-level faithfulness eval that runs in CI.","architect":"Sets the per-use-case hallucination budget and verification tier, decides where human review is mandatory, and makes abstention a first-class product state rather than an error.","fde":"Runs error analysis with customer SMEs to separate retrieval failures from generation failures, and calibrates the judge on their labelled examples before any hallucination rate appears in a report."},"check":{"q":"A customer reports that your RAG assistant \"hallucinates about 10% of the time\" on their policy\ndocuments. Walk through how you would diagnose and reduce it, and name one thing you would refuse to do.","a":"First get the failing examples labelled and split them. Did retrieval return the passage containing\nthe answer? If not, it is a corpus or retrieval problem: chunking, embeddings, missing documents. If it did,\ndid the model contradict the passage (intrinsic) or add detail the passage does not support (extrinsic)?\nBoth are faithfulness failures with different fixes. Was the question\nanswerable at all, in which case abstention is the correct output and the eval must reward it? Then fix\nretrieval first, since it is usually the largest share; require citations with quoted spans; add an\n\"insufficient information\" path; lower the temperature on the answer step; add claim-level verification for\nhigh-stakes categories; and calibrate the LLM judge against the SME labels before reporting any rate. I\nwould refuse to fine-tune the model on the policy documents as a knowledge fix, or to add \"do not\nhallucinate\" to the prompt and call it done."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does setting temperature to 0 not eliminate hallucination?","options":["Temperature only affects the first generated token","Greedy decoding still picks the most probable token from a distribution that may be wrong; it removes sampling variance, not the underlying knowledge gap","Most APIs ignore the temperature parameter","Temperature 0 disables retrieval"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Temperature rescales the distribution the sampler draws from. When the model does not know the answer, the peak of that distribution is already a fabrication, and taking the argmax reproduces it every time. That is why greedy decoding yields consistent, not correct, errors."},{"q":"In a RAG system, which observation most strongly indicates that a reported \"hallucination\" is actually a retrieval failure?","options":["The answer is longer than usual","The model was run at a low temperature","The retrieved passages do not contain the needed information, and the model's answer fills the gap with plausible detail","The answer contradicts a passage that was retrieved"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If the evidence was never retrieved, no generator could have produced a grounded answer, so the fix is in chunking, embeddings or corpus coverage. Contradicting a retrieved passage is the opposite case, a faithfulness failure of the generator."},{"q":"Why can post-training with human preferences or binary-graded benchmarks increase confident wrong answers?","options":["Reward models prefer short answers","Hedging and abstention are scored as failures, so the policy learns that a confident guess has higher expected reward than \"I don't know\"","RLHF removes the model's access to its training data","It raises the default sampling temperature"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When an abstention earns zero and a guess earns a reward whenever it happens to be right, the optimal policy guesses. Calibration measurably worsens under such training, which is why evals must give credit for correct abstention on unanswerable items."}]},{"key":"model-internals/reasoning-models-test-time-compute","sources":["Wei et al., 2022 — Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models","Wang et al., 2022 — Self-Consistency Improves Chain of Thought Reasoning in Language Models","Snell et al., 2024 — Scaling LLM Test-Time Compute Optimally Can Be More Effective Than Scaling Model Parameters","DeepSeek-AI, 2025 — DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning"],"why":"- **Compute per token is fixed.** A decoder-only transformer spends L layers, one pass, on every token.\n  Whether it completes \"2 + 2 =\" or \"the smallest prime factor of 8,633 is\", the depth is identical, so\n  problems needing more sequential steps than the layer stack can compose get a guess.\n- **Chain of thought adds passes.** Wei et al. (2022) showed that writing intermediate steps lets the model\n  use its own output as working memory, turning one hard prediction into many easy ones. Each emitted token\n  is another forward pass, so a longer trace is literally more compute spent on the problem.\n- **It became a scaling axis.** Self-consistency (Wang et al., 2022) majority-votes several chains;\n  accuracy rises because wrong paths disagree while correct ones converge. Coverage, the chance at least one\n  of N samples is correct, rises roughly log-linearly in N over a wide range, so anything that recognises a\n  correct answer, from a unit test to a reward model, can harvest it.\n- **It can beat a bigger model.** Snell et al. showed in 2024 that for a fixed compute budget, spending it\n  at inference can beat a larger model, with the best allocation depending on question difficulty. A bigger\n  base model is expensive to pre-train and still does fixed compute per token, so it does not remove the\n  constraint.\n- **Prompted reasoning is shallow, so it is trained.** Prompted chains imitate reasoning from pre-training\n  data, rarely backtrack and do not know when to stop. Reasoning models (OpenAI's o-series, DeepSeek-R1, the\n  extended-thinking modes of Anthropic and Google models) use RL on checkable maths, code and logic.\n  Rewarding the correct final answer is enough to learn exploring, self-verification and self-correction;\n  the DeepSeek-R1 report describes these emerging without demonstration.","how":"### Where test-time compute pays off in an agent\n| Agent step | Spend extra compute? | Strategy |\n|---|---|---|\n| Initial plan or task decomposition | yes | reasoning mode with a moderate budget |\n| Choosing among many tools with subtle differences | often | reasoning, or a cheap model with escalation |\n| Writing code or a patch | yes, if tests exist | parallel best-of-N selected by the tests |\n| Recovering after a failed tool call | yes | sequential revision with the error in context |\n| Parsing a tool result or formatting the answer | no | fast model, low temperature |\n\n### Tokens as computation\n- **Each generated token costs a forward pass,** about 2N FLOPs plus attention over the growing context, and\n  its result is written into the context where later steps read it through attention.\n- **A 2,000-token thinking trace** is therefore 2,000 sequential passes of extra computation with an\n  explicit scratchpad.\n- **Serial depth comes from the token dimension, not the layer dimension,** which is why chain of thought\n  provably extends what a fixed-depth transformer can compute.\n- **The exchange rate is explicit:** cost and latency grow linearly with trace length, and the trace\n  occupies context and KV-cache memory.\n\n### Four strategies for spending inference compute\n| Strategy | How | Best when |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Sequential | one path, more steps: longer chain of thought, self-critique, revision loops | easy questions where the first attempt is nearly right |\n| 2. Parallel | N independent samples at temperature above zero, aggregated by majority vote on the final answer (needs a canonical answer) or by a verifier or reward model choosing the best (best-of-N) | hard questions needing diverse attempts |\n| 3. Search | expand solutions step by step, score partial solutions with a process reward model, keep the best (beam or tree search over steps) | hard problems where whole samples rarely contain a fully correct path |\n| 4. Trained reasoning | the model internalises the first three in one long trace through RL | you control a budget rather than orchestrating samples |\n\n- **Difficulty decides.** Snell et al. found sequential revision wins on easy questions and parallel\n  sampling and search on hard ones; a difficulty-aware policy that picks per question was reported to be\n  several times (more than 4×) more compute-efficient than a best-of-N baseline.\n\n### Verifiers\n- **Outcome vs process reward models.** Outcome reward models score only the final answer; process reward\n  models score each step, and Lightman et al. found step-level supervision more reliable for maths.\n- **Use a ground-truth checker wherever one exists,** such as unit tests, a calculator or a symbolic solver:\n  it is exact and free.\n- **That is why maths and code improved most.** The reward there is verifiable, so RL has something clean to\n  optimise.\n- **Voting needs discrete answers.** For free-form outputs you fall back to an LLM judge or pairwise\n  comparison, which brings its own error rate.\n\n### How a reasoning model is trained, in outline\n1. **Start from a strong pre-trained model** and run RL on problems with checkable answers.\n2. **GRPO (DeepSeek-R1).** Sample a group of responses per prompt (say 16), score each with rule-based\n   rewards for a correct final answer and the required format, and give each an advantage equal to its\n   reward minus the group mean, divided by the group's standard deviation.\n3. **The policy is pushed** toward above-average responses and away from below-average ones.\n\n- **Why the group baseline matters.** PPO needs a learned value network as large as the policy to estimate\n  \"how good was this state\". GRPO replaces it with the other samples for the same prompt, which removes a\n  policy-sized model from memory and avoids training a critic on long, sparse-reward traces.\n- **Why rule-based rewards.** A learned scorer can be gamed over long RL runs, while \"does the answer equal\n  42\" or \"do the tests pass\" cannot.\n\n| Stage | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| R1-Zero | RL directly on the base model; trace length grew as the policy found checking its work raised reward, but traces mixed languages and were hard to read |\n| R1 cold start | a small supervised stage on curated long-reasoning examples before RL |\n| R1 later stages | supervised fine-tuning on rejection-sampled traces plus general data, then a final RL stage covering general helpfulness as well as reasoning |\n| Distillation | fine-tuning smaller models on the big reasoner's traces gives small models that reason far better than their size suggests, with gains concentrated on the distilled domains |\n\n### What you control at inference\n- **A thinking budget or effort level,** under vendor-specific names.\n- **Trace visibility:** whether it is returned, hidden or summarised.\n- **Your own parallel sampling:** temperature and N, plus stopping rules such as an agreement threshold.\n- **Budget forcing (open weights).** Cap thinking by inserting the end-of-thinking marker, or extend it by\n  suppressing that marker and appending a word such as \"Wait\" (the s1 work). Crude but direct for tracing\n  an accuracy-versus-tokens curve.\n- **Costs to remember:** thinking tokens are billed as output tokens, the whole trace precedes the first\n  answer token, and the trace competes with your prompt for context.\n\n### Limits\n- **Overthinking.** On easy questions long traces add cost without accuracy and can even hurt; some models\n  loop in very long traces.\n- **Faithfulness.** The visible trace is not guaranteed to reflect the factors that actually drove the\n  answer (Turpin et al., 2023); it is not an audit log.\n- **Domain.** Gains concentrate where answers are verifiable; on open-ended writing or questions\n  bottlenecked by knowledge rather than reasoning, retrieval helps more than thinking.\n- **Variance.** Results fluctuate between runs more than single-shot answers, so evaluate with several runs\n  and report pass@k or pass^k, not one number.\n\n| Metric | Meaning | Right when |\n|---|---|---|\n| pass@k | probability at least one of k attempts succeeds | a verifier will pick the winner |\n| pass^k | probability all k attempts succeed | a user or agent gets one shot each time and must be reliable |","example":"### Self-consistency with early stopping and an escalation signal\n- **Why voting helps.** If a single chain is right 70% of the time and wrong chains scatter across\n  different values, a majority of five is right well above 70%. With independent samples and a binary\n  outcome the figure is about 84%, and scattered wrong answers make it better.\n- **Agreement doubles as confidence.** High agreement stops spending early; low agreement routes the\n  question to a stronger model or a human, a practical version of difficulty-aware allocation.\n\n```python\nimport collections, re\n\ndef llm(prompt, temperature=0.7):\n    \"\"\"Call your model provider here and return the text.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n\ndef solve(question, n_max=8, n_min=3, stop_agreement=0.8):\n    prompt = f\"{question}\\nReason step by step, then finish with a line 'ANSWER: <value>'.\"\n    votes, spent = collections.Counter(), 0\n    for i in range(1, n_max + 1):\n        out = llm(prompt)\n        spent += len(out.split())                       # proxy; use the provider's token count in practice\n        m = re.search(r\"ANSWER:\\s*(.+)\", out)\n        if m:\n            votes[m.group(1).strip().rstrip(\".\")] += 1   # normalise the answer string before voting\n        if i >= n_min and votes:\n            best, count = votes.most_common(1)[0]\n            if count / i >= stop_agreement:              # paths converged: stop spending\n                return {\"answer\": best, \"agreement\": count / i, \"samples\": i, \"tokens\": spent}\n    best, count = votes.most_common(1)[0] if votes else (None, 0)\n    return {\"answer\": best, \"agreement\": count / n_max, \"samples\": n_max, \"tokens\": spent,\n            \"escalate\": count / n_max < 0.5}             # low agreement: route to a stronger model or review\n\nprint(solve(\"A train leaves at 09:40 and arrives at 13:05. How many minutes is the journey?\"))\n# Correct: 205 (3 h 25 min). A common slip is 275 (4 h 35 min, subtracting the minutes the wrong way\n# round). If, say, 3 of the first 3 samples say 205, agreement is 1.0 and the loop stops after 3 calls;\n# a 2-vs-1 split (0.67) keeps sampling, which is where the extra compute goes.\n```\n\n- **Prefer a verifier when one exists.** Run the unit tests on each sampled patch and return the first that\n  passes, which turns coverage directly into accuracy.\n\n### Agent scenario: thinking on every step versus planner-only\nA 15-step agent: 3 planning or recovery steps and 12 routine tool steps. Each step emits ~200 answer\ntokens; thinking adds ~2,000 tokens where enabled. Decode speed is an illustrative 50 tokens/s.\n\n| Policy | Output tokens per task | Decode time per task | Extra context if traces stay in history |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Thinking on all 15 steps | 15 × 2,200 = 33,000 | ~660 s | up to 30,000 tokens by the last step |\n| Thinking on the 3 planning steps | 3 × 2,200 + 12 × 200 = 9,000 | ~180 s | up to 6,000 tokens |\n\n- **Reading.** Planner-only thinking cuts output tokens about 3.7×. Run both policies on the agent eval: if\n  task success is the same, the extra 24,000 tokens per task bought nothing.\n- **Context.** Dropping earlier traces from history (where the API allows it) keeps the transcript and the\n  KV cache from growing by the trace length every step.\n\n### pass@k, pass^k and the GRPO advantage, in numbers\nRunnable, pure Python. The pass@k form is the standard unbiased estimator from n runs with c successes;\npass^k is computed the same way for \"all k succeed\".\n\n```python\nfrom math import comb\nfrom statistics import mean, pstdev\n\ndef pass_at_k(n, c, k):      # at least one of k succeeds\n    return 1 - comb(n - c, k) / comb(n, k)\n\ndef pass_hat_k(n, c, k):     # all k succeed\n    return comb(c, k) / comb(n, k)\n\nn, c = 10, 7                 # 10 runs of one task, 7 succeeded\nfor k in (1, 3, 5):\n    print(f\"k={k}: pass@k={pass_at_k(n, c, k):.2f}  pass^k={pass_hat_k(n, c, k):.2f}\")\n\nrewards = [1] * 4 + [0] * 12                   # group of 16 samples, 4 correct\nmu, sd = mean(rewards), pstdev(rewards)\nprint([round((r - mu) / sd, 2) for r in (1, 0)])   # advantage of a correct and a wrong sample\n```\n\n| k | pass@k | pass^k | Reading |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 0.70 | 0.70 | identical for one attempt |\n| 3 | 0.99 | 0.29 | a verifier almost always finds a winner; three clean runs in a row are unlikely |\n| 5 | 1.00 | 0.08 | great for best-of-5 with tests, poor for an agent that must succeed every time |\n\n- **GRPO advantages for that group:** a correct sample gets about +1.73 and a wrong one about −0.58, so the\n  rare correct answers are pushed up hard. Implementations differ in details such as the standard\n  deviation estimator.","patterns":["**Think per agent step, not per agent.** Enable reasoning (or a larger budget) on planning, tool selection among many similar tools and error recovery, and keep routine parsing and formatting steps on a fast setting. Log thinking tokens and latency per step type, and compare task success against all-steps thinking on the agent eval.","**Difficulty-aware routing.** Send traffic to a fast model first and escalate on low agreement, a failed verifier or a failed schema check, as in `solve` above. Monitor escalation rate, cost per solved task and p95 latency per tier.","**Set the thinking budget per route, not globally.** Use the vendor's knob, for example `budget_tokens` in Anthropic extended thinking or `reasoning_effort` on OpenAI reasoning models, and pick the knee of the accuracy-versus-tokens curve measured on each route's eval set. Leave `max_tokens` room for both the trace and the answer.","**Verifier-gated best-of-N for code.** Sample N patches in parallel, run the test suite on each, and return the first that passes. Monitor pass@1 against pass@N and track flaky or missing tests, because weak tests let wrong patches through.","**Account for thinking tokens explicitly.** Log thinking and answer tokens separately in cost dashboards, and keep earlier traces out of agent history unless the API requires them (Anthropic, for example, needs the thinking block passed back with tool results inside the same turn), so they do not fill the context window.","**Evaluate with repeated runs.** Run each eval item several times and report pass@k for verifier-backed flows and pass^k for one-shot user-facing flows, with means over seeds rather than a single run.","**Keep prompts plain for reasoning models.** Drop chain-of-thought instructions and reasoning few-shots that several vendors advise against, and A/B the plain prompt against the old one on the eval set."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agents plan, choose tools and recover from errors, which is exactly the\n  multi-step work reasoning helps with. But thinking tokens are billed, delay the first answer token and\n  fill the transcript, and an agent pays that on every step it is enabled.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Thinking-budget or effort settings on the model API (for example\n  `budget_tokens` or `reasoning_effort`), rules for passing thinking blocks back with tool results,\n  best-of-N with tests in coding agents, and pass^k in agent evals.\n- **What you control.** Which agent steps think and how much, whether you sample several attempts and\n  pick with a verifier, how old traces are kept out of history, and when to escalate to a reasoning tier.\n- **What you can skip.** GRPO maths and the R1 training stages, unless you train reasoners. Keep \"tokens\n  are compute\", the four strategies, and pass@k versus pass^k.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Planner steps in multi-step agents.** A reasoning setting on the decomposition step, a fast setting on\n  the tool calls that follow.\n- **Coding agents with tests.** Best-of-N patches selected by the test suite, escalating unsolved tasks to\n  a reasoning tier.\n- **Difficulty-aware routing.** A fast model answers first; low agreement or a failed verifier escalates\n  to a reasoning model.\n- **Agent evals.** Reporting pass^k for agents that must succeed every time, and pass@k where a verifier\n  picks the winner.","mini_project":"**Find the right thinking budget per agent step (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show where reasoning helps a small agent and where it only adds cost.\n- **Steps:** build a 5-step agent (plan, two tool calls, check, answer) on 30 tasks with checkable answers;\n  run it with thinking off, on for the plan step only, and on for all steps, three runs each; log task\n  success, thinking tokens and wall-clock per step.\n- **Done when:** a table shows task success (mean and pass^3), tokens and latency per policy, and you can\n  name the cheapest policy whose success is within run-to-run noise of the best (about one task in 30)."},"typed":{"type":"concept","misconceptions":["**\"Chain of thought is just the model explaining itself.\"** Each generated token is another forward pass whose result later steps read through attention, so a longer trace is literally more serial computation the answer can depend on. That makes it working memory, not a faithful explanation (see the audit-log point below).","**\"Turn reasoning on for the whole agent and it gets smarter everywhere.\"** Gains concentrate on planning, recovery and verifiable steps; on parsing or formatting, long traces add cost and can hurt. In the 15-step example, thinking on every step costs about 3.7× the output tokens of planner-only.","**\"The thinking trace is an audit log of why the agent acted.\"** Visible traces are not guaranteed to reflect the factors that drove the answer, so log tool calls, inputs and verifier results as the record instead.","**\"More samples always help.\"** Majority voting needs discrete answers and wrong paths that scatter; if the model is consistently wrong, more samples agree on the wrong value. Higher coverage (pass@k) becomes accuracy only when a verifier or a working vote can pick the correct sample.","**\"A high pass@k means the agent is reliable.\"** pass@k counts at least one success and fits best-of-N with a checker. An agent that gets one shot needs pass^k: 7 of 10 runs succeeding gives pass@5 of 1.00 but pass^5 of only 0.08."]},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"Question\"] --> S[\"Sample n_min chains\"]\n    S --> V{\"Verifier available?\"}\n    V -->|\"yes\"| T[\"Run tests on each sample\"]\n    T -->|\"one passes\"| OUT[\"Return answer\"]\n    T -->|\"none pass\"| ESC[\"Escalate: reasoning model or human\"]\n    V -->|\"no\"| A{\"Agreement at or above threshold?\"}\n    A -->|\"yes\"| OUT\n    A -->|\"no, budget left\"| MORE[\"Sample one more\"]\n    MORE --> A\n    A -->|\"no, n_max reached\"| ESC","caption":"Difficulty-aware allocation in practice: sample cheaply, stop when paths agree or a verifier passes, and\nescalate only the questions that stay contested."},"interview":[{"q":"What is test-time compute, and what are the main ways to spend it?","a":"- **Definition:** extra inference computation per query to get a better answer, which works because each\n  generated token is another forward pass with a scratchpad.\n- **Ways:** sequential (longer chains, revision), parallel (N samples with a vote or verifier), search over\n  partial solutions scored by a process reward model, or a trained reasoning model with a budget knob.\n- **Which wins:** sequential revision on easy questions, parallel and search on hard ones, per Snell et al."},{"q":"When would you report pass@k and when pass^k?","a":"- **pass@k** when a verifier picks the winner, such as tests choosing among k patches; it measures whether\n  any attempt succeeds.\n- **pass^k** when a user or agent gets one shot each time and must be reliable; it measures whether all k\n  attempts succeed.\n- **They diverge fast:** 7 successes in 10 runs gives pass@5 of 1.00 but pass^5 of about 0.08."},{"q":"Your agent got more accurate with a reasoning model but each task now takes four times as long. What do you change?","a":"- **Find the decisive steps:** log success and thinking tokens per step type; usually planning and error\n  recovery carry the gain.\n- **Think only there:** keep routine tool steps on a fast setting or a fast model, and measure task\n  success against the all-steps baseline.\n- **Trim the rest:** drop old traces from history where the API allows, and set budgets per step at the\n  knee of the accuracy-versus-tokens curve."},{"q":"How would you use test-time compute in a coding agent that has a test suite?","a":"- **Parallel best-of-N:** sample several patches and run the tests on each; the first that passes wins,\n  turning coverage into accuracy at near single-attempt latency.\n- **Escalate the misses:** tasks where no sample passes go to a reasoning model or a human.\n- **Guard the verifier:** weak or flaky tests let wrong patches through, so track pass@1 against pass@N and\n  review test coverage."},{"q":"After enabling a reasoning mode for all traffic, cost rose fivefold with no quality gain. How do you fix it?","a":"- **Segment the traffic:** most requests are probably simple extraction or chat, where long traces add cost\n  and latency without accuracy.\n- **Route by difficulty:** fast model first, reasoning tier only on low agreement, failed verifiers or\n  task types that showed gains on the eval.\n- **Tune the budget:** measure accuracy against thinking tokens per route and set the budget at the knee,\n  since quality is not linear in budget."},{"q":"Can you use a reasoning model's thinking trace as an audit log for its decisions?","a":"- **No.** The visible trace is not guaranteed to reflect the factors that drove the answer (Turpin et al.,\n  2023), so it can read soundly while a bias it never mentions drove the verdict.\n- **Instead** log inputs, outputs, tool calls and verifier results, and evaluate decisions for bias\n  directly."}],"tradeoffs":["**Smarter steps vs slower agents.** Thinking improves planning and recovery, but its cost and latency repeat on every enabled step of a long loop; it pays on the few decisive steps and rarely on routine tool calls.","**Accuracy vs cost and latency.** Accuracy on verifiable multi-step tasks rises with inference budget, but cost and latency rise linearly with tokens; the trade flips when the task is easy, high-volume or latency-bound, where a fine-tuned small model wins.","**Parallel vs sequential.** Parallel sampling keeps latency near one sample but multiplies cost by N; sequential long traces can need fewer total tokens for the same gain on some problems, but the latency is the bill.","**Verifier selection vs majority vote.** A verifier extracts far more from N samples than a vote, but needs a verifier: with tests or solvers it is nearly free, with an LLM judge you have added error and cost.","**Reasoning models vs plain tasks.** Large out-of-the-box gains on maths, code and planning, but traces are billed and consume context, and on extraction or classification they add cost with little gain.","**Distilled small reasoners vs teacher quirks.** Much of the capability cheaply, but they inherit the teacher's trace style and tendency to overthink, and gains are strongest on the distilled domains."],"pitfalls":["**Turning reasoning on for every request.** Symptom: cost per request rises several-fold and p95 latency explodes with no measurable quality change on the simple tasks that dominate traffic. Fix: enable it per route where the eval shows a gain.","**Treating the thinking trace as an explanation.** Symptom: a trace that reads soundly while the answer was driven by a bias the trace never mentions. Fix: audit inputs, outputs and decisions directly.","**Majority-voting free-text answers.** Symptom: near-zero agreement because no two strings match. Fix: normalise the answer or use a judge.","**Evaluating with a single run.** Symptom: swings of several points between runs of the \"same\" eval. Fix: report means over several seeds or pass^k.","**Forgetting the trace in context budgeting.** Symptom: truncated answers or length errors deep in agent loops as thinking tokens fill the window. Fix: budget context for trace plus answer and drop old traces.","**Loading a reasoning model's prompt with chain-of-thought instructions and reasoning few-shots.** Several vendors advise against it because it can degrade results. Fix: keep the prompt plain and let the model think.","**Assuming the budget knob is linear in quality.** Symptom: paying for 4× the tokens and getting the accuracy of 2× because nobody measured the accuracy-versus-tokens curve on the real task. Fix: measure the curve per route and set the budget at the knee."],"when":"- **Use in agents for:** planning, decomposition, choosing among similar tools, recovering from a failed\n  call, and code steps where tests can select among samples.\n- **Use when** the task has multi-step structure and a checkable, or at least judgeable, answer: maths, code\n  generation and debugging, planning inside agents, complex data transformations, and hard classifications\n  where a rationale changes the verdict.\n- **Measure** the accuracy-versus-tokens curve on your own eval at several budgets and sample counts, pick\n  the knee, and add difficulty-aware routing: a cheap model first, escalation on low agreement or a failed\n  verifier.\n- **Avoid for** latency-bound chat, simple extraction, or questions bottlenecked by knowledge, where\n  retrieval wins.\n- **Interviews:** explain why extra tokens are extra compute, contrast parallel and sequential strategies and\n  when each wins, sketch how R1-style RL works, and raise the faithfulness caveat unprompted.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements thinking budgets, sampling with early stopping and verifier gates, tracks thinking tokens in cost dashboards, and keeps traces out of the next prompt unless they are needed.","architect":"Decides which tasks earn a reasoning tier, defines routing between fast and reasoning models by difficulty signals, and sets latency and cost budgets per tier.","fde":"Shows a customer the accuracy-versus-cost curve on their own hard cases, so the choice between a bigger model, a reasoning mode and more samples is made with their numbers rather than a benchmark."},"check":{"q":"A team's coding agent solves 60% of tasks with a standard model. They can switch to a reasoning\nmodel at roughly five times the token cost, sample the standard model five times and pick with the test\nsuite, or fine-tune. How do you reason about the choice?","a":"Start from the verifier: they have unit tests, so best-of-five with the suite as selector is cheap\nand exact, coverage rises steeply with N, the five samples parallelise so latency stays near one attempt,\nand the cost is similar to the reasoning model's. Its weakness is incomplete tests, which let wrong patches\npass. The reasoning model likely raises single-shot accuracy and helps most on tasks needing long-horizon\nplanning, where sampling rarely finds a complete solution, and where tests are weak. The pragmatic design is\nto combine them: standard model first with test-based selection, escalation to the reasoning model on\ntasks that fail. Fine-tuning helps only if failures are stylistic or convention-related and there is data.\nDecide on pass@1, pass@5 and cost per solved task measured on their task set."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does writing out intermediate steps let a transformer solve problems it fails on when asked for the answer directly?","options":["The steps are copied from similar problems in the training data","Each generated token is another forward pass, so the trace adds sequential computation and an external working memory beyond the fixed per-token depth","It lowers the effective sampling temperature","Intermediate steps reduce the size of the KV cache"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A forward pass has fixed depth, so multi-step problems need more passes. Emitting tokens provides them, and attention lets later steps read earlier results. The trace grows the KV cache rather than shrinking it, and temperature is unchanged."},{"q":"When does parallel sampling with majority vote fail to help, even with many samples?","options":["When the problem is a maths word problem","When the temperature is above zero","When the model's errors are systematic, so wrong paths converge on the same wrong answer, or when answers are free text that cannot be matched","When the model is a reasoning model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Voting works because independent wrong paths disagree while correct ones agree. A shared misconception breaks that assumption, and free-text answers break the matching step; a verifier or a judge is needed in both cases."},{"q":"Which claim about a reasoning model's thinking trace is correct?","options":["It is a faithful record of the computation and can serve as an audit log","It is billed as output tokens, consumes context, and may not reflect the factors that actually drove the answer","It is free because it is discarded before the answer is produced","Longer traces always improve accuracy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Traces are generated tokens with all the usual costs, and research on chain-of-thought faithfulness shows they can omit the real reasons behind an answer. Longer is not always better; on easy tasks it adds cost and can reduce accuracy."}]}]});
